\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GMA COVER STORY AUTHOR REVEALS MIRROR DIAGNOSIS FROM HER NOVEL\b0\par\par\uc2 736 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TURBULENT START\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, AIRPORT, SINKHOLE, COHEN, ROAD, GAS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORMS, CALIFORNIA, WILDFIRES, BYLE, DOWD\par \par \uc2 CASTRO, MURDER, BLANCHE, CUBA\par \par \uc2 RIOTERS, TRUMP, FUND, HODGES, DUNN, FITZPATRICK, LANKFORD, TILLIS\par \par \uc2 REPORT, SCREEN, TIME, SCHEDULE\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , RODGERS, STEELERS, THUNDER, SPURS, \uc2 KNICKS\uc2 , CAVS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DROUGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAINFALL POTENTIAL\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM BUDWEISER COMMERCIAL AD\par \par \uc2 EBOLA, BIOHAZARD, FIRE, ENGINE, MT. EVEREST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN DOCTOR "FEELING GOOD"\par \par \uc2 SHARK, "LULU'S LAW", GRIBBINS, MACDONALD\par \par \uc2 ED, HUMANOID, ROBOT, CHINA, TSARYNNY\par \par \uc2 KNICKS\uc2 , SUPERFAN, WILES, MCBRIDE, TICKETS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD WARMTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MEMORIAL DAY TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TURBULENT START\par \par \uc2 MEMORIAL DAY, TRAVEL, SINKHOLE, THUNDERSTORMS, DELAYS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 MEMORIAL DAY ROAD TRAVEL, TRAFFIC, GAS PRICES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 39 MILLION AMERICANS TO HIT THE ROAD\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ, AVERY, CORDREY, MURDER-FOR-HIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MURDER-FOR-HIRE PLOT?\par \par \uc2 HORMONE RECEPTOR, RETATRUTIDE, CLINICAL TRIAL, WEIGHT LOSS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW WEIGHT LOSS DRUG\par \par \uc2 SHETTERLY, BREAST CANCER, "THE GULF OF LIONS"\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A little peak outside here in New York City. And we're back with our "GMA Cover Story."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHOR REVEALS MIRROR DIAGNOSIS FROM HER NOVEL\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Author Caitlin Shetterly sharing a stunning diagnosis, the exact same cancer that her character has in her latest novel. Sophie Flay has this story for us. Good morning, Sophie.\par \par \uc2 SOPHIE FLAY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Michael, good morning. The author's second novel was supposed to be fiction, instead she ended up writing her own future.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN SHETTERLY (AUTHOR "THE GULF LIONS: A NOVEL")\par \par \uc2 It is a shock. It feels like the rug has been pulled right out from underneath you.\par \par \uc2 SOPHIE FLAY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The narrator of Caitlin Shetterly's new novel, "The Gulf of Lions," is a woman living with breast cancer. The author herself later met with the same shocking disease.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN SHETTERLY (AUTHOR "THE GULF LIONS: A NOVEL")\par \par \uc2 I was diagnosed with the exact same cancer in the exact same breast as my character Alice.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHOR CAITLIN SHETTERLY TALKS STUNNING DIAGNOSIS\par \par \uc2 SOPHIE FLAY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Alice had stage 2A cancer while Caitlin's was 1B. Both were hormone positive. The 51-year-old author and Maine native researching her book for an entire year, including all the medical aspects.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN SHETTERLY (AUTHOR "THE GULF LIONS: A NOVEL")\par \par \uc2 I had meticulously researched cancer, this kind of cancer, this woman's cancer. And in it, I wrote this long before I had cancer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 SOPHIE FLAY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Breast cancer is the most common cancer in women who are being diagnosed at younger ages. The sharpest rise has been seen in those under 50, with steep increases among Hispanic and Asian Americans.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN SHETTERLY (AUTHOR "THE GULF LIONS: A NOVEL")\par \par \uc2 I wrote a book about a woman who doesn't just survive cancer, but she thrives after it. And that was really important to me.\par \par \uc2 SOPHIE FLAY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) "The Gulf of Lions" is about a mother recovering from breast cancer who takes her daughters on a transformative camping trip across France.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIFE IMITATING ART FOR AUTHOR CAITLIN SHETTERLY\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN SHETTERLY (AUTHOR "THE GULF LIONS: A NOVEL")\par \par \uc2 I wrote this character. But as soon as this happened to me, she became a best friend who had gone through this very similar thing. And that was very profound and meaningful to me.\par \par \uc2 SOPHIE FLAY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She grew tired of reading books to her two boys, where the mother was either dead or dying. Ultimately, she found comfort in her protagonist, Alice.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN SHETTERLY (AUTHOR "THE GULF LIONS: A NOVEL")\par \par \uc2 I remember thinking that if Alice can pack up her daughters and go to France and travel alone then, my gosh, I can do this too. And I had to love my life. And if nothing teaches you that any better than anything else, it's cancer.\par \par \uc2 SOPHIE FLAY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the same way that so many of us turn to a good book for comfort, the author says she turned to friends and family, saying, quote, "I had no idea I was worthy of such concentrated love." "The Gulf of Lions" is available now. Robin?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She is definitely worthy. So kind of her to share her story like that. Thank you, Sophie.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260604em5l0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT RULING FOR ABORTION PILL ACCESS MIFEPRISTONE CAN CONTINUE TO NE SENT BY MAIL\b0\par\par\uc2 334 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDFIRES RAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEARTLAND ON ALERT\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, HUMIDITY, STORMS, WIND, HAIL, LIGHTNING\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, XI, HIGH STAKES, IRAN, TRADE\par \par \uc2 HANTAVIRUS, QUARANTINE, MONITORED, HEWLETT, KORNFELD\par \par \uc2 RAIL, STRIKE, COUNTDOWN, COMMUTER, UNIONS, FIRE\par \par \uc2 CUBA, RADCLIFFE, BLACKOUTS, EMBARGO, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, \uc2 IRS\uc2 , FUND, TREASURY, ALLIES, TAXPAYERS, COMEY\par \par \uc2 MIFEPRISTONE, SUPREME COURT, RULING, MAIL\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you so much for that, Rachel. We're gonna turn now to the Supreme Court ruling that the abortion bill, Mifepristone, can continue to be sent by mail. Let's go to Elizabeth Schulze with the details. Good morning, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT RULING FOR ABORTION PILL ACCESS\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Michael. So this Supreme Court ruling does mean that the abortion pill will stay available by mail across the US, at least for now. The justices put on hold a decision by a lower appeals court that would have meant that doctors in every state could not prescribe Mifepristone via telemedicine, while this legal challenge plays out.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  did dissent from this decision. Mifepristone was approved as safe and effective by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  back in 2000, it's used about in two-thirds of all abortions in the US, also commonly used in miscarriages. Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe V. Wade we've seen 20 Republican led states ban or severely limit abortion access.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In Louisiana, which has a strict ban, is now trying to further restrict access to the abortion pill by mail nationwide, saying that it is not safe to take outside of the supervision of a doctor. About one in four people who get an abortion today obtain the pill via telemedicine. So for now, Supreme Court is preserving the status quo, but this is a legal battle that is it not fully over yet. George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260528em5f00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION DRUG BATTLE SUPREME COURT & ABORTION ACCESS\b0\par\par\uc2 368 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SPIRIT, SHUTDOWN, TRAVELERS, DUFFY, EMPLOYEES, AIRFARE\par \par \uc2 UNITED, COLLISON, TRUCK, LIGHT POLE, PASSENGERS,\par \par \uc2 WAR, STANDOFF, STRAIT, TRUMP, IRAN, GAS, PRICES\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, VICTIMS, INJURIES, MANHUNT, HEARNE\par \par \uc2 MISSING, SOLDIERS, TRAINING, SEARCH, HIKE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, ACCESS, MIFEPRISTONE, MAIL, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But hopefully they will be found soon. Jay, thank you. Now, the battle over abortion pills sent through the mail. Two manufacturers are asking the Supreme Court to weigh in after a federal appeals court temporarily blocked access. Elizabeth Schulze is in Washington with more for us. Good morning to you, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION DRUG BATTLE\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you, Robin. So the Supreme Court is now once again at the center of a legal battle over access to the abortion pill. Late Friday, that federal appeals court in Louisiana ruled that doctors across every state could no longer prescribe the pill, Mifepristone, by mail or telemedicine while a legal challenge plays out. So now two drug companies that make the pill are appealing to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They say that this lower court ruling causes immediate confusion and dramatic upheaval for patients across the country. Mifepristone was approved as safe and effective by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  back in 2000. It's used in about two thirds of all abortions in the US, and it's also commonly used in miscarriages. Louisiana and other states are trying to restrict its access. And since the Supreme court overturned Roe V Wade, we have seen 20 Republican led states now ban or severely limit all abortion access.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT & ABORTION ACCESS\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) While many Democratic led states have passed laws to protect providers who prescribe the abortion pill by mail. It is not clear if or when the Supreme Court will weigh in on this. It did already reject a similar attempt to restrict access to the pill two years ago. But the bottom line here this morning, patients nationwide right now cannot get the medication by mail unless the high court steps in. George.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260515em5400007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LIVELY & BALDONI SETTLE EPIC BATTLE PRIVATE SETTLEMENT OVER ACTRESS' RETALIATION CLAIMS\b0\par\par\uc2 597 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 IRAN, WAR, AIRSTRIKES, CEASEFIRE, TRUMP, GAS PRICES, UAE\par \par \uc2 UNITED, DUFFY, NEAR-COLLISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS IN NEAR CATASTROPHE\par \par \uc2 QUINN, SHOOTOUT, SECRETE SERVICE, NATIONAL MALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECRET SERVICE SHOOTOUT NEAR MALL\par \par \uc2 HANTAVIRUS, CRUISE SHIP, ROSMARIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIRUS SUSPECTED IN CRUISE SHIP DEATHS\par \par \uc2 SPIRIT AIRLINES\uc2 , FUEL COSTS, SHUTDOWN, BANKRUPTCY, SUMERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOCKWAVES FROM SPIRIT SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 LIVELY, BALDONI, SETTLEMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIVELY & BALDONI SETTLE EPIC BATTLE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There is late word tonight involving that bitter legal battle between actors Blake Lively and Justin Baldoni. They're dueling allegations over filming of the movie, "It Ends with Us." What just happened in this case tonight. And here's Aaron Katersky.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRIVATE SETTLEMENT OVER ACTRESS' RETALIATION CLAIMS\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) They came face-to-face in "It Ends with Us." One directing, both acting. Blake Lively and Justin Baldoni will not come face-to-face in a courtroom. Tonight, two weeks before trial, Lively and Baldoni's production company reached a settlement, ending one of Hollywood's highest profile feuds.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE LIVELY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I'm talking to you as a friend at this point.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Before filming started, Lively confided in Baldoni. Their relationship soured on set. She accused him of sexual harassment and launching a smear campaign to ruin her reputation. He denied it and sued her for defamation. A judge tossed that out. Tonight, both sides saying, "We acknowledge the process, presented challenges and recognize concerns raised by Ms. Lively deserve to be heard. We remain firmly committed to workplaces free of improprieties and unproductive environments." The settlement concludes a year and a half of contentious litigation that drew in other celebrities like Hugh Jackman, Gigi Hadid, and Taylor Swift. In this text, Swift celebrated the use of "My Tears Ricochet" in the movie writing, "Wow, I love how they use the song." Lively responded, "I'm obsessed with you."\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Terms of the settlement were not disclosed, David, but both sides said they hope it brings closure. They also called for a respectful environment online. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Aaron Katersky following this case. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION PILL ACCESS RESTORED\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  temporarily restoring access to the abortion pill, mifepristone, keeping it available through telehealth mail and pharmacies for now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A federal appeals court in Louisiana had ruled doctors in every state could no longer prescribe the pill by mail or telehealth. It's used in about two-thirds of all abortions in the US. Two drug companies appealing to the Supreme Court. The drug approved as safe and effective by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  back in the year 2000.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back tonight, disturbing images this evening. The small plane crash right into a building. Two people killed. We'll have more on this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also, outside New York, the massive warehouse fire forcing evacuations. We also have news on Dolly Parton tonight. She has canceled all of her shows in Vegas. And what she's now revealed herself about her health. And you'll hear from her in a moment. And tonight here, we remember legendary \uc2 Yankees\uc2  broadcaster John Sterling, 36 years behind the mic. He was one of a kind. And we'll listen to all those calls. In a moment here.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020260506em5400007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WORLD NEWS SATURDAY\b0\par\par\uc2 443 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a popular discount airline abruptly shutting down. What this means for air travel, and why this could be a very costly summer for drivers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPIRIT SHUTTERING SCRAMBLES FLYERS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 First, \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2 , its final flights landing overnight. Signs removed from airports. Passengers scrambling to rebook with other carriers. And more than 15,000 employees out of work. Why a last-minute deal involving a government takeover fell apart, and what the airline blames for its demise.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING PRESSURE ON U.S. ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Gas prices surging again. And now oil executives warning the worst may still be ahead. Their painful forecast for the summer driving season. Why prices could climb even if the Strait of Hormuz reopens.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IRAN: WAR WITH U.S. 'LIKELY' TOR RESUME\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Iran now saying the war with the US is likely to resume and the growing rift between the US and its allies. President Trump now withdrawing thousands of troops from Germany.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COURT BLOCKS ABORTION PILL ACCESS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, the major decision that blocks access to abortion pills sent by mail. A federal appeals court ruling that mail orders of mifepristone violate state abortion bans. That drug, one of the most common means of abortion, can now only be distributed in person or at clinics.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MOM CHARGED FOR SON'S ALLEGED E-BIKE CRASH\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, a deadly crash. Police say a 14-year-old on an e-bike allegedly hitting and killing a Vietnam veteran. Why the boy's mother is now charged with aggravated manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 16 ARRESTED IN $1 MILLION AUTO THEFT RING\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Gone in seconds. Stunning images of what authorities are calling a million-dollar theft ring. Teams allegedly stripping wheels, rims and catalytic converters. The DA saying they worked as quickly as a pit crew. Sixteen people now facing charges.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DRAMATIC PLAYOFF FINISHES\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Down to the wire in the \uc2 NBA\uc2  playoffs. The game-winning overtime shot while LeBron and the Lakers move on to round two.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURPRISE PASSENGER DELAYS FLIGHT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The surprise passenger buckled in. Why this robot caused a big delay for one flight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICA STRONG\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And "America Strong" tonight. He's keeping Americans safe in the sky at 90 years young. The big surprise and what he says keeps him going.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News world headquarters in New York, this is "World News Tonight."\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020260505em5200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SNOOKI OPENS UP SNOOKI TALKS CERVICAL CANCER UPDATE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,479 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, CASON, REARDON, EDWARDS, DROUGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDFIRE EMERGENCY IN THE SOUTHEAST\par \par \uc2 NAVY, PHELAN, IRAN, HEGSETH, CEASEFIRE, TRUMP, POLL\par \par \uc2 SPIRIT, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 PATEL, REPORTER, "NEW YORK TIMES", "THE ATLANTIC"\par \par \uc2 REFINERY, CHEMICAL, GAS, LEAK\par \par \uc2 WALES, TOURISTS, COLLINS, RESCUE, WAVES, CLIFF\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , \uc2 PISTONS\uc2 , MAGIC, THUNDER, SUNS, \uc2 KNICKS\uc2 , HAWKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "DANCING WITH THE STARS"\par \par \uc2 TRAIN, CRASH, VRABEL, COUNSELING, MARINERS, CATCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DENMARK TRAIN CRASH\par \par \uc2 AIRFARE, KIRBY, SUMERS, TIPS, CRUISE, STARLINK\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , DRAFT, LOVE, MENDOZA, RAIDERS, MCFARLAND\par \par \uc2 QUIN, BAILEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIDEO CLIP FROM LAKE TAHOE, CALIFORNIA\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, DROUGHT, SOUTHEAST, SMOKE PLUMES\par \par \uc2 RIVERA ORTEGA, DEPORTATION, IMMIGRATION, SERRANO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WIFE OF ARMY SERGEANT DETAINED\par \par \uc2 PEABODY AWARD, ABC NEWS, JOURNALISM, 2025 WILDFIRE\par \par \uc2 "DANCING WITH THE STARS," HIGGINS, MILLER\par \par \uc2 POLIZZI, CERVICAL CANCER, EARLY DETECTION, PAP SMEAR, VACCINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOOKI OPENS UP\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, now to our "GMA Cover Story." "Jersey Shore's" Nicole "Snooki" Polizzi sitting down to talk about her recent cervical cancer diagnosis. It's an ABC News exclusive. I know, Lara, you've been talking about it.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I sure did talk to her. Nicole or Snooki, as America knew her quickly became a reality darling with her signature pouf hairstyle and her wild antics. The show "Jersey Shore" ratings juggernaut for \uc2 MTV\uc2 . And now, more than a decade after exploding onto the scene, the mom of three is opening up about her new reality with cervical cancer and an important message for other women about their health.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I've known you a very long time. You're a mom of three.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And with a real reality check that you're brave enough to share with others.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah. So, it took me a while. I feel like I need to share it because nobody talks about this.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this is cervical cancer.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) They were the biggest reality stars on the planet, making several detours to "GMA" on their way to the "Jersey Shore."\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 Party's here.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Nicole "Snooki" Polizzi, known for being the life of the party and self-described meatball. But today, the reality star is all grown up and facing a very real-life challenge.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 And it came back, stage one cervical cancer.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You must have been terrified.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 I was terrified. I was hysterically crying in my car, like, what am I going to do? I have three kids. I got to do my will. I haven't done my will yet. I'm, like, thinking about all these things.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CERVICAL CANCER\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) According to the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2 , over 13,000 new cases of cervical cancer will be diagnosed this year alone. It's most commonly found in women between the ages of 35 and 64, and almost universally, the result of a human papillomavirus infection, which can largely be prevented with a vaccine.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOOKI TALKS CERVICAL CANCER UPDATE\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nicole, who is unvaccinated, believes she could have avoided the diagnosis had she gone to her doctor recommended follow-up visits.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 I just kept putting it off. I didn't like it. I would make it a year and a half and then he calls me again and he's screaming in my voicemail, "Nicole, you got to get this done."\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you have a lot of regret?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 I do. Like, why were you so scared?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nicole recently underwent a PET scan, where the results thankfully showed her cancer had not spread. However, the doctor recommended Nicole undergo a hysterectomy to avoid the possibility of its return.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 No, we're not putting off any more appointments.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because I will call you myself.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 They're already yelling at me to schedule the hysterectomy, which I didn't.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I will yell at you.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 I'm traveling.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know, but this is your life.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 No, I know. I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You are the mom of three beautiful children who adore you.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 I know. I'm not going to lie. I'm scared.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know, and I understand that. But you know what's going to be more scary if you don't do it.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 It escalates.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If you get this hysterectomy, do the doctors feel like you will be way ahead of the curve?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How do you feel about the idea of getting a hysterectomy? You're a very young woman.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 I hate it. When she first said we have to do this or it's going to escalate to stage 2. I know I'm done with kids. Just knowing that you can't, I don't know, it made me feel like I'm not a mom or I don't know a lot of emotions went into mourn.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm sure you mourn. You mourn the loss of your ability to have more children.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Even though I'm done.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do they grasp that mom's going through this?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah. And they keep asking, are you dying? And I'm like, no, I'm not dying. I'm just sick and I need to figure it out, and then mommy will be fine.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How did your castmates react when they heard the news?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 Oh, they were freaking out. I consider them my, my brothers and sisters.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And now, Snooki and her eight roomies, as she affectionately calls them, will take one more ride to the "Jersey Shore."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOOKI TALKS "JERSEY SHORE"\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 I feel like us as a cast, we always said, we'll continue filming until we're in a nursing home. Like we love filming. We love so...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, it's not over for you guys.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 It's not over for us. We don't know where we're going yet. But we had a great relationship with MTV. It's bittersweet, but I feel like we still got more.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How has "Jersey Shore" shaped you?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's my whole life. It's shaped who I am today. So, yeah, I'm so glad that it happened because I can't picture my life anything else.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What would you tell the Snooki we first met all those years ago on "Jersey Shore" about your life today and what you're going through?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SNOOKI POLIZZI ("JERSEY SHORE")\par \par \uc2 Keep shining, girl. Live your life, but also stay in your doctor's appointments. Keep going to your doctor's appointments.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know what, she was so honest and it's great advice to all women. And Cancer Society recommends routine pap smears starting at age 21 to screen for cervical cancer with repeat testing every three to five years, depending on your age and your prior testing results. The HPV vaccination is also recommended for early prevention, and you can see more of my conversation with Snooki, Nicole tonight on "Nightline." And the new season of "Jersey Shore: Family Vacation" premieres Thursday, May 7th on MTV. And you heard it. They're not done yet. Filming until the nursing home.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I can just imagine that.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She's, she's lovely.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She is, and she speaks her mind.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And I think opening up is really going to help a lot of women so...\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good advice for other people think about.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Lara.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260505em4n0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMERICA STRONG CHERYL LADD REVEALS BREAST CANCER BATTLE\b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , CEASEFIRE, POPE LEO, MURPHY, THREATS, IRAN, WAR\par \par \uc2 IRAN, WAR, KITTLESON, KIDNAPPING, RELEASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IRAN VOWS TO RETALIATE ACROSS REGION\par \par \uc2 SCHOOL SHOOTING, MOORE, MCKEE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRINCIPAL INJURED IN HIGH SCHOOL SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 ICE, SHOOTING, HERNANDEZ, IMMIGRATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FBI\uc2  PROBES ICE-INVOLVED SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 SCHOOL BUS, HAMPTON, TRAIN, BREEDEN, ARREST, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BUS DRIVER CHARGED IN TERRIFYING INCIDENT\par \par \uc2 HOOKER, BAHAMAS, SEARCH, BAHAMAS, AYLESWORTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEARCH FOR AMERICAN WHO FELL OVERBOARD\par \par \uc2 ARTEMIS II, IMAGES, WISEMAN, GLOVER, PRODUCT PLACEMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRIKING IMAGES FROM HISTORIC FLYBY\par \par \uc2 RIP CURRENT, JENNINGS, GIBSON, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FATHER DROWNS SAVING CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 WEST, WINTER FESTIVAL, BANNED, LADY GAGA, RESPIRATORY INFECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE INDEX\par \par \uc2 "CHARLIE'S ANGELS" JACKSON, LADD, SMITH, CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: #AMERICASTRONG\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, "America Strong." The actresses from "Charlie's Angels" and what they have faced together.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, 50 years after their iconic show, the incredible bond between the stars of "Charlie's Angels." They gathered overnight in Hollywood to mark the 50th anniversary of "Charlie's Angels." Jaclyn Smith, Kate Jackson and actress Cheryl Ladd who stepped in for Farrah Fawcett. It was Cheryl Ladd who revealed last night in front of the crowd that she has battled breast cancer. "Mine was an aggressive form," she said, thanking her husband and saying, "I had wonderful doctors, but I was bald for quite a while. It was a humbling experience." With her very personal reveal last night, we now know that all three angels have battled breast cancer, a bond the women now share.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Actress Kate Jackson is a two-time breast cancer survivor and an advocate for early detection. And Jaclyn Smith, who underwent a lumpectomy after her breast cancer diagnosis, saying Cheryl Ladd "was so brave, the first thing I did was send her my wigs." Adding, "Early detection is key." Crediting "the power of girlfriends." All three co-stars saying they miss Farrah Fawcett, who died of cancer. But overnight, on the red carpet, it was a celebration of 50 years since their show and of their bond long after that show went off the air, telling our Chris Connelly, the friendship is what they carry with them.\par \par \uc2 KATE JACKSON (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 These two ladies are, are my sisters.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN SMITH (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Time didn't change the friendship or the kinship.\par \par \uc2 CHERYL LADD (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 We were all such individual women. But women who really cared for each other. Just as the show was, we were there for each other.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) An iconic show and a bond all these years later. I'll see you tomorrow. Good night.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020260414em470000b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GMA COVERY STORY: AMANDA PEET TALKS BREAST CANCER DIAGNOSIS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,575 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 March 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 LAGUARDIA, COLLISION, PLANE, TRUCK, DEAD, INJURIES\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, TSA, AIRPORTS, LINES, ICE, HOMAN, TRAVELERS\par \par \uc2 AIRPORTS, ICE, DEPLOYMENT, CHAOS, SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 IRAN, STRAIT, TRUMP, ULTIMATUM, ATTACKS, ISRAEL\par \par \uc2 HAWAII, RAIN, FLOODING, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 MUELLER, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , 9/11, SPECIAL COUNSEL, DEATH, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 METEORITE, TEXAS, SKIES, PLUMMET, HOME, JAMES, BETTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL TIME MARCH RECORDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TIMBERLAKE, DWI, VIDEO, ARREST, POLICE\par \par \uc2 CAR, PURCHASE, MIKULA, COSTS, GOODFRIEND\par \par \uc2 TOURNEY, SWEET 16, UPSETS, MEN'S, WOMEN'S\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH TEMPERATURES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 CRASH, GAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY CRASH AT LAGUARDIA\par \par \uc2 PEET, BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're back now with our "GMA Cover Story." Actress Amanda Peet revealing her breast cancer diagnosis in a personal essay in "The New Yorker." Peet says it was caught early thanks to extra monitoring precautions she was taking, and Rhiannon Ally has the story.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "THE WHOLE NINE YARDS"\par \par \uc2 AMANDA PEET (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 It is you. Mister Tudeski, you don't understand. I'm one of your biggest fans.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMANDA PEET ON BREAST CANCER JOURNEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "FOR MANY YEARS, I'VE BEEN TOLD THAT I HAVE 'DENSE' AND 'BUSY' BREASTS - NOT AS A COMPLIMENT BUT AS A WARNING THAT THEY REQUIRE EXTRA MONITORING. I HAD BEEN SEEING A BREAST SURGEON EVERY SIX MONTHS FOR CHECKUPS.?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, actress Amanda Peet revealing a life changing cancer diagnosis in an essay she penned for "The New Yorker" titled "My Season of Ativan," named for the anxiety medication she was prescribed to confront her own health journey, coupled with managing her parents Hospice care. The 54-year-old chronicling her breast cancer diagnosis and treatment journey, saying, "For many years I've been told that I have dense and busy breasts, not as a compliment, but as a warning that they require extra monitoring. I had been seeing a breast surgeon every six months for checkups." Peet says, her doctor grew concerned after a routine scan and later performed a biopsy.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADDITIONAL TESTING TO LEARN ABOUT RECEPTOR STATUS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "...WHICH INDICATES HOW TOUGH YOUR STRAIN OF CANCER IS. IT'S LIKE DOGS..."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "YOU HAVE POODLES ON ONE END AND, ON THE OTHER, PIT BULLS."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXT FROM DOCTOR WHILE PREPARING FOR FATHER'S FUNERAL: "ALL POODLE FEATURES!"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMANDA PEET TEST RESULTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "I WAS HORMONE-RECEPTOR POSITIVE AND HER2 NEGATIVE." MORE FAVORABLE FOR TREATMENT\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Preliminary reports showing the emergence of a small tumor. The actress says she underwent additional testing to learn about her receptor status, which she says indicates how tough your strain of cancer is. It's like dogs, her doctor explained, "You have poodles on one end and on the other pitbulls." Pete would mourn the loss of her father over the course of her diagnosis. While preparing for his funeral, she says she got a text from her doctor with her results, giving her the good news, saying they were all poodle features. The mother of three says she was hormone receptor positive and HER2 negative, which are more favorable for treatment. A second mass was later discovered in the same breast by Peet's radiologist.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECOND MASS DISCOVERED IN SAME BREAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "...WE FOUND OUT THAT THE SECOND MASS WAS BENIGN, AND THAT I WOULD ONLY NEED A LUMPECTOMY AND RADIATION, NOT A DOUBLE MASTECTOMY OR CHEMO."\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We found out that the second mass was benign and that I would only need a lumpectomy and radiation, not a double mastectomy or chemo. Peet says the cancer was stage one and following radiation treatment, the actress says she received her first clear scan at the beginning of this year.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, ABC News Chief Medical Correspondent, Doctor Tara Narula, joins us now. And doc, I'm glad that she's okay. And she caught it early.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So what can women learn more about, what can they, how can they learn more about the breast cancer screening needs for themselves?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. First of all, this was a beautiful essay for anyone who hasn't read it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNDERSTANDING YOUR BREAST CANCER SCREENING NEEDS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But certainly when we think about screening, you want to understand what a woman's personal risk factor profile is. That's really what dictates how she gets screened. So there are different risk factors ranging from things like, are you a carrier of the BRCA gene to when did you have your first child to whether you have a personal or family history. What a lot of doctors and what patients can do is to use risk calculators to figure out your personal risk. And so you can go online. They're easy to use. There are several different kinds. And they will each ask you different things. So you may get a different risk based on which calculator you use. But when you plug in your information, you will get essentially either a five, a five year risk of breast cancer development, ten year or even a lifetime risk.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER SCREENING RECOMMENDATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AVERAGE RISK: MAMMOGRAM EVERY OTHER YEAR FROM AGES 40 - 74\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DENSE BREASTS: MAY BE OFFERED ADDITIONAL IMAGING\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Then based on that, we kind of tend to group women, average risk less than 15 percent lifetime risk of developing breast cancer, moderate risk 15-20 percent or high risk greater than 20 percent. If you are average or moderate risk, you tend to get screened with mammograms generally starting at the age of 40. If you are high risk, then your doctor will likely recommend mammograms, as well as additional supplemental testing like MRI, for example.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dense breast tissue.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This changes the game for a lot of women.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It does, and so many women don't know about this.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW SCREENINGS DIFFER FOR DENSE BREASTS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But it's really important. So dense breasts what is this? Essentially the breast is a combination of fatty tissue and fibrous and glandular tissue. Depending on what the breakdown is that you have of those particular components, you either have dense breasts or non-dense breasts. The more fatty it is less dense, the more fibrous and glandular more dense. We grade them A, B, C, D. If you are a C or D, meaning more of that fibrous or glandular tissue, you are going to have be considered to have dense breasts. Now, as of September 2024, all mammogram reports are required to report whether you have dense breasts or not. What is the problem with dense breasts?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They are both associated with a higher risk of developing breast cancer, but also they make it much harder to find cancer on mammograms. Dense breasts look white on the mammogram.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yep.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So do cancer lesions or masses. So it's hard to differentiate that. So it is really important if you have dense breasts. Many times doctors will recommend a 3-D mammogram as opposed to a 2-D mammogram and also potentially supplemental testing like MRIs. I have to tell you for, for me just personally, no history, I got a report of dense breasts last year and went from getting just regular mammograms to now every six months, a mammogram and an MRI, a mammogram and an MRI with dense breasts being the only risk factor for me.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Based on that risk calculator that my doctor did.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's on that calculator.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's important information.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is there. And I'm so grateful in 2007, before all these guidelines, I was diagnosed with breast cancer. The mammogram did not detect it, but fortunately my doctor had ordered the additional screening because of my dense breast tissue, and it had not been for that. They would not have detected the cancer.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's what's so important.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And men should get tested as well.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Men can get breast cancer too. We should not forget that.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. We both have brothers who have gone through that.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you so much, Doctor Narula.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's go now to Ginger.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260407em3n00061\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PRESIDENT TRUMP SUGGESTS "TAKING CUBA WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF REVEALS BREAST CANCER DIAGNOSIS\b0\par\par\uc2 456 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, FORECAST, SNOW, POWER OUTAGE\par \par \uc2 BARKER, DEGS, BARKER, DHS, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN, FUNDING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR STORM GROUNDS THOUSANDS OF FLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 IRAN, WAR, ISRAEL, LARIJANI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IRAN LAUNCHES NEW STRIKES IN MIDDLE EAST\par \par \uc2 IRAN, WAR, ECONOMY, OIL PRICES, STRAIT OF HORMUZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POTENTIAL ECONOMIC IMPACT OF WAR WITH IRAN\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY, VACCINES, CHILDREN, RACINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL JUDGE TEMPORARILY BLOCKS CHILDHOOD VACCINE OVERHAUL\par \par \uc2 CUBA, BLACKOUT, OIL, TRUMP, WILES, BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TRUMP SUGGESTS "TAKING CUBA\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to Cuba, facing its third major blackout over the past four months. Cuba's energy minister claims they're making progress towards restoring power as the country experiences an energy and economic crisis intensified by President Trump's oil blockade. Senior political correspondent Rachel Scott is at the White House. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, good morning to you. And three weeks into this war with Iran, and the President is now setting his sights on Cuba, floating the idea of taking it over saying, quote, "I think I could do anything I want with it."\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It comes, as you mentioned, as the country deals with an island-wide blackout after Cuba's energy grid collapsed. The Cuban government blaming the United States for food and gas shortages after the US cut off access to Venezuelan oil. But the threats by President Trump are just the latest example of a more aggressive foreign policy than the President originally campaigned on. He pledged to keep America out of foreign conflicts, but it was just months ago that the United States captured Venezuelan dictator Nicolas Maduro.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just weeks ago, the United States launched war in the Middle East, and the President has also threatened to seize control of not only Cuba, but also Greenland, too. George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Rachel. Meantime, the President also announced that his chief of staff, Susie Wiles, is facing a breast cancer diagnosis?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF REVEALS BREAST CANCER DIAGNOSIS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, George. And this is the President's most trusted adviser, both the President and Susie Wiles announcing that Wiles is, has now been diagnosed with an early stage of breast cancer. Wiles says that she is encouraged by this early diagnosis and that she will continue to work here at the White House as she receives treatment. Lawmakers on both sides of the aisle wishing her well this morning, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So do we. Rachel Scott, thanks very much. Robin?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hope she makes a full recovery.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260402em3h0005r\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WH CHIEF OF STAFF HAS BREAST CANCER TRUMP CALLS SUSIE WILES "AN AMAZING FIGHTER\b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 March 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, FORECAST, STORM, TORNADO, WINDS\par \par \uc2 STAFFING, TSA, BARKER, MACK, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TSA STRAINS WORSEN TRAVEL WOES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , PISTORIUS, STARMER, IRAN, WAR, SIMMONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP URGES NATO, CHINA TO HELP\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY, VACCINE SCHEDULE, RACINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RFK JR. BLOCKED ON VACCINE SCHEDULE\par \par \uc2 WILES, BREAST CANCER, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WH CHIEF OF STAFF HAS BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, one of President Trump's closest advisers, Chief of Staff Susie Wiles, revealing her battle with breast cancer, and what she's saying tonight about millions of other women who have battled this, too. Here's Rachel Scott.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP CALLS SUSIE WILES "AN AMAZING FIGHTER\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, President Trump announcing White House Chief of Staff, Susie Wiles, one of his closest and most trusted advisers, is battling, quote, "early-stage breast cancer."\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 She's an amazing person and amazing fighter and she announced a minor difficulty, which will be in good shape, good shape for, she'll be around for a long time.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President saying that as Wiles undergoes treatment, she will be spending virtually full time at the White House.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 She's going to take care of it immediately as opposed to waiting. Let's do it now. I said, do it immediately, because with that particular ailment, the faster the better. That's the only thing I know about it. The faster, the better.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The 68-year-old Wiles, the first woman to serve as White House chief of staff saying, quote, "Nearly one in eight women in the United States will face this diagnosis. Every day, these women continue to raise their families, go to work, and serve their communities with strength and determination. I now join their ranks."\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Susie Wiles says that she has already started her treatment and will continue to work here at the White House, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and with powerful words for all of the other women fighting this, too. Thank you, Rachel. When we come back tonight in New York City, the deadly inferno, a massive fire breaking out.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Four people killed, including a child. Also tonight, the daring Coast Guard rescue, and you'll see it on the ice. The ice actually breaking apart in the middle of the rescue. Dolly Parton's first public appearance in months, and what she's now revealed about her health. You'll hear straight from her in a moment. And the Oscars, the best actor, his Oscar in hands. Celebrating with his mom right there, and then where he headed after. It sounded pretty good to us. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020260319em3g00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW REPORT ON WOMEN & HEART DISEASE WOMEN OF COLOR ARE AT INCREASED RISK\b0\par\par\uc2 1,112 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARATHON STATE OF THE UNION\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ADDRESS, CLASHES, DEMS, COMBATIVE, SPANBERGER\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ADDRESS, ATTACKS, IRAN, DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 MIDDLE EAST, TRUMP, IRAN, MILITARY, TENSIONS\par \par \uc2 STORM, SNOW, OUTAGES, ROADS\par \par \uc2 EPSTEIN, TRUMP, ALLEGATIONS, DEMOCRATS, DOJ, \uc2 FBI\par \par \uc2 MEXICO, AMERICANS, VIOLENCE, TRAVEL, SHELTER, JOHNSON, BUCARO\par \par \uc2 KIDNAPPING, RESCUE, HERNANDEZ, VOLLMERT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DAYTIME WARMING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH TEMPERATURES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE, ABDUCTION, PLEA, VIDEO, REWARD, DONATION\par \par \uc2 WALSH, GUTHRIE, REWARD, INVESTIGATORS, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 SNOW, ICE, ROADS, DRIVING, DANGER, FORD\par \par \uc2 LAVONTE, SINGING, MAILMAN, GRANDMOTHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE WEATHER DANGER\par \par \uc2 STATE OF THE UNION, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, SNOW, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 HEART, DISEASE, WOMEN\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're back now with our "GMA" cover story on heart disease. It remains the leading cause of death in women in the United States, and the \uc2 American Heart Association\uc2  is out with a new report this morning predicting cardiovascular disease in women will rise sharply over the next 25 years. Our chief medical correspondent and cardiologist Dr. Tara Narula is here with more for us. And doc, there were some major findings in this report. What is it saying?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW REPORT ON WOMEN & HEART DISEASE\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, so as you said, this was a statement by the \uc2 American Heart Association\uc2  sort of forecasting what's gonna happen with women, cardiovascular disease, and their risk factors over the next 30 years. And I gotta tell you, Michael, the picture is not pretty. And it really is a wake up call and a call to action for all of us to do better for what is the leading cause of death for women.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, currently in this country, about 45 percent of women live with some form of cardiovascular disease. By 2050, that will increase to about 60 percent of women.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When you look at what types of cardiovascular disease, it's all of it. It's coronary heart disease, heart failure, arrhythmias like AFib and stroke. When you break it down by risk factors, we see a sharp rises. So, for example, hypertension and obesity will affect 60 percent of women in 2050. Diabetes, about 25 percent. Is there any good news in this report? There was a little bit. So, total cholesterol is going to drop by about 20 percent. They did see or forecast improvements in smoking rates, exercise, and improvements in diet. But, again, overall, really a big problem in this country that so many women are not aware of.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, what are some of the disparities in the groups that are at a higher risk.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is so important too, Robin. So, all women of color are at increased risk of cardiovascular disease, but in particular, black women. They are at the highest risk of cardiovascular disease, again, all forms, and they are projected over the next 30 years to have the biggest increase. When you look at the risk factors for black women, hypertension and obesity will impact about 70 percent, 70 percent of black woman by 2050.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) About 30 percent will have diabetes. They are the least likely to have access to care, to get diagnosed, to get treated, and then most likely to die. When you look at Asian women, they were forecasted to have the biggest risk of increase of obesity. Hispanic women, the biggest increase in hypertension over the next 30 years. And even young girls, black girls for example, again, highest rates of hypertension and diabetes.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, why might this be? We talk a lot about social determinants of health. So, where you live, do you have access to food? Do you live in a food desert? The stress of discrimination and racism, poverty, all of this playing into what happens to your health. In addition to kind of the breakdown in terms of Asian, black, Hispanic they also looked at age groups. And sadly, the biggest increase that was forecasted for cardiovascular disease and risk factors was in 22 to 40-year-old women. Young women.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Young.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, right now, cardiovascular disease affecting about 25 percent of that age group of women. It's projected to increase to about 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There's so many challenges, but how do young woman get ahead of this?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, all women need to really get ahead of this, and we have to approach this in a multifaceted way. So, first of all, prevention is key. Prevention, prevention. How do we do that? We have to start by reaching women where they are. So, schools, by community organizations, in the OB-GYN offices, at the pediatrician. We have to do better with tailoring strategies to treat hypertension and diabetes in women of color, and we need to focus on studying stress, and hormones, and sleep, and GLPs in women and really understanding the biology.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We know that cardiovascular disease affects women differently than men. We do not have the same biology. And there are types of cardiovascular disease that women are more prone to, for example, so we need a better handle on that. Right now, women don't receive as much treatment as men, so we need to improve that. They're not going to get as much guideline recommended treatment as men do.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why is that?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, when they go to the hospital, they're more often told they have anxiety and stress. They're less likely to get sent for stress tests, less likely to get stints and procedures. So, you know, this is a big, big wake up call for all of us.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Boy, it sure is.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, shocking. What you're saying is really shocking. Well, thank you, doc.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We always appreciate you bringing us the best information.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sam?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260320em2p00064\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW REPORT ON HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY BENEFITS IS HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY RIGHT FOR YOU?\b0\par\par\uc2 1,219 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 February 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER PRINCE ANDREW RELEASED\par \par \uc2 ANDREW, ARREST, EPSTEIN, INVESTIGATION, KING, GIUFFRE, ROBERTS\par \par \uc2 ANDREW, ARREST, EPSTEIN, FERGUSON\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , IRAN, ULTIMATUM, MILITARY, REGIME, NUCLEAR\par \par \uc2 DEREMER, SEXUAL ASSAULT, "NYT", AFFAIR, DOJ, BANNER\par \par \uc2 OLYMPICS, HOCKEY, SKATING, LIU, KNIGHT, SLALOM, SHIFFRIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOOD THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "TOY STORY 5"\par \par \uc2 DANE, ALS, "GREY'S ANATOMY", LEGACY, TRIBUTES, ADVOCACY\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE, DISAPPEARANCE, CLUES, NANOS, TIPS, OCHOA\par \par \uc2 OLYMPICS, CURLING, MOP, VACUUM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD WARMTH\par \par \uc2 ANDREW, ARREST, EPSTEIN\par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, INDIANA, BEARS, STADIUM\par \par \uc2 HORMONE, REPLACEMENT, THERAPY, MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're gonna turn now to a "GMA" health alert. New research out this morning suggests hormone replacement therapy during menopause may have more benefits than previously known, and our chief medical correspondent Dr. Tara Narula is here with more. It's nice to see you, Dr. Narula.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, break down what this could mean.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rebecca, so many women want to know, is it safe to take hormone replacement therapy? So, this study was encouraging for a lot of women. It was a large study out of Denmark that looked at about 800,000 women starting at the age of 45 and followed them on average for about 14 years. 100,000 of those women got hormone replacement therapy. The average time they took it was about one to two years. They found that those women who got the hormone replacement therapy, there was no increased risk of death or dying from any cause.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They also saw no increased risk if the women started hormone replacement therapy older, at age 52 or even at age 57. There was no increased risk that, it didn't depend on the duration or length of use of the hormone replacement therapy, and there was no difference in terms of the types of deaths, cancer, stroke, or heart disease.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Specifically when they looked at transdermal hormone replacement therapy, so that's given with a patch or a gel, they actually saw a little bit of a lower risk of dying for the women who took that versus the women who did not get hormones. And then a small group of women, about 700, who had to have their ovaries removed who were ages 45 to 54 and got hormone replacement therapy, they had a reduction in their risk of dying by about 27 to 34 percent, compared to women who didn't have ovaries and didn't have take hormone therapy.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is a significant reduction.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and there are so many women going through menopause looking for solutions, something to make it easier. Would you recommend that everybody in that stage of life actually consider HRT?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IS HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY RIGHT FOR YOU?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, the answer is not a one size fits all approach. But really, what we have learned, and what has become the standard recommendation is that it depends on both timing and who you are, so it's personalized to you. And by timing we mean if you are within 10 years of menopause, or under the age of 60, or having moderate to severe menopausal symptoms, and are generally considered low risk, you are someone that definitely could consider taking or should consider taking hormone replacement therapy.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, really what you want to do is speak to your doctor, talk about your personal preferences, your family history. What is your risk? So, for example, did you have a risk of breast cancer or have a history of breast cancer, or blood clots, or even cardiovascular disease which might increase your risk of taking hormones.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, having that really personalized discussion is important. It's also important to know, Rebecca, there are lots of options for HRT. There are pills, and patches, and gels, and creams. There are two FDA approved non-hormonal treatments for women with menopausal symptoms. But, you know, we're finally at the point where we really have options for women, more women are taking them, which is a great thing. It's important to know it's, it's not a cure-all for everything. But, you know, I just saw a woman yesterday at my practice who told me it really changed her life. She's sleeping better, she's feeling better, and she, she never wants to go off of it because it's really improved her quality of life so much, so.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that is wonderful to hear, and I also love that you continuously say to advocate for yourself, talk to your doctor about these things. Don't be afraid to speak up on your own behalf.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right, especially when it comes to your quality of life. You know, women shouldn't have to suffer if they don't have to. And so if there's something out there that can help, have that conversation.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks, Dr. Narula, as always. We appreciate it. Coming up here in our "GMA" morning menu, can the royal family and the monarchy survive this scandal? We hear from Queen Elizabeth's former press secretary.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROYAL FAMILY IN CRISIS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also ahead, new details about the highly anticipated "Toy Story 5." And what our favorite toys are up against in their new adventure.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, Will Ganss is scrubbing in and going behind the scenes of the new "Scrubs" revival series. What the stars of the show are telling us about the reunion 16 years in the making. And, and Sam, I see you in Miami with two of my favorite people. Michael Symon is one of my best friends. Ask him why he's dressed like Walter White in the later years of "Breaking Bad."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "SCRUB"-BING BACK IN!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTH BEACH WINE & FOOD FESTIVAL!\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He wants to know why you're dressed like Walter White in the later years.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SYMON (CELEBRITY CHEF)\par \par \uc2 Oh, you know what? You're lucky you're not here, Strahan. You are a lucky man.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are, and thank you to the Lowe's hotel that we are in South Beach Wine and Food Festival, two superstars of the cooking world. You just met Michael. Alex Guarnaschelli is there cooking some of their favorite dishes for us this morning. We're gonna give you the menus because there's still time. You can still get here. Can you get a ticket, Lee, if they get here?\par \par \uc2 LEE SCHRAGER (FOUNDER & DIRECTOR OF SOUTH BEACH WINE & FOOD FESTIVAL)\par \par \uc2 For select events.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (OC) All right. All right. You can get here and still have fun. We'll be back on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260318em2k00039\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BREAKING DOWN THE LATEST SKIN-TOK TRENDS PERIMOPAUSE/MENOPAUSE: POSSIBLE EFFECTS ON SKIN\b0\par\par\uc2 1,457 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, WINTER, STORM\par \par \uc2 POWER, OUTAGE, ICE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, SNOWSTORM\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, SNOWSTORM, AIRLINE, DELAYS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER\par \par \uc2 ICE, MINNEAPOLIS, PROTEST\par \par \uc2 MANGIONE, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 WEDDING, ARRESTED\par \par \uc2 MEASLES, OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 NATO\uc2 , \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , COACH, HIRINGS, FIRINGS\par \par \uc2 STORM, PREP, FUGATE, LEONARD\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , RIVALS\par \par \uc2 OSCAR, NOMINEES\par \par \uc2 STORY 2:\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WINTER STORM, BOCLEAR, 18 STATES, 200 MILLION, 2,000-MILE-WIDE, NEW MEXICO, MAINE, OKLAHOMA, GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 9,000 FLIGHTS CANCELED, AMERICAN AIRLINES, DELTA, UNITED, 20 STATES\par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA, TRUMP, NATIONAL HISTORICAL PARK, NATIONAL PARK SERVICE, INTERIOR DEPARTMENT, LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 MEASLES OUTBREAK, CDC, SOUTH CAROLINA, 416 CASES, 14 STATES\par \par \uc2 KNICKS\uc2 , \uc2 76ERS\uc2 , WARRIORS, \uc2 TIMBERWOLVES\uc2 , LAKERS, MAVERICKS, LUKA\par \par \uc2 JONES, MUSHER, LEEN, PANCREATIC CANCER, BAYLOR COLLEGE OF MEDICINE, V FOUNDATION\par \par \uc2 CARPENTER, MISS PIGGY, KERMIT, RUDOLPH, ROGEN, DION, KEZA, BAILEY, CORENSWET, JORDAN, STORRIE, WILLIAMS\par \par \uc2 MASSAGE BALLS, SEAT CUSHION, BRAS, FOOTWEAR, GLOVES & HATS, WEARABLE BLANKET\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM HARRY STYLES' NEW SONG "APERTURE"\par \par \uc2 CDC, PEDIATRIC DEATHS, POLIZZI, \uc2 MATTEL\uc2 , LEE, BARBIE\par \par \uc2 ENGELMAN, SKIN-TOK, K-BEAUTY, GLASS SKIN, CERAMIDE, ADULT ACNE, MULHERIN\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, but we are so excited for our next guest, board-certified dermatologist Dr. Dendy Engelman, and some of the hottest topics going viral right now on Skin-Tok. Skin-Tok is a place that we love to be, Dr. Dendy. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING DOWN THE LATEST SKIN-TOK TRENDS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we're excited to have you. Let's start with Korean skin care. It's, it's all the rage.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 It's all the rage.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A hashtag that comes back with two and a half million hits.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oooohh.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And specifically, the glass skin trend, which is also really buzzy. What can you tell us about it?\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, so I love everything K-beauty. I think that we really do forecast what's gonna come to the US based on what's going on in Korea. And the glass skin trend is very real, and we can achieve it and the three steps that we need to do. Make sure that you're cleansing your skin really properly. At least once or twice a week, you're gonna use a good exfoliator like glycolic acid or an alpha hydroxy acid. And then you wanna stack your serums. So, you wanna look for hyaluronic acid, niacinamide, antioxidants, peptides that really helps to give the glow that we're trying to create with the glass skin trend.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: K-BEAUTY TREND: "GLASS SKIN"\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that, and - hydration is obviously a huge...\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...part of that. And I, I use a lot of these ceramide products. A lot of them are making...\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...big wave on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So why are these important?\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 So those are so important. If we think about the skin as a brick wall, so the keratinocytes, the skin cells, are the bricks and the mortar, the glue that hold those together, are made of ceramides and free fatty acids. And as we age, when we hit 30, our ceramide production has dropped by 46%.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CERAMIDES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE BUILDING BLOCKS OF HYDRATED SKIN\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 When you're in your 40s, it's down by 64%. So, we really need to - replenish through our skin care to keep those barriers tight, to keep hydration in, and bad stuff out. So, it's really important to you have ceramides in your skin care as well.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, really interesting. Another topic that we're hearing a lot about is adult acne. We're seeing...\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...plenty of TikTok's - on this. Let's take a look at one here.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MULHERIN (TIKTOK INFLUENCER)\par \par \uc2 This is for my 30-something-year-old women with hormonal acne. What are we doing about this? These, like, little tiny bumps here. I have sprayed him on, but this is bigger over here. I'm on spironolactone. How do we treat this at home? Can somebody weigh in?\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ohhh.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, doctor, your take on this, you've seen a lot of patients as well.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 So many. And I actually woke up this morning to four different - patients...\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No way.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 ...being, like, "Look at my skin," and they looked just like that TikTok. And the reality is the numbers through the American Academy of Dermatology are quite real. We're seeing that women age 20 to 29, still 50% of those women are breaking out. And even 40 to 49, it's 25%. So, we used to think of acne as an affliction of puberty, that you either had it or didn't, and you didn't have it throughout adulthood, but the reality is we're seeing so many other adults who are suffering with it. And the ways to, to really prevent it are to use over-the-counter retinoids, like adapalene or benzoyl peroxide, salicylic acid. Those are great ingredients to look for. And even these little patches, these acne patches.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TREATING ADULT ACNE\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 I love them for multiple reasons.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah. You're seeing that everywhere.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Everywhere.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 And I think it's cute that people are so, like, it's so ubiquitous. Now, they're like, "Yeah, I have a breakout. I'm going to wear this...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 ...star on my face." But I like that the hydrocolloid allows for a healing environment.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 And it also serves as a reminder, don't pick. Because if you have this...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 ...patch on, you know, the tendency is so much that when we have a breakout, we wanna pick it. That can make it so much worse, so these kinda serve two purposes, to heal and to remind to keep your hands off.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oooohh.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is, that is so important because when we're talking about adult hormones, it's a whole thing. I've been using the blue light on my face...\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...to really help with some of this, Dr. Dendy. What does menopause mean for skin care?\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmmm.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I mean, a whole myriad of problems. It can be that you have dryness in one area and breakouts in the other. Some women report increased sensitivity to products that they used to be able to use forever. Certainly, it doesn't just affect the skin. It can get affect brittle nails. Hair loss is a big thing that we see, and then hair growth in areas we don't want it. So, the good news is we are talking much more about menopause, and there's so many other outlets now and resources for these women, because it really does wreak havoc on all organ systems, and so elicit the help of those who are trained to help you with it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PERIMENOPAUSE/ MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow, yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Amazing. Dr. Dendy, thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're fantastic.\par \par \uc2 DR. DENDY ENGELMAN (BOARD-CERTIFIED DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Still coming up here on "GMA," one chicken, four delicious meals. Look at Jason over there. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260212em1o0003o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MAJOR NEW STUDY ON ACETAMINOPHEN & PREGNANCY WHEN PREGNANT WOMEN USE TYLENOL, IT DOES NOT CAUSE THEIR CHILDREN TO BECOME AUTISTIC\b0\par\par\uc2 190 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 January 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ICE, MINNEAPOLIS, GOOD\par \par \uc2 FLU, DEATHS\par \par \uc2 ACETAMINOPHEN, TYLENOL, PREGNANCY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, GREENLAND, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 IRAN, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 OHIO, MURDERS, TEPE, MCKEE\par \par \uc2 KIRK, MURDER\par \par \uc2 MORTGAGE, RATES, FALL\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2 , ARTEMIS II\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP OF "AVENGERS: DOOMSDAY"\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, COSTS\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , COLLEGE, FOOTBALL\par \par \uc2 MLK, WEEKEND, SALES\par \par \uc2 THEODORE, BABY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ICE, GOOD, WALZ, FREY, PROTEST, OPERATION METRO SURGE\par \par \uc2 FLU DEATHS, CDC, SUBCLADE K, NEW JERSEY, NEW ORLEANS\par \par \uc2 TYLENOL, PREGNANCY, AUTISM, ATTENTION DEFICIT, HYPERACTIVITY\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Important warning there, Morgan. Thank you. We wanna talk about a major new study just released on Tylenol.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR NEW STUDY ON ACETAMINOPHEN & PREGNANCY\par \par \uc2 RHIANNON ALLY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A team of scientists calling it the strongest evidence ever, showing that when pregnant women use Tylenol, it does not cause their children to become autistic. Researchers in the Lancet saying they combed through data of 60 studies, and they say despite the position taken by the \uc2 US government\uc2 , they did not find taking Tylenol during pregnancy causes autism, attention deficit, or hyperactivity in children. Janai?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260209em1h0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WHAT MEN SHOULD KNOW ABOUT MENOPAUSE MENOPAUSE AND PERIMENOPAUSE WITH TAMSEN FADAL\b0\par\par\uc2 1,528 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 January 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ICE, PROTESTS, GOOD\par \par \uc2 OIL, TANKER, SEIZED\par \par \uc2 IRAN, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SWITZERLAND, BAR, FIRE\par \par \uc2 MANGIONE, DEFENSE, DEATH, PENALTY\par \par \uc2 PARENTS, ARRESTED, ABUSE, DAVIS\par \par \uc2 TEPE, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 POLICE, CARS, FRONT LOADER\par \par \uc2 INDIANA, PEACH BOWL, MIAMI\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF BRUNO MARS MUSIC VIDEO\par \par \uc2 ICE, SHOOTING, PROTESTS, GOOD\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, EDWARDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDCARD WEEKEND\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DISNEYWORLD, MARATHON, WEEKEND\par \par \uc2 STORY 2:\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GOOD, FREY, ICE, ROSS, VANCE, PROTEST, SHOOTING, MINNEAPOLIS, KILLING\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , FIFTH OIL TANKER, VENEZUELA, NAVY, MARINES, MADURO, US MILITARY\par \par \uc2 CDC, SUBCLADE K, FLU VACCINE, MEASLES OUTBREAK, SOUTH CAROLINA,\par \par \uc2 INDIANA HOOSIERS, OREGON DUCKS, PEACH BOWL, NATIONAL CHAMPIONSHIP, MIAMI\par \par \uc2 DURHAM, JOB SEARCH, EVENTBRITE, LINKEDIN, NETWORKING, 10% RULE\par \par \uc2 DONALD, DAISY, WINTER, WOODY, JESSIE, BUZZ LIGHTYEAR, ZURG, HEIDI HOWLER\par \par \uc2 SOCKS, INSOLES, HOT & COLD THERAPY, BACK & BODY TOOLS, POPCORN, BRA\par \par \uc2 IRAN, TRUMP, MANGIONE, THOMPSON, RAMS, PANTHERS, PACKERS, BEARS\par \par \uc2 FADAL, HOW TO MENOPAUSE, THE (M) FACTOR 2 BEFORE THE PAUSE, FIVE "M"S, MOSCONI\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We certainly are. Now, we are turning to what women want men to know about menopause and perimenopause with Tamsen Fadal, author of the book "How to Menopause." Let's take a look at a clip from her forthcoming documentary "Before the Pause."\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I was just so lost.\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 You could, as always, be honest with me if you're having those hard days. I can make them easier. Or if you need me away from you, - I'll get away from you. And if you, and if you want me close, I'll be close to you. I don't, you know, because I, I, like I said, sometimes I just can't read you, and trying to guess what you're feeling could definitely make for worse days.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF A DOCUMENTARY FROM BEFORE THE PAUSE\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love that. If you want me away from you.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Holy moly. Wow.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 We have those days.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 We have those days.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tamsen, it is so great to have you here. That - I'm sure there are so many people watching that who can relate.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Oh, gosh, right?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's start with what you call the five "M's" that men need to know.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah. This always excites me because when we're having a conversation about menopause and men...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 ...this is where it begins. You know, the first one I think is mojo. - That's the big thing.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Because I think that women are very confused during this time. Men are equally confused. And I always put that at the top because that's sort of where this conversation starts. Women have a low libido during this time, so mojo is, you know, something that they don't have, and men get confused as to why. I think the next one, mood. Mood is a big one. You know, that goes without saying. When you lose hormones, when your hormones just start to Peter off, you're feeling different. You're feeling anxious. You've got rage. You're not remembering things quite so well. And your mood can really decline during this time. So, it's important to keep an eye on things so you know what's happening.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE FIVE "M"S\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's so important. Okay, so, in your experience, what are the questions that partners should be asking the, the women...\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...who are going through this right now?\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, absolutely. I mean, there are some questions, I think, that are really important. One, of course, is, "Is there something I can do to help?" You know, I don't think that support means you have to have expertise.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 I think support means you have to be there to listen.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 I'm here to listen. What can I do? How can I help? Is there something you wanna talk about?\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Say that again because it's so important. You don't need to be an expert.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 You don't have to be an expert...\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 ...to be supportive, and I think sometimes that, that's where men worry. Men worry that they have to actually understand everything about menopause. I think where we are right now is really exciting, because when we talk about those five "M's", we talk about all sorts of things. We talk about marriage and mood and mindset. And mojo and making conversation. And I think what we're finally seeing right now in this - menopause conversation is that companies are listening. Telehealth companies like Midi are reaching out to me saying, we want to bring men into this conversation too.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 And that's what's so important. Because if men understand the type of questions to ask, I think women feel, you know, they're understood somehow, and not trying to explain it all.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, those benefits of the conversations.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It not only helps, it, it helps both people in the situation, right?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MEN & MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONVERSATIONS CAN BE GAME CHANGERS\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Oh, gosh, doesn't it? Well, I think, first of all, a woman feels validated, right? And then I also think that when you have that active listening going on, you can communicate with each other about what's happening so you're not left confused. If a guy doesn't understand, you know, or your partner doesn't understand what's going on with you...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 ...they can assume the worst and go down another road and not realize what's happening. And I think women have been silenced way too long about this conversation. The fact that we're just starting to talk about menopause and perimenopause is mind-boggling to me. So, I get really excited to have it.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, absolutely. And this documentary, "Before the Pause," it's not just for women. It can help men answer a lot of questions as well.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. Well, you saw the couple that's in the documentary. What's important about it is we actually went to a men's group in Brooklyn, and we talked to a bunch of men who were talking with each other about menopause and how they talk to their wives and their partners about it. So, it's really exciting. You know, Dr. Lisa Mosconi, one of the renowned neuroscientists, is part of it. So, she talks about the mind and the part that it plays, and we also talk about hormonal households in this film. So, it's really understanding the years leading up to menopause, perimenopause, which can be up to ten years of our life.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I know. So, it's really important we have this conversation. So, we call it the prequel...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 ...to menopause, which is before the pause.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, long overdue...\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...at the same time, right on time, Tamsen.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 There with you.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is so great to have you here. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Thank you both. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That was great.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And "M Factor 2, Before the Pause" premieres March 19th on PBS. Congratulations again.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, we will all be watching.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And coming up, Coach Herm Edwards is back, and you know, we're gonna have a lot of fun. There it is, Herm's with whom? Stick around.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260130em1a0000s\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE "HOW TO SURVIVE MENOPAUSE"\b0\par\par\uc2 1,706 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 January 2026\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2026, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 BEE, MENOPAUSE, SHEPHARD, AWARENESS, ADVOCACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. Thank you for joining us, and Happy New Year. Tonight, comedian Samantha Bee bringing her hilarious take on menopause to the masses, a topic that suddenly seems to be everywhere. She says it's about time. Here's my "Nightline" co-anchor Juju Chang with another look.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 How's it going, everybody? Oh, I am so excited to talk to you about menopause here tonight.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You might have noticed menopause is having a moment.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I did tell a room full of, like, Hollywood type people that I wanted to do a show about this topic, and one of them said, oh my God, that's great, menopause is superhot right now. To which I said, sir, you truly have no idea.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Packed audiences laughing uproariously about how to survive menopause.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tell me about the name of your tour.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I needed to know how to survive menopause and I was like, I think that will state very up front with the show is about.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So you're like a girlfriends guide to menopause.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 A girlfriend's guide, which like, you really need girlfriends in this period of your life.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Comedian Samantha Bee got famous for her sassy female energy on "The Daily Show."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "THE DAILY SHOW"\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART ("THE DAILY SHOW")\par \par \uc2 I don't, I don't think we have five.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 Shush, easy little John, mama's in control now.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And her irreverent late night show "Full Frontal With Samantha Bee."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "FROM FRONTAL WITH SAMANTHA BEE"\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I'm going to do something that should have been done decades ago, I'm going to teach sex-ed to senators.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's now nearly an empty nester, the wife and mom of three teenagers, on the road selling out theaters and talking menopause across the country.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I feel like you are really breaking this conspiracy of silence around it.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 It actually was really a process. And I feel like if one person can just not be embarrassed by it, that helps the people who come behind, you know what I mean? I'm trying to like untether from shame.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How did you get over your own embarrassment about it?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I got over my sense of shame about it by just grinding it out. I felt an energy in the audience and I thought, I'm going to lean even further into it because there's a need, because there's actually like a need and a desire to just, like, talk about it all together all at once, just like, get it out.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's not the only one. Influencers and celebrities are also speaking out. This year Melanie Sanders hit a nerve with her we do not care club.\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I started this club for all women in perimenopause, menopause and post menopause. We are putting the world on notice that we simply just do not care much anymore.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Melanie's millions of followers, including actress \uc2 Ashley Judd\uc2 , joining in on the honesty.\par \par \uc2 ASHELY JUDD (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I probably have cellulite, I don't care.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Other stars also chiming in about the change.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I'm in menopause. Okay?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Oscar winning actress Halle Berry, a big advocate for awareness, getting real in her support of a Senate bill to increase menopause funding and education.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 It has to be destigmatized. The shame has to be taken out of menopause. We have to talk about this very normal part of our life that happens.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Goop founder and actress Gwyneth Paltrow making this admission on her site.\par \par \uc2 GWYNETH PALTROW (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I think menopause gets a really bad rap and needs a bit of a rebranding.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Even Oprah Winfrey giving the topic the Oprah treatment in a primetime ABC special, "The Menopause Revolution."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION"\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY ("THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION")\par \par \uc2 We can stop the stereotypes and we can change this conversation.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Half the population goes through it, so you'd think we'd know what menopause is.\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB-GYN)\par \par \uc2 We've diagnosed it as a time frame in a woman's life, when she hasn't had a cycle consecutively for 12 months. The symptoms of menopause most commonly can be hot flashes, night sweats, mood changes, disturbances in sleep, decrease in libido.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Jessica Shephard is a Dallas based, board certified OB/GYN, and author of "Generation M: Living Well in Perimenopause and Menopause."\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB-GYN)\par \par \uc2 There's a full range of how women want to approach the treatment and management of their symptoms. So some of the things that we see is going to be hormone therapy, which may give you back enough of those hormones to allow you to feel better, to function better because your organs and your body needs it.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Recently, the FDA announcing it will remove the black box warning labels from those hormone replacement therapy drugs used to treat menopause. For Sam Bee, cracking jokes about an awkward topic helps bring awareness.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 None of this is really all that fair.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her show, based on her own experiences, delivers on that idea.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 There is so much practical advice out there for every problem that you can imagine. How to survive tornadoes, zombie attacks. How to contend with an erection that lasts more than four hours. But when it comes to menopause, everyone's like, I don't know, have you even tried drinking water?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The inevitable hormonal change has long been dramatized, but rarely explained, from television's "The Golden Girls."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "THE GOLDEN GIRLS"\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I am not dying, Rose, but I might as well be. It's menopause.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To movies like "Fried Green Tomatoes."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "FRIED GREEN TOMATOES"\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Simple, honey, you're going through the change.\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB-GYN)\par \par \uc2 We see television and social media placing emphasis on a topic that for so long, women would not talk about. I think that this allows women to say, you know what, maybe this is some funny some days. Or maybe I don't have to feel alone in the moments where I'm sweating half to death or I can't sleep.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There's a lot of misunderstanding. Give me the litany of symptoms that you were going through.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 Ears itching. My hair really started to fall out. I started to get, like my moods were so erratic. But primarily I really could not sleep through the night. And my job was so demanding that waking up at 2:00 in the morning, every single night was, like, not helping.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her act also taking on the seeming double standard of treatment in the medical community.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 If your (censored by network) stops working all available dollars are spent ensuring that you can still get rock hard at the age of 97.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 We take men's bodies seriously. You know, we have symptoms of things that appear differently in men's bodies and women's bodies.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so when a man's organs versus a woman's organs.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 Well, the ovaries are one of the only organs in the human body that when they start to slow down, we kind of all just go, oh, well, you know, like I wipe my hands clean of that. I mean, if your heart starts to beat erratically, everybody takes it very seriously. They will put in a pacemaker immediately. Everybody is like, all systems go. And if your penis, I mean, if you are entering penile winter, there's so much technology available to you.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And yet there are men in your audience.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 There are lots actually.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So what are you, what are you trying to say to the men?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I love having men in the audience. That actually shocked me. I did think that it would be just like large groups of women, but actually a lot of them bring their partners because, again, it is a shared experience with our loving partners.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You've had a late night talk show, variety show, and now you're out on the road. How does your career evolve?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I've done the like the kind of like larger scale work that I really wanted to do, and I kind of said everything that I wanted to say in that realm. I really did a lot of soul searching about what it was that I felt I had left to say. This topic is so interesting to me, so it is pure joy for me to do it. It's like driven out of pure love for the audience that I'm serving.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 Together we are all going to survive menopause. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHANGE YOUR EYE COLOR?\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Juju. When we come back. These aren't colored contacts, they're the results of a pricey, controversial procedure to change the color of your eyes permanently. After the break.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020260106em1100002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE NEW APP BRINGING THRIVERS TOGETHER THE HEALTH BENEFITS OF ONLINE COMMUNITY\b0\par\par\uc2 707 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 December 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, WINTER, STORM\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, WAR\par \par \uc2 ISIS\uc2 , TERRORISTS, NIGERIA\par \par \uc2 IDAHO, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THREE INJURED AFTER GUNMAN OPENS FIRE IN IDAHO\par \par \uc2 BUZZARD, MURDER, PLEA\par \par \uc2 SMITH, MURDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER CHILD BROADWAY STAR KILLED IN NEW JERSEY\par \par \uc2 FLU, NEW YORK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NY STATE REPORTS RECORD NUMBER OF FLU CASES IN SEVEN-DAY PERIOD\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, SALES\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , FOOTBALL, WEEK 17\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, ACHO\par \par \uc2 RETURNS, FEES\par \par \uc2 "THE YEAR", 2025\par \par \uc2 POP, QUIZ, 2025\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SNOW, SLEET, RAINS, FLASH FLOODS, MUDSLIDES\par \par \uc2 SEVERE WEATHER, HOLIDAY TRAVEL, LAGUARDIA AIRPORT\par \par \uc2 BUZZARD, MURDER, UTAH\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , ZELENSKYY, PEACE DEAL TALK, WAR IN UKRAINE, KUSHNER, WITKOFF, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 NEW APP, MEENAGH, MERRILL, JAMES, CANCER DIAGNOSES, ONLINE CANCER SUPPORT\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back now with our "GMA" cover story. Cancer diagnoses. They're on the rise in younger people, and Morgan Norwood has the story of how a dating app inspired a new way for thrivers to find community.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE NEW APP BRINGING THRIVERS TOGETHER\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Devon Meenagh was 28 years old when she was diagnosed with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 DEVON MEENAGH (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 The doctors were starting to share more information about next steps, and I had to cut them off at one point and say, "I'm, I'm not hearing anything you're saying. I'm, I can barely breathe." I have to call my mom, who's a three-time survivor.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) During treatment, Devon says she found it difficult to reach out to family for emotional support.\par \par \uc2 DEVON MEENAGH (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 My dad, I, I get almost the most emotional thinking about him, because he saw my mom go through it three times, and then he had to watch me go through it. But if he called and asked how I was doing, if I actually told him how I was doing, it would have broken his heart.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, she turned to a support group at her cancer center.\par \par \uc2 DEVON MEENAGH (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 It was specifically for people of my age, and I was the only one that showed up for several weeks in a row. It didn't help in making me not feel alone.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But then she came across cancer buddy creator Christina Merrill, who says the platform was inspired by dating apps.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA MERRILL (FOUNDER & CEO BONE MARROW & CANCER FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 I was swiping and saw this incredible platform and technology of being able to match with somebody that had your interests and likes and geographical location. And I thought, how come this is not in the cancer community?\par \par \uc2 DEVON MEENAGH (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 What I like about it is it humanizes the person using it. It does include your interests, your age group, things outside of just the cancer, and I have formed some really good friendships with people that we could have been friends, even without the cancer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONLINE CANCER SUPPORT RESOURCES\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Experts say online resources like Cancer Buddy, Belong.Life, Cancer.Net Mobile and others can contribute to better health outcomes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE HEALTH BENEFITS OF ONLINE COMMUNITY\par \par \uc2 MONIQUE JAMES MD (PSYCHIATRIST MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CTR)\par \par \uc2 It does reduce the sense of loneliness, sense of isolation, and it also literally reduces anxiety scores, depression scores as well. And a lot of the fear that goes along with the cancer diagnosis and treatment.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Devon says the connections she's made through the app provided a community and offered her a sense of normalcy.\par \par \uc2 DEVON MEENAGH (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I also have a friend on there who she and I, we don't even really talk about cancer stuff. We mostly send each other memes. You really feel like a lab rat when you're going through it, so talking to other people as a person, not a patient, reminds you that you are a person.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For "Good Morning America," Morgan Norwood, ABC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, really great story.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260115elcr00092\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVES FIRST PILL FOR LOW LIBIDO IN POSTMENOPAUSAL WOMEN "GMA" HEALTH ALERT\b0\par\par\uc2 1,018 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 NERONHA, MCKEE, SMILEY, FINDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 REINER, MURDER, ARREST, MCDONNELL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROB REINER'S SON ARRESTED IN PARENTS' MURDERS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, REINER, MASSIE, VANCE, KIRK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TRUMP CRITICIZED OVER ROB REINER COMMENTS\par \par \uc2 VENEZUELA, BOAT, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S. ANNOUNCES THREE MORE STRIKES ON ALLEGED DRUG BOATS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, BONDI BEACH, AUSTRALIA, AHMED, AKRAM, \uc2 ISIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW VIDEO & DETAILS IN BONDI BEACH TERROR INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 "MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL", STEELERS, MAHOMES, SURGERY\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SERIES, "GENERAL HOSPITAL"\par \par \uc2 WALSHE, VERDICT, SIMMONS, THEFT, WARD, FLAGG\par \par \uc2 TERROR PLOT, DAVIS, ESSAYLI, CARROLL, ARREST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FOUR ARRESTED IN ALLEGED NEW YEAR'S EVE BOMB PLOT\par \par \uc2 JOBS REPORT, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON NEW JOBS REPORT\par \par \uc2 REINER, TRIBUTE, SUTHERLAND, BACON, MURDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REINER FAMILY IN THE SPOTLIGHT AFTER TRAGIC KILLINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT MANHUNT\par \par \uc2 BROWN, SHOOTING, IMAGES, MANHUNT\par \par \uc2 BYHEART, RECALLED, INFANT, BOTULISM\par \par \uc2 GEARY, "GENERAL HOSPITAL", LUKE, LAURA, FRANCIS, ROYALS, TAYLOR, MATHISON\par \par \uc2 CHALAMET, "MARTY SUPREME," PALTROW, O'LEARY, WHEATIES, ESDEEKID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD WARMTH\par \par \uc2 FLUFFCO, LINENS & HUTCH, SPONGELLE, BREED, ROSETTA STONE, ROSES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SATELLITE AND RADAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 FDA\uc2 , PILL, LIBIDO, MENOPAUSE, ADDYI\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we are looking forward to that. Thank you, George. We're going to go now to a "GMA" health alert. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has just approved the first and only treatment for low libido in women who've gone through menopause. And ABC News Chief Medical Correspondent Dr. Tara Narula is here to tell us about it. Great to see you, Dr. Narula.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVES FIRST PILL FOR LOW LIBIDO IN POSTMENOPAUSAL WOMEN\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You too.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, this drug is called Addyi. It's been on the market for a while, but the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has just approved it for something new.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HYPOACTIVE SEXUAL DESIRE DISORDER (LOW LIBIDO)\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AFFECTS ABOUT 1 IN 8 POSTMENOPAUSAL WOMEN (NIH)\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Correct. Yes. So, this was approved in 2015, Addyi. It is a drug for hypoactive sexual desire disorder. It was approved in 2015 for - premenopausal women. Now they have expanded the approval to postmenopausal women under the age of 65. Now, this, as you mentioned, is the first drug for postmenopausal women for low libido. It was approved in Canada in 2021, and that kind of paved the way for what we're seeing here now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVES ADDYI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POTENTIAL SIDE EFFECTS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is a pill, Rebecca, that you take every day at bedtime. And there can be some side effects with it like dizziness, sleepiness, fatigue or nausea. It's important that women who take it don't have underlying liver disease, or there are certain medicines they can't take it. And alcohol is a big one to be cautious with taking with this drug because it can cause low blood pressure and fainting.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, essentially, how the drug works is by changing the chemistry in the brain. It was actually initially looked at as a mood or kind of an antidepressant drug. So, it increases levels of dopamine and norepinephrine in the brain and lower serotonin, and that is essentially how they believe it works to help increase that desire of libido.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and this is pretty prevalent. One in eight postmenopausal women are experiencing low libido. How should women talk to their doctors about this? How should they know if this might be something for them?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right. Well, we want to kind of differentiate should we, differentiate between generally low libido and hypoactive sexual desire disorder, and that meets, you have to meet certain criteria. So essentially, it is defined by a sense or lack of desire for sexual activity, a decrease in sexual thoughts or fantasies that actually causes personal distress and interpersonal difficulties. So, you would meet with a doctor. They would rule out - any underlying medical conditions, mental health conditions, medications that might be causing it.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And then they would take a sexual health history and give you a screening test that would basically help decide if you really meet that criteria. And, if you do, then there are treatments, things like therapy, counseling, treating any underlying medical conditions and then medications. There is actually one other drug available since 2019 that's an injection for premenopausal women with hypoactive sexual desire disorder.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) An injection.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, a pill is a very different kind of delivery mechanism.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Totally.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) People might be more inclined to take that.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. Exactly. Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the conversation around menopause now suddenly we are always talking about this now.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is something that we actually are speaking about again.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Finally. And it's so important because we want women in that phase of life to be feel comfortable talking about the many symptoms they may develop and also specifically sexual health, right? There shouldn't be any taboo or shame around discussing this with your doctor. As we've mentioned, there are treatments. We've talked a lot recently about hormone replacement therapy, which can help some women with this but it's nice to have another option. Again, go to menopause dot org, you can find a certified menopause practitioner in your area by your zip code.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Great information, Dr. Narula, as always. Thank you. All right. After the break, Robin Arzon and Jess Sims are gonna show us how to cross train at home. We're suiting up.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260112elcg0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MIRACLE BABY BORN AFTER RARE & DANGEROUS PREGNANCY RYU'S PARENTS SHARE THEIR STORY\b0\par\par\uc2 881 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR ESCALATION\par \par \uc2 VENEZUELA, OIL, TANKER, TRUMP, HOULAHAN, MADURO\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, EVACUATIONS, SNOW, TRACKING, RAIN\par \par \uc2 HOMENDY, \uc2 NTSB\uc2 , BILL\par \par \uc2 SKYDIVER, MISHAP, VIDEO, ATSB\par \par \uc2 RIVERS, \uc2 NFL\uc2 , COLTS, RETIREMENT, GRANDPA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORNING WIND CHILLS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEXT WEEK'S TEMP TREND\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 U-M, MOORE, FIRED, MEEK, THAMEL\par \par \uc2 LOPEZ, PREGNANCY, RYU, OZIMEK, DAYANIM\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks, Whit. Now to the newborn that doctors are calling a miracle baby. He was born after a rare and dangerous pregnancy. His parents sharing their story first on "GMA" with Morgan Norwood. Good morning, Morgan.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIRACLE BABY BORN AFTER RARE & DANGEROUS PREGNANCY\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you, George. Just an incredible story here. So, Cynthia Lopez stepped into surgery preparing for the fight of her life, removing a 22-pound cyst hanging on her ovary. But what doctors found was life itself, a baby growing outside of the womb, far from where any child would or should. And yet against all odds, he held on.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, a medical marvel. Suze Lopez went in for surgery at \uc2 Cedars-Sinai\uc2  to remove a more than 20-pound ovarian cyst, but came out holding the very child she never knew she was carrying.\par \par \uc2 SUZE LOPEZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I knew that I was pregnant for about five days.\par \par \uc2 NURSE (CEDARS-SINAI MATERNAL FETAL CARE UNIT)\par \par \uc2 Very good.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The standard preop pregnancy test revealing the shocking discovery.\par \par \uc2 SUZE LOPEZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I wasn't even believing it at first because I have ovary problems I, so I was thinking it was, like, a false positive.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Suze giving her husband Andrew the good news.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW LOPEZ (FATHER)\par \par \uc2 I thought she was joking and it didn't really sink in until I actually got a little gift from her, and it was Dodger game. It was surreal.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doctors ordering an MRI and were stunned at what they saw.\par \par \uc2 DR. JOHN OZIMEK (MED DIR OF LABOR & DELIVERY CEDARS-SINAI MATERNAL FETAL CARE)\par \par \uc2 He was located outside of the uterus posterior to, to this big ovarian mass in the abdomen, so his head was up on the left side of her abdomen under the spleen. And it makes sense that she didn't know because the, the baby was in her abdomen behind the mass and it just looked like the mass was growing.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Suze had what's called an abdominal ectopic pregnancy, extremely rare and life-threatening for both mom and baby.\par \par \uc2 NURSE (CEDARS-SINAI MATERNAL FETAL CARE UNIT)\par \par \uc2 He's still very sick.\par \par \uc2 NURSE (CEDARS-SINAI MATERNAL FETAL CARE UNIT)\par \par \uc2 He okay? Let me know (inaudible) .\par \par \uc2 NURSE (CEDARS-SINAI MATERNAL FETAL CARE UNIT) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 And we're gonna, we're needing to do a lot to support him.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) On August 18th, a team of 30 medical professionals removing the giant cyst and delivering the full-term baby named Ryu.\par \par \uc2 SUZE LOPEZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Hi, Ryu.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Weighing eight pounds and in remarkably good health.\par \par \uc2 SUZE LOPEZ (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 How are you today? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DR. SARA DAYANIM (NEONATOLOGIST/CEDARS-SINAI GUERIN CHILDREN'S)\par \par \uc2 I was amazed by this little guy. He came out of anesthesia pretty quickly and he was like a feisty little guy by the time we got into the NICU.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Suze recovering quickly, now at home surrounded by her husband and teenage daughter Kyla (sp?) . Doctors calling Ryu a miracle and after 17 years of praying for a second baby, mom and dad say he's nothing short of a gift.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW LOPEZ (FATHER)\par \par \uc2 Miracles happen and he definitely was a miracle for us.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ohh.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Truly a miracle. And the whole desk says "aww" because he's adorable.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible) .\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's so cute.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They named him Ryu after the street fire, fighter character because he is truly a fighter.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, right?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And then also, today dad is graduating from nursing school. So, just good day for the Lopez family.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh. What a wonderful (inaudible) .\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Great, great story. Thank you, Morgan.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Appreciate that. Coming up next, with exactly two weeks until Christmas, the "GMA" dream team is standing by with tips on last-minute shopping. Lara, you've got the shipping news for us?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 7:41/CHRISTMAS CRUNCH TIME\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Oh, we do indeed. We'll have the dates you need to know to make sure your gifts arrive in time to go under the tree. Plus, tips on how to save a little bit of money on that shipping.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020260106elcb0000a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 A MOTHER'S DILEMMA FETAL HEARTBEAT DETECTED IN BRAIN DEAD MOTHER\b0\par\par\uc2 2,513 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 SMITH, NEWKIRK, GUY, CAPLAN, BERGHELLA, POURKAVIANI, LIFE ACT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. Thank you for joining us. We're joined tonight by ABC's senior political correspondent Rachel Scott with the story stirring deep emotions.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Byron, and it is a tragic story. It's an example of the confusion that has happened in some states after the fall of Roe versus Wade, raising questions about what doctors and families can do in the rare instance where a woman is pregnant and legally dead.\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 You know, you just want to scream. You just want to scream, you know? She's my daughter, my first daughter.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's an unimaginable pain that April Newkirk has held on to for months.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you want people to know about Adriana, your daughter?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 She was just like a ray of sunshine. She loved her family and her friends. She was very, very smart.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In February, her daughter Adriana Smith, was nine weeks pregnant, expecting her second child when, suddenly, her health spiraled. Her story making headlines across the country.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Adriana Smith has been on life support since February.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 A pregnant woman kept alive after being declared brain dead in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's one of the most shocking and rarest cases in the world. A 30-year-old brain dead woman was kept on machines, continuing her pregnancy for four more months.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did the doctors give you any choices to make? Any options?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 The option was that there's a law in place and they have to follow the guidelines of the law. So, I guess the Heartbeat Law, and the baby did have a strong heartbeat, he was going to be treated as the patient, not Adriana.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The case raising serious ethical, moral and legal questions about continuing a pregnancy for a woman who has already been pronounced dead.\par \par \uc2 DR SHALIZ POURKAVIANI (BOARD-CERTIFIED NEONATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 It is extremely unprecedented to have a mom who is brain dead, sustained on life support starting from the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR CAPLAN PHD (BIOETHICIST NYU GROSSMAN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 The biggest and most startling and disturbing fact is this one was nine weeks. That baby's going to be inside a dead body for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 DR VINCENZO BERGHELLA (DIR MATERNAL-FETAL MEDICINE JEFFERSON HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Amazingly enough, there were only four cases in which the brain death was diagnosed in a pregnant woman before 14 weeks.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR CAPLAN PHD (BIOETHICIST NYU GROSSMAN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Is it worth it to try and save a fetus to become a child in a dead woman?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This case sparking a nationwide debate about a state's controversial abortion law, the possible unintended consequences, and the family's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 I feel like we should have had a choice, but we didn't. I really think it should be a choice. I just do.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) April's grandson, Chance, is now six months old and still fighting for his life in the hospital, unable to breathe on his own. April sitting down with ABC News for an exclusive network interview.\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 I'm going to stand up and I'm going to speak for my daughter, and I'm going to advocate for other women to.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) April says Adriana had big plans, a new job, school, and then shared even bigger news. She was pregnant, expecting her second child.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When did you start to realize that something was wrong with Adriana?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 I think she was having really, really, really bad headaches. Really, really bad to the point where that's when she went to the hospital. She got a prescription, something like, you know, a little stronger than Tylenol that they give pregnant women, but she didn't have a CT-scan done.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) April says Adriana was sent home by the hospital, but then things took a turn.\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 She was unresponsive. She was gasping.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This time, Adriana was rushed to a different hospital, this one part of the Emory Healthcare System, where she worked as a registered nurse. Her diagnosis was devastating.\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 CT-scan showed that there were blood clots everywhere. Everywhere. They waited a day to see, you know, what was going on and run tests and stuff like that. I stayed overnight. I didn't leave her. Yeah, she never woke up.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Adriana was placed on life support on February 9th.\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 They declared her brain death February 19th. And actually, that's what's on the death certificate February 19th. The fetus at the time had a heartbeat, so they said that they would treat the baby as the patient and she was no longer the patient because of the law that they have here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you feel in those moments that you had clarity from the doctors?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 The clarity was, there was nothing that I could, you know, nothing that they can do but treat the baby as the patient. And me and her dad, you know, we talked about it. We were really like, we really don't have a choice. So, I was just like, well, maybe she'll come back to us. Maybe she'll find her way back to us.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When did you start to feel that hope was gone?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 I did see her changing, her skin changing, her body changing. So, when I first saw her, she looked like my daughter. You know? As time went on, you know, she just started changing.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We reached out to \uc2 Emory Healthcare\uc2  and April for consent to release Adriana's medical records to ABC News. April declined to give permission. Georgia's law, known as the Life Act, went into effect after Roe versus Wade was overturned in 2022. It bans abortions as early as six weeks of pregnancy. The law only allows narrow exceptions for medical emergencies, rape and incest, and medically futile pregnancies. Georgia law also deems fetuses living distinct persons with the same rights.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE GUY (ANTI-ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 We were so thrilled to finally have legislation that, at least from a heartbeat on, would save countless lives and spare precious women the regret, pain, and sorrow of abortion.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Suzanne Guy is an anti-abortion rights advocate. She and her daughter lobbied for the bill.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, her mother says that the doctors kept pointing back to the law.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE GUY (ANTI-ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that was the reason why they told her she didn't have a choice in this.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE GUY (ANTI-ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 A mom being brain dead, it doesn't even qualify, if she were to be taken off life support, that does not even qualify as abortion under our law. And our heartbeat law, in no way, shape or form mandates keeping a pregnant mom on life support.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Georgia law does not specify what happens if the pregnant person is brain dead.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you think there should have been an exception in Adriana's case?\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE GUY (ANTI-ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 Rachel, I don't. That little boy is valuable and deserved to be protected and fought for.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In a statement to ABC News, the \uc2 Georgia Attorney General's Office\uc2  said, "There is nothing in the Life Act that requires medical professionals to keep a woman on life support after brain death. Removing life support is not an action with the purpose to terminate a pregnancy." In initial statements in May, \uc2 Emory Healthcare\uc2  said they make "individualized treatment recommendations in compliance with Georgia's abortion laws and all other applicable laws." But in a recent statement to ABC News, they did not mention abortion laws, saying in part, "We are foremost guided by patient autonomy. And in situations where patients cannot express their preferences, we rely on their advanced directive, health care agent or legally authorized surrogate decision maker. This extends to healthcare decisions at the end of life."\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR CAPLAN PHD (BIOETHICIST NYU GROSSMAN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 We have murky questions about how is the law in the state to be interpreted? Are people too afraid of getting in trouble somehow if they don't act in a certain way?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Doctors say keeping the fetus healthy inside the womb of a brain-dead mother can be difficult, but it can be done.\par \par \uc2 DR VINCENZO BERGHELLA (DIR MATERNAL-FETAL MEDICINE JEFFERSON HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 If the placenta is getting enough blood flow, so that the baby is getting enough oxygen, the baby can do okay.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dr. Vincenzo Berghella is a maternal-fetal medicine specialist who has studied all 35 known cases of brain death in pregnancy worldwide. Only four were in the first trimester before 14 weeks. Only two of those babies survived.\par \par \uc2 DR VINCENZO BERGHELLA (DIR MATERNAL-FETAL MEDICINE JEFFERSON HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 These are very rare cases. On nine weeks, chances are, even if you want to continue the pregnancy, that the baby is not going to make it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Adriana's pregnancy on life support lasted 16 weeks.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR CAPLAN PHD (BIOETHICIST NYU GROSSMAN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Is there no protocol? There's no agreed upon method. To be blunt, we don't know what we're doing. I'd be worried if we presented this as a treatment. It's not. It's an experiment.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) World-renowned bioethicist Arthur Caplan says Adriana's case raises significant ethical concerns.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR CAPLAN PHD (BIOETHICIST NYU GROSSMAN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 The ethics of whether you'd be willing to do that come up against a pretty strong principle, do no harm. And here, you're worried that you're going to harm that fetus. It's really unlikely that we're going to be able to give you a life that isn't short and one of suffering. Is that worth pursuing? I think it's cruel to the fetus.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In June, when Adriana was around 26 weeks pregnant, April received a call from the hospital.\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 They told me they have to do an emergency C-section that, someone called they're in the process of doing it right now.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When you arrive at the hospital, was Chance born already?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 Mm-hmm. Chance was born not through skin to skin. I put him on my chest.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you decide on the name Chance?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 I did.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why? Why choose that name?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 Because I feel like he had a second chance.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Chance weighing just 1 pound and 13 ounces.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How was his health?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 Well, his lungs are underdeveloped. He's not your regular premature baby. She did not feed him through food that she ate. She was fed through IV. So, there's a big difference. He's struggling, but he is gaining weight. He's struggling with his breathing.\par \par \uc2 DR VINCENZO BERGHELLA (DIR MATERNAL-FETAL MEDICINE JEFFERSON HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 These babies can be in for months in the hospital, can have lung problems, heart problems, eye problems, ear problems, may need to be on oxygen for a long time.\par \par \uc2 DR SHALIZ POURKAVIANI (BOARD-CERTIFIED NEONATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Those born less than 28 weeks are 10 times increased risk of cerebral palsy. These babies are also at increased risk of intellectual disabilities.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Four days after baby Chance was delivered, Adriana was taken off ventilation. This was one of those emotional last moments.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Can I ask what those final goodbyes were like?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 It was hard. It was hard. It was hard. It was hard. And every day, I think about her every day.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Baby Chance, now 6 months old, weighing just 11 pounds. He's still in the NICU and still can't breathe on his own. April says he won't be coming home anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 DR SHALIZ POURKAVIANI (BOARD-CERTIFIED NEONATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Each case needs to be fully discussed with the families because, ultimately, they're left with that hardship if the baby is able to make it past the NICU discharge.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) April is also raising Adriana's 7-year-old son, Chase.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When he asked about her now, what do you tell him about her?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 That God needed her more?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For April, it's not just about the loss of her daughter, but women across the country hoping her pain can help spare someone else.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When you think about Adriana, what do you think she would have wanted in this scenario?\par \par \uc2 APRIL NEWKIRK (MOTHER OF ADRIANA SMITH)\par \par \uc2 She would have wanted for her parents to have a choice to make, the decisions for her, because she couldn't. That's what she would have wanted.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel, this story sparked so much pain and so many questions. What's the hospital say about how they treated this case?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Byron, we did ask Emory for specific details on the decisions made around Adriana's care. They went to the family and asked to waive the privacy rights, but were turned down. But what we do know tonight is that Baby Chance is still in the hospital and still unable to breathe on his own.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel, thank you so much for covering this story with so much grace and clarity. We appreciate it. And we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020251211elc900002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 JESSIE J GRAMMY-NOMINATED SINGER OPENS UP ABOUT BREAST CANCER DIAGNOSIS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,011 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS, TACOPINA, GUARDIANSHIP, DEMENTIA, ALCOHOL, CROLL\par \par \uc2 JESSIE, J, GRAMMY, CANCER, INSTAGRAM, KATE, "BANG BANG", "HAPPY"\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back. Jessie J is pulling back the curtain on a major health scare that arrived just as everything in her life seemed to be aligning. The Grammy-nominated singer tells our Maggie Rulli she handled the news by jumping right in and taking her 10 million-plus social media followers along on the journey.\par \par \uc2 MUSIC VIDEO CLIPS FROM JESSIE J/"BANG BANG"/"DOMINO"\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's the Grammy-nominated voice behind hits like "Bang Bang" and "Domino."\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 Hello, everyone.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The delightfully effervescent.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 Hello, back there. Hello.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jessie J.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're ready.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 Let's do it.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And this morning, the 37-year-old mom is getting candid about the shock breast cancer diagnosis that came last March, just days before she was set to return to the music world after a four-year hiatus.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 When I got diagnosed with breast cancer, I wasn't scared. I felt a little bit out of control. But I am a sharer and I wasn't going to sit at home and cry about it. And it's just giving me deeper perspective and I'm really riding that wave of living in the moment and like, enjoying life.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Can you take me back to the moment when you first realized something was wrong?\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 I felt a lump and I said, oh, that doesn't feel right. And I went and got looked at. I had an ultrasound. They were like, everything looks fine. And I said, but I've got this achy arm, and I feel like - I kept waking up with pins and needles and all these like, things going "bing, bing, bing bing bing." Maybe this is what it's like. Is that anything? They're like, okay, well those let's do a biopsy. I remember finding my mum first, and I was just like, well, I can't die because I have Sky.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With her two-year-old son Sky and partner, Chanan Colman, by her side, Jessie J underwent a mastectomy in her right breast in June, documenting the process for her more than 14 million \uc2 Instagram\uc2  followers.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know you said that you're a sharer.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 Oh yeah.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So why was it so important for you? Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) cut the cameras.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But why was it so important for you to share all of this with the world?\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 I'm just like, we need to talk about these things. I've been very lucky, super lucky to find it so early. It wasn't invasive, thank God. I know there's a chance it could come back, but until then...\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 We're living life.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The singer-songwriter recently sharing this moment of connection and a hug with Princess Kate.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did Princess Kate say anything to you, mom to mom?\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 She just said "Thank you. I know you've gone through a tough time." And I said, "I know you have too." And I just said, "I just want to acknowledge like mom to mom, you know, cancer survivor to cancer survivor. I see you and I feel you. Can I give you a hug?" And she said, "Of course you can." I'm grateful that she received it in the way it was meant.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, eight years since her last album, Jessie J is back with "Don't Tease Me with a Good Time," singing about the highs and lows of the past few years, from "H-A-P-P-Y" with a video that includes a special cameo from Sky.\par \par \uc2 MUSIC VIDEO CLIP FROM "H.A.P.P.Y"\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 No, he's just honestly the love of my life.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 I - went through many journeys to find him, and I'm just so grateful that he held on to me.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To the emotional "I'll Never Know Why," about the death of her bodyguard and friend.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 This album covers the journey of grief to finding joy again and the circle again.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Your album's like a therapy session.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. I remember Justin Timberlake saying to me once, "Your music needs to be like a slice of your life." And I feel like this is like a pizza, you know, like it's not a slice, it's a full pizza, but they kind of all go together.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (SINGER/SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 And I don't think this is the comeback. I think this is the warm up for the thing.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Maggie, "Don't Tease Me with a Good Time" is available now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE NOMINEES ARE...\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 When we come back from "Wicked for Good."\par \par \uc2 MOVIE CLIP FROM "WICKED FOR GOOD"\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA ERIVO (ACTOR/"WICKED FOR GOOD")\par \par \uc2 I'm off to see the Wizard.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 To "One Battle After Another."\par \par \uc2 MOVIE CLIP FROM "ONE BATTLE AFTER ANOTHER"\par \par \uc2 LEONARDO DICAPRIO (ACTOR/"ONE BATTLE AFTER ANOTHER")\par \par \uc2 Viva la revolution!\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Who's in line for this year's Golden Globes?\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020251210elc800003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 JESSIE J TALKS BREAST CANCER BATTLE & NEW MUSIC\b0\par\par\uc2 1,074 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 December 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE BIG CHILL\par \par \uc2 TEMPERATURES, RAIN, SNOW, WIND CHILLS, WIND\par \par \uc2 ARCTIC, TEMPERATURES, WIND CHILLS, SNOW, SLEET\par \par \uc2 DRUG BOAT, STRIKES, LAWMAKERS, CONTROVERSY, WAR CRIME\par \par \uc2 EVIDENCE, MANGIONE, MCDONALD'S, AUDIO, VIDEO, DEFENSE\par \par \uc2 ARREST, PIPE BOMBS, JAN 6, SUSPECT, BONDI, COX\par \par \uc2 JAMES, GRAND JURY, REJECTED, INDICTMENT, TRUMP, HALLIGAN\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , LIONS, COWBOYS, COLLEGE, MATCHUPS, PLAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTHEAST DROUGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MURDER, WALSHE, TRIAL, TESTIMONY, FASTOW\par \par \uc2 FYRE FESTIVAL, MCFARLAND, HONDURAS, MONTANA, PRICES\par \par \uc2 HEPATITIS B, VACCINE, SCHEDULE, CDC, VOTE, ASIP\par \par \uc2 JAMES, LAKERS, POINTS, STREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH TEMPERATURES\par \par \uc2 SHIPPING, DEADLINES\par \par \uc2 JACKMAN, SURPRISE, HIGH SCHOOL\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J, BREAST, CANCER\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back with another "GMA" cover story. Grammy-nominated singer Jessie J is talking about her breast cancer diagnosis and how her new music is helping her heal. Maggie Rulli spoke with her. Good morning, Maggie.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JESSIE J TALKS BREAST CANCER BATTLE & NEW MUSIC\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, George. Good morning. Yeah, it's been eight years since her last album, but guys, after sitting down with Jessie J, I can tell you she still has every ounce of her trademark personality, even when she got candid with us about her breast cancer diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's the Grammy-nominated voice behind hits like "Bang Bang" and "Domino." The delightfully effervescent...\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 Hello back there.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...Jessie J.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're ready.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 Let's do it.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And this morning, the 37-year-old mom is getting candid about the shock breast cancer diagnosis that came last March, just days before she was set to return to the music world after a four year hiatus.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 When I got diagnosed with breast cancer, I wasn't scared. I felt a little bit out of control. But I am a (inaudible) and I wasn't going to sit at home and cry about it. It's just given me deeper perspective, and I'm really riding that wave of living in the moment and, like, enjoying life.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Can you take me back to the moment when you first realized something was wrong?\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 I felt a lump and I said, ohm that doesn't feel right. And I went and got looked at, had an ultrasound. They were like, everything looks fine. And I said, but I've got this achy arm and I feel like, I kept waking up with pins and needles, and all these, like, things going ping - ping. Maybe this is what it's, like, is that anything? And they're like, okay, with those, let's do a biopsy. I remember phoning my mom first. And I was just like, well, I can't die because I have Sky.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With her 2-year-old son, Sky, and partner Chanan Colman by her side, Jessie J underwent a mastectomy in her right breast in June, documenting the process for more than 14 million \uc2 Instagram\uc2  followers.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know you said that you're a sharer.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, why was it so important for you?\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 Uh oh. Cut the cameras.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But why was it so important for you to share all of this with the world?\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 I'm just like, we need to talk about these things. I've been very lucky, super lucky to find it so early. It wasn't invasive, thank god. I know there's a chance it could come back. But until then, we're living life.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The singer-songwriter recently sharing this moment of connection and a hug with Princess Kate.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did Princess Kate say anything to you mom to mom?\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 She just said thank you. She, I know you going through a tough time, and I said I know you have too, and I just said, I want to acknowledge that mom to mom, you know, cancer survivor to cancer survivor, I see you and I feel you. Can I give you a hug? And she said, of course you can. I'm grateful that she received it in the way it was meant.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, eight years since her last album, Jessie J is back with "Don't Tease Me With a Good Time," singing about the highs and lows of the past few years. From "H.A.P.P.Y" with a video that includes a special cameo from Sky.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 He's just, honestly, the love of my life.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 I - I went through many journeys to find him. And I'm just so grateful that he held onto me.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To the emotional, "I'll Never Know Why" about the death of her bodyguard and friend.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 This album covers the journey of grief to finding joy again and the circle again.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Your album is like a therapy session.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. I remember Justin Timberlake saying to me once, your music needs to be like a slice of your life. And I feel like this is like a pizza. You know? Like, it's not a slice. It's a full pizza.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE J (ENTERTAINER)\par \par \uc2 But they kind of all go together, and I don't think this is the comeback. I think this is the warm-up for the thing.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And guys, she also says her biggest advice to people is just trust your instinct, whether it's in your career or your health. She says, you know your mind. You know your body. Trust that. Well, we can all see Jessie J as she says, bring her full self to the stage. She's kicking off the North American leg of her tour in January. Guys?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, so talented and so thoughtful. Thank you, Maggie.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, bound to save lives, George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251231elc50000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW STUDY ON PREGNANCY & AUTISM SIGNS AND SYMPTOMS TO LOOK FOR\b0\par\par\uc2 916 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 November 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THANKSGIVING TRAVEL TURBULENCE\par \par \uc2 STORMS, TRAVEL, THANKSGIVING, AIRPORTS, ROADS\par \par \uc2 STORMS, SNOW, WINDS, LOW PRESSURE, LAKE EFFECT\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2 , DEMOCRATS, KELLY, VIDEO, SERVICE MEMBERS, ILLEGAL\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, DEAL, WITKOFF, ALLEGATIONS, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 FIRE, HIGH RISE, DEAD, BAMBOO, RESIDENTS, TRAPPED\par \par \uc2 MEDICARE, PRICES, PHARMA, PRESCRIPTIONS, DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 RESCUE, CAPSIZE, COAST GUARD, WOODS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAKE EFFECT SNOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "DANCING WITH THE STARS"\par \par \uc2 LOUVRE, ARREST, HEIST, JEWELS, SECURITY\par \par \uc2 MISSING, FUGITIVE, TURNER, SEARCH, DAY, SOLICITATION\par \par \uc2 BLACK FRIDAY, DEALS, CONSUMERS, HOLLY, RETAIL\par \par \uc2 IRWIN, CARSON, "DANCING WITH THE STARS"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAKE EFFECT SNOW\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, THANKSGIVING\par \par \uc2 MORTGAGE, RATES, DROP\par \par \uc2 PREGNANCY, AUTISM, STUDY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have a "GMA" health alert now. New research out this morning finds a link between low thyroid hormone levels during pregnancy and an increased risk for autism. Who better than our chief medical correspondent Dr. Narula to break this study down for us.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY ON PREGNANCY & AUTISM\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. Well, Robin, autism spectrum disorder is felt to be really this combination of genetics and some sort of environmental factor that comes into play during a very sensitive time in the prenatal or fetal development period. And so people have looked at what could that environmental factor be.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sure.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And one of the things that has come up is could it be some alteration in maternal thyroid hormones. We know that maternal thyroid abnormalities seem to be associated with decreases in cognition, in IQ, and problems with language. Maternal thyroid hormone, really important in that very first trimester when the fetus isn't producing its own.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so researchers here in Israel looked at a single center, and they looked at about 51,000 births, and they looked at the mom's thyroid hormones, and then what happened to those children. And they in fact found, if the mothers had chronic hypothyroidism, meaning low thyroid before pregnancy, there was no association with autism spectrum disorder.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) However, if they have chronic hypothyroid and developed gestational hypothyroid, so hypothyroid during pregnancy, there was a two times increased risk of those kids developing autism spectrum disorder. In addition, the more trimesters that the thyroid level was low, the higher the risk of autism spectrum disorder. Children that were subjected to those lower thyroid hormones, more severe autism symptoms. So, this was an interesting study. Definitely need more studies to kind of help us out a little bit more, yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But still very, very interesting.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, what are some of the signs or symptoms that women should look for with this condition?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, what's interesting is that, you know, we, it's, there's no universal sort of screening recommendation at this point for the first trimester for pregnant women for thyroid. So, a lot of people practice very differently. And really what happens now is if you have high risk for hypothyroidism or thyroid disorder, you would have a blood test to try to look for that. But certainly women can pay attention to symptoms which would be changes in hair, skin, fatigue, cold or heat intolerance, constipation, diarrhea, anxiety, palpitations.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lot of these nonspecific symptoms actually that you may even have during pregnancy, so it could be hard to tease out. Certainly, if you have chronic hypothyroid before, you would want to make sure your levels are nicely in check. But definitely bring it up to your doctor.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Again, really important to point out, this was not a causation study. I don't want people to get alarmed. It's an association. And, in fact, a lot of those women who have those thyroid abnormalities in this study also have had IVF and high gestational high blood pressure. We're older, had, you know, hypertension. So, it may be some other factor.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Some factors.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Not necessarily the thyroid, but definitely eye-opening, definitely important to do more research in this area.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, and eye opening that you did this so well with your mom and dad in the wings right there.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We had a chance to meet them during the commercial break. I know how proud you are.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Aw, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And how proud they are of you. We can see how they're looking at you right now.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I was watching them the entire time you were talking. The smiles could not go away. It was great.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, yes, I love them so much. I'm so happy to have them in my life still and to be here for Thanksgiving, so it's special.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very special.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251217elbq0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE "HOW TO SURVIVE MENOPAUSE"\b0\par\par\uc2 1,788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 BEE, MENOPAUSE, COMEDY, SHEPHERD, BERRY, PALTROW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks for joining us. Tonight, comedian Samantha Bee bringing her hilarious take on menopause to the masses with her send up of the topic that suddenly seems to be everywhere. She says it's about time.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 So how's it going, everybody? Oh, I am so excited to talk to you about menopause here tonight.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You might have noticed menopause is having a moment.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I did tell a room full of, like, Hollywood type people that I wanted to do a show about this topic, and one of them said, oh my God, that's great. Menopause is super-hot right now. To which I said, sir, you truly have no idea.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Packed audiences laughing uproariously about how to survive menopause.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tell me about the name of your tour.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I needed to know how to survive menopause and I was like, I think that will state very up front with the show is about.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So you're like a girlfriends guide to menopause.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 A girlfriend's guide, which like, you really need girlfriends in this period of your life.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Comedian Samantha Bee got famous for her sassy female energy on "The Daily Show."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "THE DAILY SHOW"\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART ("THE DAILY SHOW")\par \par \uc2 I don't I don't think we have five.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 Shush. Easy little Jon, mama's in control now.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And her irreverent late night show, "Full Frontal With Samantha Bee."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "FROM FRONTAL WITH SAMANTHA BEE"\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I'm going to do something that should have been done decades ago, I'm going to teach sex-ed to senators.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's now nearly an empty nester, the wife and mom of three teenagers, on the road selling out theaters and talking menopause across the country.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I feel like you are really breaking this conspiracy of silence around it.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 It actually was really a process, and I feel like if one person can just not be embarrassed by it, that helps the people who come behind, you know what I mean? I'm trying to like untether from shame.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How did you get over your own embarrassment about it?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I got over my sense of shame about it by just grinding it out. I felt an energy in the audience, and I thought, I'm going to lean even further into it because there's a need, because there's actually like, a need and a desire to just, like, talk about it all together all at once, just like, get it out.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's not the only one. Influencers and celebrities are also speaking out. This year Melanie Sanders hit a nerve with her we do not care club.\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I started this club for all women in perimenopause, menopause and post-menopause. We are putting the world on notice that we simply just do not care much anymore.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Melanie's millions of followers, including actress \uc2 Ashley Judd\uc2 , joining in on the honesty.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY JUDD\uc2  (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I probably have cellulite, I don't care.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Other stars also chiming in about the change.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I'm in menopause, okay.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Oscar winning actress Halle Berry, a big advocate for awareness, getting real in her support of a Senate bill to increase menopause funding and education.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 It has to be destigmatized. The shame has to be taken out of menopause. We have to talk about this very normal part of our life that happens.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Goop founder and actress Gwyneth Paltrow, making this admission on her site.\par \par \uc2 GWYNETH PALTROW (GOOD FOUNDER & ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I think menopause gets a really bad rap and needs a bit of a rebranding.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Even Oprah Winfrey giving the topic the Oprah treatment in a primetime ABC special, "The Menopause Revolution."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM: "THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION"\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY ("THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION")\par \par \uc2 We can stop the stereotypes and we can change this conversation.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Half the population goes through it, so you'd think we'd know what menopause is.\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB-GYN)\par \par \uc2 We've diagnosed it as a time frame in a woman's life, when she hasn't had a cycle consecutively for 12 months. The symptoms of menopause most commonly can be hot flashes, night sweats, mood changes, disturbances in sleep, decrease in libido.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Jessica Shepard is a Dallas based, board certified OB-GYN and author of "Generation M: Living Well in Perimenopause and Menopause."\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB-GYN)\par \par \uc2 There's a full range of how women want to approach the treatment and management of their symptoms. So some of the things that we see is going to be hormone therapy, which may give you back enough of those hormones to allow you to feel better, to function better because your organs and your body needs it.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This week, the FDA announcing it will remove the black box warning labels from those hormone replacement therapy drugs used to treat menopause. For Sam Bee, cracking jokes about an awkward topic helps bring awareness.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 None of this is really all that fair.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her show, based on her own experiences, delivers on that idea.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 There is so much practical advice out there for every problem that you can imagine. How to survive tornadoes, zombie attacks, how to contend with an erection that lasts more than four hours. But when it comes to menopause, everyone's like, I don't know, have you even tried drinking water?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The inevitable hormonal change has long been dramatized, but rarely explained, from television's "Golden Girls."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "THE GOLDEN GIRLS"\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I am not dying, Rose, but I might as well be. It's menopause.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To movies like "Fried Green Tomatoes."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "FRIED GREEN TOMATOES"\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Simple, honey, you're going through the change.\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA SHEPHERD (BOARD CERTIFIED OB-GYN)\par \par \uc2 When we see television and social media placing emphasis on a topic that for so long, women would not talk about, I think that this allows women to say, you know what, maybe this is some funny some days. Or maybe I don't have to feel alone in the moments where I'm sweating half to death or I can't sleep.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There's a lot of misunderstanding. Give me the litany of symptoms that you were going through.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 Ears itching. My hair really started to fall out. I started to get, like my moods were so erratic. But primarily I really could not sleep through the night, and my job was so demanding that waking up at 2:00 in the morning every single night was, like, not helping.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her act also taking on the seeming double standard of treatment in the medical community.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 If your (censored by network) stops working, all available dollars are spent ensuring that you can still get rock hard at the age of 97.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 We take men's bodies seriously. You know, we have symptoms of things that appear differently in men's bodies and women's bodies.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so when a man's organs versus a woman's organs.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 Well, the ovaries are one of the only organs in the human body that when they start to slow down, we kind of all just go, oh, well, you know, like I wipe my hands clean of that. I mean, if your heart starts to beat erratically, everybody takes it very seriously.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 They will put in a pacemaker immediately. Everybody is like, all systems go. And if your penis, I mean, if you are entering penile winter, there's so much technology available to you.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And yet there are men in your audience, you know.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 There are lots actually.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So what are you, what are you trying to say to the men?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I love having men in the audience. That actually shocked me. I did think that it would be just like large groups of women, but actually a lot of them bring their partners because, again, it is a shared experience with our loving partners.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You've had a late night talk show, variety show, and now you're out on the road. How does your career evolve?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I've done the like the kind of like larger scale work that I really wanted to do, and I kind of said everything that I wanted to say in that realm. I really did a lot of soul searching about what it was that I felt I had left to say.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 This topic is so interesting to me, so it is pure joy for me to do it. It's like driven out of pure love for the audience that I'm serving.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 Together, we are all going to survive menopause. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOMORROW: I'M GOING TO GET YOU"\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What a lifesaver. And now a look ahead at tomorrow's show. A young woman survives a brutal assault, vowing that one day her attacker will be brought to justice.\par \par \uc2 VICTIM (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 And the whole time, all I remember saying is, I'm going to get you. You're not going to get away with it.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) How her determination helped solve a cold case murder.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHANGING YOUR EYE COLOR\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And when we come back, these aren't colored contacts, they're the results of a pricey, controversial new procedure to change the color of your eyes permanently. After the break.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020251117elbd0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BREAKING AT 8 HEADLINE NEWS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,791 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WINTER, SNOW, ARCTIC AIR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 WINTER, WIND GUST, SNOW, WEATHER, TRACK\par \par \uc2 BILL, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN, THUNE, JEFFRIES, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE PASSES DEAL TO END GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 SNAP, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN, BENEFITS\par \par \uc2 PLANE CRASH, JAMAICA, MOSER, WURM, SMITH, MARILL, COLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY PLANE CRASH INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 MLB, GAMBLING, CLASE, ORTIZ, BETTING LIMIT, MALONEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MLB TAKES ACTION AFTER PITCHERS CHARGED IN GAMBLING CASE\par \par \uc2 BABY FORMULA, RECALL, BOTULISM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BABY FORMULA RECALL OVER BOTULISM OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , "MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL", DABOLL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW OVERNIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EAGLES BEAT PACKERS IN "MNF" DEFENSIVE BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "THE GOONIES"\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN, BILODEAU, DUFFY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAPPENING NOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FRUSTRATION GROW AS \uc2 FAA\uc2  INCREASES FLIGHT REDUCTIONS\par \par \uc2 KELLY, TRAVEL, REFUND, CANCELLATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIVE ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POINTS GUY ANSWERS YOUR TRAVEL QUESTIONS\par \par \uc2 PRINCE WILLIAM, CANCER, PARENTING\par \par \uc2 VETERANS DAY, WOODRUFF, STAND UP FOR HEROES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, COLD, SNOW, HRT, BLACK BOX WARNING, REMOVAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARCTIC BLAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) First, looking at the top stories breaking at 8:00. We start with millions of Americans waking up to a blast of winter. Freezing temperatures from the northeast to deep south. Let's go back to Janai Norman in Syracuse. Good morning, Janai?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, George, and we are feeling that lake effect, the impacts of the lake effect snow here in Syracuse where the snow is still falling.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SYRACUSE, NY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8: MILLIONS WAKING UP TO BLAST OF WINTER\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's part of that wintry blast that 200 million Americans are waking up to and that's thanks to the polar vortex from Canada that is pushing that arctic air south and with wind expected up to 50 miles per hour in some areas, that is bringing some shocking temperature drops and the first major snow of the season. The Midwest already hit with white out conditions in parts of Michigan, lake effect snow in Chicago and had created some issues for drivers in Indiana, where police responded to more than 50 car crashes on Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The icy conditions there expected from Vermont all the way down to North Carolina. Here in upstate New York, there are parts of the area that proactively cancel classes because of the expected five inches or so that's expected to fall. It's still falling and Syracuse. You can see where we are, it's not quite sticking to the roadways that are getting a lot of traffic, but it is beautiful against the fall foliage. The backdrop of the colorful leaves a little bit of a silver lining and a reminder of what an early taste of winter this is, guys.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I'm gonna give you an early taste of your mama. Where's your hate? Because you know your mama's saying that right now. "Where's my girl's hat?" Come on. Okay.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I have my gloves, though. I've got the gloves, though.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Alright, okay. Thank you, Janai. Now we have a "GMA" Health Alert on hormone replacement therapy.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HORMONE REPLACEMENT THERAPY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The FDA announcing it is removing the black box warning on labels that target women going through menopause. Our chief medical correspondent Dr. Tara Narula is here with more on this.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) People really wanted to hear more about this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8: FDA REMOVES "BLACK BOX" WARNING FROM HORMONE THERAPY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This was initially to protect women, so tell us more about it and why it's being removed now.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Robin, back in the early 2000s there was a large study and that study of women who used hormone replacement therapy showed there was an increased risk of breast cancer and cardiovascular disease and that really lead to physicians pulling back on prescribing, patients being afraid to ask for this and also, the FDA to place this black box morning, it's the highest safety risk warning, on all types of hormone replacement therapy, pills, gels, patches, that said that it might be associated with breast cancer, probable dementia and cardiovascular disease.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What has happened in the last several years, however, is a reanalysis of that initial data and when they did that reanalysis, they found that those risks were really overstated. Why? Because the population of women that took hormones in the study were older. They were in their sixties and seventies. Very different from the younger women that may be taking hormone replacement therapy. In that subset, they did not see those risks and they actually saw some benefits that might outweigh the risks and some protective effects.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that has really led to where we are now, which is a more nuanced approach to hormone replacement therapy that looks at the age of women, how far they are out from menopause, what their underlying risk might be. And so, now, with this removal of the black box morning, there's no question we're going to see probably more prescriptions, women asking about this, doctors prescribing it and really opening the door to conversation.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think it's important, which is a good thing, to say however, that with every drug there are always risks and there may be certain women for which hormone replacement therapy is not ideal. Maybe they have a history of breast cancer or blood clots and so, it is going to be important for women to still talk to their doctor about this and also, you know, I think the messaging that came out yesterday, it's important to say this is not a panacea and a cure for everything, right? But the Menopause Society, which is really a respected group that looks at this...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very much so.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...yes, they basically have said that if you are essentially younger and healthier in general, the risks are lower. If you are older and farther away from menopause, again, the risks may be higher and that for some women, systemic estrogen may still some carry risks and you do need to talk to your doctor. The removal of the black box morning on vaginal estrogen, I think, everybody really is in favor of because it really is safe and effective. But bottom line, this is, I think, a really good thing for women's health. For those of us in the women's health space, we are talking about this, women are asking about it. Hopefully, we'll start seeing education in medical schools and residencies, which we have not had and more investment in funding in this.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, younger women should consider taking it?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, really, it's, again, George, it's about timing. That is the key.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONSIDERING HRT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN WITHIN 10 YEARS OF MENOPAUSE/UNDER 60\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TALK TO A DOCTOR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EVALUATE PERSONAL SYMPTOMS & UNDERLYING MEDICAL CONDITIONS/RISK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MANY TYPES OF HRT & FORMULATIONS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, if you are under 60 or within ten years of menopause, this is the population of women that should be talking to their doctor if they're having symptoms of perimenopause or are concerned about bone loss. Also, when they have that shared decision-making, individualized discussion, they should look at a woman's symptoms, her personal preferences, her underlying risk and risk factors and her family history. Women should know that there are different formulations they can take. There are now two non-hormonal types of treatments for perimenopausal symptoms, as well.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you can go to the menopause.org, the Menopause Society...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...put in your zip code and find a certified menopause practitioner who can help you kind of weed through all of this.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is amazing. I mean, the - everybody is just so comfortable in talking about this now and it's so necessary.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Robin, there was a time where I remember when I was told, "We're not gonna talk about menopause on morning television."\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know. I know. But...\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have come a long, long way, thank God. We have a long way to go, but...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep, that we do.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \u8230\'20\'26yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Alright, thank you, Dr. Narula.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:10 LEGACY OF TUSKEGEE AIRMEN\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming up in our "GMA" Morning Menu, the legacy of the Tuskegee Airmen changing the lives of student pilots, now able to pursue their dreams to fly thanks to an innovative new program.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:47 FALL COOKING WITH ALISON ROMAN\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also ahead, our "GMA" chef-in-residence, best-selling cookbook author Alison Roman is here with pantry-friendly family meals perfect for fall.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:38 MATTHEW RHYS LIVE\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus joining us to tell us about his new show, there he is, "The Beast In Me." And Lara and Sam are with a special guest.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:30 KE HUY QUAN TALKS "ZOOTOPA 2"\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A few special guests.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I was gonna say that, too.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But among them, the one only Ke Huy Quan with us. He's gonna talk to us about his new role as Gary De'Snake...\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Gary.,,\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...in "Zootopia 2."\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \u8230\'20\'26De'Snake. His last name is De'Snake, right? Alright, and he's bringing us along for a fang-tastic ride, if you will, in the movie. So, what do you think, Gary?\par \par \uc2 KE HUY QUAN ("ZOOTOPIA 2")\par \par \uc2 We shall succeed.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we do. Alright, that's all coming up right here on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Bye, guys.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wanna race? Wanna race?\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251124elbb0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WORKING WOMEN & MENOPAUSE WOMEN MISSING WORK BECAUSE OF MENOPAUSE SYMPTOMS\b0\par\par\uc2 631 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIR TRAVEL CHAOS\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, FLIGHTS, REDUCTIONS, CHAOS, TRAVELERS\par \par \uc2 SEYMOUR, SHUTDOWN, AVIATION, TRAVELERS, CHAOS, REFUND\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, SNAP, MCCONNELL, TRUMP, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 UPS, CRASH, VICTIMS, MISSING, MAINTENANCE, \uc2 FAA\par \par \uc2 KNEELAND, NFL, COWBOYS, SUICIDE, TRIBUTES\par \par \uc2 FARMERS' ALMANAC, WEATHER, FOLKLORE, PUBLISHING, AMERICANA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORM TRACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ANDREW, EPSTEIN, HOUSE, GARCIA, GIUFFRE\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, PANTRIES, SNAP, SCOTT, FLEMING, FAMILIES\par \par \uc2 SNAP, SHUTDOWN, DONATIONS, NESTLE, MOE, GRAVES\par \par \uc2 NURSES, MOTHERHOOD, AEROMEDICAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAKE EFFECT\par \par \uc2 FLIGHT, REDUCTIONS, GOVERNMENT, SHUTDOWN, \uc2 FAA\par \par \uc2 WEIGHT, LOSS, DRUG, DEALS, WEGOVY, ZEPBOUND\par \par \uc2 MENOPAUSE, DIFFICULTIES\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're gonna turn now to our "GMA" cover story. Something women have known for a long time but now more and more are talking about it, the difficulty that menopause can have on your working life. Elizabeth Schulze has a look at how growing attention on the issue is leading to some change. Good morning, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORKING WOMEN & MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Rebecca. So, recent research is starting to show the economic toll of women missing work because of their menopause symptoms, and now we are seeing a push from employers and even from one state to try to help change that.\par \par \uc2 NAOMI WATTS (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 This morning I woke up in a delicate puddle of sweat, nature's way of saying good morning during menopause.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, as the conversation around menopause turns more public, with celebrities like Naomi Watts, Oprah, and Halle Berry opening up about their menopause journeys, women like Khris Rogers in California are also speaking out about the need for accommodations in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 KHRIS ROGERS (QUIT JOB DUE TO MENOPAUSE SYMPTOMS)\par \par \uc2 I just couldn't do it anymore and it became a big issue.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She quit her office job after experiencing menopause symptoms, including mood swings, joint pain, and heavy bleeding at work.\par \par \uc2 KHRIS ROGERS (QUIT JOB DUE TO MENOPAUSE SYMPTOMS)\par \par \uc2 I was having to go to the bathroom, like, every half an hour, and it's really disruptive to your work day.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's not alone. Stanford researchers called economic losses the menopause penalty, the report estimating that working women's earnings may decline 10 percent in the four years after they were diagnosed with menopause, when they had to work fewer hours, or leave the work force altogether because of their symptoms.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUSAN LOEB-ZEITLIN (OB-GYN AT NEWYORK-PRESBYTERIAN & WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 We mid-life women are definitely at the peak of our careers and we're needed in the work force. And if we can't perform our jobs, then that's really significant.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Companies and policy-makers are taking note. This summer, Rhode Island became the first state to pass a law requiring that employers provide reasonable accommodation to women going through menopause. Doctors say that can include offering better ventilation or giving the option to opt out of a uniform to help women manage hot flashes, and setting time aside for more frequent bathroom breaks.\par \par \uc2 KHRIS ROGERS (QUIT JOB DUE TO MENOPAUSE SYMPTOMS)\par \par \uc2 Half of our population is women, and every single woman that lives long enough is going to go through menopause. And most of us are going to have symptoms of some kind. I think there just needs to be more flexibility in general in our workplaces.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The \uc2 FDA\uc2  did just approve a second non-hormonal drug that can help treat hot flashes during menopause, so those are taken once daily. They could help more women deal with their symptoms during the work day, guys.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251119elb70000i\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PERSON OF THE WEEK MOMBIES RAISING MONEY FOR BREAST CANCER RESEARCH\b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DELTA AIRLINES, CAPE AIR, NEAR-COLLISION, ATC, WALSH\par \par \uc2 TERROR THREAT, RAID, ARRESTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FBI\uc2  SAYS HALLOWEEN TERROR PLOT FOILED\par \par \uc2 SNAP, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN, TRUMP, MUTHIAH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE ORDERS GOVT. TO FUND BENEFITS\par \par \uc2 STORMS, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD RAIN SETS OFF DEADLY FLOODING\par \par \uc2 PRINCE ANDREW, GIUFFRE, EPSTEIN FILES, ROBERTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GIUFFRE FAMILY SPEAKS OUT ON ANDREW FALL\par \par \uc2 ROLLER COASTER, SEATBELT, EVANS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARROWING ROLLER COASTER RIDE\par \par \uc2 PRESCHOOL, KIDNAPPING, PLANE CRASH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE: MAN SMASHES WINDOW TO GRAB STUDENT\par \par \uc2 GILGEOUS-ALEXANDER, ROBBERY, DAYLIGHT SAVING TIME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE INDEX\par \par \uc2 MOMBIES, BREAST CANCER RESEARCH, DONATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PERSON OF THE WEEK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Finally, tonight here, the moms we're cheering for this Halloween and what you've done.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, on this Halloween, so many of you responding to something you saw right here this week. The mombies. Not zombies, they call themselves Mombies for a reason. They're raising money for \uc2 Breast Cancer Research\uc2 . Vanilla Ice, Beyonce, Michael Jackson's "Thriller." Fifty moms in all from Fairfield, Connecticut, 10 years strong. And after our report this week, the mombies telling us, "World News Tonight" viewers donating more than $80,000 and one anonymous viewer then promising to match it, doubling it to more than 160 grand. And tonight, just in, they were getting ready for their big Halloween performance in Molly's kitchen. Suzanne doing Molly's makeup.\par \par \uc2 MOMBIE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 You do a scar like nobody else, don't you?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And many of these mommies are not only supporters, but cancer survivors themselves. Cassandra.\par \par \uc2 MOMBIE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Is being a mombie extra special because you're a survivor?\par \par \uc2 MOMBIE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It absolutely is.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And right here tonight, moments before they performed.\par \par \uc2 MOMBIE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The mombies grateful to all of you at home.\par \par \uc2 MOMBIE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We've just learned that, thanks to ABC viewers, we're $50,000 shy of the million-dollars raised for Sloan-Kettering Metastatic Breast Cancer Research.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And here it is, the Mombies moments ago performing to Lady Gaga in front of a town hall in Fairfield.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOMBIES' PERFORMANCE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) On this Halloween night, a message to all of you.\par \par \uc2 MOMBIES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, ABC, and happy Halloween.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so we choose the Mombies. Mombies.org. I'll see you Monday. Good night.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020251104elav0000a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 40 YEARS OF PINK FATHER & DAUGHTER BATTLE BREAST CANCER TOGETHER\b0\par\par\uc2 933 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE MELISSA ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 MELISSA, JAMAICA, RESIDENTS, WINDS, FLOODING, DAMAGE, TOURISTS\par \par \uc2 MELISSA, TRACK, CUBA, WINDS, SURGE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , ASIA, TRADE, XI, THIRD TERM\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, STALEMATE, SNAP, FOOD, GOP, DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, FOOD BANKS, SNAP, FURLOUGHED, VELEZ\par \par \uc2 US, STRIKES, BOATS, DRUGS, TRUMP, EVIDENCE\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, STRIKES, GAZA, KILLED, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , \uc2 IDF\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , MARKETPLACE, BETTING, CRYPTO, CONFLICT\par \par \uc2 WORLD SERIES, \uc2 BLUE JAYS\uc2 , DODGERS, TIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WIND & FIRE DANGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "DANCING WITH THE STARS"\par \par \uc2 DRAKE, ONLINE, GAMBLING, LAWSUIT, STAKE, BETS, DARDEN\par \par \uc2 FED, CUTS, INTERESTS, MORTGAGES, CREDIT CARDS, LOANS\par \par \uc2 SNAP, FOOD, APPS, SAVINGS, STAPLES, BODGE\par \par \uc2 WINGS, "HOT ONES VERSUS"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD WARMTH\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE, MELISSA, CUBA\par \par \uc2 MASSEY, TRIAL, DELIBERATIONS\par \par \uc2 PAYPAL, OPENAI, SHOPPING\par \par \uc2 TECHNOLOGY, PARALYZED, HOPE, \uc2 WANDERCRAFT\uc2 , EXOSKELETON\par \par \uc2 GMA\par \par \uc2 ANYDAY COOKWARE, NOKBOX, CHI, GEOMETRY, ELEMIS\par \par \uc2 "DANCING WITH THE STARS", "ONLY MURDERS IN THE BUILDING", CAREY\par \par \uc2 BREAST, CANCER, FLEMING\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back with 40 Years of Pink, celebrating 40 years of Breast Cancer Awareness Month. And this morning, Janai Norman is gonna bring us a story of a father and his daughter who we're learning they'll be fighting breast cancer together?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 40 YEARS OF PINK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FATHER & DAUGHTER BATTLE BREAST CANCER TOGETHER\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, guys. The thing about it, for a family to have one person fighting breast cancer, this family had two. 33-year-old Joyelle Fleming and her dad Alton always had a lot to share, but nothing prepared them for 2023, when just weeks apart both were diagnosed with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In January 2023, a gynecologist found a lump in Joyelle Fleming's breast during an annual exam just five months before her wedding.\par \par \uc2 JOYELLE FLEMING (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 She was kind of like, oh, do you not feel that? And I was like no.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Despite having no symptoms, tests showed the Chicagoan had stage two triple positive cancer. She was 31 years old.\par \par \uc2 JOYELLE FLEMING (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I had, like, a little self-care/pity party. And then I was like, cool we're going to tackle this head-on.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Joyelle and her fianc\u233\'00\'E9 Michael decided to postpone their wedding a year. Joyelle undergoing chemo and a double mastectomy at Northwestern Memorial in Chicago in early 2023. But six months into Joyelle's treatment, her father, Alton, who lives in Houston, noticed a lump while on a family vacation.\par \par \uc2 JOYELLE FLEMING (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I just felt pain in, in my left breast. It was hard. It just hurt.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A mammogram and biopsy showed the married dad of three also had breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 ALTON FLEMING (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 It was somewhat of a shock because if you look at the statistics, only about 1 percent of breast cancer cases are men.\par \par \uc2 JOYELLE FLEMING (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I reacted worse to that news that I did my own diagnosis. Is he going to be okay?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Genetic testing showed the two had different types of cancer and no genetic markers, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 DR. REGINA STEIN (ONCOLOGIST AT NORTHWESTERN MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 I would have absolutely suspected that there would've been a genetic link. There probably still is. But we haven't been able to identify it yet.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Cancer transforming into a team effort, and what a better coach for Alton than his own daughter.\par \par \uc2 ALTON FLEMING (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Encouragement that she gave me throughout the process was fabulous.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) June 22nd, 2024, Joyelle and Michael finally had their wedding.\par \par \uc2 JOYELLE FLEMING (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I can say I'm officially cancer free.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Alton bald from chemo on the big day, walked Joyelle down the aisle, and had a mastectomy two days later. Today, they're both cancer free and closer than ever.\par \par \uc2 ALTON FLEMING (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I am so proud of how she handled her breast cancer diagnosis as she went through the process.\par \par \uc2 JOYELLE FLEMING (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 That's so sweet, dad. Makes my heart happy knowing that I was able to do that for my dad.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A challenging road that they've been able to walk together, all worth it to be able to have Alton walk Joyelle down the aisle. And something that she has also talked about separately, because she was just 31 years old when she was diagnosed, she had to think about preserving her fertility because of going through cancer treatment. So, that was another thing on her plate while dealing with all this.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So much.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Handling it so well. They're a dynamic duo, those two.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right? I know. Incredible.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's a good lesson that men should get checked as well.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) My brother went through it. And, yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And my brother did too. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. Important reminders.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Very much so.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Janai.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251107elat0003k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MOMBIE APOCALYPSE MOMS DRESS UP AND DANCE FOR BREAST CANCER RESEARCH\b0\par\par\uc2 149 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LOVATO, ALBUM, "IT'S NOT THAT DEEP", TOUR, ADDICTION, MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 "BUGONIA", STONE, PLEMONS, LANTHIMOS\par \par \uc2 MOMBIES, BREAST CANCER, RESEARCH, DANCE, "THRILLER"\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And finally, tonight they're back.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) They call themselves the mombies. A group of mothers dressing up and dancing for breast cancer research. Two years ago, it was Michael Jackson's "Thriller," airing on ABC, raising $80,000, bringing their multiyear total to more than a half million. This year, rehearsing their dance moves and gearing up to raise even more.\par \par \uc2 MOMBIES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Thanks ABC and Happy Halloween.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good for them. That's "Nightline" for this evening. Catch our full episodes on \uc2 Disney\uc2 + and \uc2 Hulu\uc2 . We'll see you right back here same time tomorrow. Thanks for the company, America. Good night.\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020251031elat0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMERICA STRONG THE MOMBIES ARE BACK, BETTER THAN EVER\b0\par\par\uc2 521 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HURRICANE MELISSA, JAMAICA\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE MELISSA, TRACK, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 BOAT, STRIKES, TRUMP, PAUL, KELLY, VENEZUELA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 14 KILLED IN MULTIPLE BOAT STRIKES\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN, LAWSUIT, SCHUMER, SNAP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FOOD AID TO RUN OUT FOR 42M+ PEOPLE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , JOHNSON, JAPAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP HITS AT MILITARY IN U.S. CITIES\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, AI, LAYOFFS, UPS, HUANG, ULLRICH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON CUTTING AT LEAST 14,000 JOBS\par \par \uc2 MONKEYS, SEARCH, COVID, HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUCK HAULING RESEARCH MONKEYS CRASHES\par \par \uc2 POLICE CHASE, SALDIVAR, ARREST, FALL, STADIUM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECT NAMED IN MOTORCYCLE CHASE\par \par \uc2 STABBING, \uc2 LUFTHANSA AIRLINES\uc2 , DR. SEUSS, BOOK, WORLD SERIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE INDEX\par \par \uc2 MOMBIES, BREAST CANCER AWARENESS, MARTINI, SULLIVAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: #AMERICASTRONG\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Finally, tonight here, Halloween is almost here and so are the Mombies, and they are "America strong."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, with Halloween three days away, the moms are back. They're not zombies. They call themselves the Mombies. Moms dressing up together, dancing together, raising money for breast cancer research. Michael Jackson's "Thriller."\par \par \uc2 MOMBIE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Let's go.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) We first met them right here two years ago. We've celebrated them every year since. And tonight, they tell us ABC "World News" viewers have donated more than $150,000. And those mombies are back with what you told them, your generosity, your kind words.\par \par \uc2 MOMBIE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Jet (PH) from North Carolina. He said, "The mombies are an inspiration."\par \par \uc2 MOMBIE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 From Elizabeth in Grand Junction who said, "From a very grateful cancer survivor."\par \par \uc2 MOMBIE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Norman in Virginia, in honor of his wife, a 49-year cancer survivor.\par \par \uc2 MOMBIE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Carlos from Texas who said, "Go, mommies, you are doing so much good in the world." All of this really filled our cauldron.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Fifty moms in all from Fairfield, Connecticut, ten years strong. Among them, two you met right here before, moms battling breast cancer. Karen Sullivan practicing her dance moves during chemotherapy. Joy Martini, her nurses dancing with her. Tonight, Joy now tells us she's healthy, no signs of cancer. And joining us tonight, Joy with Karen who is still in the fight, getting chemotherapy and hopeful.\par \par \uc2 KAREN SULLIVAN (CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for all you have done, that you're doing for those of us with metastatic breast cancer, including myself.\par \par \uc2 JOY MARTINI (CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 And putting the mombies on the screen every year, we totally appreciate it. It's so important in our efforts to raise money for people dealing with what you're dealing with.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And tonight, all of the moms rehearsing their newest routine with three days to go and a new message from those mombies.\par \par \uc2 MOMBIES (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, ABC, and happy Halloween.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) They are terrifying. The mombies, we sure do love you. If you'd like to help them out and join the fight against breast cancer, visit mombies.org. I'm David Muir. I'll see you right back here tomorrow. It's not Halloween without them. Good night.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020251030elas0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 40 YEARS OF PINK SUPERCHARGE YOUR DIET WITH \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\b0\par\par\uc2 1,609 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE HOUSE DEMOLITION\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , WHITE, HOUSE, DEMOLISHED, CLINTON, TILLIS\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH, DRUG, BOAT, TRUMP, RUSSIA, SANCTIONS\par \par \uc2 NYC, MAYOR, DEBATE, CUOMO, MAMDANI, SLIWA, ADAMS\par \par \uc2 MASSEY, MURDER, GRAYSON, FARLEY, TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 RECALL, EGGS, SALMONELLA, PRICE\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , MAVS, FLAGG, WEMBANYAMA, SPURS, EDGECOMBE, SIXERS, ANUNOBY,\par \par \uc2 COPELAND, BALLET, ABT, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TROPICAL OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , BAD BUNNY, GOODELL, "SNL"\par \par \uc2 CHARLES, LEO, VATICAN, SHIP, ANDREW, EPSTEIN, GUIFFRE\par \par \uc2 RANCHERS, BEEF, TRUMP, ARGENTINA\par \par \uc2 KELLY, CRUISE, AI, BOOKING, DEALS\par \par \uc2 ROMAN, DACHSHUND, EAGLES, JERSEY\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM SEATTLE, WASHINGTON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FUTURECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEXT WEEK'S PRECIP OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOUVRE HEIST VIDEO\par \par \uc2 LOUVRE, ROBBERY, DNA, MANHUNT, FRANCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 SNAP, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN, WILLIAMS\par \par \uc2 WEIGHT LOSS DRUGS, CARDIOVASCULAR DISEASE, STUDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEIGHT LOSS & HEART HEALTH\par \par \uc2 BON JOVI, WORLD TOUR, KEATON, EDGECOMBE, \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GOOGLE\uc2 , QUANTUM COMPUTER, AI, KELLY, NEVEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE FUTURE BEYOND AI\par \par \uc2 DISCOUNT, SHOPPING, DEALS, FREE SHIPPING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FREE SHIPPING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIG SAVINGS ON SOMETHING FOR EVERYONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 "GMA", ANNIVERSARY, SPORTS\par \par \uc2 BEAUVAIS, "TAKEN AT A TRUCK STOP"\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, DIET, NUTRITION, BELLER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 40 YEARS OF PINK\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to our series, "40 Years of Pink," celebrating Breast Cancer Awareness Month. And this morning, let's talk about foods we could eat that could help lower the risk of cancer. Dietician, nutritionist, best-selling author and a dear friend of the book, "SpiceRack." \uc2 Rachel Beller\uc2  is here to show us some meals to supercharge our body all the long. The crew loves it when you're here. They love it. We know we're going to do it. So, just tell us a little bit about how diet could impact cancer risk.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPERCHARGE YOUR DIET WITH \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 So, the first thing I want to say is that there is no single food or diet that can prevent or cure cancer. It's all about consistently, you know, what you eat over time for the cumulative effect. So important. So, it can make a difference on that front, but it's important to mention that. And I have some great strategies. We're going to talk about three strategies that can help us.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're going to supercharged some everyday meals.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Everyday meals. Yes, exactly. We're going to do breakfast, lunch, even dessert. We get to start our day with a cookie and end our day with a cookie.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, wow. It's like, what does this, get 10 before 10 strategy?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes. So, what I find is that when you have a strategy, you're more likely to do something when you know what you're going for. So, 10 grams of fiber before 10:00 AM, because I recommend 30 to 35.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACHEL'S TIP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 10 GRAMS OF FIBER BEFORE 10AM\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 You get a third out-of-the-way, you're good to go. Such a good way to start your day. So, I've got these apple pie breakfast cookies. These are amazing.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Those are good.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EASY APPLE PIE BREAKFAST COOKIE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIBER, PROTEIN & PROTECTIVE PLANT COMPOUNDS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 And two of these gives you 10 grams of fiber, so it's easy, it's portable. I take it anywhere I go, travel. And what's amazing is that it has oats in it, rich in beta-glucans, supports our immunity. It has almond protein powder. I love single ingredient protein powders. Just simple, simply great. Applesauce for some sweetness, no added sugar, a date in there.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So good.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 ) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So good. And chia seeds, a fiber booster. You always want to use fiber booster. You're going to get five to seven grams of fiber. You could use basil seeds or chia seeds.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. And you always talk about fiber. So, why is it so important in our diet?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Fiber is important. It supports gut health, our immunity, hormonal balance. It helps remove excess estrogen out of the body.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIBER BENEFITS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Our weight management. Really, really important. And I've spice these up also.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Always.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Of course. So, I've added the morning boost blend by Beller Nutrition, or you can make your own. Ceylon cinnamon, nutmeg, allspice, clove, packed with antioxidants, anti-inflammatory properties. So, good stuff there.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, ready to go. Such a great start. Okay. So, now protein. Now, a double duty protein. You talked about how plant-based protein.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 That's right. Yes. In honor of Breast Cancer Awareness Month, we are sharing double-duty proteins that have fiber and protein.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 We want that dual effect. So, things like chickpeas, lentils, edamame. So, I've chosen chickpeas here. And we supercharge them, we made some lettuce cups here. So, things have to be simple.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPERCHARGED CHICKPEA LETTUCE CUPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAST, 5 INGREDIENT MEAL PACKED WITH NUTRIENTS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, tell us how you made this.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes, so what I did is I took cauliflower, which is rich in indole-3-carbinol, cruciferous vegetable, some chickpeas. And then I added your favorite. This is the...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, the veggietude. I'm telling you, it is so good.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 The veggietude.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) It just adds that little spice.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, and it just takes it to the next level. Antioxidants, anti-inflammatory properties and it's an all-purpose blend. So, it's got garlic, onion, turmeric, black pepper, and parsley. And you just sprinkle that on, a little avocado oil, put it in the oven, put it in lettuce cups. It's budget friendly, it's easy. Great stuff. So, then we've got to power it up with some probiotic-rich foods. Like, I've got some sauerkraut here, so you can add that.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, that's always good for the gut, yes.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Pickled onions, yeah, or you can add avocado.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Avocado.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Simple, easy, great protein.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Looking at ways to supercharge. Okay, we got to end it with a cookie. We started with a cookie, we got to end with a cookie.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Start your day, you got to end your day with a cookie.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Treat yourself.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Of course, treat yourself. It's very important. But it's also nice when these extra delicious treats have ingredients that yield a return, that are good for us.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 So, what I've got here is some almond flour, rich in vitamin E, fiber, protein. And then we've added some date syrup to add sweetness. It's not a refined sugar. We still want to be moderate, though, but we added that for sweetness. Walnut oil, which is rich in omega 3s.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOLDEN CHOCOLATE CHIP COOKIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PACKED WITH ANTIOXIDANTS, PROTEIN & HEALTHY FATS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 And then we've got chocolate chips, dark. And then, of course, we're spicing it up again.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) As always.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Spice synergy is very important because, again, easy to include, more antioxidants and anti-inflammatory properties. So, this the golden breakfast blend, which has pure raw cacao, Ceylon cinnamon, and turmeric.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, the turmeric.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 And look at that golden color. Tastes great. And, yeah, people can get these at bellernutrition.com if they want. But you can make your own.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. They are supercharged chocolate chip cookies.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) But this is, again, it's just the little things that we can do to just add a little spice.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 And keeping it simple. That's the key to success because with nutrition it's about the cumulative effect over time.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 It's not what we do sometimes, it's what we do most of the time that makes a difference. So, you have to keep it realistic.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And having a strategy. I like that, too.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you as always, Rachel. Coming up, we'll kick off our exciting new partnership supporting athletes from youth to pro both on and off the field. And they could be ready to go with this.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (REGISTERED DIETITIAN AND NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Oh, yeah, supercharged.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251103elan0000t\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE NEW FACES OF CANCER YOUNG WOMEN DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER ON THE RISE\b0\par\par\uc2 894 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: $230 MILLION PAYOUT PUSH\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, DOJ, $230 MIL, PROSECUTIONS, TAXPAYERS, ETHICS\par \par \uc2 ARREST, PARDON, JAN 6, RIOTER, JEFFRIES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , PUTIN, ABOUT-FACE, UKRAINE, WHITE HOUSE, DEMOLITION\par \par \uc2 ICE, NYC, RAIDS, VENDORS, MIGRANTS, SHOT\par \par \uc2 LOUVRE, HEIST, JEWELS, THIEVES, RECRIMINATIONS\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, JOBS, ROBOTS, REPLACE, AUTOMATION\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , OKC, ROCKETS, BANNER, LAKERS, WARRIORS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TROPICAL OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 STORY 2: HAS STRONG WORDS FOR FORMER CO-STAR'S HUSBAND\par \par \uc2 STAMOS, LOUGHLIN, DIVORCE, GIANNULLI, INTERVIEW\par \par \uc2 COYOTE, DOG, HOUSEKEEPER, CAMERA, INJURIES, RABIES, MCGILL\par \par \uc2 CARS, NEW, USED, PRICES, PETCHENIK, TRADE-INS, DRURY\par \par \uc2 MAILATA, SINGING, EAGLE, "VALERIE"\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT, SHUTDOWN, IMPACT\par \par \uc2 WALKING, STEPS, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 COFFEE, PRICE\par \par \uc2 "ALL'S FAIR", "DANCING WITH THE STARS", "SCREAM", MASK\par \par \uc2 VISP, THE DB METHOD, MOSHN, CHIRP, CROSSROPE, CUBITT\par \par \uc2 GMA, ANIMAL, MOMENTS\par \par \uc2 CANCER, DIAGNOSED\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, we are back now with the first part of our new series, The New Faces of Cancer, looking at the changing face of people being diagnosed with the disease, often younger and more diverse. And this month, as we celebrate 40 years of pink, Maggie Rulli joins us with a closer look at breast cancer. Good morning, Maggie.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE NEW FACES OF CANCER\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Rebecca, good morning. Yeah, you know, we're used to hearing that women should start getting mammograms by about age 40. But now we're seeing more and more women in their 30s and even 20s being diagnosed with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dallas Carol was 29 and pregnant when she did her breast exam and felt a lump.\par \par \uc2 DALLAS CAROL (DIAGNOSED WITH CANCER)\par \par \uc2 My heart sank. Within that moment, I just instantly knew what it was.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Britta Knight was 33 when she felt a stabbing pain in her breast.\par \par \uc2 BRITTA KNIGHT (DIAGNOSED WITH CANCER)\par \par \uc2 I'm think I'll find something small, I'll be able to go back to work in the morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUNG WOMEN DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER ON THE RISE\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Both women diagnosed with metastatic breast cancer, cancer that has spread throughout the body. Breast cancer is the most common cancer in women who are being diagnosed at younger ages. The sharpest rise has been seen in those under 50, with steep increases among Hispanic and Asian Americans. Meanwhile, rates of metastatic breast cancer in women under 40 rose almost 3 percent each year between 2004 and 2021. While genetics, heavy drinking, and the environment may play a role, researchers still can't fully explain the increase.\par \par \uc2 DR. AIXA SOYANO (BREAST ONCOLOGIST AT MOFFITT CANCER CENTER)\par \par \uc2 It could be exposure to different foods, environmental agents. We believe that a combination of everything is what's making us see more cancer in young people.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And the impact on young people can be severe, with patients starting families and careers. The good news, breast cancer treatments have improved significantly in the past decade. Britta, now 43, has been living for ten years with metastatic breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 BRITTA KNIGHT (DIAGNOSED WITH CANCER)\par \par \uc2 The one thing my oncologist did say was this is not a terminal disease. This is a chronic illness.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) With two babies and a stepdaughter at home, Dallas, now 33, opted for an early cancer vaccine trial at \uc2 Moffitt Cancer Center in Tampa\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DALLAS CAROL (DIAGNOSED WITH CANCER)\par \par \uc2 I'm very much go big or go home.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Researchers there reeducating her own cells to recognize and attack breast tumors more effectively. Today, with the addition of chemo and radiation, Dallas is doing great.\par \par \uc2 DR. AIXA SOYANO (BREAST ONCOLOGIST AT MOFFITT CANCER CENTER)\par \par \uc2 I'm amazed of the responses that we've gotten. They do think that we're getting closer to curing a lot of (inaudible) from cancer.\par \par \uc2 DALLAS CAROL (DIAGNOSED WITH CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Cancer has taken so much from me, but I vowed to myself I wasn't going to let it take my happiness or my spirit. And I will, I will watch my kids grow up.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dallas says she had to beg doctors to take her lumps seriously while pregnant, and says it is so important for women to advocate for themselves. And guys, we also have a really important update from our story yesterday. You remember Stephanie Wysaski? She was the woman who discovered she had breast cancer while she was pregnant. Look at these photos here. She gave birth to her little girl, who looks just about perfect, doesn't she?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, love to see that.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) They named her Georgie Belize. Now, you might also have noticed there was a happy anniversary balloon, because it's also their eighth anniversary.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. So much to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) What a celebration.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Thank you so much for following that up.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's good to...\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is great news.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Maggie.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251031elam0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 40 YEARS OF PINK BATTLING BREAST CANCER THROUGH PREGNANCY\b0\par\par\uc2 886 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CAGLE, ARREST, DICKENS, SCHIERBAUM\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON, JEFFRIES, DUFFY, THOMAS, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN'S GROWING IMPACT\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , BALLROOM, WHITE HOUSE, RASKIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PARTS OF WHITE HOUSE EAST WING DEMOLISHED\par \par \uc2 AMAZON WEB SERVICES\uc2 , OUTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON: ISSUES BEHIND MASSIVE WEB OUTAGE RESOLVED\par \par \uc2 UNITED AIRLINES\uc2 , EMERGENCY LANDING, RAMSEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHATTERED PLANE WINDSHIELD PROMPTS EMERGENCY LANDING\par \par \uc2 BABY, SUBWAY, CRICHLOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEWBORN FOUND IN NYC SUBWAY STATION\par \par \uc2 WORLD SERIES, \uc2 TORONTO BLUE JAYS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORONTO BEATS SEATTLE TO ADVANCE TO WORLD SERIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL\par \par \uc2 LOUVRE, BURGLARY, DATI, MANHUNT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INSIDE THE LOUVRE AFTER DARING HEIST\par \par \uc2 CELL PHONE, BAN, LEARNING, STUDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY ON CELL PHONE BANS IN SCHOOL\par \par \uc2 GAS, PRICES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GAS PRICES FALL BELOW $3 PER GALLON\par \par \uc2 WALMART\uc2 , GROCERY PRICES, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 WALMART\uc2  CEO LIVE\par \par \uc2 WADE, STORY TIME, VIRAL VIDEO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIRPORT ATTACK AVERTED?\par \par \uc2 AIRPORT, SHOOTING, OBESITY, CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 KEELER, GETTYSBURG COLLEGE, ASSAULT, JUSTICE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN SPEAKS OUT AFTER 12-YEAR BATTLE FOR JUSTICE\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, PREGNANT, WYSASKI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLING BREAST CANCER THROUGH PREGNANCY\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Sam. Now our series, 40 Years of Pink, celebrating four decades of Breast Cancer Awareness Month and the advancement we've seen since then. This morning we're going to meet a woman who learned she had the disease when she was pregnant. Maggie Rulli, here with the story. Hey, Maggie.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, George, good morning. You know, when you're pregnant you already are nervous and have so many questions. So, just imagine going through this and then getting a cancer diagnosis in your first trimester. Stephanie shares her story with honesty and bravery, and guys, she has a special update for us today.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: BATTLING BREAST CANCER THROUGH PREGNANCY\par \par \uc2 DR ANNABELLE VEERAPANENI (NORTHWESTERN MEDICINE ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Any issues with chest discomfort or...\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) 35-year-old Stephanie Wysaski is fighting for her own life while nurturing a new one.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE WYSASKI (MOTHER AND BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Who ever thinks about getting a cancer diagnosis while pregnant?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Just weeks after learning she was expecting her fourth child, a TV segment about breast self-exams prompted her to do one.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE WYSASKI (MOTHER AND BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 And I feel a lump, a pretty sizable lump high on my chest. I was still nursing my then-11-month-old, I was newly pregnant. Things get lumpy when you're nursing.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INVASIVE LOBULAR CARCINOMA\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tests confirmed the worst. A breast cancer called invasive lobular carcinoma, accounting for 10 to 15 percent of all breast cancers, that's harder to detect on mammograms.\par \par \uc2 DR ANNABELLE VEERAPANENI (NORTHWESTERN MEDICINE ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 It was growing pretty rapidly, and the fact it moved to the lymph node, we're dealing with something that we really need to address as quickly as we possibly can.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE WYSASKI (MOTHER AND BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 It was a shock, and it changed the course of my pregnancy, all of it.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This was not a cancer that could wait for treatment. When the baby was 15 weeks, doctors at Northwestern Oak Brook prescribed a chemo regimen both Stephanie and the baby could tolerate.\par \par \uc2 DR ANNABELLE VEERAPANENI (NORTHWESTERN MEDICINE ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 The mainstay of treatment that is given during pregnancy is very, very safe.\par \par \uc2 CHEMOTHERAPY TECHNICIAN (NORTHWESTERN MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 We are split into two syringes of 24.5 (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Chemo ended at the end of August. It was tough, but Stephanie is tougher, physically and emotionally.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE WYSASKI (MOTHER AND BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 As much as I'm in my own head thinking cancer, cancer, cancer and spiraling, my kids still have school pick-up and drop-off, and they still want cuddle time. I just didn't have time to worry. Life doesn't stop.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While she mourns the pregnancy she hoped to have, she's looking forward to meeting her baby girl, who will be delivered today, early, at 34 weeks.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE WYSASKI (MOTHER AND BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 And she's kicking, rolling, punching, it's almost, like, a good sign. How I take it is just that she's strong in there, she's tough, she's letting me know that she's all good.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Aw.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, well, Stephanie will meet her baby girl any moment now this morning, and she told us she's just trying to focus on how excited she is to meet her, and to pretend they're just normal people having a baby. They don't want to think about everything else that is going on. And guys, she also told us they're still trying to decide on a name.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Oh (inaudible)...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, they have a couple of hours left.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They have a few hours left, exactly.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, what a beautiful family.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Maggie. Yeah. Thank you. Let's go to Sam.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251106elal0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 8:00 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CAGLE, ARREST, DICKENS, SCHIERBAUM\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON, JEFFRIES, DUFFY, THOMAS, GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN'S GROWING IMPACT\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , BALLROOM, WHITE HOUSE, RASKIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PARTS OF WHITE HOUSE EAST WING DEMOLISHED\par \par \uc2 AMAZON WEB SERVICES\uc2 , OUTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON: ISSUES BEHIND MASSIVE WEB OUTAGE RESOLVED\par \par \uc2 UNITED AIRLINES\uc2 , EMERGENCY LANDING, RAMSEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHATTERED PLANE WINDSHIELD PROMPTS EMERGENCY LANDING\par \par \uc2 BABY, SUBWAY, CRICHLOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEWBORN FOUND IN NYC SUBWAY STATION\par \par \uc2 WORLD SERIES, \uc2 TORONTO BLUE JAYS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORONTO BEATS SEATTLE TO ADVANCE TO WORLD SERIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL\par \par \uc2 LOUVRE, BURGLARY, DATI, MANHUNT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INSIDE THE LOUVRE AFTER DARING HEIST\par \par \uc2 CELL PHONE, BAN, LEARNING, STUDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY ON CELL PHONE BANS IN SCHOOL\par \par \uc2 GAS, PRICES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GAS PRICES FALL BELOW $3 PER GALLON\par \par \uc2 WALMART\uc2 , GROCERY PRICES, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 WALMART\uc2  CEO LIVE\par \par \uc2 WADE, STORY TIME, VIRAL VIDEO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIRPORT ATTACK AVERTED?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. It's 8:00 am. Potential mass shooting stopped inside the world's busiest airport.\par \par \uc2 POLICE OFFICER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Put your hands behind your back, sir.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Police in Atlanta say they foiled a plot.\par \par \uc2 POLICE CHIEF (ATLANTA)\par \par \uc2 He was streaming on social media that he was headed to the airport, in their words, "To shoot it up."\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 This morning, how they arrested the suspect just in time, and what police found just steps away in his truck.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER WHILE PREGNANT\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Battling breast cancer while pregnant.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE WYSASKI (MOTHER AND BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 It was a shock. That changed the course of my pregnancy, all of it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A mom of three with one on the way, shares her breast cancer journey, and the happy news happening now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JEREMY RENNER\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And Jeremy Renner's here live...\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: "MAYOR OF KINGSTOWN"\par \par \uc2 JEREMY RENNER (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 There's no problem in here that I can't solve for you.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ...talking about the new season of his hit show "Mayor of Kingstown," this time around, joined by Edie Falco.\par \par \uc2 EDIE FALCO (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 This is my castle now.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 It's all ahead, as we say, good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live from ABC News headquarters in New York City, it's Tuesday. Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And good morning, America. I hope you're doing well this Tuesday morning. Really looking forward to catching up with Jeremy Renner.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We are, and we're also going to show you some of the most beautiful places in the world to visit, like this remote area. It's north of Tokyo and is one of NatGeo's best destinations in the world. We'll have more of their annual list, coming up.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We also have this new study looking at obesity and cancer rates in young people.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251106elal0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 KATIE THURSTON TALKS STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER FIGHT FORMER "BACHELORETTE" KATIE THURSTON ON HER BREAST CANCER FIGHT\b0\par\par\uc2 1,215 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THREAT\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , VENEZUELA, STRIKES, DRUGS, HIMES, PAUL, MADURO\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, LAYOFFS, TRUMP, JEFFRIES, WORKERS\par \par \uc2 GAZA, CEASEFIRE, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , HOSTAGES\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS, VOTING, ACT, REDISTRICTING, CONSTITUTION, AGUI\u209\'00\'D1AGA\par \par \uc2 ARREST, MURDER, DNA, DONNELLY, BILODEAU, FUSCO\par \par \uc2 HEATING, COSTS, ELECTRICITY, AI\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , STEELERS, BENGALS, RODGERS, FLACCO\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM ANCHORAGE, ALASKA\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM KIPNUCK ALASKA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALASKA STORM\par \par \uc2 MUSIC VIDEO CLIP FROM ALANIS MORISETTE/"IRONIC"\par \par \uc2 SCOTT, SEARCH, ARREST, KING, VANORE, KOZLOWSKI\par \par \uc2 AI, PRANK HOMELESS, MAN, IMAGE, BURKE\par \par \uc2 RECLINE, SEAT, CHARGE, WESTJET, AIRLINES, NELSON\par \par \uc2 MOUNT, EVEREST, MORRISON\par \par \uc2 MUSIC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TROPICAL OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT'S WARNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THREAT\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , DRUG CARTEL, STRIKES, VENEZUELA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , ARGENTINA, BAILOUT, FARMER\par \par \uc2 AIRTAG, BURGLARY, ARREST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRAZEN ROBBERY\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, THURSTON\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Nice crisp day here in New York City. And welcome back to "GMA," everybody. Our nationwide "50 States in 50 Weeks" tour (inaudible) into Tennessee tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOMORROW ON GMA, TENNESSEE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And one thing you might not know about the 16th state and that it's really into off-roading. Rachel Scott will get behind the wheel live. Anything can happen. Anything can happen. All right, Robin, what you got for us?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's tomorrow. But right now, we have the "GMA Cover Story."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KATIE THURSTON TALKS STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER FIGHT\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) All month long, we're honoring Breast Cancer Awareness Month and 40 years of pink. And this morning, former "Bachelorette" Katie Thurton is here to talk about her metastatic breast cancer fight.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Since revealing her stage 4 diagnoses earlier this year, she's been open about her journey on social media and has made it her mission to advocate for others. Katie, it is wonderful, wonderful to see you.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Thank you. Thank you for having me today.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know you've been on medication-type treatment.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) How's everything going?\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 It's great. We actually just did my six-month scan earlier this week, and the medicine is still working. The tumor is still shrinking. And when you're stage 4, that's really all you can hope for.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's it. I'm so happy to hear that. So happy to see that in your face.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) You are diagnosed at 34 years old. What do you want especially younger women to know?\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 That breast cancer really can happen before your 40. I think we're kind of tricked to thinking mammograms, 40, cancer is reserved for later in life and that's just simply not the case. And I've met so many woman under 40 who have been diagnosed.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you've been working with the \uc2 Breast Cancer Research Foundation\uc2 . Can you tell us a little bit more about that work?\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Yes. So, they are an amazing nonprofit. They show up for the community, they've shown up for me. But more importantly, they are the largest private funder for metastatic breast cancer research, which is so important because for us stage 4 there's really not a cure at this time.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that's why I wanted to make sure that we mentioned it was metastatic.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And because so many times people going through breast cancer, and we talk about different types, and it's not one-size-fits-all. But we tend not to talk so much about metastatic. How do you keep your hopes up? And how do you keep the smile on your face and knowing that one day, one day, the next day, just keep moving forward?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER "BACHELORETTE" KATIE THURSTON ON HER BREAST CANCER FIGHT\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 You know medical advancements have come so far in the past 10 years, that I refuse to view this as a terminal illness, or a death sentence. I really trust medicine and the future of breast health. And that's kind of what keeps me going each day.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you found a committee online. You're very active online, very positive online. How has the community help you?\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Oh, I mean, they say it's, you know, worst club, best members. And it's truly the case. I've met so many women from so many different parts of life who have really just helped give me their perspective and their advice. And without them, I wouldn't, I wouldn't be as confident and as happy I think as I am now.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, you know, you give confidence and happiness to others. You really do with your post and that.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I should say, congratulations. You're a married woman. Yay.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) In March, right?\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Yes, we got married in March.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) You and Jeff, comedian Jeff?\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, how has he been by your side?\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 He's been great. I mean, he postponed his tour in the beginning when I was first diagnosed. He's now in Australia, but he continues to show up however he can. Most recently, dedicating $1 of each ticket sale to BCRF and then doing a matching donation. So, in total we're looking at about $80,000 that we're going to be donating.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's awesome. That's a (inaudible). Please do give him our very best. Do you have a final message that you want to share with people?\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 I think the biggest thing is be so familiar with your breasts and bring it to your doctor if something feels off or looks off. You know, age is nothing but a number at this point.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is. Well, you are a beautiful messenger and thank you for the message you're sending out today.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We continue to wish you all the best, Katie. All right, you and Jeff. All right.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (DIAGNOSED WITH STAGE 4 BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251030elag0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 9:00 HALF HOUR WEEKEND MOVIES AND SHOWS TO WATCH\b0\par\par\uc2 547 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TENNESSEE, PLANT, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FEDERAL, WORKER, LAYOFFS, SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT, SHUTDOWN, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , CHINA, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, GAZA, CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ, VIDEO\par \par \uc2 MANGIONE, CHARGES, EVIDENCE\par \par \uc2 ACES, MERCURY, WNBA, CHAMPIONSHIP\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP OF "CHAD POWERS"\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, FOOTBALL\par \par \uc2 CRYPTO, BITCOIN, SCAM\par \par \uc2 "CHAD POWERS", "I LIKE ME", "WOMAN IN CABIN 10", "VICTORIA BECKHAM", "PARENT TRAP"\par \par \uc2 STORY 2:\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. It's our second hour. Devastating blast. The urgent search for at least 18 people missing and feared dead at an explosives manufacturing plant in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF CHRIS DAVIS (HUMPHREYS COUNTY TENNESSEE)\par \par \uc2 It's probably been one of the most devastating situations that I've been on in my career.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 The blast heard miles away. Now, federal investigators on the scene, looking into what went wrong.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT SEARCH AND INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEVASTATING BLAST\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 The inspiring story of a husband and wife, both diagnosed with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 JOANNA CLAUSEN (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 It was very hard to process the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Now, making a difference with the organization that stepped up to help them.\par \par \uc2 MARK CLAUSEN (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I think it's a, it's a lifeline for us.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And how you can support your own loved ones through the journey.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOUBLE DIAGNOSIS: BREAST CANCER HUSBAND & WIFE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER AWARENESS MONTH\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Is Miranda Priestly ready for some mayhem?\par \par \uc2 STANLEY LUCCI (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 Gird your loins.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 What's in store for "The Devil Wears Prada 2"? Fans are going Gaga over the announcement.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: READY FOR "MAYHEM?"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE DEVIL WEARS PRADA SEQUEL ADDS SOME STAR POWER\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 A day at Disneyland. Insider tips to stretching your dollar.\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Are we ready?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Our Becky Worley went for a ride.\par \par \uc2 CARRIE HOENER (MEMBER OF FREE CONSULTATION SERVICE)\par \par \uc2 The Disneyland app is gonna be your best friend.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And has all the tricks to supersize your \uc2 Disney\uc2  fun, as we say, good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW TO STAY ON BUDGET AT DISNEYLAND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A DAY AT THE PARKS\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live from \uc2 ABC News\uc2  headquarters in New York City, it's Saturday. Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Happy Saturday. Happy weekend to you.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. So great to have Rachel Scott here with us.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Yes, always.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Coming up, planning for a comfortable retirement, even if you think you've put it off for too long.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Yeah, never...\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (inaudible), exactly.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Never too early. That's what I was gonna say.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Yep.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 True. Of course.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Is that what you're going to say?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Of course. We also have a lot of news to get to this morning, where we go live to Israel with the ceasefire holding. The big question now, when will the hostages be released as Gazans head home?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251029elab0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LOBULAR BREAST CANCER ON THE RISE MOST COMMON BREAST CANCER IS DUCTILE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,115 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING GRIDLOCK\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, AIRPORTS, ATC, FURLOUGHED, TRUMP, DANIELS, LEE\par \par \uc2 COMEY, ARRAIGNED, BONDI, SENATE, EPSTEIN, LUTNICK\par \par \uc2 SURGEONS GENERAL, KENNEDY, OP-ED, SCIENCE, HEALTH, CARMONA\par \par \uc2 TROPICS, TEMPS, COOLDOWN, FROST, JERRY, ANTILLES\par \par \uc2 MLB, \uc2 YANKEES\uc2 , \uc2 BLUE JAYS\uc2 , JUDGE, MARINERS, TIGERS\par \par \uc2 RESCUE, FALL, EEYORE, DEPUTY, ADEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRACKING PRISCILLA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ, CHARGES, TOLE, MEARS, STABBED, AGGRESSOR, MAY\par \par \uc2 PARTON, HEALTH, PRAYERS, RESIDENCY, CANCEL, CARTER\par \par \uc2 GOLD, SURGE, HIGH, INVESTMENT, TRADING, DEPRECIATE\par \par \uc2 FRANCINE, MISSING, SCHNEIDER, SIDA, RETURN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRACKING PRISCILLA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLASH FLOOD THREAT\par \par \uc2 AIR, TRAVEL, SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , ISRAEL, WAR\par \par \uc2 BREAST, CANCER, LOBULAR\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now we have a "GMA" health alert as we celebrate 40 years of breast cancer awareness this month. A new study out this morning reveals that a specific type, lobular carcinoma, has doubled since 1975. We're gonna bring in our chief medical correspondent, Dr. Tara Narula. You need to break this down and the impact it could have. One in ten? 10 percent?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOBULAR BREAST CANCER ON THE RISE\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Correct, yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, when we think about breast cancer, there are a couple of different types. The most common is ductile breast cancer. Michael, you were asking, this is cells that come from the ducts that line, the cells that line the ducts where the milk travels, the milk travels down. That's ductile breast cancer. That's the most common. The second most common, 10 percent of cases, is lobular breast cancer. The lobules form the milk. We have seen a rise since 1970 almost doubling a number of cases of lobular cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, about 3 percent increase in cases of lobular per year compared to less than 1 percent of the other types of breast cancer. So, about 30,000 women will be diagnosed this year with lobular breast cancer. This cancer we are seeing increase in women of all ages, older and younger, all races and ethnicities, although it is most prevalent in white women. It is interestingly more difficult to see on mammograms, more difficult to find on exams.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why? Because it grows differently. It tends to not form a lump, but grows more in lines or sheets. And so women may see nipple retraction, skin dimpling or thickening, fullness of the breast, or redness, as opposed to a mass or lump. So, you know, we know that researchers think that some of this maybe in part due to changes in risk factors. So, obesity, sedentary lifestyle, alcohol, but also these cancers, Robin, are hormonally sensitive. And so the fact that women are getting their periods earlier, having menopause later, having kids later, less kids, and even hormone replacement therapy, all of this chronic long-term longer exposure to hormones may be fueling some of these lobular cancers we are seeing.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, so that could be part of the case here?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, so as you said, it's hard to detect with a mammogram.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, what should we take away from this study?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, so, you know, unfortunately we haven't really studied this type of cancer as well as we should have. We really lumped it in with a the ductal so we need to do better research. Overall, the prognosis for lobular is pretty good at five years, six years, about 90 percent survival. The problem is when you go out past seven years, the survival for lobular drops below that of other breast cancers like ductile.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why? Well, we tend to find it at a larger size, at a later stage. We also know it's less responsive to some types of chemotherapy. There tends to be later recurrence of this type of breast cancer, and it could also happen in the other breast. So, lots of different reasons, again, why we would need to do a better job understanding lobular. But for women, important to know your family history, obviously recognize any changes you might see in your breast.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Pay, take care of those risk factors, so make sure you're exercising, eating well, avoiding too much alcohol, if any alcohol. And then also, when you speak to your doctor, obviously the standard of care is mammograms. But for this type of cancer, breast MRI and contrast enhanced mammography may do a better job.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, okay. All right.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, the doctor may recommend that if you're at higher risk.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So many factors involved here.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, as always, thank you, Dr. Narula. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up in our "GMA" morning menu, Diane Sawyer here with her interview with Julia Roberts. She's talking about her new movie. It's getting rave reviews.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JULIA ROBERTS ONE-ON-ONE\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also ahead, Oscar-winner Jared Leto is here live. Look at him lighting up the screen in the beloved "Tron" franchise.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JARED LETO LIVE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also, stars of "Wizards Beyond Waverly Place" are here, David Henrie and Janice LeAnn Brown are giving us a preview of season two. And Lara and Sam are with Lori Bergamotto.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "WIZARDS BEYOND WAVERLY PLACE" STARTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEAL DROP DAYS!\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and it is time, Sal. Crank up the conveyor belt.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh. Oh, Sal, from the other side today. This is so scary. It's Deal Drop Days. But, look, we barely have room because we got wizards, we got Diane Sawyer, we've got "Tron" bikes in the studio.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jared Leto.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, but, Jared Leto is here. \uc2 Walmart\uc2  bargain days begin. We've got those.\par \par \uc2 LORI BERGAMOTTO (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Amazon Prime days.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And prime days.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So many, Sam.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, just for all of that, stay right here.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251016ela80003a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 40 YEARS OF PINK BREAST CANCER AWARENESS: SCREENINGS, DETECTION & MISINFORMATION\b0\par\par\uc2 960 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , WAR\par \par \uc2 COMBS, SENTENCED, PRISON\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT, SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 DRUG, BOAT, ATTACK\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN, CHURCH, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, REFINERY, FIRE\par \par \uc2 ACES, MERCURY, WNBA\par \par \uc2 WNBA, COMPLAINTS\par \par \uc2 DAD, SAVED, NEIGHBOR, SCHAEFER, WALKER\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, FOOTBALL\par \par \uc2 BREAST, CANCER, AWARENESS, COLEMAN\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back now with our "GMA" cover story, and 40 Years of Pink. That's 40 years of October being Breast Cancer Awareness Month, and early detection is key. But if you're one of so many women with dense breasts, detection can be more difficult. So, joining us now with guidance is Dr. Elizabeth Comen, a breast medical oncologist from NYU Langone. Thank you so much for being here. Let's start with defining dense breasts.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER AWARENESS: SCREENINGS, DETECTION & MISINFORMATION\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 So, first of all, dense breasts are really common. About 40 to 50 percent of all women have dense breasts, and it's not something that you can see or feel. It's something the radiologist will tell you. And really what it refers to is really just the ratio between how much fibrous breast tissue you have versus fat on a mammogram. And the issue is that fat will appear black on a mammogram and cancer and breast tissue can also appear white. So, when you're looking for a cancer, it's like finding a snowflake in a snowstorm.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow, that's a great way to put it. So, how is technology catching up to help with early detection for women with dense breasts?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 So, that's a great question. So, all women now are required to be informed on their mammogram if they have dense breasts. If you have dense breasts, you really need to talk to your doctor about whether you need additional imaging such as a breast ultrasound or breast MRI to detect an early cancer. And we have some newer technologies that are under investigation like artificial intelligence, contrast enhanced mammograms, and there's lots of new hope that we can detect cancers even earlier now.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. So let's say a woman goes in for a mammogram. She is now notified because she has to be that she has dense breasts. There are further options, as you said, for either an ultrasound or an MRI.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) You need to talk to your doctor about that though.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because it's not necessarily going to automatically be recommended, right?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 I wish it were, but it really puts women in the position of having to advocate for themselves.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Make sure to talk to your doctor about whether you need additional screening, but also what age you should start screening. So, most women of average risk should start screening at age 40. But if you have a family history or other personal risk factors, you may need to start earlier than age 40 with your screening.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, and that can be challenging, of course, because insurance may not cover it. All of these things, but just for women out there listening to know you have to be your own advocate, you have to push for these things.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ask more questions, whether additional testing is needed. Turning now to breast cancer and medical disinformation, you're hearing from patients skeptical of traditional science-backed treatments. Why do you think that that's happening?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Oh, there are so many reasons. If we look at the landscape of health care today, it's understandable why a lot of patients have distrust in science and our health care system, and we need to work, we as doctors and our health care system, to rebuild that trust. But there's an awful problem out there and that is the misinformation that's on social media.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 I see every day how women are being sold a false bill of goods on social media about magic bullets and things that are going to, you know, miraculously cure them, all cancers for that reason.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 And it's terrifying, and it's heartbreaking because we often will miss a window to cure a woman of breast cancer or other types of cancers because they've been sold this false information.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this is why early detection is so key, right? Even if you're afraid.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes. Early detection but also investing in the science and research that will actually improve the landscape of women's health care.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. So much of this is so important, and we have to keep it in mind. So, so great that you're here to talk to us about it.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you for being with us. And coming up here on "Good Morning America," it's Taylor time. From her new album to the official release party of "a Showgirl," Taylor Swift fans cannot get enough. Plus, what the singer is revealing about Travis Kelce's marriage proposal. Will Ganss has it next.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAYLOR TIME!\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251023ela40008r\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVES NEW GENERIC ABORTION PILL \uc2 HHS\uc2  TO REVIEW WOMEN'S ACCESS TO ABORTION PILLS\b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE EXPLOSION AT OIL REFINERY\par \par \uc2 EXPLOSION, REFINERY, FIRE, RESIDENTS, EVACUATION\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, TRUMP, DEMS, GOP, LAYOFFS, PROGRAMS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, CARTELS, VENEZUELA, CONGRESS, VANLANDINGHAM\par \par \uc2 ATTACK, SYNAGOGUE, DEAD, INJURED, SUSPECT\par \par \uc2 FDA\uc2 , APPROVAL, ABORTION, KENNEDY, ACCESS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Shocking for everyone. Lama, thank you so much for that. Gonna turn now to the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approving a second generic form of the abortion pill, Mifepristone. But it comes as Health Secretary \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , is promising to investigate all forms of medication for abortions. Elizabeth Schulze joins us from Washington with more. Good morning Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVES NEW GENERIC ABORTION PILL\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, Michael. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  is expanding the availability of the abortion pill, approving a new generic version of the drug Mifepristone. These are medications used in about two thirds of all abortions nationwide. They also help manage an early miscarriage. Antiabortion advocates have been urging the Trump administration to limit access to Mifepristone, so approval of this second generic version is now sparking pushback.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 HHS\uc2  TO REVIEW WOMEN'S ACCESS TO ABORTION PILLS\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) The \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  group saying "This reckless decision by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is unconscionable." Health Secretary \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , tried to address the backlash saying "Federal law requires approval when an application proves that the generic is identical to the generic brand name drug.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now just last week Kennedy pledged to conduct a review of women's access to abortion pills. Since the end of Roe V. Wade, at least 16 states have banned nearly all abortions. But some people in those states have still been able to get access to the abortion pill through telehealth appointments and the mail. Mifepristone was first approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  in the year 2000, and repeated medical studies have shown that it is safe. George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251016ela300006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 40 YEARS OF PINK LET'S TALK\b0\par\par\uc2 4,020 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE EXPLOSION AT OIL REFINERY\par \par \uc2 EXPLOSION, REFINERY, FIRE, RESIDENTS, EVACUATION\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, TRUMP, DEMS, GOP, LAYOFFS, PROGRAMS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, CARTELS, VENEZUELA, CONGRESS, VANLANDINGHAM\par \par \uc2 ATTACK, SYNAGOGUE, DEAD, INJURED, SUSPECT\par \par \uc2 FDA\uc2 , APPROVAL, ABORTION, KENNEDY, ACCESS\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, STRIKES, INFRASTRUCTURE, PUTIN, EUROPE\par \par \uc2 IMELDA, COSTAL, RAIN, FLOODING, SURF\par \par \uc2 MLB, PLAYOFFS, \uc2 YANKEES\uc2 , CUBS, TIGERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEELS LIKE SUMMER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 COMBS, SENTENCING, CHARGES, TESTIMONY, PROSECUTION, DEFENSE\par \par \uc2 KILLED, NIOTIS, SALAS, E-BIKE, SUV, SUSPECT\par \par \uc2 SWIFT, "THE LIFE OF A SHOWGIRL", FANS, CARTER\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT, SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 MATERNITY, WARDS, MEDICAID\par \par \uc2 PRINCE WILLIAM, KATE, LEVY\par \par \uc2 SWIFT, GOLDEN RETRIEVERS\par \par \uc2 CONTENT\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, OWALA, HOKA\par \par \uc2 BREAST, CANCER, AWARENESS\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ETHERIDGE (21-YEAR SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Hey, everybody. Melissa Etheridge here. I'm a 21 year breast cancer survivor.\par \par \uc2 JOAN LUNDEN (11-YEAR SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Everybody, I'm Joan Lunden, and I'm proud to say that I'm an 11 year breast cancer survivor.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ETHERIDGE (21-YEAR SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 It's about thriving. It's about putting yourself first.\par \par \uc2 JOAN LUNDEN (11-YEAR SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 If breast cancer is detected early, survival rates are now close to 90 percent.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ETHERIDGE (21-YEAR SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I know we like to put everybody else first sometimes, because that's what we do. But we're no good for anybody else unless we put ourselves first.\par \par \uc2 JOAN LUNDEN (11-YEAR SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Knowledge is power. It can save your life.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to "GMA." And as Joan Lunden said, knowledge is power. Well, this morning, we're celebrating 40 years of pink. Four decades since the launch of Breast Cancer Awareness Month. We are happy to have an audience filled with thrivers. That's right. And also these three experts to bring us the latest in breast cancer care and answer questions from the women here.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have our chief medical correspondent, Dr. Tara Narula, Dr. Shanthi Sivendran of the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2 , and Dr. Monique James of Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center. Welcome to all of you. Welcome. It is, give it up one more time. Great to have you all here, and, and thank you three for, for being here to - to answer all of our questions. And Dr. Sivendran, I want to start with you. Where are we with survival rates, with treatment, and, and the latest advances that matter most to patients right now?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHANTHI SIVENDRAN (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Well, this is an, really incredible time, celebrating 40 years of progress in breast cancer. If I take you back to 1985, one in three women were getting their mammograms, and we had really limited access to innovative treatments and screening technologies. And now, 40 years later, 2 out of 3 women are getting mammograms. We've had a 40 percent reduction in breast cancer mortality, over 5 million breast cancer survivors.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHANTHI SIVENDRAN (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 And this is all thanks to this amazing innovation in breast cancer. As we look ahead to the next 40 years, some of those things that you're talking about, like vaccines, AI, those are really important parts of jumping forward into the next 40 years. So, something like cancer vaccines is actually prime time for some cancers right now.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHANTHI SIVENDRAN (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 We use it in bladder cancer, we use it in melanoma, we use it in prostate cancer, and we'll continue to make that kind of progress with cancer research. And when we think about AI, you know, that's great technology to help us do things like augment our ability to read mammograms and augment our workforce. So, when I look forward, I think this is a really exciting time for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is so exciting as somebody who my mom has overcome breast cancer, and so many people here are, are dealing with it in so many different places in their life. Dr. James, and want to talk to you a little bit about managing. Because now, lives are being prolonged.\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) People may not even be cured, but they are managing that cancer. How do they deal with the emotional impact of that and the mental health impact of that in the long run?\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Such a huge and important question. And with 40 years of breast cancer awareness and research, people really are living longer with the disease. And so you move from a state of shock, a state of crisis, even a state of survival mode into more of a state of maintenance. It's almost like living with a chronic medical illness.\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And so how do you do your best to go with that? You actually learn to walk with uncertainty. You learn how to walk with unpredictability. And a lot of the mental and emotional challenge is how you do that. It's interesting, right? One of the best ways of walking with uncertainty is actually planning, actually doing, actually having action. Right? You plan for the now so that you can actually plan for the future, but you start now. You start in the present.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hope takes action.\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 That's right. That's right. It's a verb.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Another keyword, resilience. You've just written a book, Dr. Narula, called "The Healing Power of Resilience." How can women cancer cultivate it?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, George, I mean, when you are sitting in that exam room or you're at home and you get that call that says you have cancer, it is literally a moment where you are paralyzed, for many women, frozen in fear, and your whole world is turned upside down. And so, really in that moment, what we hope to do with the book is say that you can change a stress response into a resilient response. And there are ways to do that.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Psychology tells us, in the book, we talk about things like acceptance, and flexible thinking, and exercising, supporting your body strength, focusing on hope and optimism and connection. So, there are strategies you can use in that moment so that you can really move forward, and you're never going to be the same, ever. But you can find a new normal, you can thrive, and you can have great quality of life and meaning in your life. So, for me, resilience is that light that really helps you find the path in the darkness. It's a great skill to cultivate.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's beautiful. Indeed. Dr. Sivendran, after breast cancer surgery, a lot of women have decisions to make about reconstructive surgery. What can you tell women about the options available today for them?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHANTHI SIVENDRAN (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, it's a great question. So, reconstructive options have come a really long way. And when I think about those reconstructive options, sort of divide them up into what we call flap options and implant options. So, flap options are ones where we're taking tissue from some other part of our body and we're moving it to the chest and creating a breast that way. And that can be taken from various different parts of the body, and we're using skin, and blood vessels, and tissues, sometimes muscle, and moving it to the chest.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHANTHI SIVENDRAN (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 And then there are implant options, so using saline or silicone implants to try to recreate the breast. But, I think it's important to also understand that reconstructive surgery is not the only option. Right? So, there are many women who decide that they just don't want to go through another surgery. Right? That, that can be really hard.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHANTHI SIVENDRAN (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 And embracing the figure that they have now. Right? And that's, that's a great option. And then the second is for women who don't want to go through a surgery but still want to have some shape underneath their clothes. There are special bras, prostheses under, in those bras that you can use to create the shape. But at the end of the day, it's what is, what the patient themselves values and what they can work together with their health care team to create.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Great. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's so great to have you guys and, and to talk about the options. But, Dr. James, now that we have options, we have to make choices. So, you say, because at one time we didn't have options, right? But you say there are some questions that people should ask themselves to choose these, so what are they?\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Definitely. So, one of the first questions I always ask, right, is what do your breasts mean to you? Right? It's not what have they meant, but what do they mean right now.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do they.\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And when you ask that kind of question you get the values, right, of that person. You get how they see themselves as they move through the world, right, how they walk through the world and present themselves. Once you have those kinds of values about that person, we can start to help guide them a little bit better with the decisions.\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And a big thing that we say all of the time, right, it's not about a worse or a better outcome, it's that it'll be different. Right? So, it's not going to be the same. It'll be different. But different is something that we can tackle, something that we can cope with, something that we can adapt to. And so remembering that we still have all these tools with us for that.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Dr. Narula, you're a cardiologist. You said women should pay attention to their heart health, especially after going through radiation and then chemo. What questions should they be asking their doctors?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is so important, Michael, because as we've seen advances in breast cancer treatment, and we have 5 million survivors, what a lot of survivors don't know is that cardiovascular disease is still lurking as a big threat. We know it is the leading cause of death for women, and in fact, with breast cancer, there are a couple of reasons why they could be at risk. One is that there are shared risk factors. So, things like obesity, sedentary lifestyles, smoking, puts you at risk for breast cancer and also cardiovascular disease.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) But the second is, as you mentioned, many of the treatments that we give women, chemotherapy, radiation, can cause early accelerated coronary artery disease or plaque in the arteries. It can damage the heart valves. It can damage the heart muscle. That can lead to heart failure or even heart rhythm problems. So, it's important when you get discharged to make sure you have a record of your chemo and radiation, that you set up care with a cardiologist to look at your risk factors, and also potentially get screening with ultrasounds and other tests that they might recommend.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, thank you three for answering our questions. But, but coming up, our thrivers, our audience of beautiful thrivers...\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're gonna ask doctors their questions on live after treatment when we come back with more "GMA."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, we are back now with 40 Years of Pink, and we're having this great conversation, and now it's time to hear from the thrivers in our audience this morning. Lara and Sam are speaking with all of them. Take it away, guys.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Will do, Rebecca, and I'm here now with Amanda McElroy. Come on up. And, Amanda, you have a question. Go ahead and share it.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA MCELROY (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Yes. I'm 42 years old, and I'm a four year survivor of stage four triple negative breast cancer. What strategies help manage fatigue, cognitive changes, or other side effects while returning to work or other daily routines?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHANTHI SIVENDRAN (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Amanda, thank you for bringing attention to really important topic. So, what you're describing is a side effect of going through cancer treatment. That can be chronic. It can make it difficult for patients to, you know, return to the workforce, or to just be able to return to their normal that, you know, where they were before. And that can be mild or it can be debilitating. Right? And anywhere in between.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHANTHI SIVENDRAN (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 So, as I think about some potential sort of action items that a person can do, the first one is get moving. Get your body moving and your brain moving. Right? So, going for those walks, being deliberate about exercises, puzzles, all of those things. The second is they're all things that we value in our lives, and prioritizing those things of value and putting our energy behind those. Right?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHANTHI SIVENDRAN (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Sometimes we try to do it all, and that's really difficult. There is, everybody has a team of people that they can lean on, and that's the time when you lean on the team to help you do those other tasks. Right? And so, and then if despite some of those things that we're talking about, that you're still having trouble, it's really important to loop in your health care team. There can be some really formal ways to rehabilitate both physically and mentally, and get that additional help that someone needs.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA MCELROY (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good question, good answer. Shannon Vick, you've got a question. Shannon, if you would stand up, I was reading over your shoulder, your question, it's a good one. I think a lot of people will, will have the same thing. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON VICK (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I'm 46 years old with four children, and I'm a 12 year thriver of metastatic breast cancer. My question is the word cancer is scary for an adult, let alone a child. So, do you have any advice for mothers on how to talk to their children?\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Such an important question. And 12 years, right? It's just amazing.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 So, kids, we all have different kinds of kids in our life. Right? Sometimes they're our own children. Sometimes they might be nieces or they might be nephews. Right? The first thing to remember is that most kids of all ages can usually sense or realize that something is up with their parents, with the adults in their life. Right? And so with that, you never want the child to be alone with the questions that they might have.\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 So, the number 1 recommendation is to say something. So, what do you say? Right? That's, that's the big thing here. It's okay for parents to feel unsure. It's okay if you don't know exactly what to say. It's okay if you even practice what to say. But our recommendations are that its age-appropriate, that it's familiar language that you use with the kids. You might also think about who you want with you to tell them. Right? And that it might actually be a series of conversations that you tell the child. Right?\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 You give them a heads up maybe of any physical changes that you might have. And the last thing, of course, the most important thing coming from, from me, the psychiatrist, right, is that you allow them to express their emotions. You allow them to express their feelings. And they might see the emotions in you. That's okay too, right? Because you'll model for them how to deal with the emotions and feelings.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. Very, very important. Appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Important stuff. Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is Lisa Lefebvre. Hi, Lisa,\par \par \uc2 LISA LEFEBVRE (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Lara.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) What would you like to ask the doctors?\par \par \uc2 LISA LEFEBVRE (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you. I am 41 years old. I am a year breast cancer survivor. One of my biggest fear as a breast cancer survivor is a recurrence. So, what advice do you have for a breast cancer survivor who wants to enjoy life, but still carries that fear?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's such a great question, Lisa, and, you know, we talked earlier about what happens in that moment when you get diagnosed, and I think fear is so normal. And I would ask everyone here, how many of you felt afraid or still might feel afraid? Almost everyone. Right? So, the important thing is to normalize it, to understand that that's part of the reaction. And I hear this a lot with my patients who have cardiovascular or have had a heart attack or stroke. What if it happens again?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the advice I often give them is to say don't focus on the future. Focus on the present moment. Put one foot in front of the other. Don't worry about something that might never happen and let it take away the joy from the moment that's happening right now. And you will also find that, as time passes, time really is a healer. We say that, but as you get farther, and farther, and farther away from the event, the fear gets less and less. It never goes away, but it gets less present.\par \par \uc2 LISA LEFEBVRE (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) An excellent question, Lisa, and all of you. Thank you for your questions. Thanks to all of you for the great answers. So much value. Ginger, over to you.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Rebecca. And as we've just heard, and now I'm surrounded with thrivers. They're thrivers here. We know that a diagnosis of breast cancer is a life-changing moment, and the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  wants to help. They have a wide range of items from wigs, to bras, to post surgery recovery tools like a lot of those that you see right here. They're all available to support you through this challenging time and help you remain ever you. For more information, you can scan the QR code on the screen, and let's go ahead and get a check a little closer to home.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL MAP\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, let's hear it for the thrivers in our audience and everyone who's at home watching.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And coming up, we're gonna have more experts answer some questions. And this has been so good. So, if not all your friends are watching, call, tell them to watch. This is great.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we are back with Dr. Tara Narula, Dr. Shanthi Sivendran, and Dr. Monique James, our audience of thrivers. We have some more questions, Lara.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you so much. Janelle, Janelle, Janelle Adams?\par \par \uc2 JANELLE ADAMS (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hi, Janelle. Come on up here.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE ADAMS (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) You have a question for our doctors?\par \par \uc2 JANELLE ADAMS (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I do. I'm 37 years old. I'm two years in remission from stage three triple negative breast cancer. My question is, how can survivors be more included in shaping clinical trials and researchers so our voices are heard?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHANTHI SIVENDRAN (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 That's a great question Janelle. So, I'm gonna leave everybody here with two action items today. The first is your voice is powerful in advocating for cancer research. Cancer research is a nonpartisan issue. Right? We need to use our pressure to make sure that cancer researcher is continued to be funded so that we can move this progress forty years into the future.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHANTHI SIVENDRAN (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 The second is, be a participant in clinical trials. Clinical trials are the way that we advance innovation in cancer medicine. It can be difficult to take part in clinical trials. There are a ton of barriers to, in, in taking part in clinical trials. We recognize that at the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2 , and I encourage you to check out our access to clinical trials and support program where we help get you matches to eligible clinical trials, but also help mitigate those barriers, like transportation, lodging, financial difficulties, to getting you to that clinical trial. So, use your voice to do cancer research advocacy, and to participate in clinical trials.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Great. I think we've got time for one more question. Candacy, do you want to take it?\par \par \uc2 CANDACY JEFFREY (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Sure.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Candacy Jeffrey.\par \par \uc2 CANDACY JEFFREY (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Thank you. I'm a seven year breast cancer survivor this month. My question is, there are many women who decide on the holistic way of treating cancer. Is there a safe way to go both routes holistically and medically?\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Great question, and thank you for that. So, the first thing with holistic medicine is that you do have to talk to your oncology team about it. Right? Sometimes with, with these medicines, even though they're over the counter, they might actually interact with chemotherapy, with immunotherapy, with even hormone therapy.\par \par \uc2 DR. MONIQUE JAMES (MEMORIAL \uc2 SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 So, the number one thing is to talk with your oncologist about it so that we can see if we can then incorporate both. A lot of times people want to holistic medicine for a state of wellness and well-being. And so that's what we always want to prioritize with our patients. And then this way we can see if we can do both of them at the same time.\par \par \uc2 CANDACY JEFFREY (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you. Thank you, doctors. Thank you to all of our thrivers.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Thank you guys.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) For more information, scan the QR code to take you to goodmorningamerica.com, and we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) (inaudible) big thank you to all of our thrivers.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you for coming in this morning. Thank you to our doctors as well, and we all, hope all of you have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 CREW MEMBER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251016ela30000n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 8:00 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE EXPLOSION AT OIL REFINERY\par \par \uc2 EXPLOSION, REFINERY, FIRE, RESIDENTS, EVACUATION\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, TRUMP, DEMS, GOP, LAYOFFS, PROGRAMS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, CARTELS, VENEZUELA, CONGRESS, VANLANDINGHAM\par \par \uc2 ATTACK, SYNAGOGUE, DEAD, INJURED, SUSPECT\par \par \uc2 FDA, APPROVAL, ABORTION, KENNEDY, ACCESS\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, STRIKES, INFRASTRUCTURE, PUTIN, EUROPE\par \par \uc2 IMELDA, COSTAL, RAIN, FLOODING, SURF\par \par \uc2 MLB, PLAYOFFS, \uc2 YANKEES\uc2 , CUBS, TIGERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEELS LIKE SUMMER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 COMBS, SENTENCING, CHARGES, TESTIMONY, PROSECUTION, DEFENSE\par \par \uc2 KILLED, NIOTIS, SALAS, E-BIKE, SUV, SUSPECT\par \par \uc2 SWIFT, "THE LIFE OF A SHOWGIRL", FANS, CARTER\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. It's 8:00 am. President Trump threatens to fire federal workers as the Senate returns for a vote that could end the shutdown. The President eyeing cuts to what he calls Democrat agencies as Democrats refuse to back down.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE HOUSE THREATENS MASS LAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 This morning, Prince William getting personal.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RARE INSIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRINCE WILLIAM GETS PERSONAL\par \par \uc2 PRINCE WILLIAM (UNITED KINGDOM)\par \par \uc2 I am ultimately, I'm generally a very optimistic person.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 About the King, and Princess Kate's battles with cancer. His plans for the throne, and the thing that's important for them to do as a family.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And celebrating four decades of Breast Cancer Awareness Month. Our team of experts are here answering questions about treatment, prevention, and more as we fill our studio with thrivers. And they're saying...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER AWARENESS MONTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 40 YEARS OF PINK\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER SURVIVORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live from \uc2 ABC News\uc2  headquarters in New York City, it's Friday. "Good morning America."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. There they are. The thrivers. They're here this morning. Yes. Showing us what the fight against breast cancer looks like. Empower in action and looking to the future. We're gonna hear from these inspiring women and get the latest science to treat and beat the disease from our team of experts. That is coming up.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Really, really looking forward to that conversation. Also ahead, we are celebrating three years of The Right Stuff. Lori Bergamotto is here with the stuff our "GMA" staff swears by. We're turning the tables a bit today.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Oh, today.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251016ela30000f\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DEALS & STEALS 40 YEARS OF PINK\b0\par\par\uc2 246 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMER