on the screen takes you there as well. Coming up, oh, look at Sheryl Lee Ralph over there, talking about the new season of "Abbott Elementary."\par \par \uc2 SHERYL LEE RALPH ("ABBOTT ELEMENTARY")\par \par \uc2 I love this.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL LEE RALPH ("ABBOTT ELEMENTARY")\par \par \uc2 That, LYS, I love that makeup.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, see there?\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Work it, girl. (inaudible) work.\par \par \uc2 CREW MEMBER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Nice.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251014ela10000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 40 YEARS OF PINK PROGRESS IN FIGHT AGAINST BREAST CANCER\b0\par\par\uc2 1,395 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNMENT SHUTDOWN\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, TRUMP, DEMS, GOP, FURLOUGHS, CUTS\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, FAMILIES, FURLOUGHS, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 MILITARY, TRUMP, HEGSETH, GATHERING, POLICIES, WELCH\par \par \uc2 EXPLOSION, BOMB, THREAT, OKTOBERFEST, SECURITY\par \par \uc2 EARTHQUAKE, RESCUES, DEAD, HOSPITALS, AFTERSHOCKS\par \par \uc2 COLD, CASES, INCREASE, RISKS, HYGIENE\par \par \uc2 MLB, PLAYOFFS, DODGERS, \uc2 YANKEES\uc2 , RED SOX FANS, TICKETS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TROPICAL FUTURECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "DANCING WITH THE STARS"\par \par \uc2 GIBSON, DOGS, DEATH, PARKER, WARRANT\par \par \uc2 COLLIER, WNBA, ENGELBERT, OFFICIATING, WILSON, REEVE\par \par \uc2 "WHEEL OF FORTUNE", DEREVJANIK, WINNER\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT, SHUTDOWN, JOHNSON, JEFFRIES\par \par \uc2 NIGHT\par \par \uc2 BREAST, CANCER, AWARENESS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Beautiful shot of New York right there. And we are back now with our "GMA" cover story. It is October 1st. The first day of Breast Cancer Awareness Month, and this year we are celebrating 40 Years of Pink.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 40 YEARS OF PINK: PROGRESS IN FIGHT AGAINST BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) When the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  launched this mission to bring attention to breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hard to believe it's been 40 years of doing this.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) 40 years.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) There has been life-changing and lifesaving progress from diagnoses, to screening, to treatment, to access to care. Dr \uc2 Alpa Patel\uc2  of the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  is here with the latest in the fight. Good morning to you, Dr. Patel. Always good to see you, yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you for coming in. What are the biggest changes you've seen in outcomes over the last 40 years? Where's the progress now?\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 There's much to celebrate around breast cancer and the progress that we've made in the last 40 years. You can sum it up with, I think, one statistic, and that is that we've seen a 42 percent decrease in the breast cancer death rate over that time, which means more women are surviving than ever before. What's exciting to me though is that that progress is not just in one specific area.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 We know so much more about the risk factors for breast cancer so that we can prevent them better. We know how to get women to get screened, and we're improving the technologies around screening, and we have so much more to offer in terms of treatment, including for metastatic breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we know that, we know that young women, particularly women of color are facing breast cancer diagnosis more often, which is your area of - of specialty. Why are these disparities happening and, and what's being done to close that gap?\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 This is a really alarming trend and one that we need to figure out quickly. We're seeing that there is an increase in breast cancer among women, particularly under the age of 50. And we have to understand why that's happening. The most alarming part of these trends are the disparity between black women and white women. So, black women are actually less likely to hear the words, you have breast cancer...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) But.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 But more likely to die.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 If diagnosed. And we know that we have to do those things that we already have in place, which is ensure access to high quality screening and access to cutting-edge treatments. But we also have a lot that we have left to learn, and that's why I'm so excited that last year we launched a new initiative partnering with 100,000 black women across the country called Voices of Black Women, so that we can understand so much more about why that exists, and work towards eliminating.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Have to understand it, because I'll never forget at the age of 46 being told, being diagnosed with breast cancer and saying as a black woman you're less likely to get it, but more likely to die if you get it. And it's, you hear those words. You're like, what, what do you possibly do with that? Also, there's been a lot of discussion about the new guidelines when it comes to dense breast. Just explain a little bit more about what dense breasts are and those new guidelines.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Yes. Yeah, so, so many women, about 40 percent of us have dense breasts, but so many women don't know what that means. So, there's different types of breast tissue in, and fatty tissue in your breasts. If you have a higher proportion of breast tissue, that's going to make your breasts more dense, and that actually shows up, and you can calculate breast density by getting a mammogram. And as of last year, it's now required to be recorded in your mammogram report. So, please pay attention to that report. Most of us are looking for those words, no evidence of cancer detected, but they, we don't read the rest.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Read the rest, because your density is reported there. And then based on that score, talk to your doctor, because we know that it puts you at higher risk of developing breast cancer, but it also makes it harder to pick up a cancer in the mammogram. So, think about trying to spot a polar bear in a blizzard versus on a bright blue sunny day.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Got it. Wow.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 It's a lot harder to find that. So, you may need to get screened using a different technique, or you may need to be screened more frequently. So it's important to have that conversation.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a big highlight I guess to talk about today, but also, I keep seeing exciting things like vaccines and maybe using AI to maybe find that dense, you know, through that, how, how does that work?\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 So, we're actually doing some really interesting work ourselves where we're taking, just imagine taking that facial recognition technology and applying that to deeper learning on a mammogram or on a tumor slide. There's so much exciting work that can happen with AI, and vaccines as well. There's a lot of promising early studies underway. We already know that vaccines are effective in treating other types of cancer. We're seeing them used for bladder cancer, prostate cancer, and melanoma.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 So, over the next few years, I expect that we'll see a lot more coming out in the space.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you for coming in.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And can we, can we just, because we have both been impacted, our, our brothers.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Breast cancer. I know we always talk about women...\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And they are the vast majority, we are the vast majority that are affected, but we, we should also give the nod to men.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. Men, make sure that you are aware that you too can get breast cancer and to be aware of those risk factors.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dr. Patel, thank you so much for all your information.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALPA PATEL (AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We really appreciate you. Thank you. And on Friday, we will have a panel of experts and survivors with more on Breast Cancer Awareness Month.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251014ela10000h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MEDICAL GROUPS PUSH BACK AS TRUMP ADMIN LINKS AUTISM & TYLENOL WHAT PREGNANT WOMEN NEED TO KNOW?\b0\par\par\uc2 743 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TYLENOL, AUTISM, RISK, TRUMP, MAKARY, OFFIT, SANCHEZ\par \par \uc2 TYLENOL, PREGNANCY, RISK, AUTISM, LEUCOVORIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MEDICAL GROUPS PUSH BACK AS TRUMP ADMIN LINKS AUTISM & TYLENOL\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to bring in \uc2 ABC News\uc2  chief medical correspondent Dr. Tara Narula. So, can you just help us have a better understanding of this?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, so there's a few things to cover here. First of all, autism, there's not one single cause for autism. We know that it is a complex disorder characterized by hundreds of genes, different environmental influences.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTISM SPECTRUM DISORDER\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And part of the reason, Robin, why we have seen these rising numbers that President Trump alluded to is because we are screening more, it's because we changed the diagnostic criteria. It's important to look for environmental influences.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And researchers have tried to look back over the last decade to see, is there an association between Tylenol and neurodevelopmental disorders? The research has come down on both sides. There is research, as you heard in the piece, from Sweden. That was the largest study that shows there was not an association.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And there are other studies that do the same. There was just a research study a few months ago out of Mount Sinai and Harvard that did show there was an association. And so, really, the research at this point is inconclusive.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's one thing. The second thing is that association is not causation. That's a really important point. So, when we look at an association, it could be that the women who took Tylenol, the reason we saw that lane, so to speak, was not because of the Tylenol, it was because of the fever that they took the Tylenol for or the chronic pain condition, or the infection, or maybe they were taking some other medications. It's hard to tease that out. So, really important. But there is a biology there that does make sense. We know Tylenol crosses the placenta. It can cause stress on cells, it can disrupt hormones. So, there is a plausible explanation as well.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, if you are pregnant, what do you do at this point? And if you're unsure, is there an alternative to treating pain or fever?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right. So, this is a very nuanced discussion. It's not as simple as don't take Tylenol, I think, as we heard of the President say. And women really should be speaking to their doctors at this point about making an informed individualized decision. And really, like any medication, it comes down to weighing the risks and benefits. So, for some women, it may be, I don't want to take it if I have a headache, or maybe I want to take the lowest dose for the shortest time. And some women may not change their practice. It is important to point out that groups like American College of OBGYNs and Maternal Cytofetal Medicine still support Tylenol as a safe. And contrary to what the President said, there is a downside to not treating fever and pain in pregnancy, which is preterm birth, birth defects, miscarriage, maternal anxiety and depression, and high blood pressure.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RISKS OF UNTREATED FEVER & PAIN\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) The administration also announced that it's starting approval process for this drug called leucovorin.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Leucovorin is a currently used drug. It's a derivative of vitamin B9 or folate that we use to treat side effects of chemotherapy.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) We know that it's important, in some cases, for certain types of autism. We have seen benefits in terms of getting fully into the brain.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR TARA NARULA (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, for certain kids with ASD, it may be beneficial. It's not a cure. It's not for every person with autism.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dr. Narula, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020251010el9n00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MENOPAUSE MOVES FITNESS MOVES FIT FOR MENOPAUSE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,820 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ICE, RAIDS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, THREAT\par \par \uc2 AUGUST, JOBS, REPORT\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, "THIS WEEK"\par \par \uc2 DEPARTMENT OF DEFENSE, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 DENVER, PLANE, CRASH\par \par \uc2 MARYLAND, MURDER, MENDES\par \par \uc2 COVID, VACCINATION, ACCESS\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, ADAMS\par \par \uc2 AIRLINE, PASSENGER, RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 MCNEIL\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, \uc2 NFL\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, COLLEGE, "COLLEGE GAMEDAY"\par \par \uc2 POPE LEO, LGBTQ, MASS\par \par \uc2 POWERBALL, LOTTERY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ICE, CHICAGO, RAID, IMMIGRATION, HYUNDAI, PRITZKER, SCHRANK\par \par \uc2 TOPIC\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, JOBS REPORT, ECONOMIST, FEDERAL RESERVE\par \par \uc2 EMERSON, ALASKA AIRLINES, PLEA DEAL, BANNED\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE FOOTBALL, KANSAS, MISSOURI, MANNING, KIFFIN, MICHIGAN, OKLAHOMA\par \par \uc2 SCHOOL BUS, TAYLOR, MCMANUS, BUSPATROL, ROBINSON, STRING, MARPLE NEWTOWN\par \par \uc2 BIEBER, SWAG II, JONAS BROTHERS, COFFEE ICE CREAM\par \par \uc2 PAN, SCISSORS, TABLECLOTH, PILLOWCASE, PILLOW, HAIR TOWEL\par \par \uc2 SHARK ATTACK, POWERBALL, US OPEN\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE PETS, STEVENS, STETSON, CHRISTMAN, ECKERD, NOCERA, LAYDEN\par \par \uc2 THREADMILL, AIR PURIFIER, TOOTHBRUSH, NAIL CARE, FOOT CARE\par \par \uc2 MENOPAUSE, FITNESS, HEALTH, EXERCISE, HUNT, DESANTIS, STEVININ\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're back now on "GMA" with a look at how menopause can impact your health. Rhonda Hunt is a fitness trainer at Barry's and is here with a couple of fellow trainers to help show us the ropes in ways to get healthy as we enter menopause. So, Rhonda, you say you see menopause as a challenge, and there are things that women should remember?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FITNESS MOVES FIT FOR MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, absolutely. And challenge is an understatement. Your body goes through so many changes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 And I think one of the most important things to keep in mind is you are not alone. Every woman is going to come to this place in their life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENOPAUSE & MENTAL HEALTH\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 And it's gonna be different for each one of us. So, you need kind of like a community of people around.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hop into your community.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 You gotta have a tribe. Someone, like - your family members, your girlfriends, your doctors.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Definitely ask all the questions. Share your vulnerabilities. Say, hey, I'm having a tough time with this.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 And absolutely, you gotta laugh because these things start happening to you, and you don't know, like, who is this person.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, so much is out of your control.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 What is going on?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) So find the humor in it to...\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...a way to roll through it.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) How does menopause affect your overall health, body, and fitness goals?\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Well, everybody - has, is gonna have different symptoms.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 And, and their intensities, and I think what's really important is that as you go through these changes, this isn't the beginning of the end. Like, we've still got a third of our life to live.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 And we wanna live it well. Keep moving. Stay strong and keep that confidence going.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, keep moving. And what should women experiencing menopause focus on when it comes to exercising and to improve bone density?\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Most important for bone density, resistance training.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Strength training.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 But you also need flexibility and cardio.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 It's good for your, your heart, your brain, your whole circulation system.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All of these things. We're gonna get into it. What's the first move that you're gonna show us?\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Oh, shoulders. Hips and shoulders are big joints.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 They take a brunt, so we're gonna...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oooohh.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 ...go for great big - shoulder circles.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Because open it up feels good already, right?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Feels good to open up, yeah.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 If you have really tight shoulders and you wanna make it a little deeper, you can always grab a band or even a broomstick.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENOPAUSE MOVES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRETCH HIPS AND SHOULDERS TO IMPROVE FLEXIBILITY AND ALLEVIATE PAIN\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Take that up and over. You're gonna feel. You're gonna love this.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Especially if you are hunched over a desk all day long.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Do you love it?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Love it.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) That feels so good.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 It feels so good, right?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Something very easy to do.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 It is.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can just do this at home, standing up.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA HUNT (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, so Antonia, how does this next move help us? I keep, you hear a lot about, like, lift heavy and things like this.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Which we should all do.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 But listen to your body, always.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 We'll be doing a strength training move. We'll be doing a squat.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Which is a great compound movement that incorporates all of the muscles in your lower body.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 So, let's get this set up, yeah?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 All right. Feet are hip width apart.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Toes are slightly turned out. You're looking for a chair behind you.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Hip hinge back, body weight in your heels, and you come on up. Beautiful. Push through your entire foot. So, we're working our quads, the front of our legs, hamstrings, back of our legs, our glutes, our booty.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADD WEIGHTS TO SQUATS FOR STRENGTH TRAINING\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, big one. And then we're working our calves. So, we can also take this up a notch. We've got some dumbbells.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 So, feel free to grab some. You're gonna hold them by your sides, roll the shoulders back, same setup. And this time, you're picking up your grocery bags. I just say that because that's how I pick up...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 ...my groceries. And you are sitting back, and thighs are parallel. You go as low as you can go.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love this.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Your form is perfect. (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you. I ran yesterday and didn't stretch afterwards, so that felt really good.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 You did great, great. Great job.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Alycia, cardio, so important. What are you gonna show us?\par \par \uc2 ALYCIA STEVININ (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 I'm gonna show you high and low cardio moves.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 ALYCIA STEVININ (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Because some people won't be able to do.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ALYCIA STEVININ (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 So, you just go, tap out to the right and left.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 ALYCIA STEVININ (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Right?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ALYCIA STEVININ (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 So, if you have any joint issues, anything like that. And then, if you wanna level it up, you can just do a regular jumping jack.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Full?\par \par \uc2 ALYCIA STEVININ (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Right here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADD CARDIO TO STRENGTH TRAINING TO MAINTAIN BONE DENSITY\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm in heels, so I'm not gonna do that.\par \par \uc2 ALYCIA STEVININ (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) But okay, good stuff. And these are, like, easy to do at home...\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...in your living room while you're watching "GMA." I even got a little bit out of breath. Thank you, ladies, so much for this.\par \par \uc2 ALYCIA STEVININ (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA DESANTIS (FITNESS TRAINER BARRY'S)\par \par \uc2 Great job.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And stick around. We'll be right back here on "GMA." Yeah, those were good. And the squats...\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 It was nice to get the band back together today, right?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 It was.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 It really was. (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 You had a trip. I had a trip.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 You guys make some great music together.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Oooohh.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 This man right here.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 It was back to school.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Especially during commercial breaks.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 We sent Will Ganss off to class this week and everything.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 The backpack. Yeah, make sure you get an apple for the teacher. Thank you, Will.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (inaudible) Thanks for starting your day with us.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And more news on \uc2 ABC News\uc2  Live. Have a great weekend, everyone.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 See ya.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250915el960000y\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "GLAM-MA" OLDER WOMEN USING PLASTIC SURGERY TO LOOK AS GOOD AS THEY CLAIM TO FEEL\b0\par\par\uc2 1,216 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 August 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, O'HARA, WESTMAN, HALSNE, GARCIA, SCALLEN\par \par \uc2 JOJO, RECORD LABEL, LEGAL BATTLE, NEW EP, "NGL"\par \par \uc2 OLDER WOMEN, PLASTIC SURGERY, MORAES, SMITH, NAGEL\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And welcome back. Women of a certain age are redefining what it means to grow older. From glow-ups and glam-ma makeovers to tummy tucks and facelifts, some ladies are hitting the OR in order to look on the outside like they say they feel on the inside. Here's another look.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) From Cher rocking the \uc2 Victoria's Secret\uc2  Fashion Show at age 78 to "Real Housewives of Atlanta" star Nene Leakes telling Conan she is not a grandma.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: "CONAN"\par \par \uc2 NENE LEAKES ("REAL HOUSEWIVES OF ATLANTA")\par \par \uc2 I'm a glam mom.\par \par \uc2 CONAN O'BRIEN ("CONAN")\par \par \uc2 Okay. Very nice.\par \par \uc2 NENE LEAKES ("REAL HOUSEWIVES OF ATLANTA")\par \par \uc2 When you're this young and this fabulous, it's called glam mom.\par \par \uc2 KELLEY L. CARTER (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're seeing a new type of older woman right now. Older women are not staying at home and knitting socks for their children and their children's children.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And to look as good as they feel, some are turning to plastic surgery.\par \par \uc2 MARIA INES MORAES (PLASTIC SURGERY PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I think this is to me, this is the best age (inaudible). You are much more confident. You are so sure of yourself or what you want. You don't care about what people say. You make your own choices like plastic surgery.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Maria In\u233\'00\'E9s Moraes was 61 when she decided to get a tummy tuck.\par \par \uc2 MARIA INES MORAES (PLASTIC SURGERY PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 To me, it's just a matter of self-care. Like, when, you know, we all take care of our teeth, we exercise, we eat healthy and to me, plastic surgery is just something natural like that. It's part of life.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) So Maria turned to doctor Darren Smith, a plastic surgeon in New York City.\par \par \uc2 DR. DARREN SMITH (BOARD-CERTIFIED PLASTIC SURGEON)\par \par \uc2 Plastic surgery is continuing to become more accepted and I think that people are feeling more empowered to do what they want for themselves at any age.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doctor Smith specializes in the so-called "mommy makeover," documenting the before and afters on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DR. DARREN SMITH (BOARD-CERTIFIED PLASTIC SURGEON)\par \par \uc2 A "mommy makeover" almost always involves abdominal surgery, usually a tummy tuck or abdominoplasty and some form of breast surgery, which could be anything from a breast augmentation to a breast lift or a combination of those things.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) But increasingly, he's doing similar surgeries on even older women.\par \par \uc2 DR. DARREN SMITH (BOARD-CERTIFIED PLASTIC SURGEON)\par \par \uc2 We've seen a 20 to 30% uptick in women over 50 over the past year or so and I think that's a pretty interesting change in trends.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BODY SURGERIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AGE 70+ UP 30%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCE: \uc2 AMERICAN SOCIETY OF PLASTIC SURGEONS\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) In patients over the age of 70, the \uc2 American Society of Plastic Surgeons\uc2  is reporting a 30% increase in body surgeries, like tummy tucks and butt lifts, from 2022 to '23.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 15% UP IN AGES 55-69\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) In patients ages 55 to 69, the increase is 15%.\par \par \uc2 DR. DARREN SMITH (BOARD-CERTIFIED PLASTIC SURGEON)\par \par \uc2 I really think it's a new, a new found freedom to be able to take the reins and say, "Hey, I might be a little older, but I still have the power to look the way that I want to look for me."\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Gina Nagle is enjoying that freedom.\par \par \uc2 GINA NAGEL (PLASTIC SURGERY PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I feel younger and more vital today than I did in my 20s, because now I'm doing things for me. I'm doing what I love to do. It's, like, I do what makes me happy.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Gina has two adult sons and a four-year-old granddaughter.\par \par \uc2 GINA NAGEL (PLASTIC SURGERY PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 She's the most amazing human alive, of course.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) She decided to have a breast reduction and a tummy tuck.\par \par \uc2 GINA NAGEL (PLASTIC SURGERY PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 When I was at the process of raising my children, to me at that time, it seemed, it seemed frivolous or vain. I thought about it, but I was just, like, there's always something more that I could be doing with this money. Like, I could be sending my children to, you know, music lessons or doing something like that.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Recently divorced, she says she began a journey of self-discovery, one that led her to the operating table.\par \par \uc2 GINA NAGEL (PLASTIC SURGERY PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 At this point in my life, it wasn't because I had to or because I need to. I wasn't trying to impress anybody. I was comfortable in my own skin. I was doing it because I wanted to, because now it was, like, this, this body no longer reflects who I am.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (BOARD-CERTIFIED EMERGENCY MEDICINE PHYSICIAN)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think talking about things that you've gotten done, for example, cosmetic procedures, is helpful again to reduce the stigma. But I also, with that, think it's so important to also talk about what risks did you discuss with your provider?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Like any medical procedure, plastic surgery is not without its risks.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (BOARD-CERTIFIED EMERGENCY MEDICINE PHYSICIAN)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As we get older, we're, more likely use variable medications. These can interact with medications that we're using to sedate. I focus on sedation because that is one of the most highest risk parts of a procedure and many people don't realize how risky it is.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And although there have been some improvements on how we age...\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (BOARD-CERTIFIED EMERGENCY MEDICINE PHYSICIAN)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think we're more aware. We're more conscious of what we're putting in our bodies. We're more aware of this health-dot-lifestyle that benefits us and then also, the advancement of public health measures. You know, these things change the way the population looks for good reason.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some are still choosing to get by with a little help from their surgeon friends.\par \par \uc2 DR. DARREN SMITH (BOARD-CERTIFIED PLASTIC SURGEON)\par \par \uc2 Aging gracefully means different things to different people and a large part of that is personal satisfaction and comfort.\par \par \uc2 MARIA INES MORAES (PLASTIC SURGERY PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I'm coming back here soon. I've been talking to Dr. Smith for my next procedures, and I happily, I've, every time I come, I come in the morning, I said, wow, what a way to start a day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRACKER BARREL\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And when we come back, Cracker Barrel doing an about face on their recent decision to modernize their logo. What might have played into the company's decision.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020250829el8r00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LINSEY DAVID REVEALS HEALTH STRUGGLE MILLIONS OF WOMEN HAVE BEEN STRUGGLING WITH FIBROIDS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,309 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 August 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, HEAT, TROPICAL STORM ERIN\par \par \uc2 TARGET, SHOOTING, CARJACKING, ARREST, DAVIS, TORRES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THREE KILLED IN SHOOTING OUTSIDE TEXAS TARGET\par \par \uc2 US STEEL, EXPLOSION, AIR QUALITY, BROWN, MEADE, PIKE, QUINN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEATH TOLL RISES IN PENNSYLVANIA STEEL PLANT EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, PUTIN, SUMMIT, ZELENSKYY, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STRIKES IN UKRAINE AHEAD OF TRUMP-PUTIN MEETING\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , BOWSER, CRIME RATE, HEGSETH, ANTONI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP PUTS DC POLICE UNDER FEDERAL CONTROL\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE'S, BANKRUPTCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLAIRE'S FILES FOR SECOND BANKRUPTCY IN SEVEN YEARS\par \par \uc2 SWIFT, "THE LIFE OF A SHOW GIRL", KELCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW OVERNIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAYLOR SWIFT ANNOUNCES NEW ALBUM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUGUST 12\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THROWBACK\par \par \uc2 GAUDREAU\par \par \uc2 FIBROIDS, SURGERY, HENLEY, BRAXTON, HAWKIN\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) I hope that you will stop whatever you are doing right now because we have a medical story that hits home for us here at \uc2 ABC News\uc2 . I know you're going to make a revelation about your health.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. So, I have struggled for years with fibroids. And, like many women, I just kept going, pushed through for years.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LINSEY DAVID REVEALS HEALTH STRUGGLE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) But now, I've decided to have a surgery to get rid of the fibroids and, at the same time, I feel it's really important to bring attention to something that millions of women suffer with, often silently.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) I've always been relatively healthy but, in a few days, I'll be undergoing a major surgery. For year, I've been plagued by fibroids. And until recently, I rarely talked about them even as they silently shaped parts of my life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UTERINE FIBROIDS\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) Fibroids are noncancerous tumors that grow in the uterus. About five years ago, I had a surgery to have them removed, but they grew back. More of them and bigger.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Good evening, everyone. I'm Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) For years, I powered through painful menstrual cycles, extreme fatigue, and severe bloating, all while smiling through newscasts and brushing off any discomfort. But it did give me pause this past March.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 I'm Linsey Davis along with the ever-dapper \uc2 Whit Johnson\uc2 . Real-life Oscar statuette in the flesh.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Come on now.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) When the fibroids caused by belly to protrude so much on the red carpet, people on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  thought I was pregnant. Uterine fibroids affect roughly 70% of all women by the age of 50, but they're three times more common and often more severe in black women and they typically go undetected until some kind of discomfort begins.\par \par \uc2 DR BARBARA HENLEY (UROGYNECOLOGIST NORTHSIDE CENTER FOR UROGYNECOLOGY)\par \par \uc2 Fibroids can be absolutely miserable and debilitating. Some women cannot go to work when they're on their period because they are in misery due to the cramping and the abundance of bleeding.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) In recent weeks, celebrities like Venus Williams and Lupita Nyong'o have also tried to break the taboo, posting their own battle with fibroids. While fibroids are typically a topic many shy away from discussing, I'm now leaning into the conversation with others who are brave enough to share their struggles publicly.\par \par \uc2 TAMAR BRAXTON (SINGER / SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 I didn't know that it was fibroids. I thought it was a normal period, a heavy, ridiculous normal period, where you miss school, you have insane cramps. And that was not a conversation in our household full of women, black women, that we should have been sitting around on this couch speaking about.\par \par \uc2 DIAGNOSED WITH FIBROIDS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Everyone was suffering in silence.\par \par \uc2 TAMAR BRAXTON (SINGER / SONGWRITER)\par \par \uc2 Suffering in silence.\par \par \uc2 DR SOYINI HAWKIN (FIBROID & PELVIC WELLNESS CENTER OF GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 It's a public health issue. The fibroid burden in this country alone is upwards of nearly $4 billion. More than most cancers put together that women suffer from, and we just don't know it.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) I just feel like this is so important. I have, in 25 years, I have been recording, I have never heard so much feedback already just in the last 24 hours from women who said, "I'm so glad that you shared this, I have this same thing." "I'm trying to figure out what to do." "I'm thinking that this might be what my symptoms are." And really, I feel like that is the issue. It is so unhealthy and problematic to normalize abnormal behavior. And that applies to the fibroids. You start thinking, maybe this is just something I've got to deal with, and you power through. But it doesn't have to be that way.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) No. And so, how are you feeling? And tell us about the procedure.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sure. So, you know, I've made peace with this. At this point, it's been like a year plus that I've been making this decision. And ultimately, I'm taking the most drastic measure, but it's the only way to really make it final. And I'm still a few years away from menopause. And that's the only other option. I would have just kind of had to wait. And so, I'm, you know, praying for the best and looking forward to this chapter being over, to be honest.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) I didn't even realize that five years ago that you had the surgery and that they could grow back, as they did.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. Yes. You know, that was something the doctor said was possible.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, it did take, you know, a few years to realize, oh, my gosh, it's happening again and it's worse this time. And so, I think that is why I'm just kind of at the point of, let's just get it over.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. So, what has made you want to make your message?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh. You know, because I think there are so many people, you know, I didn't have my mom, my mom did not suffer from fibroids. My sister doesn't either. So, as I was going through becoming anemic, becoming just so exhausted, dealing with, you know, extreme pain and menstrual cycles, I felt, I just have to go through this, right? And I didn't know that other people, so many other people, are dealing with this as well. And so, as a result, I feel just if two people, if three people, are going to be better as a result of this, it's worth it. And it's something that I really did debate. I didn't want to make this about me or had this be a different story than what it really is intended to be. But ultimately, I just decided that this would be beneficial for other people.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, bless you.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it is. Thank you. Appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you, Linsey. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:04, WEIGHT LOSS & PREGNANCY\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And coming up later, "Breaking at 8," Dr. Tara Narula breaks down a new study on weight loss and getting pregnant.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 7:46, NBA SCHEDULE REVEAL!\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (\uc2 ABC NEWS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (VO) And next, \uc2 ESPN\uc2 's Malika Andrews is here with the exclusive reveal.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250822el8c0000c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 URGENT MANHUNT AFTER MINNESOTA LAWMAKER KILLED, ANOTHER SHOT GOV TIM WALZ CALLS SHOOTINGS "POLITICALLY MOTIVATED"\b0\par\par\uc2 782 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 June 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISRAEL & IRAN TRADE STRIKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ESCALATING ATTACKS\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, IRAN, AIRSTRIKES, NEGOTIATIONS, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISRAEL & IRAN TRADE MORE STRIKES, DEATH TOLL CLIMBS ON BOTH SIDES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ATTACK, ISRAEL, IRAN, NEGOTIATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP TALKS TO WORLD LEADERS ABOUT ISRAEL-IRAN CONFLICT\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, BRULEY, WALZ, CARLSON, BOELTER, HOFFMAN, HORTMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT MANHUNT AFTER MINNESOTA LAWMAKER KILLED, ANOTHER SHOT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We turn now to the urgent manhunt for a suspect in what authorities believe is a politically motivated assassination.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A Minnesota state lawmaker and her husband murdered inside their home. Another lawmaker and his wife shot but survived. ABC's Faith Abubey is at the scene in Brooklyn Park, Minnesota, with the latest. Faith, good morning.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, good morning to you, Whit. Investigators spent hours here yesterday at the home of the former state House speaker who was shot and killed along with her husband yesterday. Police say they encountered that suspect right here in this driveway, and it turned into a gun battle. He then took off through the house, through the back door, running.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, an urgent manhunt under way after a man investigators say was posing as a police officer allegedly shot and killed a Minnesota lawmaker and her husband in their home. Police say the same suspect was involved in a separate attack just 90 minutes earlier, allegedly shooting and wounding another lawmaker and his wife.\par \par \uc2 DISPATCHER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Suspect posing as police officers shot two victims.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Police responding to reports of gunfire around 2:00 AM Saturday at the home of Democratic Senator John Hoffman and his wife, Yvette, just outside Minneapolis. Officers then checking on Democratic Former House Speaker \uc2 Melissa Hortman\uc2  and her husband, a few miles away, at their Brooklyn Park home, only to find the two fatally shot.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF MARK BRULEY (BROOKLYN PARK POLICE)\par \par \uc2 I noticed there was a police vehicle in the driveway with the lights, emergency lights on, and what appeared to be a police officer at the door coming out of the house. When our officers confronted him, the individual immediately fired upon the officers, who exchanged gunfire and the suspect retreated back into the home.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  calling the deadly shooting politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 Speaker Hortman was someone who served the people of Minnesota with grace, compassion, humor, and a sense of service. She was a formidable public servant. A fixture and a giant in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOV TIM WALZ CALLS SHOOTINGS "POLITICALLY MOTIVATED"\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Authorities now naming 57-year-old Vance Boelter as the suspect. Investigators said he was dressed as an officer, wearing a vest, a badge and a taser and was driving an SUV with police lights. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  releasing images they say are of him in a cowboy hat and wearing a latex mask outside one of the victim's homes. Investigators say they've recovered several firearms from Boelter's vehicle along with No Kings flyers and writings mentioning the names of the victims, some abortion providers, and other Democratic lawmakers. Sources telling ABC News, law enforcement is looking into whether he may have been motivated by extreme views on abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID CARLSON (FRIEND OF VANCE BOELTER)\par \par \uc2 Through the '90s he was, he really hated abortion. We hardly ever talked, I haven't talked really about abortion with him ever for years.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) His longtime friend David Carlson saying, the suspect had been staying with him. Carlson's home raided Saturday. This just the latest in a string of violent attacks against lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 I've seen increased security for me as have my colleague, Senator Smith, and all our members as well as, of course, legislators.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And right now, there are hundreds of agents spread out across the area in this urgent manhunt. They have detained and questioned several people, but no one is in custody at this point. In the meantime, though, authorities have increased security for some elected officials and others they believe may be at risk. There's a $50,000 reward on the table for information leading to that suspect's arrest. And, Gio, I've talked to several of the neighbors here, some of them just heartbroken that this tragic event happened right here. Gio?\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Heartbreaking and incredibly concerning. Faith, thank you so much for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250620el6f00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TWO DEMOCRATIC MINNESOTA LAWMAKERS TARGETED IN SHOOTING POLICE: GUNMAN IMPERSONATED COP, CARRIED LIST OF OTHER TARGETS\b0\par\par\uc2 2,782 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 June 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2025 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 MINNESOTA, SHOOTING, BOELTER, WALZ, HOFFMAN, HORTMAN, CARLSON, KLOBUCHAR\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But we begin today with the urgent manhunt for the suspect wanted in those brazen shootings in Minnesota. Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  calling them an act of targeted political violence. ABC's Faith Abubey is on the ground in Minnesota with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TWO DEMOCRATIC MINNESOTA LAWMAKERS TARGETED IN SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the urgent manhunt continues in the Minnesota Twin Cities region for 57-year-old Vance Boelter, the suspect police say killed a beloved Minnesota lawmaker and her husband and wounded another legislator and his spouse.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 This was an act of targeted political violence.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAW ENFORCEMENT ASSISTANCE, VANCE L. BOELTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE: GUNMAN IMPERSONATED COP, CARRIED LIST OF OTHER TARGETS\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 FBI\uc2  putting out these images they say are of Boelter not long after the attack in a cowboy hat, offering a 50 thousand dollar reward for information leading to his arrest. Boelter, a Minnesota resident, ran a private security company with his wife.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM NEWS BRIEFING\par \par \uc2 DREW EVANS (BUREAU OF CRIMINAL APPREHENSION)\par \par \uc2 Do not approach him. You should consider him around and dangerous.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Around 2:00 am Saturday, police in Champlin, north west of Minneapolis, responding to reports of a shooting at the home of State Senator John Hoffman and his wife, Yvette.\par \par \uc2 AUDIO CLIP FROM EMERGENCY DISPATCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXT OF DIALOGUE\par \par \uc2 911 DISPATCHER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Suspect posing as police officers, shot two victims.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOCATION MAP\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Eight miles away in Brooklyn Park, a fast-thinking sergeant proactively checking on the home of former House Speaker \uc2 Melissa Hortman\uc2  and her husband, Mark, finding them fatally shot.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MANHUNT FOR ALLEGED SHOOTER ENTERS SECOND DAY\par \par \uc2 MARK BRULEY (BROOKLYN PARK POLICE CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 I noticed that there was a police vehicle in the driveway with the lights, emergency lights, on, and what appeared to be a police officer at the door coming out of the house. When our officers confronted him, the individual immediately fired upon the officers, who exchanged gunfire, and the suspect retreated back into the home.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) They say the suspect fled on foot, was dressed as a police officer, complete with a badge, vest and taser, and a realistic-looking latex mask covering his hair, allegedly seen here in doorbell camera images obtained by ABC News. Investigators say they've recovered several firearms from Boelter's vehicle, along with a list of 70 potential targets including other Democratic lawmakers and abortion providers in the state. Sources telling ABC News law enforcement is looking into whether he may have been motivated by extreme views on abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID CARLSON (FRIEND OF SUSPECT)\par \par \uc2 Through the '90s he was, he really hated abortion. But we hardly ever talked, I haven't talked really about abortion for him, forever, for years.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) His longtime friend, David Carlson, saying the suspect had been staying with him. Carlson's home raided Saturday. \uc2 Melissa Hortman\uc2  was a mother of two who served as State House Speaker for six years with more than two decades in public office. News of her murder stunning lawmakers in her home state and nationwide. Minnesota's Governor, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , heartbroken.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 Speaker Hortman was someone who served the people of Minnesota with grace and compassion, humor and a sense of service. She was a formidable public servant, a fixture and a giant in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Martha, the shootings here are just the latest in a series of attacks on political figures that have shaken US politics. President Trump himself the target of two assassination attempts last year, releasing a statement yesterday that such horrific violence will not be tolerated here in the United States of America. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Faith Abubey, thanks to you. Joining us now is Minnesota Senator Amy Klobuchar. Condolences to you, Senator Klobuchar, all your colleagues, everyone there in Minnesota. I know both legislators were...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...were close friends of yours and especially Speaker Hortman. Tell us about her.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MN STATE REP. \uc2 MELISSA HORTMAN\uc2 , HUSBAND KILLED IN THEIR HOME\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Melissa Hortman\uc2  is a woman that I wish everyone around the country knew. When you think about a true public servant, who goes into it for the right reasons, it's Melissa. You know, she started out as a mom with little kids. She was working at her dad's used auto parts company, and I got to know her then, and I saw how she door knocked and knew practically every person in her district. She was able to juggle being a Sunday School teacher, being a Girl Scout leader, training service dogs for veterans, and she and Mark, and I think of their kids today on Father's Day, they lost their mom and their dad.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 They were able to juggle it all, and maybe that's what helped her be this incredible legislator who worked her way up to Speaker of the House, who helped lead the efforts to pass this incredible work on paid family leave where Minnesota is on the map in such a big way to help people, or preschool lunch, or the work she just did last year with the Governor, where we had a literally tied legislature and she and her Republican counterpart, another woman, were able to negotiate a budget and get that done so we didn't go into government shutdown in a very difficult time.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MN STATE SENATOR JOHN HOFFMAN, WIFE SERIOUSLY WOUNDED\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 She was a true leader and loved her work but was always so grounded and such a decent person. I think that's probably the best word to describe her. You look at her pictures and you know what she was about. So, we miss her greatly. And so, when you hear about statistics, about political violence and threats, I just want people to know who we're talking about, or Senator Hoffman who is equally respected, got into politics because his daughter has spine bifida and she couldn't get insurance and he wanted to advocate for her. Decent people who didn't deserve this to happen to them, 2:00 in the morning in the middle of the night, when all they're doing is trying to represent the people that they were elected to represent.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Senator, the suspect still at-large. What more can you tell us about the investigation and whether or not they believe they are zeroing in on this man? And tell us more about him.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 Sure, Martha. I hope you show his picture because the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and law enforcement is asking the public if they see this guy, do not approach him. He's evil. He is smart. He has killed without a second thought. And there's a 50 thousand dollar award. One of the most chilling pictures for me is the one that was taken right before he killed my friends, right at the, with a simple doorbell camera.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Ring camera.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 Right. And it just shows he was wearing a mask in that, but they wanted people to see that. There's another picture of him in a cowboy hat afterward, and he's someone who was able to soup up a police car, talk his way into making them think he was in law enforcement, dressed like law enforcement. And so, what I know is that for a while they had, they thought they had him pinned down in a certain area near the murders. Now they are just following every lead, asking for the public's help. We couldn't be getting more help from law enforcement. And the one thing that I've noted is that when they came onto, the local the local cops came on the crime scene of Senator Hoffman, they didn't just stay there.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 They went and they thought, "Wow, we better check on the Speaker." She, they had already been shot, but then they were able to get information about who this person was because they had the foresight to go to the scene of her home, having no idea if anything had happened there. That has allowed them to do better at knowing who he was and asking for the public help. So, those local police officers at that moment not only appear to have saved Senator Hoffman and his wife's life but also have given us major leads to who he was and why he was doing this.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And give us the sense of the political motivation that people have, have talked about, Governor Walz, you have, too, what is the motivation there and, as far as you can tell from writings from the suspect if, if, in fact, he is captured, what about that list?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOV. WALZ: ATTACKS AN 'ACT OF TARGETED POLITICAL VIOLENCE'\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 So, I obviously haven't seen any of this and can't confirm exactly what's in it, but there are some signs he had included on the list, and this has been reported, but the list isn't out, were \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and a number of other places and leaders who have advocated in, in favor of women's rights. They were on there. I believe that there are some things in the manifesto that would lead you down that trail to believe that this is one of his motivating forces. Now, we're not certain if that was the only thing. Maybe he had somehow known these two legislators. Maybe there were other reasons. But that is clearly one of them, but it may not be the only one.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 I will also say that it wasn't just the Governor and I. We joined with the Republican members of Congress in our delegation, our entire congressional delegation in Minnesota. Congressman Emmer and myself and others, Senator Smith, Representative McCollum and the rest of our delegation, actually joined in a statement and said we speak with one voice to condemn this political violence. There's no place for this politically motivated violence in our democracy. And when you look at all the threats and the increase in threats against elected officials, something like 17 hundred back in 2016 and now we're up to nine thousand, this is a rampant problem that the public and all of us deal with, and also against judges.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And all of us should condemn all of that violence. We thank you for joining us this morning and, again, condolences to all.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECURITY OFFICIALS GRAPPLE WITH MOST COMPLEX THREATS SINCE 9/11\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And for more on the manhunt and the heightened threat environment across the country, let's bring in our Chief Justice Correspondent Pierre Thomas and former FBI Agent and ABC News Contributor Brad Garrett. Good morning, gentlemen. Pierre, I want to start with you. How much more difficult is it to catch the suspect at this point?\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The longer this goes, Martha, the more complicated it gets. This is the person who has military training, some training in law enforcement as well based on how he operated yesterday. So, law enforcement now has to really focus in on narrowing the net and trying to pin this person down because he's got the wherewithal to make this a long manhunt.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Brad, you were an \uc2 FBI\uc2  profiler. We talk about lone wolves. Both of you talk a lot about lone wolves. This suspect seems very different. This is not a guy living in his mother's basement who's, you know, 18-years-old loner.\par \par \uc2 BRAD GARRETT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, he's in his mid to late 50s. And if you look at his life quickly, you know, he's apparently had a lot of failures and apparently was in financial, had financial problems before the shooting. And that these things build up in people and they just, you know, they go basically from anger, sadness, envy and typically then to rage. And once they get to rage, it just flips a switch about inhumanity and their ability to justify going and killing people.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, Pierre, we talk about the threat environment. It's extraordinary high this year, and this really brings that home in such a terrible, terrible way. We've had assassination attempts on the President. We've had Josh Shapiro's house set on fire. It, it's a pretty high threat environment out there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECURITY OFFICIALS GRAPPLE WITH MOST COMPLEX THREATS SINCE 9/11\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Martha, I've been doing this for 25-plus years, and I cannot think of a more dangerous season than we're currently in. You mentioned the assassination plots. We just a few weeks ago had that beautiful couple gunned down in front of a museum here who were tied to the Israeli Embassy. We also had the New Orleans New Year's Day terror attack, a fertility clinic bombing where the person used an ANFO bomb similar to Oklahoma City.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, we're in a season where people with various ideologies are going and taking specific violent action against the American public. And, also, we see a situation where these mass shootings continue and authorities are worried about what's going on between Israel and Iran right now, the threat of cyberattacks. So, law enforcement is being pressed to the max.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, Brad, just, just your thoughts on that as well and this threat environment. You, you were an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent for a very long time. You've seen a lot of things, social media playing a part.\par \par \uc2 BRAD GARRETT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All of that.\par \par \uc2 BRAD GARRETT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Typically what happens with people is that they get to a point where they feel powerless and they feel like the only thing they can do is go out and commit some mass shooting or blow something up or whatever it might be. And it's like for a brief moment they now have power. And if, for example, he went after these two politicians because of their beliefs about pro-choice, that may be enough for him, if that's even accurate, for him to hang on that and use that as a justification to take them out.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHORITIES WARN WELL-KNOWN INDIVIDUALS 'VULNERABLE TO ATTACK'\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just, just, quickly, the suspect had a list, say police. Is that something he would go back to now or is he just thinking you would think about escape?\par \par \uc2 BRAD GARRETT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, I think he was going to keep chipping away on that list that the law enforcement...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But now, but now that they're after him?\par \par \uc2 BRAD GARRETT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, now they're after, no, I don't think so. I mean, now it's, it's a matter of survival, and he made a comment, apparently, to a roommate that, you know, he might be dead soon, so that's another possibility here.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Martha, if I could quickly say one thing. It's the concentration that's different. Day after day, week after week, we're seeing these things sporadically happen on a routine basis.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We certainly are and we hope it stops. Thank you both for joining us this morning. Up next, could the US be drawn into another Middle East conflict? We head overseas where intense fighting between Israel and Iran is raising concerns about the prospect of all-out war. We're back in two minutes.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020250619el6f00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FORMER \uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ ANANDA LEWIS DIES AT 52 REMEMBERING FORMER MTV VJ ANANDA LEWIS\b0\par\par\uc2 822 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 June 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 AIR, INDIA, BOEING, CRASH\par \par \uc2 IRAN, ISRAEL, WAR, NUCLEAR\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, IMMIGRATION, ICE, RAIDS, BASS, FAVILA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, PARADE, KENNEDY, CENTER, DURBIN, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 UNFI, GROCERY, STORES, CYBERATTACK\par \par \uc2 WILSON, BEACH BOYS, "SULLIVAN", "PET SOUNDS", JARDINE, PHILLIPS, STAMOS\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM SAN ANTONIO, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 MUSIC VIDEO CLIP FROM THE NEW KIDS ON THE BLOCK\par \par \uc2 READ, MURDER, RETRIAL, O'KEEFE, RENTSCHLER\par \par \uc2 NAVY, SAILOR, RESENDIZ, VIRGINIA, CASTLE\par \par \uc2 SCHEFFLER, VENMO, GAMBLERS, GREENBERG\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , FINALS, \uc2 PACERS\uc2 , THUNDER, HALIBURTON, CLARK, MATHURIN\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM ST. PAUL, MINNESOTA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIR QUALITY CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PASSENGER PLANE CRASH\par \par \uc2 PLANE, CRASH, AIR INDIA, RAMESH\par \par \uc2 TRIAL, COMBS, JUROR DISMISSAL\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, FLOODING, RESCUE, STORM, TRACK\par \par \uc2 LEWIS, BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're also going to remember right now Ananda Lewis. The former \uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ and talk show host died at the age of 52 following a fight with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER \uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ ANANDA LEWIS DIES AT 52\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Eva Pilgrim back with a look at her life. Hello, Eva.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, George. Ananda Lewis had been very public about her cancer journey, in large part because she wanted to raise awareness for other women. This morning, family and friends mourning her passing.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, fans are celebrating the life of Ananda Lewis, one of the most popular video jockeys on \uc2 MTV\uc2  during the heyday of "Total Request Live."\par \par \uc2 ANANDA LEWIS (\uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ AND JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 I'm Ananda, and we're counting down the top 10 videos.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) On Wednesday, her sister sharing Lewis passed away at 52 years old after a several-years-long battle with breast cancer writing, "She's free and in His heavenly arms."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SHOW, "TEEN SUMMIT"\par \par \uc2 ANANDA LEWIS (\uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ AND JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to Teen Summit.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lewis started in TV in 1995 as a host of BET's "Teen Summit."\par \par \uc2 ANANDA LEWIS (\uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ AND JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Well, it's 10:00 AM. And you know what that means, mail time.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Before becoming a fixture on \uc2 MTV\uc2 , hosting TRL.\par \par \uc2 ANANDA LEWIS (\uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ AND JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Do you get to see any of your old friends from home?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And "Hot Zone."\par \par \uc2 ANANDA LEWIS (\uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ AND JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Chilling out on the beach with Britney Spears.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Eventually getting her own syndicated talk show, "The Ananda Lewis Show" in 2001.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SHOW, "THE ANANDA LEWIS SHOW"\par \par \uc2 ANANDA LEWIS (\uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ AND JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 We're going to have some fun today, guys.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In 2020, Lewis revealing she was diagnosed with stage 3 breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 ANANDA LEWIS (\uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ AND JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 I have been fighting to get cancer out of my body for almost two years.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Opening up to our Kelly Carter last year, saying she opted to forego a doctor-recommended double mastectomy, choosing instead homeopathic treatment, which is not recommended by doctors as effective.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REMEMBERING FORMER \uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ ANANDA LEWIS\par \par \uc2 ANANDA LEWIS (\uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ AND JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 They cleaned up my diet. Right? And so, the high-dose vitamin C IVs combined with hyperbarics, at the same time, I was being monitored by doctors. I didn't just turn my back on medicine. I spent 16 weeks in Arizona. I did fractionated chemotherapy. So, it's 10% to 20% of what you normally do.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In 2023, Lewis learned her cancer had advanced to stage 4.\par \par \uc2 KELLY L CARTER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What is it that you hope people say about you, people remember about you?\par \par \uc2 ANANDA LEWIS (\uc2 MTV\uc2  VJ AND JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Maybe that I loved hard and lived loud and didn't back away from problems and loved this life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ANANDA LEWIS, 1973 - 2025\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ananda said she really didn't want her treatment choice to become the story, as many who've been diagnosed with cancer or if you have loved ones who have you know, this is an incredibly personal decision. There are a lot of factors into how and why people choose a treatment. Even doctors have told us, you have to respect patient's decisions in those moments. And this morning, we are thinking of her loved ones, guys.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We sure are. Glad you said that at the end. It is a personal choice.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She was wonderful.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She really was.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She was really talented.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, very talented.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250630el6c0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 COVER STORY NEW DEVELOPMENTS IN WOMEN'S HEALTH\b0\par\par\uc2 970 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, THREAT\par \par \uc2 NEW ORLEANS, JAIL, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 NJT\uc2 , STRIKE, TRANSIT\par \par \uc2 COMBS, COURT, CASE, TESTIMONY, VENTURA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, DRONE, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , NUCLEAR, DEAL, IRAN\par \par \uc2 POPE LEO XIV\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , BASKETBALL\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP OF BASEBALL GAME\par \par \uc2 MEMORIAL DAY, TRAVEL, TIPS\par \par \uc2 FLORIDA, BEAR, ATTACK, 911\par \par \uc2 WNBA, BASKETBALL\par \par \uc2 DOG, ANGEL, MAX\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TORNADO, STORM\par \par \uc2 ESCAPE, COMEY, NEW ORLEANS, JAIL\par \par \uc2 NEW JERSEY, TRANSIT, STRIKE, UNION\par \par \uc2 COMEY\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK KNICKS\uc2 , \uc2 BOSTON CELTICS\uc2 , \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 BRACHO-SANCHEZ, MAMMOGRAM, WOMEN, HEALTH, PAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're back now with our ''GMA'' Cover Story. New developments this morning when it comes to women's health. Dr. Edith Bracho-Sanchez, a medical doctor here in New York City at Columbia Presbyterian Hospital, is here to break everything down. Thank you for being here, Dr. Edith. We always appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DEVELOPMENTS IN WOMEN'S HEALTH\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 Of course.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's start with something that you say is so important. It's new guidance released by the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  on managing pain during common in-office procedures like IUD insertions and biopsies. Tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 Yes, it is an acknowledgement that what women go through during these procedures is serious and should be taken seriously. So, the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  saying the physicians should partner with patients to offer a wide range of options during gynecological procedures like IUD insertions, like cervical biopsies. Some of those options may include creams, they may include sprays, they may include injections. But the call, again, is to not underestimate the pain that patients go through, to take it seriously. And it's an acknowledgement that, listen, we haven't always been the best at acknowledging and appropriately treating women's pain. So, a great, great step.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And for some patients, that can almost feel like gaslighting when they're saying that they're in pain and the doctor's maybe not taking...\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...it seriously.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Important to note.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And also the first study, it's a new way of analyzing mammograms. So, what does that mean?\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 So, I love this so much, Whit, because what it does is it says, let's use radiomics, which is a technique that allows researchers to extract information from images that have already been done. So, we're not putting women through additional tests or procedures. What we're doing is applying mathematical algorithms to images that have already been done. And what they did was, using this technique, classify women's breast tissue density, or texture, I should say, into six different categories and then match them to future cancer risk. The hope, of course, being that this will allow early detection and then allow us to intervene a little early, but very exciting technology.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER SCREENING STUDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TECHNOLOGY COULD HELP IDENTIFY CANCER RISK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TECHNOLOGY RADIOMICS COULD REDUCE THE RISK OF FALSE NEGATIVES\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, what does that mean for women?\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 So, it means really that we're going to be able to, again, without undergoing additional testing, without undergoing additional procedures, potentially detect cancer earlier.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 And what's exciting is that this was true regardless of BMI, breast tissue density, and also race. So, the result and the ability to match this to future cancer risk, depending on tissue textures, was true for both Black and White women.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Important updates there. And real quick, I know we're running out of time, but can we bring in little William? Because...\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 Oh, my gosh, of course (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...I think it is incredible, Dr. Edith, that you joined us here on a weekend and you're also Mommy too.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 Come, buddy.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You are a busy doctor.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 I am Mommy. I am Mommy.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And bringing your baby.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 And can I just say...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We appreciate you so much.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 Oh, my gosh, of course. Can I just say one thing? We're talking about very exciting things for women and...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 ...for women's health. But the call is that we can't really use these treatments and these early detections if you don't make it to the physician to begin with.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Great.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Great point.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 So, come see us. And everyone who has a woman in their life, who loves a woman, right? All the moms out there...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDITH BRACHO-SANCHEZ (COLUMBIA-PRESBYTERIAN HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 ...just make it. Please make it. Let's keep up with our screenings because it matters.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There you go.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Super mom. Right from the super mom right there.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250627el5h0003g\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVES AT-HOME TEST FOR CERVICAL CANCER COULD HELP WOMEN WHO MIGHT OTHERWISE SKIP THEIR SCREENINGS\b0\par\par\uc2 449 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 INDIA, PAKISTAN, TENSION, STRIKES\par \par \uc2 POPE LEO XIV, CONCLAVE\par \par \uc2 FAA\uc2 , FLIGHTS, NEWARK\par \par \uc2 TARIFFS, US, CHINA\par \par \uc2 SOUTER, JUSTICE, DIES, 89\par \par \uc2 TUFTS, STUDENT, RELEASED, ICE\par \par \uc2 COMBS, TRIAL, JURY\par \par \uc2 CERVICAL, CANCER, TEAL, WAND\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to approval of an at-home test that doctors hope will lead to more women being screened for cervical cancer. ABC's Morgan Norwood has those details for us. Good morning, Morgan.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVES AT-HOME TEST FOR CERVICAL CANCER\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you, Janai. This really could be a game changer. So, yes, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has approved an at-home testing kit for HPV. That's the virus that causes cervical cancer, meaning no stirrups, no waiting room. It's called the Teal Wand. And doctors say, again, it could be a game changer when it comes to screening access and comfort. Now, until now, self-swab tests were only allowed in doctors' offices, but this is the first test approved for use completely at home. So, let's talk about how it works. You will need a prescription from a Teal Health provider before using the kit at home.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) According to the company, it is effective for women ages 25 to 65 with an average risk. It is a major step forward because, remember, cervical cancer is highly preventable with your regular screenings. But not everyone has access or feels comfortable with those routine pap smears and cervical testing. So, HPV testing every year, more than 13,000 women are diagnosed with invasive cervical cancer, and more than 4,000 women die from it. So, the hope here is that this at-home test will help more women get screened. These test kits though are expected to ship as soon as June. Janai?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Morgan, you mentioned how this could help women who might otherwise skip their screenings. How could this also help women in underserved communities?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, such a good question because cervical cancer disproportionately impacts women in underserved areas, especially where there are these so-called care deserts. Think your limited access to Ob/Gyn, transportation, et cetera. Now, according to a recent study back in March, cervical cancer cases are actually on the rise in rural communities, with death rates being 1.42 times higher to urban settings. So, these tests are more than just a matter of convenience. This really could be lifesaving, Gio.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It truly could be. All right, Morgan. Thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250626el5a00031\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "DYING FOR SEX" CREATORS TALK NEW SERIES CREATORS TALK ABOUT WHY IT'S IMPORTANT TO GET BREAST CANCER RIGHT\b0\par\par\uc2 1,620 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 April 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXTREME FIRE DANGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPRING SWINGS & SEVERE STORMS\par \par \uc2 STORM, FIRE, HEARTLAND, SOUTHWEST\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , DEPORTATIONS, BOASBERG, PROTESTS, GARCIA, HOLLEN, BONDI, BUKELE\par \par \uc2 HARVARD, \uc2 IRS\uc2 , STATUS, TRUMP, DHS, STUDENTS\par \par \uc2 FED, POWELL, TARIFFS, STOCKS, E-COMMERCE, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 ARSON, SHAPIRO, PASSOVER, BALMER, PALESTINE\par \par \uc2 RFK, CDC, AUTISM, EXPERTS, CHILDREN, VACCINES, STUDY\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM HOLLYWOOD WALK OF FAME\par \par \uc2 FYRE2, POSTPONED, MCFARLAND, TICKETS, MAKER, SALUNEK\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ, HOCHMAN, PAROLE, RISK, NUSOM, VANDERMOLEN, BARALT\par \par \uc2 WEIGHT, LOSS, PILL, TRIAL, DIABETES\par \par \uc2 SQUID, ATLANTIC, OCEAN, CAMERA, TAYLOR, HOOKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPRING SWINGS & SEVERE STORMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE DANGER EXPANDING\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, WIND, STORMS, WILDFIRES, DROUGHT\par \par \uc2 FRONTIER AIRLINES\uc2 , HARD LANDING, SOTO\par \par \uc2 TARIFFS, TRADE WAR, ECONOMY, MURPHY, COOKE, HAYMES\par \par \uc2 TECH SCAMS, \uc2 FTC\par \par \uc2 BINGE DRINKING\par \par \uc2 "ABBOTT ELEMENTARY", "ALWAYS SUNNY IN PHILADELPHIA", PAISLEY\par \par \uc2 DEALS, DISCOUNT, SHOPPING\par \par \uc2 EASTER, DESSERTS, TREATS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EASTER TREATS!\par \par \uc2 ROSENSTOCK, BOYER, "DYING FOR SEX"\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to "GMA." The FX series "Dying for Sex" is about a woman who embarks on a journey of self-discovery and sexual awakening after being diagnosed with stage 4 breast cancer. It is getting rave reviews. And we have two of the show's executive producers, Kim Rosenstock and Nikki Boyer with us this morning. It's so great to see both of you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "DYING FOR SEX" CREATORS TALK NEW SERIES\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BOYER (EXECUTIVE PRODUCER "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Hi.\par \par \uc2 KIM ROSENSTOCK (EXECUTIVE PRODUCE & CO-CREATOR "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Hi.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thrilled by what kind of acclaim the series is getting. All of the great feedback that it's getting. And, Nikki, it all began with a podcast that you shared about your friend.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BOYER (EXECUTIVE PRODUCER "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Thank you. Yes. It was a Wondery podcast that Molly and I created before she was really, really sick enough to be in the hospital. And then we continued to record in the hospital, and then we brought these recordings to Wondery and we shaped this beautiful story that sort of, I don't, it landed in a way that I didn't expect and it impacted people in such a beautiful way. And Molly's bravery really shines through. And even though it was about sex and death, which are pretty taboo, I think people really loved to hear about it in a very authentic way. So, such an honor to do it with her.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, my producer Erin says she went through the whole, watched the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BOYER (EXECUTIVE PRODUCER "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Really?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She said, it is, Michael, it is fantastic. Absolutely fantastic. And she never says anything nice about anything. She's back there.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And she puts up with a lot clearly.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, Kim, we've seen, we've seen a lot of shows that portrayed people living with metastatic breast cancer. TV shows, movies as well. How important was it for you to get this right?\par \par \uc2 KIM ROSENSTOCK (EXECUTIVE PRODUCE & CO-CREATOR "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Extremely important. Me and Liz Meriwether, my co-creator, you know, it was so important to us to not just get the medical reality of it right, but also the emotional reality of it right. What it is actually like to live with a stage 4 metastatic breast cancer diagnosis and to portray all the highs and lows. And also, the humor of it because we talked to so, so it was really important to talk to as many people as possible, to make sure we had a wide range of perspectives because every case is different. And we also made sure to have a writer's room that had people who had experienced either taking care of someone with cancer, a cancer survivor.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 KIM ROSENSTOCK (EXECUTIVE PRODUCE & CO-CREATOR "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 You know, people who had different cancer, we had all sorts of people come on who had breast cancer. An amazing woman named Sam who came on who had stage 4 metastatic breast cancer and shared all of, you know, the information. So, at every stage of making this show, it was like we just kept finding more and more people who were just, were so excited to come actually talk to us about what it's like. And they would tell us things that it was important to them to see done like if we're telling the story. And so, we kind of got a list from them of like, here's what I normally see that I don't want to. Here's something I haven't seen that it'd be really great if you could put on screen.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, I love it.\par \par \uc2 KIM ROSENSTOCK (EXECUTIVE PRODUCE & CO-CREATOR "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 And we used that list as we were making the show. You know, we had an amazing, this amazing black woman who was like, nobody talks about there's only like two wigs for us. And one of them makes us look like Bea Arthur. And so, we were like, that's great. She's like, I would love to see somebody talk about that. So, we put that in the scene, in a character. Yeah. So, stuff like that. You know, it was, it was really great. But, you know, the humor. And the humor that is in the podcast, I think, was so inspiring to us as we were making it. But, like, humor is a way through the pain. But also, that these women have, they're living. Like it's not, you don't get this diagnosis and then you're dying. You're very much living.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And I think just taking power and control maybe because you can't control your health, but maybe you can control your response and help the people around you.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BOYER (EXECUTIVE PRODUCER "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's just what I'm hearing from this. I want to see it, so let's take a look at a clip.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SHOW, "DYING FOR SEX"\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WILLIAMS ("DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Jerry, going forward, I need you to walk me through ahead of time step by step what will happen during the procedure. I need you to answer all my questions and Nikki's. I need you to listen to me when I say what I feel and hear me when I say I need a break. Now, breathe with me.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. That scene, funny and powerful. It started with the podcast.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BOYER (EXECUTIVE PRODUCER "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you think Molly would think of all of this?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BOYER (EXECUTIVE PRODUCER "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 This is when I start to get emotional every time because I think she would be so incredibly proud and so honored to have someone as amazing as Kim and Liz working on this and bringing the story to life, making her feel less invisible in the world and also empowering her. And funny, like, I think she would be just incredibly proud of how it's being received and how it's cracking people open, which is really what she really, really wanted.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Michelle Williams and Jenny Slate, you can't get two any, two people any better to tell this story in this way.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BOYER (EXECUTIVE PRODUCER "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Right?\par \par \uc2 KIM ROSENSTOCK (EXECUTIVE PRODUCE & CO-CREATOR "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Unbelievable. I mean, the way that they navigated the drama and the comedy simultaneously, it all, and they're two just truthful performers and storytellers. So, and our entire cast was just unbelievable and we were so lucky. Also, you see that clip that you just played, it was a big part of what we talked about showing and a lot of the women that we talked to that have stage 4 were like, show someone advocating for themselves within the medical system. Trust that if you know your body and if you think something is wrong, you should go to a doctor. And if that doctor doesn't listen to you, go to another doctor.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 KIM ROSENSTOCK (EXECUTIVE PRODUCE & CO-CREATOR "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Don't, don't feel crazy. You're not crazy. And I think that was a big part of Molly's story, too.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BOYER (EXECUTIVE PRODUCER "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 KIM ROSENSTOCK (EXECUTIVE PRODUCE & CO-CREATOR "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 So, that was really important to us.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BOYER (EXECUTIVE PRODUCER "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 That's why it's one of my favorite scenes, because I hope someone can watch this and see this and say, oh, I can challenge the medical system. I can push the envelope and ask the uncomfortable questions and advocate.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you're making us laugh and making a difference in your work.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you so much to both of you and to Liz Meriwether.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BOYER (EXECUTIVE PRODUCER "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KIM ROSENSTOCK (EXECUTIVE PRODUCE & CO-CREATOR "DYING FOR SEX")\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All episodes of FX's "Dying for Sex" are streaming now on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250519el4h0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW STUDY ON MAMMOGRAMS AFTER AGE 70 WHAT ARE THE GUIDELINES FOR WOMEN AFTER 70\b0\par\par\uc2 1,158 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEPORTATION SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 JUDGE, TRUMP, ABREGO GARCIA, DEPORTATION, BUKELE, HOLLAND\par \par \uc2 HARVARD, CLASH, TRUMP, POLICIES, LEGALITY\par \par \uc2 DEPORTATION, TRUMP, SCOTUS, COMPLY, HARVARD\par \par \uc2 LEAKS, PENTAGON, HEGSETH, LEAVE\par \par \uc2 STORY2 TRUMP TO NAME HUNTER BIDEN WHISTLEBLOWER ACTING IRS HEAD\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, SPEECH, SOCIAL SECURITY, \uc2 IRS\uc2 , TRUMP\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, INJURED, SCHOOL, PARENTS, STUDENTS, SMITH\par \par \uc2 MARTINDALE, GAME SHOWS, LEGACY, CELEBRATING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 STOWAWAY, DELTA, JFK, PARIS, TSA, SECURITY, FRANKEL\par \par \uc2 AUTISM, SPECTRUM, CDC, STUDY, AWARENESS\par \par \uc2 HACKMAN, VIDEO, DEATHS, INVESTIGATION, DAUGHTERS\par \par \uc2 TODDLERS, DRUMMING, DANCING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRITICAL FIRE DANGER\par \par \uc2 TARIFFS, CHINA\par \par \uc2 PLANE, FIRE, \uc2 UNITED AIRLINES\par \par \uc2 MAMMOGRAMS, CANCER\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now a new study on mammograms for women after age 70, and how they may lower their risk of late stage cancer and death. Our chief medical correspondent, Dr. Tara Narula, as promised, is back with more. So, what are the guidelines for women after 70, and what did this study find?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY ON MAMMOGRAMS AFTER AGE 70\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Robin, for the 22 million women who are over 75, there's really been a misunderstanding about what to do with them. We haven't had a lot of guidance. So, the \uc2 US Preventive Services Task Force\uc2  says there's insufficient evidence to recommend mammograms to women over 75. The \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  says if you're over 55, you can either have every year or every year mammograms if you're healthy and you're expected to live more than ten years.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, this study really looked at that question of what do we do with this population. They looked at about 13,000 women who were over 70 who had estrogen receptor positive HER2 negative cancer. And they looked at the previous five years to see who had gotten screened with a mammogram. They in fact found that those women who had, had a 54 percent lower risk of developing a late stage cancer, a 36 percent lower risk of dying from breast cancer, and if they had had three to four mammograms in those previous five years instead of one mammogram, they had a 37 percent risk of dying from breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Everybody's taking in all this information and rightfully so.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But why aren't there guidelines for women after 75? We're living longer now and, and women want to know, you know, is it one year, two years?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There's a lot of miscommunication here.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It, it really comes down to this issue of over treatment, over diagnosis. And what do we mean by that? So, for many women in those older ages, if you find a cancer, it may be clinically not significant. Neither it's so, meaning it's so slow growing, takes 10 to 30 years to grow, or would never grow at all, that when we find it, now we're subjecting women to dealing with it. And that may mean a biopsy, chemo, radiation, emotional distress, scarring, infection. All the things that come with finding a cancer that may not have ever caused a problem for them.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so that's where the question really comes in, and it has to be, really, a shared decision making experience with the doctor and the woman to discuss, what does this mean for my quality of life? What do I hope to do? The hope is that at some point in the future we're really able to better analyze those tumors to say...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...this is one that would really be clinically silent, and this is one that we could treat with maybe hormones or just watch. This is one that we need to take out. Right? And so we are seeing that with precision medicine, with personalized medicine, and hopefully, we will get there. We're not there yet. So, for now it's really that conversation.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is a conversation. Having gone through breast cancer, women reach out to me all the time, and older women, and some want to go through the treatment, and there are some who say, no, I'm good.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it is a personal choice, but you should really consult with your physician.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Definitely.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Those percentages are significant.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They really are.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, they are. They are. And they would make you say, ah-ha, I should definitely do it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But that, but it's just not that, you know, that's why we have screening recommendations because we don't just want to blanketly screen everyone because that comes with what do you do when you find something then?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, that's it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Okay. Double duty (inaudible) this morning. Thank you very much, Dr. Narula. Coming up in our "GMA" morning menu, fresh off a national championship on the court and shining on stage, there she is, the number one pick in the WNBA draft, Paige Bueckers, live here in Times Square.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PAIGE BUECKERS LIVE IN TIMES SQUARE!\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also ahead, our exclusive look inside "Vogue's" Met Gala issue and this year's dress code.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INSIDE VOGUE'S MET GALA ISSUE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus day two of Tory Johnson's spring fling Deals and Steals all about bringing the colors of the season into your look, with bargains starting at less than $15. And Lara and Sam, you're with Lori Bergamotto. Hey, guys.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS!\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we are.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, we are. We're getting ready to enter Lori's laboratory, where she has devised ways to avoid Easter egg shell shock.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because they're so expensive. Lori has the alternatives that you and your kids will actually love.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's coming up right here on "GMA," and those are really pretty.\par \par \uc2 LORI BERGAMOTTO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Give them a squeeze.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250516el4g0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 OPRAH DAILY MENOPAUSE O-WARDS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,242 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , TARIFFS, ECONOMY, STOCKS, TUCHMAN, WALTON\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, TARIFFS, ECONOMY, NETANYAHU, SCHUMER\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, DEPORTATION, SUPREME COURT, IMMIGRATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT WEIGHS IN ON DEPORTATION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 STORMS, FLOODING, BESHEAR, MULLER, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY FLOODING DEVASTATES SOUTH & MIDWEST\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORM, TRACK, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 COMBS, TESTIMONIES, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "DIDDY" PROSECUTORS WANT TESTIMONY ABOUT ADDITIONAL ALLEGED VICTIMS\par \par \uc2 NCAA\uc2 , MARCH MADNESS, FLORIDA GATORS, CLAYTON, GOLDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLORIDA WINS NATIONAL TITLE IN COMEBACK THRILLER\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "MISSION: IMPOSSIBLE - THE FINAL RECKONING"\par \par \uc2 KONIG, ATTACK, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 RESCUE, FRANKLIN, MARTIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HERO TROOPERS RESCUE TODDLER USING HEAT-SEEKING TECHNOLOGY\par \par \uc2 DIRE WOLF, DE-EXTINCTION, SHAPIRO, KLITZMAN, JAMES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE RETURN OF THE DIRE WOLF\par \par \uc2 RICHARD, \uc2 NCAA\uc2 , MARCH MADNESS\par \par \uc2 WALL STREET, ECONOMY, TARIFFS, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WALL STREET WHIPLASH AMID TRUMP TARIFFS FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , CHINA, TARIFF, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP STANDS FIRM ON TARIFFS AMID MARKET TURMOIL\par \par \uc2 MEASLES, OUTBREAK, TEXAS, DEATH, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY MEASLES OUTBREAK GROWS\par \par \uc2 FLUORIDE, KENNEDY, HEALTH, CDC, WATER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIGHT OVER FLUORIDE IN DRINKING WATER\par \par \uc2 "MISSION IMPOSSIBLE", WALK OF FAME, COACHES\par \par \uc2 SHOPPING, MENOPAUSE, OPRAH DAILY, BOX\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, I'm going to head over this way because we're turning to some...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Working.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's do this. We're turning to some relief for perimenopausal and menopausal women. Oprah Daily consulted the nation's top experts and scoured the market to find the best sleeping aids and beauty products for their first ever Menopause O-wards. Beauty director Kate Sandoval Box joins us now with a few of the winners, and you can shop all of these products using that QR code at the bottom of your screen. It is so nice to see you, Kate.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 So nice to see you. Thank you, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, and you're really covering a really broad field here of things that people are really thinking about. We need these items.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Yes, absolutely. There's so many symptoms for menopause.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, and we begin with this. What is this right here?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEST SCALP SERUM\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Yes, so this is our Best Scalp Serum award. It goes to the Highbrow Hippie Root Replenish Active Growth Serum. It's $88. This is not just my favorite, it's also Julia Roberts and Meghan Markle's go-to.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, wonderful.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Yes, it's so easy to use. One dropper full you put on your scalp, and here's why I love it. It's water-based, so it's not going to flatten your roots. It's not going to make you feel greasy. It's safe for color-treated hair. Put it on in the morning and at night, and you're good to go.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, you're telling me hair will now grow on my hand? No, I'm just kidding.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Julia Roberts told us that her strands are stronger and growing faster since she started.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, we love to hear that, okay, Highbrow Hippie, number one.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Highbrow Hippie.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEST LIP BALM\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Number two, lip butter. You can never have enough lip moisture.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 You cannot, especially when you're going through menopause. It sucks all the moisture, heat to toe, out of you, including your lips. This is our best lip balm winner.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It smells so good.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Kari Gran Lip Whip, I swear by it. It's $24. The clear one I keep by my bed, I put on my lips at night, wake up in the morning so much more hydrated and healed. And then these beautiful tints, they're so creamy and lovely and give you just enough of color during the daytime.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, just a hint of color and feels really nice and feels very moisturizing, too, I can tell. Okay, this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEST PORTABLE FAN\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, so this is our best portable fan winner.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If you're feeling hot.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 When the hot flashes come on, this is tiny enough you keep anywhere you have, whip it out. Once it starts, it lasts for eight hours. And what we absolutely love about it, so, it's so easy to turn off, it has a phone charger in the bottom.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh perfect, so you could even charge it if you're on the road.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Yes, and if you need to charge your phone, you're ready to go.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Excellent.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 So great.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEST COOLING SHEETS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, sheets. Yes, we know that part of the key to getting a good night's sleep is to be comfortable and not overheated, which could be a big issue when you're going through menopause or perimenopause.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Exactly, right. The night sweats come on fast. So, these are our best cooling sheets. We tried a ton. We absolutely loved these.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) These work.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Slumber Cloud Performance Sheet set, so soft, so gorgeous. They have a \uc2 NASA\uc2 -created\u160\'00\'A0technology woven into the fibers, so they absorb your body temperature, hold on to it, and when your night sweat is done, and you're starting to cool off, they release that heat again.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's perfect.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 So, they keep you in this perfect sweet spot temperature all night long.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cocoon all night long. Okay, and then finally this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEST MASSAGER\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Yes, okay. So, for the body aches and pains that come with menopause, the, nothing beats the Therabody Theragun Mini. This is the new mini. It's so easy to travel with. You just push it on, it has three speeds, got to hold it for a sec, and it massages you wherever you are, best massager ever.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Kids can get involved in this, too, just, just speaking from some experience.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mommy needs some help. They have fun with that, I swear.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. Not, it's a bit of a splurge at $200, but when you need it, it's worth it.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Kate, thank you so much for all of this help. And you can go to our website and shop these products there. You can also do that at the QR code right on your screen. And Kate will be back tomorrow with winners of the Oprah Daily Bath Awards. And coming up, thank you, we're looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 KATE SANDOVAL BOX (OPRAH DAILY)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, thank you.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up, Babs Costello is here. We can't wait. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORY SYNOPSES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250605el480000n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GMA COVER STORY FORMER BACHELORETTE KATIE THURSTON SHARES UPDATE ON CANCER FIGHT\b0\par\par\uc2 731 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STORM, TORNADOES, FLOODING, TRACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER SLAMS EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, TARIFFS, POLL, ECONOMY, \uc2 LEAVITT\uc2 , PETTKE, SHEFFIELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TRUMP SET TO ANNOUNCE NEW TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, TARIFFS, ECONOMY, STOCKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STOCK MARKET REACTS TO TRUMP'S NEW TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , MUSK, SCHIMEL, CRAWFORD, ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON HIGH-STAKES ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 GARDNER, COSTA RICA, DEATH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS IN DEATH OF EX-YANKEE STAR'S SON\par \par \uc2 NEAR-COLLISION, REAGAN AIRPORT, ARREST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FAA\uc2  INVESTIGATING CLOSE CALL AT REAGAN AIRPORT\par \par \uc2 MARCH MADNESS, COLLEGE BASKETBALL, HARMON\par \par \uc2 KONIG, MURDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS ON HAWAII DOCTOR ACCUSED OF TRYING TO MURDER WIFE\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN, BREAK-IN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARMED BREAK-IN AT EX-FOOTBALL STAR'S HOME CAUGHT ON CAMERA\par \par \uc2 SPACEX\uc2 , WILLIAMS, WILMORE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HISTORIC CIVILIAN SPACEX LAUNCH\par \par \uc2 TORPEDO BAT, WILBON, LEANHARDT, CHISHOLM, PORTNOY, SMITH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIT "BAT MAN" BEHIND "TORPEDO BAT" SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 PYGMY HIPPO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AHEAD ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, READ, RETRIAL, STUDY, FASTING, WEIGHT LOSS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JURY SELECTION IN KAREN READ RETRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY ON FASTING & WEIGHT LOSS\par \par \uc2 THURSTON, "THE BACHELORETTE", CANCER, STAGE 4, ARCURI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back with our "GMA" Cover Story.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Former "Bachelorette" Katie Thurston is sharing an update on her fight with breast cancer. Eva Pilgrim is back with that story. Hey, Eva.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVER STORY: FORMER BACHELORETTE KATIE THURSTON SHARES UPDATE ON CANCER FIGHT\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning again, George. Katie Thurston only recently found out she had stage three breast cancer, now learning the cancer has spread and she in fact, has stage four. She told me she is sharing her journey in hopes that her experience helps others.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: "THE BACHELORETTE" (2021)\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 Will you accept this rose?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning former "Bachelorette" Katie Thurston sharing an update on her fight with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: INSTAGRAM / @THEKATIETHURSTON\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, I did find out today that my breast cancer has spread to the liver. It is fairly small, however that does put me at stage four.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Thurston's news comes after she recently completed a PET scan.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 It did detect some spots on my liver that were a little suspicious. Because of this I did have to then do a liver biopsy to see if my cancer has spread.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE 3 TRIPLE-POSITIVE INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This diagnosis coming just 43 days after the 34-year-old found out she had stage three triple positive invasive ductal carcinoma, breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 It's so shocking. As a 34-year-old woman, it's just, you're not prepared.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Thurston and Jeff Arcuri, who got married March 22nd, sat down with me two weeks ago, sharing what it's been like navigating her new reality.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 The last thing we just did actually was IVF. Because when you're diagnosed with breast cancer, I will likely have to do chemotherapy and that's gonna impact my ovaries and the likelihood of me being able to have children. So, we just did that.\par \par \uc2 JEFF ARCURI (THURSTON'S HUSBAND)\par \par \uc2 You did great.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Thurston says her new treatment plan will begin Friday.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 I know stage four can sound very scary and it can be, however, given that I am triple positive and the spots on my liver are fairly small and detected early, I feel very optimistic on my outcome.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And she has been really vulnerable on her social media, sharing just yesterday that she had a chemo class and found herself sobbing the second it started. Katie saying she's really trying to focus on that old saying, "live like you're dying," but says she has good days and bad days and is finding that is easier said than done at times, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, but she is clinging to that optimistic outcome.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You need to.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, you have to.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250530el410003b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION\b0\par\par\uc2 757 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 March 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY STORMS\par \par \uc2 STORMS, WINDS, HAIL, DAMAGE, RESIDENTS, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 STORMS, RAIN, TORNADO, WINDS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , TERMS, CONSTITUTION, TARIFFS, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 WALL STREET, TARIFFS, DOW, STOCKS, PRICES\par \par \uc2 MUSK, WISCONSIN, CHECKS, SUPREME COURT, CRAWFORD\par \par \uc2 EARTHQUAKE, SURVIVORS, RESCUES, DEAD\par \par \uc2 FINAL FOUR, DUKE, FLORIDA, HOUSTON, AUBURN, WOMEN'S\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW SEVERE THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 HILDEBRANDT, FRANKE, CONVICTED, ABUSE, PLEA, CLARKE\par \par \uc2 PLANE CRASH, HOUSE, TOBACMAN, \uc2 NTSB\uc2 , FIRE\par \par \uc2 COULIER, CANCER, CHEMO, "PARADE", BRODY, GRANDBABY\par \par \uc2 YANKEES\uc2 , BAT, HOMERUNS, BOONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 STORMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , TERM LIMITS\par \par \uc2 TARIFFS, WALL STREET\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERLAIN, ACTOR, "THE THORN BIRDS"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 VITALE, CANCER, BASKETBALL\par \par \uc2 CRUISE, CLOTHES, "DR. ODYSSEY"\par \par \uc2 POWELL, FAST FOOD, HEALTHY\par \par \uc2 WINFREY, MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back now with a look at Oprah's ABC News special on menopause. Kayna Whitworth sat down with the TV legend to talk about why it's so important for women to understand this part of their lives, and embrace it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OPRAH TALKS MENOPAUSE SPECIAL\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY ("THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION")\par \par \uc2 Every woman will experience menopause, but when it happens, will you be prepared?\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In a new special, Oprah empowering people to speak openly about menopause.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY ("THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION")\par \par \uc2 I didn't have night twits and I didn't have hot flashes, but I had never heard that heart palpitations was a symptom of menopause. I wanted to do this because when I was going through it, there was nothing. There was nobody. I felt literally like I was going to die every single night.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The queen of daytime TV says her menopause symptoms were missed by doctors.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY ("THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION")\par \par \uc2 For two years straight, y'all, I did not sleep. I picked up a book. The pages opened to palpitations, your heart's wakeup call. That was the first time that I learned that those were symptoms that the change was coming. One time my heart rate was 212 beats per minute, because I was in an AFIB heart palpitation situation. And nobody ever once said, this is a symptom of menopause. I really thought I was going to die, and was always surprised that I woke up. The worst part for me, this is when I knew I was really in trouble, is when I couldn't concentrate reading.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY ("THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION")\par \par \uc2 I mean, I'm really gonna tear up because I love reading so much. I let go of the book club because I could not concentrate when I was reading. I could no longer finish a book. And that is the real reason why, 'cause I could no longer concentrate enough to finish a book. And I brought it back when I started taking estrogen, because I could concentrate again. But I never shared that before that the real reason I had to stop it is because I could no longer concentrate. That makes me want to cry.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENOPAUSE SIDE EFFECTS\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And while side effects like hot flashes and mood changes are well known, other symptoms like brain fog are sometimes overlooked. With this special, Oprah hoping to empower other women to advocate for themselves so they can get the best treatment that works for them.\par \par \uc2 GUEST ("THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION")\par \par \uc2 I feel like in this country I call it the anthem of anti. Anti-aging, anti-bloat. We try to avoid everything.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY ("THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION")\par \par \uc2 But you can't outfox it. It's gonna catch up to you. If you have a uterus, bless us all, it's going to happen to you.\par \par \uc2 GUEST ("THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION")\par \par \uc2 What is maybe the one thing you wish you had known?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY ("THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION")\par \par \uc2 My God, I wish I had known it was a brain thing and that everything starts with the brain. And that because it affects every cell in your body, the first time you have any of the symptoms, that's when you need to go find a doctor who will listen to you, hear you, and take action for you.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For "Good Morning America," Kayna Whitworth, ABC News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There's so much great information in this special. It is called "An Oprah Winfrey Special: The Menopause Revolution." It airs tonight 10:00 Eastern and Pacific on ABC and tomorrow on \uc2 Disney\uc2 + and \uc2 Hulu\uc2 . Right now we go to Ginger.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250603el3v00069\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY THE MENOPAUSE REVOLUTION\b0\par\par\uc2 1,889 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 March 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 MENOPAUSE, "WINFREY", WATTS, BERRY, RESPIN, MEDIA, HOLLYWOOD, FADAL, BERMAN\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We begin tonight with the subject of, for millions of women is often shrouded in darkness, menopause. But now, media mogul Oprah Winfrey, together with big name celebrities, are sharing their personal journeys to bring this taboo subject into the light. Here's ABC's Kayna Whitworth.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 Every woman will experience menopause. But when it happens, will you be prepared?\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Oprah Winfrey is ready to start a revolution about menopause.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 For two years straight, y'all, I did not sleep one time. One time, my heart rate was 212 beats per minute, and I had been to three different doctors at that point, and nobody ever once said this is a symptom of menopause. We bring you today.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP: ON LOCATION: FORSYTH COUNTY, 1987\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Oprah has dedicated much of her career to normalizing conversations about taboo topics no one else would touch, and getting America to listen.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM THE OPRAH WINFREY SHOW\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 Today.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) From weight loss and sexual assault.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 There really is no darker secret. Ooh, what happened?\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To AIDS and homophobia.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 So, did you tell your family you had AIDS?\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE MEMBER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Yes I did.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM AN OPRAH SPECIAL\par \par \uc2 GUEST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Perimenopause begins when...\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Oprah is now turning her focus to women, aging and the stigma of menopause, with a long list of surprising symptoms that can impact your brain, even your heart.\par \par \uc2 NAOMI WATTS (OSCAR-NOMINATED ACTOR & AUTHOR)\par \par \uc2 We were getting down to business, and I remembered that I had my estrogen patch on.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And she's doing it with some of Hollywood's biggest stars.\par \par \uc2 MARIA SHRIVER (JOURNALIST & FORMER FIRST LADY OF CALIFORNIA)\par \par \uc2 We need a radical shift about women's aging.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARIA SHRIVER (JOURNALIST & FORMER FIRST LADY OF CALIFORNIA)\par \par \uc2 Right? In this culture.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 The reason why I wanted to have this conversation. My friend Maria Shriver and I started talking about how there was no information for us and how we should do something to let women know that this was going on with their bodies. What has been the thing that actually has surprised you most about this phase in your life?\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 Nobody brought it up to me, ever. I thought I was going to skip it.\par \par \uc2 MOVIE CLIP FROM DIE ANOTHER DAY\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Halle Berry is a Hollywood sex symbol. Her resume includes "Catwoman," Storm in the "X-Men," and even a "Bond" girl. Now, at 58 years old, she's an advocate for menopause awareness, launching Respin, a platform that provides information and resources. She started Respin because, she says a doctor first misdiagnosed her menopause symptoms as herpes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 Something happens to you emotionally, too.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 Does it not?\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 For me, I had no concern about what anybody thought anymore. And there was a liberation that came with that, right?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HALLE BERRY (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 And a liberation when I figured out that I was in perimenopause, and I knew that I didn't have to stay stuck in this very scary, very alone, rageful place, then I felt the power. I said, wow, I feel like I'm the best me I've ever been.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Likewise, Naomi Watts, the Oscar-nominated actress known for her roles in "King Kong," "Mulholland Drive" and "The Impossible," was only in her 30s when she started experiencing symptoms.\par \par \uc2 NAOMI WATTS (OSCAR-NOMINATED ACTOR & AUTHOR)\par \par \uc2 At 36, when I was trying to have a baby, he took some bloodwork and the results came back and suggested that I was, quote-unquote, "close to menopause."\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Watts started the wellness brand Stripes Beauty, which offers products specifically designed for menopause.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM POST/INSTAGRAM\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA CONTENT PROVIDER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Menopause hotline.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The whole focus comes at a time when menopause is having a real moment, with celebrities like Gwyneth Paltrow and Drew Barrymore opening up publicly and sharing their own symptoms.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE ("THE DREW BARRYMORE SHOW")\par \par \uc2 I think I'm having my first hot flash.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM NOV 2018/\uc2 FACEBOOK\par \par \uc2 GWYNETH PALTROW (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 I can feel the hormonal shifts happening, the sweating, the moods. I think menopause gets a really bad rap and needs a bit of a rebranding.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Comedians like Chelsea Handler trading jokes for advice.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM DEAR CHELSEA PODCAST/IHEART\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA HANDLER (PODCAST HOST/"DEAR CHELSEA")\par \par \uc2 This is a part of your life. This isn't the end of your life and there is more to be excited about after...\par \par \uc2 CALLER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA HANDLER (PODCAST HOST/"DEAR CHELSEA")\par \par \uc2 ...after you're done with menopause and quite possibly during the whole process of menopause.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM THE VIEW\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And the ladies of "The View" taking on this literal hot topic.\par \par \uc2 GUEST ("THE VIEW")\par \par \uc2 When I talk about it.\par \par \uc2 JOY BEHAR ("THE VIEW")\par \par \uc2 I'll never be done. I'm one of those people that still has them.\par \par \uc2 GUEST ("THE VIEW")\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GUEST ("THE VIEW") (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JOY BEHAR ("THE VIEW")\par \par \uc2 I'll be in a casket getting a hot flash.\par \par \uc2 GUEST ("THE VIEW")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR/"HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 It's been something that has been really hush hush, very taboo. Something not talked about, something really wrapped up in ageism, which is a really sad thing. There's still so many women out there that need help. One billion women globally are in menopause this year.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Roughly half the human population will go into menopause during their lifetime, when the natural decline of reproductive hormones can drastically impact both physical and mental health. Understanding how the process happens also means empowering women to advocate for themselves.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER BERMAN (MENOPAUSE SPECIALIST AND SEXUAL HEALTH EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 The key is catching it before you get to the point where you're, like, in, in a crisis. There are treatments available now.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What is maybe the one thing you learned that you wish you had known?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 Oh my God. I wish that my mother, my aunts, Maya Angelou, anybody had had a conversation with me about what was to come. I also wish that I had known that it affected every cell in the body. And I wish I'd known that it was a major brain thing. The first time you have any of the symptoms, that's when you need to go find a doctor who will listen to you, hear you, and take action for you.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Journalist Tamsen Fadal learned just how debilitating menopause symptoms can be in the most public way possible.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR/"HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 I was doing my job anchoring the news. We were in a commercial break, and I felt this intense heat flare up from the inside. I felt my heart racing out of control where I could hear it in my ears, and I thought, I'm going to fall over. And that's when I went in search of some answers. And I got that note in my patient portal from my doctor that told me I was in menopause, and it just had those four words, in menopause, any questions?\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Yep, she had questions, and realizing there was so little information out there, prompted her to go find some answers and write a book about it.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR/"HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 I didn't know all the symptoms I'd been going through for years before, were perimenopause, because we never had that word. And so, I had had heavy cycles. I had a lot of stress. I had really dark days, sleepless nights, gaining weight, all these hallmark symptoms of perimenopause that no one had ever told me about.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doctor Jennifer Berman is an expert in menopause and sexual health.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER BERMAN (MENOPAUSE SPECIALIST AND SEXUAL HEALTH EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 The lack of sleep, the moodiness, the fatigue, the joint pain, the low libido, all of that can be hormonally related.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doctors explained that menopause actually starts with perimenopause when the hormones start to fluctuate, that can create a host of symptoms.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) For a long time, not only was menopause put a bit on the back burner, but certainly perimenopause, absolutely overlooked.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER BERMAN (MENOPAUSE SPECIALIST AND SEXUAL HEALTH EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 Overlooked, neglected, ignored and in my opinion, mismanaged. Women were treated for depression, anxiety and put on Prozac or, you know, an antidepressant instead of hormones. And so, by the time women are in their mid to late 40s, early 50s, they're fat, tired, irritable, moody, jacked up on, you know, Adderall, diet pills, mood stabilizers. Not addressing these symptoms in the perimenopause is, in my opinion, a public health risk and concern.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says not enough doctors are trained in treating menopause.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER BERMAN (MENOPAUSE SPECIALIST AND SEXUAL HEALTH EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 Women deserve to, you know, to live happier, healthier, vital, sexually fulfilled lives into our 70s and 80s, just like men are.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You give me a very optimistic sense here that women can have it all.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER BERMAN (MENOPAUSE SPECIALIST AND SEXUAL HEALTH EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 They can. We're close. We're getting there. It starts with awareness.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With this special, Oprah is hoping to empower other women to advocate for themselves so they can get the best treatment that works the best for them.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And just from the conversations I heard from the doctors in the audience and after this, the revolution has started. You have started the revolution.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 Yes! The revolution has started.\par \par \uc2 KAYNA WHITWORTH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The conversations will be different from here on out because of what you've done.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (MEDIA MOGUL)\par \par \uc2 I do hope so. I've been talking about it since I was 48 and I'm now 71. Every woman needs to decide for herself what works for her. But what I really want people to know is that you don't have to suffer through it.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Our thanks to Kayna. And you can watch "An Oprah Winfrey Special: The Menopause Revolution," streaming tomorrow on \uc2 Hulu\uc2  and \uc2 Disney\uc2 -plus. Coming up, Ruby Franke. Why is her codefendant back in court challenging her conviction? Plus, "Stranger Things" star Finn Wolfhard is adding a new title to his already impressive resume, "Director."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUBY FRANKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "STRANGER THINGS"\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020250403el3v00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SHOP THIS BOOK TAMSEN FADAL TALKS "HOW TO MENOPAUSE"\b0\par\par\uc2 1,268 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 March 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE & FLOODS\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, FLOODING, THUNDERSTORMS\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, WIND, RESIDENTS, EVACUATIONS, STEIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS ON ALERT AS WILDFIRES RAGE IN CAROLINAS\par \par \uc2 EARTHQUAKE, MISSING, RESCUES, CIVIL WAR\par \par \uc2 GREENLAND, VANCE, TRUMP, CRITICISM, INDEPENDENT\par \par \uc2 HHS\uc2  , LAYOFFS, WORKERS, BUYOUTS, CDC\par \par \uc2 KING CHARLES, HOSPITALIZED, CANCER, OBSERVATION\par \par \uc2 IMMIGRATION, STUDENTS, ICE, DEPORTATIONS, RUBIO, CHEN\par \par \uc2 TOURNAMENT, SWEET 16, TEXAS TECH, ALABAMA, DUKE, FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 KONIG, CLIFF, ATTEMPTED MURDER, COURT, BAIL\par \par \uc2 FRYE FEST , MCFARLAND, CONVICTED, MEXICO, TICKETS\par \par \uc2 23 ABD ME, DNA, DATA, JOHNSON, FAMILIES, MEDICAL\par \par \uc2 BASEBALL, OPENING DAY, DODGES, \uc2 YANKEES\par \par \uc2 FLASH, FLOOD, EMERGENCY, SOUTHERN, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 EARTHQUAKE, THAILAND, MYANMAR\par \par \uc2 CRUISE, PASSENGER, STROKES, AIRLIFTED\par \par \uc2 PAUL, "PAUL AMERICAN"\par \par \uc2 FADAL, "HOW TO MENOPAUSE"\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) From the outer limits of the manosphere to menopause. This is morning television. And for more than one billion women currently in menopause, there is a new book, "How to Menopause: Take Charge of Your Health, Reclaim Your Life and Feel Even Better Than Before." An author and award-winning, Emmy award winning journalist Tamsen Fadal is here to tell us all about the book that Halle Barry calls the menopause bible. Welcome. It is so great to see you, Tamsen.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOP THIS BOOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAMSEN FADAL TALKS "HOW TO MENOPAUSE"\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You've really started this conversation in so many ways that we weren't having. And part of it started with you. You, you weren't really sure what was going on when it began for you?\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I had no idea. I had never heard the word perimenopause. And menopause seemed like something that was far off. I was a news anchor in New York City, and I was doing the news, and I wound up getting a very intense hot flash, and my heart racing, and I ended up on the floor of the ladies bathroom during a live broadcast.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And what did you think was happening?\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 I had no idea. You know, I, my heart was the concern because it was racing. I thought, what is going on with me? Am I having a heart attack? Is this a panic attack? What is it? I went doctor to doctor after that, and I wound up getting a note in my patient portal that had four words in it. In menopause, any questions? That was it. That was the diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That was it?\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 That's how I found out what was going on, and I haven't stopped asking questions about it.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and I think it's those questions, we were just talking off camera...\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...the importance and why you're doing this. You want women to advocate for themselves.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 We have to. You know, doctors to their own admission don't get a lot of training on this in medical school. So, women walk in, they feel like they're not seen. They feel dismissed. They feel confused. And it's not okay for women to go anywhere from four to ten years feeling shame, and frustration, and, and not feeling right.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And some of those symptoms of, of not feeling right, and perimenopause, for example, what are we looking for?\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, let's talk about that, because I, I think it's really, really important. I wanted to write "How to Menopause" because we needed a girlfriend's guide to do it. Right? I'm not a doctor, I'm a journalist. But if you look at some of these symptoms that start, they can be four to ten years before you actually hit menopause.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a long time.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 It's a really long time. And we're at the height of our career, so we're talking about changes in our periods. We're talking about low libido, or painful sex, which sometimes is embarrassing to talk about. We're talking about brain fog, which is where you're talking and all of a sudden you forget. I couldn't have had this conversation with you five years ago. We're talking about weight changes, sleep disturbances, even body odor. Body odor changes. It can be real embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, and it's, you mentioned at the high of our career. That is part of the complexity in all of this because you're thriving in some ways and then you're also just trying to get by in others.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 It's so true. You're trying to get by, you're trying to go through the day, you're juggling a lot, whether you're juggling kids, or parents, or changes in your relationship, or your job. And now you're going sleepless nights and not feeling like yourself. And I hear from one woman after another saying, I just don't feel like myself anymore.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. So, we, we have a number of viewer questions, but one I want to get to is how can perimenopausal and menopausal women manage stress?\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Oh, it's, it's such a good question. Perimenopause can start in your 30s, early 40s, and go for quite a while. And so stress management is so important in all of this. I mean, one of the big things that I always talk about is the fact that you want to give yourself permission to know what's going on and have a plan, a menopause plan. That's why I want to get out there and educate and teach as many women as possible so they know what to expect because it's so important.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Once you have that plan, this is so simple, but take a walk. I don't mean a 30 minute walk, an hour walk. I'm saying, like, get up and walk away if you are feeling overwhelmed. Irritability and rage can be two of those big symptoms during this time. It can be really, really frustrating. And then I would say remind yourself it's gonna be okay, because it is. I promise there is really a lot of hope and excitement on the other side of this. We have a lot of amazing years. We have another half of our life after this. And finally, if you meditate, or do yoga, or have some kind of practice like that, I think that's really important to help bring those stress levels down.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tamsen, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You are living proof that there's better years ahead.\par \par \uc2 TAMSEN FADAL (AUTHOR OF "HOW TO MENOPAUSE")\par \par \uc2 I promise. We're gonna get everybody there. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, "How to Menopause" is available now, and you can catch Tamsen on the Oprah Winfrey special, "The Menopause Revolution." It's on Monday night right here on ABC.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250425el3s0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MORE WOMEN GOING UNDER THE KNIFE MENOPAUSE MAKEOVERS\b0\par\par\uc2 666 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON THE DEFENSE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , SECURITY, SIGNAL, WALTZ, HEGSETH, GOLDBERG\par \par \uc2 HEARING, CONCERNS, SOCIAL SECURITY, STAFFING, EMPLOYEES\par \par \uc2 VANCE, GREENLAND, CONTROVERSIAL, TRUMP, SECURITY\par \par \uc2 EVACUATIONS, WILDFIRES, WINDS, DAMAGE, WARNINGS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, VIDEO, DEAD, INJURED, STORY\par \par \uc2 CONSUMERS, CONFIDENCE, ECONOMY, TARIFFS, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 SPORTS, WOMEN, BOOM, BILLIONS, WNBA, MERCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WESTERN RECORD WARMTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GARDNER, MILLER, DEATH, TIMELINE, WIDMER\par \par \uc2 BANKRUPTCY, DNA, DATA, CUSTOMERS, DELETE\par \par \uc2 UNIV OF MICHIGAN, DATA, CHARGES, WEISS, VICTIMS, STINAR\par \par \uc2 JAILED, TIMESHARE, DISPUTE, AKEO, HULL, MANLY\par \par \uc2 BASEBALL, SMITH, ASTROS, MOTHER,\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 CONSUMER, CONFIDENCE, ECONOMY, TARIFFS, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 KONIG, DOCTOR, ARRESTED, ALLEGED, MURDER, ATTEMPT, WIFE, HAWAII\par \par \uc2 POSTPARTUM, DEPRESSION, EXERCISE\par \par \uc2 AFFLECK, CLOONEY, "GOOD NIGHT AND GOOD LUCK", CHESNEY, BROWN, CASH\par \par \uc2 ANNA FRANCESE GASS\par \par \uc2 MENOPAUSE, MAKEOVER\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're back now with the growing trend of menopause makeovers. Eva Pilgrim is back with how women are finding confidence later in life. Good morning, Eva.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE WOMEN GOING UNDER THE KNIFE FOR MENOPAUSE MAKEOVERS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Lara. Most women enter menopause somewhere between 45 and 55. Long gone are the days where 50 looks or feels like a golden girl. And that shift is showing up at the plastic surgeon's office, with more women coming in for what's being called menopause makeovers.\par \par \uc2 ANN NAVALLO (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 After having three children, gaining a lot of weight, losing a lot of weight, and then going through menopause, which was the biggest culprit, just wasn't happy with the way I looked.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Six years after losing her husband, 65-year-old Ann Navallo was reentering the dating scene when she decided to undergo a breast augmentation.\par \par \uc2 ANN NAVALLO (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 One of the best things I have ever did. I gained so much from it. Confidence. It really kind of empowered me.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Navallo just one of the growing number of women undergoing menopause makeovers.\par \par \uc2 DR. SMITA RAMANADHAM (BOARD-CERTIFIED PLASTIC SURGEON)\par \par \uc2 In my practice, a lot of face lifts, neck lifts, breast reductions, actually, which is a bit different than we see with mommy makeovers. Tummy tucks, liposuction of stubborn areas of fat.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) New Jersey plastic surgeon, Dr. Smita Ramanadham is used to seeing women for mommy makeovers. Now she's noticing a new trend, an uptick in menopausal patients. The \uc2 American Society of Plastic Surgeons\uc2  saying that vast majority of patients undergoing cosmetic procedures are between the ages of 40 to 69.\par \par \uc2 DR. SMITA RAMANADHAM (BOARD-CERTIFIED PLASTIC SURGEON)\par \par \uc2 Seeing this shift where patients are older and they're just more aware of the changes that their bodies are undergoing and that there is something that they can do to address some of those changes so that they feel their best.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Menopause occurs in most women between 45 and 55 years old and can have a variety of symptoms, including hot flashes, mood changes, and visible changes like hair thinning, wrinkles, and hormonal weight gain.\par \par \uc2 DR. SMITA RAMANADHAM (BOARD-CERTIFIED PLASTIC SURGEON)\par \par \uc2 A 50-year-old today is much different than a 50-year-old decades ago. People are a lot more active, so they want their bodies to sort of fit how they feel.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As for Anne Navallo, she's thrilled with her results and excited for the future.\par \par \uc2 ANN NAVALLO (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I think I kind of reinvented myself and highly recommend it.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Ann told us to be clear, this isn't something she thinks you need to feel empowered, but she says for her, who spent so much of her life focused on being a mom and a wife, that this was something she really wanted to do for herself. And she is, as she mentioned, really happy with her results, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Eva Pilgrim for us. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250421el3q0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FORMER "BACHELORETTE" KATIE THURSTON ON HER BREAST CANCER FIGHT KATIE THURSTON ON BREAST CANCER TREATMENT & PREGNANCY HOPES\b0\par\par\uc2 922 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 March 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOMECOMING\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2 , ASTRONAUTS, EARTH, STICH, WILLIAMS, \uc2 SPACEX\par \par \uc2 STORMS, WIND, DRY, DUST, ALERTS, ACCIDENTS, WILDFIRES\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS, REBUKE, TRUMP, FALLOUT, DEPORTATIONS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , PUTIN, UKRAINE, CEASEFIRE, ATTACKS, ENERGY\par \par \uc2 TESLA, PROTESTS, ARSON, SPRAY PAINT, MUSK, DOJ\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY, ASSASSINATION, UPDEGROVE, FILES, CIA\par \par \uc2 MARCH MADNESS, NCAA, UPSETS, MADLOCK, MEN'S, WOMEN'S\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE DANGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SPRING BREAK, MISSING, KONANKI, RIIBE, DROWNED, COURT\par \par \uc2 PADDLE BOARDERS, TEENS, RESCUE, TUMMOND, COON, PAULING, JEFFRIES\par \par \uc2 SALES, SPRING, AMAZON, DEALS, GARDENING, TARIFFS\par \par \uc2 SLOTH, PUP, SAN DIEGO ZOO\par \par \uc2 ASTRONAUTS, RETURN, WILLIAMS, WILMORE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY, VISA, TREATMENT\par \par \uc2 JOANN FABRICS, SCAM\par \par \uc2 THURSTON, "BACHELORETTE", BREAST, CANCER\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Busy here in Times Square. And we're back with our "GMA" cover story. Former "Bachelorette" Katie Thurston on her breast cancer fight. She sat down with Eva Pilgrim. Hello, Eva.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER "BACHELORETTE" KATIE THURSTON ON HER BREAST CANCER FIGHT\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, again, George. Katie Thurston says this is something she just didn't see coming. There's no history of breast cancer in her family. She was happy, newly engaged, and in a moment, she says everything she had planned and hoped for in the next year shifted.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP OF "THE BACHELORETTE"\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 Thank you. Nice to meet you.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Former "Bachelorette" Katie Thurston opening up about her recent breast cancer diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 It was the worst feeling I ever felt. You're just devastated. You're so devastated. It's so shocking. As a 34-year-old woman, it's just, you're not prepared.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says it all started when she noticed a small lump, but initially she didn't think anything of it.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 When I felt my lump, it was, it was sore. And as women, we're used to being told, like, oh, it's, it's probably your time of the month, or oh, it's from working out, so I brushed it off for a while. And then finally I decided to go to the doctor.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In February, Katie was diagnosed with stage three triple positive ductal carcinoma.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 Because of the size of my tumor, I have to do chemo first to shrink it down, and then I can have it then removed, either one side, both sides. That's a conversation I don't even know yet.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It just feels like a lot of things you've had to decide very, very quickly.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 You're on, like, survival mode. Like, you don't have a choice. You, you show or you die. And I hate to say it that way, but, like, you just, every day you have an appointment. Every day you have to show up. You don't have a choice.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The diagnosis coming just months after getting engaged to comedian Jeff Arcuri.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You just got engaged.\par \par \uc2 JEFF ARCURI (COMEDIAN AND KATIE THURSTON'S FIANC\u201\'00\'C9)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 And we thought we'd be planning a wedding. We thought we'd be trying for a baby. And instead, we, we know that this year is really dedicated to just treatment and getting better.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Arcuri canceling his world tour to be by her side.\par \par \uc2 JEFF ARCURI (COMEDIAN AND KATIE THURSTON'S FIANC\u201\'00\'C9)\par \par \uc2 I texted them. I'm like, there's no way. I couldn't see you doing anything alone in this.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 They say laughter is medicine, and...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KATIE THURSTON ON BREAST CANCER TREATMENT & PREGNANCY HOPES\par \par \uc2 JEFF ARCURI (COMEDIAN AND KATIE THURSTON'S FIANC\u201\'00\'C9)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 ...I will say, him being my partner, I, I really couldn't have asked for someone better by my side.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM INSTAGRAM THEKATIETHURSTON\par \par \uc2 JEFF ARCURI (COMEDIAN AND KATIE THURSTON'S FIANC\u201\'00\'C9)\par \par \uc2 There we go. Great job, baby.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Katie preventively harvesting her eggs following her diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 You know, we've done everything we can to be, you know, proactive for our future and our family plans. But in the event that IVF didn't work out, in the event that chemo did impact me in a, in a negative way when it comes to my reproductive health, we're also okay being a childless couple.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What is it that you, you think is important that people take away when they see your story?\par \par \uc2 KATIE THURSTON (FORMER "BACHELORETTE")\par \par \uc2 I think that's the biggest take away is, is telling people, like don't wait. You know? Be proactive. Get checked out. You could be doing yourself a favor in the future.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Katie told me that before this she really thought she would just elope. She wasn't into a big wedding and all the big planning that that requires, but now she's looking forward to the day, one day, when she's done with her treatment, when she can plan a big wedding and celebrate the beauty of life and love with all her people. Linsey?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we look forward to celebrating along with her, Eva. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250416el3j0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FULL BODY SCANS CELEBRITIES AND THE WEALTHY PAYING THOUSANDS FOR ACCESS TO LATEST HEALTH TECH\b0\par\par\uc2 2,675 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 BODY SCAN, JENNER, LACY, KAMAL, JOHN, JOHNSON, PATRICK\par \par \uc2 KRIS JENNER (REALITY STAR)\par \par \uc2 This is such a big scan because it reveals so much.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) From the Kardashians' matriarch herself...\par \par \uc2 KRIS JENNER (REALITY STAR)\par \par \uc2 I'm just excited to get my results.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...to GOOP wellness guru Gwyneth Paltrow posting on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: INSTAGRAM\par \par \uc2 GWYNETH PALTROW (ACTOR/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 (inaudible) to \uc2 Prenuvo\uc2  for my full body scan.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...celebrities with access to the best health tech out there are paying thousands of dollars out of pocket for full body medical scans, just for a peek at what's under their skin.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Former bachelorette Ali Fedotowsky and husband Kevin Manno sharing on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: INSTAGRAM\par \par \uc2 ALI FEDOTOWSKY-MANNO (REALITY STAR)\par \par \uc2 Kevin has thyroid cancer...\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MANNO (TV SHOW HOST)\par \par \uc2 I do.\par \par \uc2 ALI FEDOTOWSKY-MANNO (REALITY STAR)\par \par \uc2 ...and had zero symptoms before we found out.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The biggest players in elective body scans, \uc2 Prenuvo\uc2  and Ezra, claiming to help detect disease before symptoms even arise, sharing compelling patient testimonials.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: TESTIMONIAL\par \par \uc2 PATIENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 The feedback from the medical team was, you've caught this so early that your chances of a cure are probably about 80%.\par \par \uc2 PATIENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 So, for my 50th birthday, my husband was like, do you want to go to Paris? And I was like, "I want to go to \uc2 Prenuvo\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Could they revolutionize the future of healthcare?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW LACY (FOUNDER & CEO OF PRENUVO)\par \par \uc2 We're comprehensively looking through every organ of the body to screen for cancer, aneurysms and 500 other conditions that we cover.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRITICAL STATEMENTS FROM MEDICAL INSTITUTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "...NO DOCUMENTED EVIDENCE...EFFECTIVE IN PROLONGING LIFE..."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "LETTER FROM THE PRESIDENT: HEALTHCARE TOO MUCH AND NOT ENOUGH!"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "...MENTAL ANGUISH...HIGH RATES OF FALSE POSITIVES..."\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But not everybody's buying into the hype. Some of America's most prominent medical institutions are criticizing these popular scans, saying "there's no documented evidence body scans are effective in prolonging life" and that the risks include "mental anguish and high rates of false positives." But with alarming headlines about rising cancer rates, some people are looking beyond the recommended screening tests for answers.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR ARIF KAMAL (CHEF PATIENT OFFICER AT AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 This is exactly what we need to be talking about, because the underlying motivation for this I completely understand. People are seeing things that we're not catching in other ways and they're saying there's gotta be a different or better way. But the problem is the science actually hasn't caught up yet with the worry.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Even shrewd entrepreneur Daymond John, sharing his experience.\par \par \uc2 DAYMOND JOHN (FOUNDER & CEO OF THE SHARK GROUP)\par \par \uc2 I already got back all, like, all these images. Health is wealth and I don't have any investment. I don't know anything about the company.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He's the founder of iconic lifestyle brand FUBU...\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: FUBU ADVERTISEMENT\par \par \uc2 DAYMOND JOHN (FOUNDER & CEO OF THE SHARK GROUP)\par \par \uc2 FUBU, NBA, 99, FUBU.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: "SHARK TANK"\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Tonight on "Shark Tank."\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...and one of the original judges of ABC's "Shark Tank."\par \par \uc2 DAYMOND JOHN (FOUNDER & CEO OF THE SHARK GROUP)\par \par \uc2 I am offering you 200,000 for 20% and then we'll just get to work.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Eight years ago, this father of three beat thyroid cancer and since then has become hyper health conscious.\par \par \uc2 DAYMOND JOHN (FOUNDER & CEO OF THE SHARK GROUP)\par \par \uc2 I started going on this journey for longevity and to constantly stay on top of my health, the same way I stay on top of my businesses. I keep trying to monitor and do various things. I'm consciously trying to find better ways to bio-hack my life and my body.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: CELL PHONE VIDEO\par \par \uc2 DAYMOND JOHN (FOUNDER & CEO OF THE SHARK GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Alright, here goes the machine.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Beyond cancer checkups every six months, the 55-year-old now also includes full body magnetic resonance imaging, or MRI scans to his annual routine.\par \par \uc2 DAYMOND JOHN (FOUNDER & CEO OF THE SHARK GROUP)\par \par \uc2 I don't know if cancer's anywhere else in my body, this is a second level or a second layer of what I think is insurance to me.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That extra layer can be a double edged sword.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: SOCIAL MEDI APOST\par \par \uc2 DAYMOND JOHN (FOUNDER & CEO OF THE SHARK GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Boom. Aneurysm. I see something that says here, you know, "This may represent a small something, baker's cyst." All I saw in there was aneurysm in my leg. I started freaking out.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Out two years ago his scan came back with the finding of a possible aneurysm.\par \par \uc2 DAYMOND JOHN (FOUNDER & CEO OF THE SHARK GROUP)\par \par \uc2 No, I was very scared because I'm not a doctor. I don't know what that means, but it concerned me enough to make sure I got a check.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A follow up biopsy revealed it was actually benign, which, by all measures, is the best outcome. But it's the fear of the unknown that critics of elective scans point to as potentially stress-inducing, costly and unnecessary with follow up testing.\par \par \uc2 DAYMOND JOHN (FOUNDER & CEO OF THE SHARK GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Was it worth getting? Absolutely. 120%. I'm gonna have a \uc2 Prenuvo\uc2  scan every year, because now I even have something to compare it against.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Until recently, MRIs were only available in medical settings, requiring a referral from a doctor and were done for specific reasons. MRIs don't use radiation like CT scans. A serial tech entrepreneur, Andrew Lacy, is the founder of \uc2 Prenuvo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANDREW LACY (FOUNDER & CEO OF PRENUVO)\par \par \uc2 We have a healthcare system that is reactive, that finds disease late and because it finds it late, it causes a lot of cost to the healthcare system and worse outcomes to patients. We want to reinvent the way that we do healthcare.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAP OF UNITED STATES AND PRENUVO CLINIC LOCATIONS\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Prenuvo\uc2  has quickly expanded to 17 clinics and is on track to open 14 more.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORSO SCAN $999\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FULL-BODY SCAN $4,500\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Starting at $999 for a torso scan, up to $4,500 for the whole body, the prices are steep, not covered by insurance and yet, there's an even pricier option.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "BLUEPRINT" $5,995\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At nearly $6,000, Ezra, \uc2 Prenuvo\uc2 's main competitor, says they're struggling to meet the demand for their high scan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN JOHNSON (TECH MILLIONAIRE)\par \par \uc2 MRIs are wonderful because it allows you to diagnose things with great precision.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In a collaboration with bio-hacker Bryan Johnson, the tech millionaire who famously spends two million dollars a year on his blueprint quest to extend his longevity...\par \par \uc2 BRYAN JOHNSON (TECH MILLIONAIRE)\par \par \uc2 People who are like myself, who really enjoy the process of being proactive and measuring things, will find this scan to be very helpful.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE WORLD'S MOST ADVANCED LONGEVITY SCAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DESIGNED WITH BRYAN JOHNSON: EZRA BLUEPRINT SCAN\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...recently released Ezra blueprint is an MRI and CT scan combo.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN JOHNSON (TECH MILLIONAIRE)\par \par \uc2 The most important thing for somebody to know is, is there a code red happening right now inside their body that they don't know about?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 6% OF PATIENTS FOUND POTENTIAL CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EZRA HAS HELPED 6% OF OUR MEMBERS IDENTIFY POTENTIAL CANCER EARLY.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRENUVO: PUT YOUR HEALTH IN YOUR HANDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2.5-5%: ALERTED TO A LIFE THREATENING CONDITION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 49%: SOMETHING TO MONITOR\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Ezra claims that six percent of their patients have found potential cancer. \uc2 Prenuvo\uc2  stating that between two and a half to five percent of their patients have been alerted to a life threatening condition and 49% to something to monitor.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR ARIF KAMAL (CHEF PATIENT OFFICER AT AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 The reality is, while we might feel comforted that we've had a whole body MRI and it was clean or negative, it does not completely say that there's not a cancer in the body because of the limitations of what it can see.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) There are no current guidelines for interpreting cancer risk for asymptomatic people who elect for the whole body MRI scans. Experts acknowledge this is an area that needs future research.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 I used to be a professional dancer. I was a Rockette for 13 years. That was me. And I now am a director of a small family-based construction company.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Forty-six-year-old Danielle Patrick is a mom of three living in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 I heard about this scan that I can do and it just became a really important thing for me to get done. I have been a single mom who has been financially 100% responsible for three children. I don't buy myself new clothes and new handbags. But for me, this is where I want to spend my money.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 You want to go forward, not sideways.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Divorced for ten years and engaged to be married, her motivation is her family.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 So, it is the most important thing in my life to be there for my children as long as possible and in the best case scenario, be there for my kids' kids.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 Hi.\par \par \uc2 CHILD (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Hey.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Danielle has annual checkups and normal routine mammograms, the gold standard for breast cancer screening. But she says that's not enough for her own peace of mind. She's aware this type of test doesn't rule out breast cancer, but she wants to know even more about what else there could be lurking. She lost her mom, Mary, to breast cancer at age 53.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 She was my best friend. She was my everything. She was amazing. I wish that she had gotten scanned, and I do believe she would still be here if she had. And I don't want to ever put my children in that position to lose me. And she will forever be my driving force. And I know she'd be super proud of me.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's the day of her scan. Danielle and her fianc\u233\'00\'E9, Jeff, make their way to New York City.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 This is it. Thanks, babe.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The moment she's been waiting for.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 We are.\par \par \uc2 JEFF (DANIELLE'S FIANC\u201\'00\'C9)\par \par \uc2 We are.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 Hi. How are you?\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A quick change and she's off to the scanner.\par \par \uc2 SCAN TECH (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We're gonna come to this section over here...\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 Okay.\par \par \uc2 SCAN TECH (MALE)\par \par \uc2 ...before we begin a couple of safety questions, okay?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 Okay.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Then a little distraction\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 SCAN TECH (MALE)\par \par \uc2 What would you like to watch today, or listen to? Up to you.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...to help her get through the 45-minute head-to-torso scan.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 Let's put on the new "Squid Game."\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The MRI taking images, often called slices, of her main organs, bones, tissues and blood vessels.\par \par \uc2 SCAN TECH (MALE)\par \par \uc2 You do a little cutting here and just leave what's important.\par \par \uc2 SCAN TECH (MALE)\par \par \uc2 You did great.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The test, now over.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 Just glad to be out. It was just, yeah, tight in there. It's super loud.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 Glad it's over.\par \par \uc2 JEFF (DANIELLE'S FIANC\u201\'00\'C9)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, the hardest part, waiting for her results.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 It's been just over a week since I had the scan. It's felt like a very long week. Everyone knows that I'm getting the results today, so they're also a little bit nervous.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR VIKASH MODI (PRENUVO MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 Hello.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We're on a zoom with Danielle and a member of \uc2 Prenuvo\uc2 's medical group, who explains the results provided by a board-certified radiologist.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR VIKASH MODI (PRENUVO MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 I'm Dr. Vikash Modi. I have the privilege of going over your recent MRI scan results with you. The place I'm gonna start is just by saying overall, overall, we assess every patient's scan for risk of malignancy for cancer. And your scan registered as low risk for any active malignancy. Doesn't mean that we're zero percent...\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 Sure.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR VIKASH MODI (PRENUVO MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 ...and it doesn't mean that something can't develop tomorrow. But as of that snapshot of the picture we took, we're in good shape. Okay?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 That's amazing. Thank you. Yeah. My heart rate just completely came down.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR VIKASH MODI (PRENUVO MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 Good.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Most of Danielle's findings are favorable.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR VIKASH MODI (PRENUVO MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 So, your brain is a healthy size and shape. We see no signs of masses, tumors, malignancies of any sort. Now, what we did spot was this little thing in the circle we call a nonspecific white matter hyper intensity. And the pattern sort of matches a sort of we don't know, pattern. Okay? Which is a little scary for the patient but for us we know it's low risk.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doctor Modi reminds Danielle to follow up with her primary doctor and wraps up with this guidance.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR VIKASH MODI (PRENUVO MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 Because this needs to be sort of a roadmap now for what your next few steps are gonna look like.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 It's exceeded all expectations and to live in this day and age and be able to do this for myself and my family, I, I just feel so, so grateful and so happy. So, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR VIKASH MODI (PRENUVO MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 I'm so thrilled to be a part of it for you.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And she says the results are life changing.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PATRICK (FORMER DANCER/BUSINESS WOMAN)\par \par \uc2 Getting the results of the scan is honestly one of the best moments of my entire life. I've spent the last decade worried that things were wrong and it's a feeling of just complete and utter relief, better than I could've ever imagined. I feel like a huge weight has been lifted and those questions that I, I've always had in my head since my mom passed away, it's just completely, they're almost gone now. I just feel like I have control.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Deborah.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BLAKE VS. JUSTIN\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) When we come back, a new development in the Hollywood battle royale between Justin Baldoni and Blake Lively. Her new allegations and his lawyer's denial.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020250225el2j00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WOMEN & HEART DISEASE HEART DISEASE RISK FACTORS IN WOMEN\b0\par\par\uc2 1,155 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EAGLES SOAR TO SUPER BOWL TITLE\par \par \uc2 SUPER BOWL, EAGLES, VICTORY, HURTS, DOMINATION, CHIEFS\par \par \uc2 SUPER BOWL, EAGLES, FANS, CELEBRATIONS, LAMP POST, LINENS\par \par \uc2 EAGLES, MAILATA, SUPER BOWL, VICTORY, TEAM, SLEEP\par \par \uc2 STORMS, TRIPLE, SNOWFALL, TOTALS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , TARIFFS, MUSK, JUDGES, DEVINE, MURPHY, \uc2 USAID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE SECOND STORM THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 LAMAR, HALFTIME, "NOT LIKE US", JACKSON, WILLIAMS, SZA\par \par \uc2 SUPER BOWL, ADS, DEUTSCH, CELEBRITIES, HEARTFELT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOW & RAIN FORECAST\par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA, FOOTBALL, SUPER BOWL, VICTORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EAGLES SOAR TO SUPER BOWL TITLE\par \par \uc2 FLU, OUTBREAK, HEALTH, REPORT, MEDICINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLU ILLNESS VISITS AT HIGHEST SINCE 2009 PANDEMIC\par \par \uc2 TEEN, ARREST, HOME INVASION, MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 EAGLES, SUPER BOWL, VICTORY, FOOTBALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHAEL FLIES THE FLAG FOR PHILLY\par \par \uc2 WOMEN, HEART DISEASE, HEALTH, SIGNS, SYMPTOMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN & HEART DISEASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEART DISEASE RISK FACTORS IN WOMEN\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, George, thank you. Now our series for American Heart Month. And this morning, we're talking about women when it comes to heart disease. Women may be less likely to realize risk and more likely to miss the warning signs or not seek care. Who better to talk to about this than ABC News chief medical correspondent Dr. Tara Narula, who is also a cardiologist? It's so happy, I'm so, so glad to talk to you about this\u160\'00\'A0because it's a number one killer, it's a number one killer for women. So, what are the signs and symptoms we should be aware of?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right. We've done so much to increase awareness, Robin, and yet still, 44 percent of women only realize that this is her biggest health threat. And so, what I would love for women to do today and this month is really visualize the faces of those women in their lives they love, their So, So, sisters, their moms, their best friends. This is what threatens them, right, and so, we need to do a better job than one woman dying every minute from something that's 80 percent preventable. So, the first key is recognizing those warning signs.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) First and foremost is chest pain. Now, a lot of people say pain, but this usually is described as a pressure, tightness, fullness, heaviness. Some women say, "My bra just feels too tight." It can be shortness of breath. I get a little winded when I walk up hills, or when I walk up stairs or when I walk fast, feeling of just generalized fatigue, "I'm so exhausted, and I don't know why," lightheadedness, palpitations, fainting and even nausea or flu-like symptoms. These have to be paid attention to and brought up with your doctor.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, when you are feeling, though, any of those things, be sure to talk about it. And we also know that there are the risk factors that are unique to women that could really have an impact on the probability of having heart disease. Can you talk a little bit more about that?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, so, we've just started to really talk about this in the medical community, that women are not just smaller versions of men. Our biology is different. And we look at women across the life span. So, it starts early. If you got your period before the age of 10, that's a risk factor for cardiovascular disease. Fast-forward to midlife, and there are several things. So, pregnancy complications like high blood pressure in pregnancy, eclampsia, pre-eclampsia, gestational diabetes, miscarriage, having a baby that's premature, these are all risk factors.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Conditions like polycystic ovarian syndrome, migraines, lupus or rheumatoid arthritis, some of these auto-immune conditions, inflammatory bowel disease, Crohn's and ulcerative colitis and even psoriasis, a skin condition\u160\'00\'A0or early menopause. And then we think about later life. So, being post-menopausal or over 55 is a risk factor, as is certain types of cancer treatment, so chemotherapy and radiation. For example, we see a lot of women surviving breast cancer and then dying from heart disease because they got radiation to the left breast or chemotherapies that may be toxic to the heart.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You are raising a lot of eyebrows this morning but for the right, for the right reasons. You're a mom, you're a proud mom.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, I am, two girls, two girls.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Two girls, okay, and you're busy with your practice and also part of the women's health program at your practice. So, from careers to motherhood, to you know how, you know where I'm getting here.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I do.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Women are always saying they put others before themselves. What is your message?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I hear this all the time from my patients, "I don't have time," or they downplay their symptoms. And, you know, women are so good at multitasking and juggling a million things and putting everybody else on their priority list. And we want women to put their heart health at the top of their priority list, to know their numbers, to get screened, to pay attention to symptoms. And I'll also say this, if they see a doctor who tells them their chest pain is acid reflux or their shortness of breath is that they're just deconditioned, or they're tired because they're overwhelmed, you need to advocate for yourself and see someone who listens to you and sees that whole picture of who you are as a woman, your whole life, all those risk factors.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we often say being, self-care is not being selfish. You got to take care of yourself first.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right, absolutely, and leading cause of maternal mortality, cardiovascular disease. So, you know, important for moms, too. You want to be around for your kids' graduations, every moment in their lives.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I know.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, put yourself at the top.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Visualize that woman in your life.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right, see the faces. That's going to make it personal.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you an Eagles fan? You're wearing the green.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know what? I was last night, but I didn't intend this, but I was happy they won.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It worked out really well. All right, Dr. Narula, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250311el2a0008t\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SURROGACY BACKLASH PERSONAL JOURNEY TO MOTHERHOOD\b0\par\par\uc2 1,348 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 February 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 LAMAR, DIS, "NOT LIKE US", LAWSUIT, CARTER, LAMARRE, HIP HOP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 SURROGACY, COLLINS, BACKLASH, BENTLEY, CANCER, MOTHERHOOD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back. Beginning a family is one of life's great joys. So imagine the shock and sadness of couples using surrogates to welcome new additions, only to then be attacked. "Emily in Paris" star Lily Collins, among those facing it firsthand. Here's ABC's Stephanie Ramos.\par \par \uc2 PHOTOGRAPHER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Lily to the right.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lily Collins is used to the glare of the spotlight. She's a staple on red carpets promoting movies or her hit show, "Emily in Paris."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "EMILY ON PARIS"\par \par \uc2 LILY COLLINS (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 In Paris.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The actress and daughter of musician Phil Collins was basically born into fame. But her latest headlines likely came as a bit of a shock. Now, the actress sits at the center of the newest surrogacy controversy, as she and her husband push back against online critics, as the public weighs in about when, if ever, surrogacy is an accepted way to expand families.\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 I just kind of wanted to hug her and say, I got you, girl. Like I got you. But the more that we tell our stories, the more we will be able to maybe not change minds that can never be changed, but open minds that don't quite understand.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The "Emily in Paris" star and her husband, Charlie McDowell, made their announcement on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , introducing their baby girl named Tove, saying, quote, "Words will never express our endless gratitude for our incredible surrogate and everyone who helped us along the way. We love you to the moon and back again."\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And while many of the comments were incredibly supportive, others were not. Taking to the comments section himself, Collins husband wrote, "It's okay to not be an expert on surrogacy. It's okay to not know why someone might need a surrogate to have a child, and it's okay to spend less time spewing hateful words into the world, especially in regards to a beautiful baby girl." The topic kicking off conversations online and of course with the ladies of "The View."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "THE VIEW"\par \par \uc2 SARAH HAINES (CO-HOST "THE VIEW")\par \par \uc2 A pro-family world we live in that's all about life, you know, all the time. The second someone has it, we tear them down.\par \par \uc2 SUNNY HOSTIN (CO-HOST "THE VIEW")\par \par \uc2 And it's disgusting and despicable for people to judge them, for wanting to bring love into this world and a family into this world. I just think it's horrible.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Collins is hardly the first celebrity to use a surrogate. In recent years, many stars have been public about their difficulty expanding their families.\par \par \uc2 PARIS HILTON (SOCIALITE & ENTREPRENEUR)\par \par \uc2 Having a family and having children is what I've always been meant to do.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Paris Hilton spoke to ABC back in 2023 about the importance of motherhood. Both her children were carried by surrogates. Actress Olivia Munn welcomed her second child last fall thanks to a surrogate, after Munn was diagnosed and treated for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA MUNN (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 You never know what's going on in someone's life, you know that that privately people are battling things that that you'd never know.\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 Oh my God, oh my God, are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That's a journey Sarrah Strimel Bentley knows. Well, she was just 38 years old when she found a lump in her breast. Within days, she was diagnosed with stage two breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 The thought of my baby coming was what got me through breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Sarah posted all about her diagnosis and surrogacy journey online. She's received plenty of support, but like Lily Collins, she says there are haters too.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What are some of the things people have said to you about this journey, and how do you respond to them?\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 People said it was modern day slavery. We would get "Handmaid's Tale." We got a lot of if God wanted you to be a mom, then you'd be able to have a baby. At the end of the day I never responded to any of them. I wanted to, but you know, you can't engage with terrorists, right? You can't engage with keyboard warriors because they're, they're not there to have a conversation.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She and her now husband, James, had always planned to have children. Luckily, she was able to begin fertility treatments as she also prepped for a double mastectomy and chemotherapy.\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 We are en route to the second egg harvesting.\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 We were able to create one healthy embryo that we named Chance.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Sadly, Sarrah's doctors advised against trying to carry the pregnancy herself.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What was that like for you, that moment?\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I'm already crying. You're bringing me back to hearing those words that I think it's one of the most difficult things that you can face is as a woman who, who really has dreamt of being a mom, is to lose the ability to do that.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The search for a surrogate brought her right back to social media.\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 There's so much unknown about surrogacy and so much misinformation out there. So I started the surrogacy diaries where I told the good, the bad, and the ugly.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Sarrah says finding the right surrogate is like dating and chemistry is everything. Eventually, they were paired with Whitney, a nurse from Virginia and single mother of two.\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 We met her, and she had a smile that could light up the room, a fabulous sense of style, and I was like, I think this is my girl.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The embryo transfer was successful, and Sarrah and James traveled from New York to Virginia several times, making sure to stay involved in Whitney's pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 Hello, guy. Mama loves you. He's perfect, you guys.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Chance was born at a whopping 10 pounds in December 2023. Sarah and Whitney now consider each other family, facetiming regularly and visiting each other.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, it really seems like a solid partnership between the two of you that you developed.\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 A partnership for sure. A friendship, and then a sisterhood.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Sarrah co-founded a nonprofit called, A Chance For Life, that helps fund surrogacy for cancer survivors.\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 When I was a breast cancer warrior and now I am a warrior for fertility and for infertility. But I say warrior because I'm ready to go to battle for women who come down the pipe after me, because I know how hard it is to go through it, and I know how incredible it is. Now I have my little guy here.\par \par \uc2 SARRAH STRIMEL BENTLEY (CANCER SURVIVOR & SURROGACY ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 I'm 10 months old.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPER BOWL STAGE\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Stephanie. When we come back, count down to the big game. The amazing artists slated for Super Bowl LVII.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020250210el2600003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 RFK JR. GRILLED OVER HEALTH NOMINATIONS KENNEDY CONFRONTED WITH PAST ANTI-VACCINE COMMENTS\b0\par\par\uc2 990 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 January 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 KENNEDY, CASSIDY, HASSAN, WYDEN, CONFIRMATION HEARING\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. And we begin tonight with those fireworks on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RFK JR. GRILLED OVER HEALTH NOMINATIONS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  grilled by senators during his confirmation hearing, President Trump's pick to be the next secretary of \uc2 Health and Human Services\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) RFK Jr. challenged about his many comments against vaccines and his reversal on abortion rights. There were also questions over the programs he would lead, including Medicaid, that provides health insurance to 72 million adults and children with limited income. Some were not convinced with his answer on that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And tonight, after repeatedly casting doubts on vaccines, RFK Jr. telling senators his own children are vaccinated. And what he said about what he called the chronic disease epidemic in this country. ABC's senior political correspondent Rachel Scott leading us off. She's on the Hill again tonight.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, a contentious hearing for one of President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's most controversial nominees.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KENNEDY CONFRONTED WITH PAST ANTI-VACCINE COMMENTS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  Facing tough questions in his quest to become health secretary. From the outset, Kennedy, who has spread misinformation about the safety of vaccines, trying to get ahead of the issue.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY JR\uc2  (NOMINEE SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 News reports have claimed that I'm anti-vaccine or anti-industry. I am neither. I am pro-safety, and I believe that vaccines play a critical role in health care. All of my kids are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Those his own children are vaccinated, Kennedy has long publicly questioned the safety of vaccines. Democrats citing this 2023 podcast.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY JR\uc2  (NOMINEE SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 There's no vaccine that is, you know, safe and effective.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Senator \uc2 Ron Wyden\uc2  challenging Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RON WYDEN\uc2  (DEM) (OREGON)\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kennedy, you have spent years pushing conflicting stories about vaccines. You say one thing and then you say another.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kennedy insisting...\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY JR\uc2  (NOMINEE SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 Senator, I support the measles vaccine. I support the polio vaccine. I will do nothing as \uc2 HHS\uc2  secretary that makes it difficult or discourages people from taking either of those vaccines.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RON WYDEN\uc2  (DEM) (OREGON)\par \par \uc2 Anybody who believes that ought to look at the measles book you wrote saying, parents have been misled into believing that measles is a deadly disease. That's not true.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats casting Kennedy as a political shapeshifter, not just on vaccines, but on abortion.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (DEM) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kennedy, in 2023, you came to New Hampshire and said, quote, "I'm pro-choice. I don't think the government has any business telling people what they can or cannot do with their body." So, you said that, right?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATS QUESTION KENNEDY OVER SHIFT ON ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY JR\uc2  (NOMINEE SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (DEM) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Yep. So, Mr. Kennedy, I'm confused. You have clearly stated in the past that bodily autonomy is one of your core values.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY JR\uc2  (NOMINEE SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 Senator, I agree with President Trump that every abortion is a tragedy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Though Republicans were largely supportive, \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  of Louisiana, who is also a doctor, pressing Kennedy to outline his ideas for reforming Medicaid, the health insurance program that covers 72 million lower income Americans. Kennedy appearing stumped.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY JR\uc2  (NOMINEE SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 Listen, I think that there is are many, many options with telemedicine, with AI right now. And, you know, there's a, including direct primary care systems or seeing that movement grow across the country. There's one of the largest...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (REP) (LOUISIANA)\par \par \uc2 So, so going back to Medicaid, though, and speaking of these specific advances, how would you, what reforms are you proposing with these ideas vis-a-vis Medicaid?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KENNEDY PRESSED ON HIS PLANS TO REFORM MEDICAID\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY JR\uc2  (NOMINEE SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 Well, I don't have a broad proposal for dismantling the program.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (REP) (LOUISIANA)\par \par \uc2 I'm not saying, of course, I'm not saying that.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kennedy insists he's driven by a singular mission.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F KENNEDY JR\uc2  (NOMINEE SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 President Trump has asked me to end the chronic disease epidemic and make America healthy again. If we don't solve that problem, senator, all of the other disputes we have about who's paying and, whether it's insurance companies, whether it's providers, whether it's HMOs, whether it's patients or families, all of those are moving deck chairs around on the Titanic. Our ship is sinking.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  is back for a second day tomorrow. Also, tomorrow, David, we will see confirmation hearings for \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 . She is President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's pick for director of national intelligence. She is expected to face some tough questions about her travels to Syria to meet with Bashar al-Assad. Also, tomorrow, Kash Patel, Trump's nominee for \uc2 FBI\uc2  director, will come before senators, too. He's expected to be pressed on his promise to go after Trump's political enemies. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott leading us off on the Hill. A very busy week for you and the whole team there. Rachel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020250203el1t00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 URGENT NEW ALCOHOL & CANCER WARNING SURGEON GENERAL WARNS OF CANCER RISK FROM ALCOHOL\b0\par\par\uc2 1,070 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 January 2025\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WINTER STORM EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 STORM, ROADS, SNOW, ICE, COMMUTE, ACCIDENTS, GARDNER\par \par \uc2 STORM, SNOW, RAIN, WIND CHILL\par \par \uc2 STORM, TRAVEL, AIRLINES, CANCELATIONS, DELAYS\par \par \uc2 ATTACK, JABBAR, VIDEOS, RAIA, JACKSON, MYRTHIL, VICTIMS\par \par \uc2 CONGRESS, CERTIFY, TRUMP, HARRIS, RIOT, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, OIL, DRILLING, SOCIAL SECURITY\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , SEASON, LIONS, VIKINGS, PLAYOFFS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "GOLDEN GLOBES" AWARDS\par \par \uc2 PLANE, FIERY, LANDING, ENGINE, VIDEO, MAHAR\par \par \uc2 VEGAS, BLAST, CYBER TRUCK, LIVELSBERGER, ARRITT\par \par \uc2 GOLDEN GLOBES, GLASER, "EMILIA PEREZ", MOORE, "WICKED' "SHOGUN"\par \par \uc2 MULKEY, CONTEST, FASHION, LSU\par \par \uc2 STORM, HEARTLAND, EAST COAST, WINTER, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR WINTER STORM WREAKS HAVOC\par \par \uc2 SURGEON GENERAL, CANCER, RISK,\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT NEW ALCOHOL & CANCER WARNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURGEON GENERAL WARNS OF CANCER RISK FROM ALCOHOL\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now the alert from the surgeon general, shining a light on a leading, preventable cause of cancer. We're talking about alcohol. Here's what he told ABC News.\par \par \uc2 DR. VIVEK H. MURTHY (UNITED STATES SURGEON GENERAL)\par \par \uc2 We've seen data now building for years that's telling us that there is a causal link between alcohol and seven types of cancer, including breast cancer and colorectal cancer. Alcohol's responsible for 100,000 cases of cancer a year in the United States and 20,000 cancer deaths.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The surgeon general going on to call for a warning label to be added to alcohol containers. And here to explain more about this is Dr. Elizabeth Comen, a medical oncologist at NYU Langone Health. I know what you feel about, this\u160\'00\'A0is a very strong statement from the surgeon general. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 So, I applaud this statement. You know, most Americans drink, and yet most, over 50 percent, are not aware that alcohol is actually a carcinogen and one of the leading preventable causes of cancer.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because we've heard over the years, okay, that a, you know, if you, a moderate drinker\u160\'00\'A0or low amounts were not that detrimental to your health or wellbeing. This, of course, refutes that. So, what is the data saying?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. We've confused the public, and the data would actually point to the fact that alcohol is a cyanogen and toxic to our cells, which means that it can cause DNA damage, lead to increased risk of cancer, seven different cancer types, as the surgeon general referred to, and also a host of other problems, as well. So, we really need to be forthcoming with this messaging so that the public is aware that alcohol is simply not safe and that we encourage patients to talk to their doctors, their family and friends, about how they might decrease that consumption.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as you alluded to, and so did the surgeon general, seven cancers in particular are associated with alcohol consumption, including breast cancer. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yes, and breast cancer is my specialty.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we know that alcohol is toxic to normal healthy cells and can cause DNA damage. But with breast cancer specifically, it can also affect how our hormones behave, and in many instances, breast cancer is hormonally driven. It can also affect how our healthy cells absorb nutrients that we need. It can also lead to excess weight because it's calories, empty calories, and we know that obesity is also a preventable cause of cancer.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is true. So, what's your guidance here for folks that are tuning in this morning after the, you know, the first Monday of the new year?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, and there's been a lot of celebration and sometimes a lot of drinking, and we've normalized drinking around cultural norms in our society. So, I really encourage patients to talk to their doctor about honestly how much they are drinking because even a drink a day is associated with an increased risk of cancer, in the seven types of cancers that we talked about.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I understand you're not a fan of dry January. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Well, I mean, I'm always, I'm always a fan of New Year's resolutions, but my concern about dry January is what happens February 1. We need to be honest about the lifestyle changes we can realistically make, and if we go cold turkey on something, what happens for the rest of the months of the year? You know, in my field I see cancer 365 days of the year, and I am focused on how we lead to meaningful, impactful change that relates to patients' healthy lifestyle.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I appreciate your passion and compassion when it comes to this. Thank you so very, very much.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You got it, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Robin, coming up in our "GMA Morning Menu," we're starting\u160\'00\'A0strong in 2025 with the ultra-processed food challenge. We have the experts here to tell us what that means and how to eat and shop to be healthy.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, Zoe Saldana and Demi Moore leading the way on the red carpet. Lara will have Golden Globes fashion and more coming up in "Pop News."\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, bestselling author and motivational speaker, my friend, Gabby Bernstein, is here with ways to manifest your intentions in the new year. And Lara, I know you have a special guest.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm trying to control him, Robin. Tim Allen is with us. He's here to tell us about his new show, it's called "Shifting Gears," and he's going to give us some buzz, yeah, I did it, about "Toy Story"...\par \par \uc2 TIM ALLEN ("SHIFTING GEARS")\par \par \uc2 Wow, that's clever. Wow, that's clever.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks, pal. We'll have more with this guy coming up on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 TIM ALLEN ("SHIFTING GEARS")\par \par \uc2 We sure will.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250131el160003b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE YEAR 2024 LAW AND JUSTICE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,616 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 December 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SWIFT, TESLA, MUSIC, MENENDEZ, OLYMPICS, MOVIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE YEAR 2024, OBSESSIONS\par \par \uc2 GLOBAL NEWS, PRESIDENTIAL RACE, ISRAEL, HURRICANE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE YEAR 2024\par \par \uc2 ROMANCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS, THE YEAR 2024\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ, COMBS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS, THE YEAR 2024\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Now, Linsey Davis with the year's law and justice stories.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Breaking news as we come on the air. Music mogul \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  arrested in a New York City hotel by federal agents with Homeland Security investigations.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As 2024 comes to a close, Sean Combs still caught in legal crosshairs. One of music's biggest names arrested and charged with sex trafficking, racketeering, conspiracy, and transportation to engage in prostitution. He's pled not guilty to all charges and has vehemently denied any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The criminal prosecution against Sean Combs starts with his ex-girlfriend and protege Cassie. She came forward with a civil lawsuit that Combs quickly settled, but it caused federal prosecutors to start digging in.\par \par \uc2 BRETT ERLICH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And then at some point, there is just a break in the dam. And that was the video of Cassie.\par \par \uc2 KELLEY L CARTER (SENIOR ENTERTAINMENT REPORTER ANDSCAPE)\par \par \uc2 There were a lot of people that were still in defense of behavior. And I think when that video came out, it changed the conversation significantly.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Combs has tried repeatedly to get out on bail, but prosecutors say even from behind bars, he's been intimidating witnesses, paying off witnesses to say nice things about him, calling people he's not supposed to call.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A bombshell accusation today against music mogul Jay-Z. A woman claims that he joined \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  in sexually assaulting her when she was just 13 years old after the VMA Award show in 2000. Jay-Z is vehemently denying the accusation.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While Diddy's legal woes dominated headlines in 2024, public interest in a 35-year-old crime hit a fever pitch this year thanks to a wildly popular \uc2 Netflix\uc2  true crime series and the real life courtroom drama of brothers Erik and Lyle Menendez.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) One of the most notorious murder trials in US history. The Menendez brothers sentenced to life without parole for murdering their parents. Well, now the Los Angeles DA says he's reviewing new evidence. So could the Menendez brothers go free?\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Menendez brothers' case is now under this microscope and everybody's talking about it.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This new generation has taken up the Menendez brothers case on all kinds of social media. They're more open to understanding claims of abuse and they want to stand up for abuse victims.\par \par \uc2 ALYSSA FARAH GRIFFIN (CO-HOST "THE VIEW")\par \par \uc2 It is kind of remarkable, the generational shifts in how we view horrible things that happen in society and the things that we learn just in the course of 30 years.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And now a case that's just as confusing as it is bizarre. The so-called doomsday leader, Chad Daybell, accused with his new wife, Lori Vallow Daybell, of killing his first wife and Lori's two children because he believed they were zombies.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's all based on this crackpot religious belief that there are zombies and that you have to kill the zombies and that they were going to create this new post-apocalyptic society together, just the two of them.\par \par \uc2 JUROR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Guilty, guilty, guilty.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The pair was found guilty of all charges. Lori sentenced to life in prison and Chad sentenced to death. And America witnessed quite a show in 2024. The president-elect spent much of the year off the campaign trail and at a trial, his own trial.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are coming on air for a moment never before witnessed in American history. A former president on criminal trial.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) There were four total criminal cases. One in Manhattan, where he faced 34 counts of falsifying business records. Two federal cases where he was indicted on four charges in DC and 40 in Florida. And one in Georgia, where he was indicted on racketeering and other charges.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANTUCCI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The fact that Donald Trump, who, you know, is living the glitzy, golden life on Fifth Avenue, is suddenly spending his days in the slums of criminal court in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The prosecution went long and hard, and Trump often complained of how cold the courtroom was, reportedly falling asleep.\par \par \uc2 LARA TRUMP (FORMER CO-CHAIR REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 You're sitting there for hours on end and you're hearing just this testimony that could be very, I don't want to use the word boring, but it's a little boring.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "SATURDAY NIGHT LIVE"\par \par \uc2 ACTOR ("SATURDAY NIGHT LIVE")\par \par \uc2 I don't like being in court because they say very mean things about me while I'm trying to sleep.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANTUCCI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the thing that's fascinating about this courtroom, it's the one time in Donald Trump's life he has no control.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The jury then ultimately found the then-former president and presidential candidate guilty on 34 felony counts.\par \par \uc2 LARA TRUMP (FORMER CO-CHAIR REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 I think, you know, to hear, "Guilty, guilty, guilty," yeah, it goes straight to your heart.\par \par \uc2 DONALD J TRUMP (PRESIDENT-ELECT)\par \par \uc2 This was a rigged, disgraceful trial. The real verdict is going to be November 5th.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump now carries the burden of being the only president that is also a convicted felon.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM RNC\par \par \uc2 DONALD J TRUMP (PRESIDENT-ELECT)\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, wow.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But then shortly after winning the presidency, some good news for Trump.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Special Council Jack Smith is officially moving to drop the January 6th election interference case against President-elect Trump.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While his Georgia case is now on appeal, his New York case won't be going away any time soon. Because the judge has suspended sentencing, and there may be more appeals.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Donald Trump cannot be protected by presidential immunity in relation to his conviction in his hush money trial. That's the ruling in from Judge Merchan.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Every year, the Supreme Court hands down major decisions. But this past year was truly remarkable in the number of socially and politically significant cases.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That presidential immunity decision was a giant decision. And it was clear that this court will swing for the fences on these big issues.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Supreme Court gave Donald Trump what he wanted, deciding Colorado could not remove his name from the ballot, and that he does receive broad presidential immunity. This year we also saw the justices take up abortion rights for the first time since they struck down Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The court took on two cases, one involving the abortion pill, mifepristone, and also took on for the first time a state abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The court might've tried to get out of the abortion jurisdiction by overturning Roe versus Wade. They are going to get case after case, as women struggle to get the health care that they believe they need, in states which have banned outright or have very few exceptions for abortion.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And from the Supreme Court to the mugshot seen round the world.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When that Justin Timberlake mugshot dropped, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  popped off. TikTok popped off. That thing was everywhere.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MUSE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Boy band sensation, right. Mister suit and tie himself, is now being booked, fingerprinted, and having a mugshot taken of old blue eyes soul himself.\par \par \uc2 ROCSI DIAZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Not a great moment for Justin. Dude, Uber. What are you doing?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Justin Timberlake plead guilty to driving while ability impaired. But there was one celeb whose widely publicized legal problems have seemingly come to an end. Alec Baldwin was charged with involuntary manslaughter in connection with the 2021 death of cinematographer Halyna Hutchins on the set of "Rust."\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BUCKMIRE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, Alec Baldwin has seen a roller coaster of a legal journey in the last months or years, where the case is brought up and then dismissed and brought up again. But ultimately, it's dismissed with prejudice. And what that means is it's dismissed in a way that it can never come back.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) His defense team was able to convince the judge that the evidence against him couldn't be trusted.\par \par \uc2 BRETT ERLICH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) After years and years, I think a lot of people wanted closure, and I think we got it, the case was dismissed.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS, THE YEAR 2024\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Next, Robin Roberts with our "Can't miss" moments.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Elmo. Hi, there.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From lift up to lights out.\par \par \uc2 ECLIPSE VIEWER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You sound emotional.\par \par \uc2 ECLIPSE VIEWER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Tears. Tears in my eyes.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 When we return.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS, THE YEAR 2024\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020250117ekcq00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ADVANCED BREAST CANCER ON THE RISE STUDY SAYS BLACK & YOUNG WOMEN AT GREATER RISK\b0\par\par\uc2 1,292 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 December 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COLD, WIND CHILLS, SNOW\par \par \uc2 TEMPERATURES, PRECIPITATION\par \par \uc2 CEO, MURDER, THOMPSON\par \par \uc2 TIMMERMAN, ASSAD, SYRIA\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, INJURED\par \par \uc2 COUPLE, KILLED, MEXICO, CARDONA, AMBRIZ\par \par \uc2 DUKE UNIVERSITY\uc2 , RAPE, CASE, ACCUSER, LIED, MANGUM\par \par \uc2 MCKINSEY & COMPANY\uc2 , OPIOID, EPIDEMIC\par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA, JINGLE BELL RUN\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP OF "THE HOLIDAY JUNKIE"\par \par \uc2 DRONE, SIGHTINGS\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, GIFTS\par \par \uc2 DALLAS, COWBOYS, ALOPECIA, LATIMER\par \par \uc2 "ELF ON THE SHELF", FREEMAN\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, SNOW, COLD, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FRIGID WEEKEND FOR MILLIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEAVY SNOW & ICE FROM THE MIDWEST TO THE NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 MANGIONE, ATTORNEY, SHOOTING, THOMPSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CEO KILLING SUSPECT HIRES LAWYER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER NY PROSECUTOR TO DEFEND LUIGI MANGIONE\par \par \uc2 SOUTH KOREA, PRESIDENT, IMPEACHMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTH KOREAN PRESIDENT IMPEACHED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AFTER MARTIAL LAW CAUSED POLITICAL TURMOIL\par \par \uc2 DRONE, NORTHEAST, MYSTERY, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FURY GROWS OVER MYSTERIOUS DRONE SIGHTINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOCAL OFFICIALS DEMANDING ANSWERS& MORE ACTION\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, COURT, RULING, BAN, DEADLINE, SALE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EMERGENCY REQUEST DENIED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIKTOK TRYING TO AVOID BAN\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, INCREASE, YOUNG, BLACK, STUDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADVANCED BREAST CANCER ON THE RISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDY SAYS BLACK & YOUNG WOMEN AT GREATER RISK\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back now on "GMA" with our cover story, and a new study says advanced breast cancer is on the rise in the US in women under age 40, especially among Black women. So, we're taking a look at one young woman's battle against breast cancer and what she's doing to support other women.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) When 29-year-old Amanda Quick felt a lump in her armpit in 2021, she took it in stride.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA QUICK (LIVING WITH METASTATIC BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 I didn't think much of it. I was like, "Okay, I'm probably tired just from work."\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the lump grew, and by the end of the month, she had a diagnosis, metastatic breast cancer. Her cancer had spread to other parts of her body.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA QUICK (LIVING WITH METASTATIC BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Obviously something no one expects, but it wasn't even something that was on my radar.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Amanda is part of an alarming trend. A new study tracking nearly 80 million women in the US found the greatest increase in advanced breast cancer was seen in women ages 20 to 39, hitting women of color the hardest. Rates were 55 percent higher among Black women when compared to white women, but metastatic disease is not necessarily a death sentence, with many doctors looking at it as a chronic disease to be managed.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA QUICK (LIVING WITH METASTATIC BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Researching is picking up so it is exciting to see, you know, what's it's exciting to see what's going to be around in five, 10 years.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA QUICK (LIVING WITH METASTATIC BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 I shaved my head about a week ago, when my hair started falling out.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA QUICK (LIVING WITH METASTATIC BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Amanda says she's found purpose in supporting other women impacted by breast cancer, in part by sharing her journey on social media.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA QUICK (LIVING WITH METASTATIC BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 I think it's so important to find the communities that lift you up.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, important. Here to tell us more about this new study is Dr. Lisa Newman, chief of the Section of Breast Surgery at New York-Presbyterian and Weill Cornell Medicine. Thank you so much for being here, Dr. Newman. We were just talking, you have been doing this for nearly 30 years. You have seen in your own practice some of the findings from this new study. What, what are the key takeaways because of the new study?\par \par \uc2 DR. LISA NEWMAN (NEW YORK-PRESBYTERIAN & WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Well, for context, we have to emphasize the fact that because of advances in screening and treatment, we've made tremendous progress in reducing breast cancer death rates. However, we still have a long way to go in conquering breast cancer. Now, as this particular study shows, by looking at the patterns of breast cancer in the United States over the past two decades, we, unfortunately, are seeing steady increases in metastatic breast cancer among American women. And metastatic breast cancer is where the disease has already started causing damage to other organs, to be spread to liver, lungs, bones. This is relevant because, although contemporary treatments for breast cancer are effective in the high majority of women...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You have to catch it early.\par \par \uc2 DR. LISA NEWMAN (NEW YORK-PRESBYTERIAN & WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 ...they are unfortunately less effective for metastatic disease.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, what do we need to know?\par \par \uc2 DR. LISA NEWMAN (NEW YORK-PRESBYTERIAN & WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, well, as this study also demonstrates, there are important disparities in the burden of metastatic cancer. This study demonstrated disproportionately rising rates of metastatic disease in women younger than age of 40, and for Black women, metastatic breast cancer is 55 percent more common compared to white American women.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, of course, we see those racial disparities all across the healthcare sector. But from this study, then, what are, what are some things that younger women need to know because right now, mammograms you should start getting around 40, unless someone close to you has had breast cancer, then 10 years younger, but this study says maybe we should be doing it younger, right?\par \par \uc2 DR. LISA NEWMAN (NEW YORK-PRESBYTERIAN & WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Exactly, this study demonstrating rising rates of metastatic burden in the women who are not necessarily getting screened, women younger than ages of 40. This indicates that maybe we should relook at those guidelines. Maybe we do need to be offering mammograms to women younger than 40. And we need better technology to identify breast cancers in young women because unfortunately, mammograms are more difficult to interpret in very young women.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Especially we have to think about the dense breast tissue and all of these things, and there are little things that we can know about how to lower your rate of breast cancer, like things like breastfeeding. Any other quick tips because we're almost out of time?\par \par \uc2 DR. LISA NEWMAN (NEW YORK-PRESBYTERIAN & WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, women need to be aware of changes in their breasts. Mammograms aren't perfect. If you develop a new lump in the breast, lump in the underarm area, bloody nipple discharge, changes in the skin appearance of the breast, those problems can be caused by benign issues, but you do need to seek medical attention if you develop them, especially if you're young, and you're not necessarily getting mammographic screening. Know your family history. You might need genetic testing. You might need to start mammograms at even younger ages, or you might enhanced screening with MRIs, breast ultrasounds.\par \par \uc2 DR. LISA NEWMAN (NEW YORK-PRESBYTERIAN & WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Women do have ways of protecting themselves against breast cancer with screening and lifestyle.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But you've got to be proactive, yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. LISA NEWMAN (NEW YORK-PRESBYTERIAN & WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Avoiding obesity, staying active, exercising regularly, following a healthy, balanced diet.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Alcohol, all those things.\par \par \uc2 DR. LISA NEWMAN (NEW YORK-PRESBYTERIAN & WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Minimizing alcohol intake.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you so much. There's so much to get into, but I appreciate you, Dr. Newman, for being here to help us.\par \par \uc2 DR. LISA NEWMAN (NEW YORK-PRESBYTERIAN & WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Gio, over to you.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Such an important conversation. Janai, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020250108ekce0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MENOPAUSE & MUSCLE MASS THE EFFECT ON WOMEN'S BODIES & TIPS ON FIGHTING MUSCLE MASS LOSS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,015 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 November 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , CABINET, GAETZ\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, DROUGHT\par \par \uc2 SOUTHWEST, PLANE, BULLET\par \par \uc2 IBARRA, RILEY, MURDER\par \par \uc2 RACIST, TEXTS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, JINPING, APEC SUMMIT\par \par \uc2 WAR, UKRAINE, ZELENSKYY, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 THANKSGIVING, MEAL, DEALS\par \par \uc2 TYSON\uc2 , PAUL, BOXING MATCH\par \par \uc2 MOM, ARRESTED, RECKLESS ENDANGERMENT\par \par \uc2 MAKE-A-WISH, FAVELA, DERULO\par \par \uc2 KOHBERGER, MURDERS, IDAHO, EVIDENCE, RIGHTS, GENETICS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , NOMINATIONS, GAETZ, JOHNSON, LEAVITT, KENNEDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FALLOUT AMID MATT GAETZ NOMINATION: GROWING NUMBER OF REPUBLICAN SENATORS EAGER TO SEE HOUSE ETHICS COMMITTEE REPORT\par \par \uc2 SOUTHWEST, AIRCRAFT, BULLET, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 FOOTBALL, COLLEGE, TN, VOLUNTEERS, GA, BULLDOGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIVE ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTION-PACKED COLLEGE FOOTBALL WEEKEND: THE POWERHOUSE TEAM MATCHUPS HAPPENING FOR WEEK 12\par \par \uc2 LYON, "FOREVER STRONG", MENOPAUSE, BONES, STRENGTH\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back. Turning now to something every woman experiences in their lifetime, menopause.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVER STORY: NEW THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENOPAUSE & MUSCLE MASS: THE EFFECT ON WOMEN'S BODIES & TIPS ON FIGHTING MUSCLE MASS LOSS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Gabrielle Lyon, board certified family physician and "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestselling author of the book "Forever Strong: A New Science-Based Strategy For Aging Well," joins us to talk about the importance of maintaining muscle mass as you go into menopause. This is, it's so great to have you here, Dr. Lyon. We literally just had to push the boys off set because Gio and Whit are so excited to talk about her. You've changed all of our lives by putting protein first and now we're talking about how to apply all this to menopause. Let's start with the basics. How does menopause affect women's bodies?\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 Well, first of all, menopause affects every part of a woman's body and just like you said, it's coming whether it's coming like Christmas...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's coming.\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 ...and tax day so we better be prepared for it. And our best defense is going to be supporting the health of skeletal muscle.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENOPAUSE PHYSICAL CHANGES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOSS OF 10% MUSCLE MASS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RISK OF LOW BONE DENSITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INCREASED CHANCE OF DEMENTIA & HEART DISEASE\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 A woman might lose 10% of their muscle mass...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 ...throughout that...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's - really significant.\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 ...yes, throughout their menopause transition. And that is the, one of the things that I always hear women talk about, middle belly weight gain, loss of muscle mass, they don't feel as strong. Do you know bone density is one of the things that they lose during that time of transition?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is, it is very fascinating. I'm - not quite there yet but that's why I want to know all this information, too. So, what kind of, type of exercise can you do to fight that loss of muscle?\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 It is not inevitable, which is really important to understand.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 The loss of muscle mass, or when a woman goes through menopause, you want to build as much muscle as possible going into menopause.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, before that.\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 Yes, so right now, we're gonna do burpees after this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXERCISE TO GAIN/KEEP MUSCLE MASS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRENGTH TRAIN 2-3 DAYS A WEEK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESISTANCE OR INTERVAL TRAINING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUST BE PROGRESSIVE OVER TIME\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (makes noise)\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 We're on it. What we're gonna do about it, is we are going to change and transition the way that the, we eat and the way that we train to maintain muscle mass. So, we are going to strength train two to three times a week.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 Whole body. It is nonnegotiable and not replaceable.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. So, strength training multiple times a week, nonnegotiable.\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 Two to three times, easy.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Non-replaceable. How about adjusting your diet to maintain muscle mass?\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 Also critical. You cannot maintain skeletal muscle mass without the proper building blocks. And that is dietary protein and one would understand that right now, the average American eats around 68 grams of protein daily, average female. You need closer to one gram per pound, ideal body weight. So, a minimum of 100 grams of protein.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, we're eating on average, about half as much protein as we need to.\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 We're not optimized for healthy aging.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 Because, actually, we need more protein as we age, especially women, especially to protect their muscle mass.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. I've - I follow you on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  so I've been hearing this. Thinking about the protein first in meals and the strength training. So important, especially in the 30s as women are heading into that time into the 40s and menopause. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DR. GABRIELLE LYON (AUTHOR OF "FOREVER STRONG")\par \par \uc2 And really simple things to do. You can have a protein shake. You can have six ounces of chicken, eggs, egg whites, Greek yogurt, lean beef. There's multiple choices, but you have to make sure that you execute and you keep that standard for yourself.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. I'm starting it with breakfast. Four eggs, fewer carbs, thanks to you. Thank you so much, Dr. Lyon. Now over to Samara for another check at the weather.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020241211ekbg0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW HOPE FOR A BREAST CANCER VACCINE? PROMISING RESULTS FROM PHASE ONE STUDY\b0\par\par\uc2 657 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP'S NEW CABINET PICKS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , GAETZ, GUEST, MILLER, MURKOWSKI, HEGSETH, GRAHAM, GABBARD, BIDEN, THUNE\par \par \uc2 BRUSH, WILDFIRES, NEW YORK, NEW JERSEY, WOODS\par \par \uc2 FORECAST, FIRE, TROPICAL, STORM\par \par \uc2 CIA, RAHMAN, SECRETS, ISRAEL, IRAN, TEIXEIRA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SYSTEM GAINING STRENGTH IN CARIBBEAN, COULD IMPACT FLORIDA NEXT WEEK\par \par \uc2 COMBS, MUSIC, LAWSUIT, ARMED, GILMORE, MCCRARY, "SHYNE"\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, HAUL, MOBILE, SAVE, CHINA, TEMU, \uc2 SHEIN\uc2 , TIKTOK, SHOP\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "GOLDEN BACHELORETTE"\par \par \uc2 "MENENDEZ", NETFLIX, HOCHMAN, GASC\u211\'00\'D3N, FREEDOM\par \par \uc2 FALLING, TREES, LIFE, HOMEOWNERS, LANG, SBD, ARBORIST\par \par \uc2 WIDMER, STUDY, GLP-1, DRUGS, PREDIABETES, OBESITY, ALCOHOL, HEART\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW FINDINGS ON WEIGHT LOSS DRUG BENEFITS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDIES FOCUS ON PREDIABETES & ALCOHOL USE DISORDER\par \par \uc2 "MOODENG", PYGMY, HIPPO, SONG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , GAETZ, GABBARD, THUNE\par \par \uc2 BURGLARY, MAHOMES, KELCE\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, VACCINE, STUDY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now promising results in an early study of a breast cancer vaccine. Eva Pilgrim is here with those details. Good morning to you, Eva.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Robin. The idea of a breast cancer vaccine is incredible. And while lot of people are excited, we want to be clear upfront, this is very, very early days toward making that a reality. This is a small phase one study. So, any potential vaccine is still years away, but the results are encouraging.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW HOPE FOR A BREAST CANCER VACCINE?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROMISING RESULTS FROM PHASE ONE STUDY\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The trial at Washington University looked at 18 women who had non-metastatic high-risk triple-negative breast cancer. Following standard treatment, the patients receive three doses of the vaccine. After three years, 16 out of the 18 women remained cancer free. And this is significant because, historically, only half of the patients would have been cancer free.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A phase two trial is currently underway. There's no timetable set for when that will be finished. But there certainly is an urgency.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER IN U.S.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) According to the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2 , more than 300,000 new cases of breast cancer will be diagnosed this year and more than 40,000 women will die. Black women are more likely to die from breast cancer than any other race or ethnic group.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BLACK WOMEN & BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And that could be because black women have a higher risk of triple-negative breast cancer. The type of cancer that this study is looking at.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, while this vaccine will not be available outside of the clinal trial anytime soon, there's a lot of hope for the future, guys.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There really is, Eva. Thank you, thank you. We'll see you on "GMA 3" later.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:11, "GOLDEN BACHELORETTE" COUPLE SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming up, on our "GMA Morning Menu," we hear first from "Golden Bachelorette" couple Joan and Chock on their happily ever after.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:15, POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also ahead, "Gladiator II" star Paul Mescal ready to get into the "Hot Ones" hot seat. Robin is giving you a run for your money. We have a sneak peek in "Pop News."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:21, DEALS N& STEALS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, Tory Johnson is your personal holiday shopper this morning with "Deals & Steals." She has gifts for everyone on your list starting at just $10. And Lara has some great guests upstairs.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:48, "WIZARDS OF BAKING" STARS LIVE!\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I do. Carla Hall is with us. She's with James and Oliver Phelps, "Harry Potter" fame, of course. So happy to have you here. They're conjuring up some delicious wizard-themed baked treats. We'll explain why coming up on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020241209ekbe0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GETTING OVER MAMMOGRAM ANXIETY WHY THERE'S LESS TO FEAR THAN EVER BEFORE\b0\par\par\uc2 979 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAGING WILDFIRE\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, WILDFIRE, GARDNER, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, FIRE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , TRANSITION, BIDEN, HARRIS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, WRAY, SMITH, RIOTERS, CABINET, RFK, MUSK\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, REPUBLICANS, CONGRESS, HOUSE, SENATE\par \par \uc2 WAR, UKRAINE, RUSSIA, NORTH KOREA, TROOPS, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 WALL, STREET, STOCKS, FED, INTEREST, RATES, BORROWING, MORTGAGE, CREDIT\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "DANCING WITH THE STARS"\par \par \uc2 KOHBERGER, MURDERS, IDAHO, DEATH, PENALTY, GONCALVES\par \par \uc2 PAYNE, DEATH, INVESTIGATION, 911, HOTEL, DRUGS, EMPLOYEE, DEALER\par \par \uc2 RETAILERS, RETURN, POLICY, FRAUD, REI, TARGET, AMAZON, WARDROBE, SHOPLIFTING\par \par \uc2 "CIRCULATION," EXERCISE, BLOOD, PRESSURE, HEART\par \par \uc2 BRANSGROVE, INSTAGRAM, DANCE\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING AT 8\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , HARRIS, TRANSITION\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , SMITH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP'S LEGAL BATTLES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPECIAL COUNSEL TO WIND DOWN JANUARY 6TH & CLASSIFIED DOCUMENTS CASE\par \par \uc2 "DANCING WITH THE STARS", MAHER, BERSTEN\par \par \uc2 MAMMOGRAM, ANXIETY, CIGLER, COMEN, KAMAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GETTING OVER MAMMOGRAM ANXIETY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY THERE'S LESS TO FEAR THAN EVER BEFORE\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, now we have anxiety that many women live with. It's getting a mammogram. But no matter how long it's been since you got one, you shouldn't wait any longer. Erielle Reshef, she is here. You're going to tell us ways to overcome the anxiety?\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. And you can relate to this, Robin, it does create anxiety. I have mine actually scheduled tomorrow. And whether it's the exam itself or the potential results, for many women, the idea of mammogram can be very daunting. But doctors say screening has shown to reduce breast cancer deaths by 22%. And we spoke to two women about how they tackled their fear and anxiety and made their appointments.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Fifty-one-year-old Megan Kennedy (PH) skipped her mammogram for two years during COVID. Nervous, she thought maybe it would be easier not to know her cancer status.\par \par \uc2 PATIENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I just didn't have time for cancer.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Breast cancer survivor Deborah Hall (PH) worries about a recurrence before scheduling hers.\par \par \uc2 CANCER SURVIVOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Initially, it was a kind of terror. But I would say it's evolved to maybe high anxiety at this point.\par \par \uc2 DR TESSA CIGLER (ONCOLOGIST WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE / NY-PRESBYTERIAN)\par \par \uc2 I see this all the time with women. When women are overdue for a mammogram, it becomes more emotionally difficult to schedule their mammogram because they're feeling anxious or guilty or nervous because they're late.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But despite the dread, these women did schedule their mammograms. And doctors say, so should you.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Look, there's a ton of anxiety around getting mammograms. That's completely understandable. But it's incredibly important.\par \par \uc2 DR ARIF KAMAL (CHIEF PATIENT OFFICER AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 So, if a woman says, well, it's been two years, what's the point? I say, no, there's still an opportunity to catch it early now. The earlier we find it, the less likely it is to have spread and the more likely that we can use simpler methods to cure it with cure rates greater than 95%.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER DEATH RATE\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That's right. There's actually less to fear than ever before. The breast cancer death rate has dropped over 44% since the late '80s because of advancements in early detection and treatment. And with updated mammogram recommendations starting at age 40 for those not at high risk, the chances of catching a cancer early are better than ever.\par \par \uc2 DR ARIF KAMAL (CHIEF PATIENT OFFICER AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY)\par \par \uc2 The good news is, 95% of the time, we don't find something that's of worry that needs a follow-up.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (BREAST ONCOLOGIST NYU LANGONE HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Also made treatments far less toxic and far less invasive than they used to be. So, there's lots of reason to have hope.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A good way to cope? Make your mammogram a group effort.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW YOU CAN COPE WITH THE WORRY\par \par \uc2 CANCER SURVIVOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Get a buddy to go with you. Get someone who will push you out of the door, if that's necessary, to go get it.\par \par \uc2 PATIENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 My very dear college friends and I made a pact that we would hold each other accountable for screenings.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Whether you missed your mammogram for a year or missed it for ten years, doctors say, there's no time like right now to get it done.\par \par \uc2 DR TESSA CIGLER (ONCOLOGIST WEILL CORNELL MEDICINE / NY-PRESBYTERIAN)\par \par \uc2 It's never too late. Even if you're overdue, just remember to do your screening mammogram.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hopefully, this is taking a big deep breath for all of us. Experts say, breast cancer detected at stage 2 or 3, even if it's not the earliest, still allows for a wide range of effective treatments that can lead to long-term survival. If you are worried about how to pay for your mammogram and you may not have insurance, check your local Department of Health. They may offer free screenings. That is critical, trying to get rid of some of those reasons we, those excuses we build up.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm. Right.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You've got to go even if it produces a little bit of anxiety.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know. Fear cancer, don't fear the mammogram. All right.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a very good point.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. And you're going tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm going tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Thank you, Erielle, very much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020241125ekb70000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CLOSER LOOK AT KEY ISSUES HOW IMMIGRATION & ABORTION IMPACTED THE ELECTION\b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE YOUR VOTE 2024\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, VICTORY, ELECTION, HISTORIC, SUPPORTERS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , HARRIS, ELECTION, TRANSITION\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, VICTORY, CAMPAIGN, BATTLEGROUNDS\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, CAMPAIGN, DEFEAT, TRUMP, DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 BRAZILE, PRIEBUS, CHRISTIE, TRUMP, VICTORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLASH FLOOD THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, IMMIGRATION, BORDER, INITIATIVES\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rebecca, our thanks to you. We're gonna get to some of the other top issues. The border and reproductive rights. Elizabeth Schulze is in the border state of Arizona. And, Elizabeth, Arizona also just one of 10 states where abortion was on the ballot. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLOSER LOOK AT KEY ISSUES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW IMMIGRATION & ABORTION IMPACTED THE ELECTION\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Robin. In those 10 states, seven of them, including here in Arizona passed ballot initiatives to expand abortion access. So propositions in Arizona, Colorado, Maryland, Montana, Missouri, Nevada and New York passed with broad support. So basically that means that those state constitutions will now be amended to enshrine the right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION ON THE BALLOT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW THE INITIATIVES IN 10 STATES FARED\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this is especially notable in Missouri where the total ban there will now be reversed. On the other hand, in Florida, voters failed to meet a 60% vote threshold there. So they did not pass the measure, its six week ban will stay in place. Voters are also keeping in place a total abortion ban in South Dakota and a 12 week ban in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So the bottom line, this is still a galvanizing issue for voters, including in some red and swing states. But the winning streak for abortion rights initiatives on the ballot since the end of Roe V. Wade, has now come to an end, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Elizabeth, what can you tell us about the immigration ballot measure that passed there in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLOSER LOOK AT KEY ISSUES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW IMMIGRATION & ABORTION IMPACTED THE ELECTION\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is a proposition that passed with broad support, more than 60% of the vote based on the current tally. What this will do is make it a state crime for anyone crossing into the US from the Mexico. So that means that basically local and state law enforcement could arrest anyone who they suspect crossed into the border without proper authorization.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Judges would also be able to issue deportation orders under this proposition. It mimics a similar law that was passed in Texas, but that law, Robin, is still winding its way through the courts.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We do remember that. All right, Elizabeth, thank you. Michael.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020241122ekb60005t\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION ON THE BALLOT HOW THE INITIATIVES IN 10 STATES FARED\b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE YOUR VOTE 2024\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, VICTORY, ELECTION, HISTORIC, SUPPORTERS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , HARRIS, ELECTION, TRANSITION\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, VICTORY, CAMPAIGN, BATTLEGROUNDS\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, CAMPAIGN, DEFEAT, TRUMP, DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 BRAZILE, PRIEBUS, CHRISTIE, TRUMP, VICTORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLASH FLOOD THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, IMMIGRATION, BORDER, INITIATIVES\par \par \uc2 MILLER, TRUMP, PRESIDENCY, BORDER, ECONOMY, VOTERS\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, SENATE, HOUSE, REPUBLICANS, DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, LEGAL, CIVIL, CRIMINAL, SPECIAL COUNSEL\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, CRIMINAL, CIVIL, SMITH, SENTENCING, JUDGEMENTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE DANGER\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION, TRUMP, COALITION, VOTING\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION, TRUMP, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION, ABORTION, RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Another top issue, abortion on the ballot in ten states. Let's go back to Elizabeth Schulze in Phoenix with how those initiatives fared. Good morning again, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning again, Robin. This is a win for abortion rights advocates here in Arizona. It's one of seven states, including Colorado, Maryland, Montana, Missouri, Nevada, and New York where voters directly decided to amend their state constitution to enshrine the right to an abortion. Similar initiatives in three other states, Florida, South Dakota, and Nebraska failed.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So their total or strict abortion bans will now stay in place. When you look at the exit polling data, 19 percent of women say abortion is the most important issue to them. That goes up to 42 percent among women under the age of 30. Up until now, every time that abortion had been on the ballot since the end of Roe v. Wade, the abortion rights position won. This is clearly still a motivating issue for voters, but we are seeing there might be a ceiling to some of that support. Robin?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We will see that. And Elizabeth, I know you've been up all night long. Thank you so much for being with us this morning. Appreciate you as always.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020241122ekb600064\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TRUMP ELECTION VICTORY FIRST CONVICTED FELON TO WIN PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION\b0\par\par\uc2 1,887 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 MOUNTAIN FIRE, VENTURA COUNTY, BROAD FIRE, MALIBU\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , ELECTION, VICTORY, FELON, HARRIS, KING, VOTERS\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Melissa. We turn now to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's decisive election victory over Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . It was a race like no other, with Trump surviving two assassination attempts and Harris jumping onto the ballot with just over 100 days left in the race. But how did the former president clinch the win?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  clinching an historic victory.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We're gonna make our country better than it ever has been.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The president-elect, now set to return to the White House as the first former president to return to power after losing an election in more than 130 years. He's also the first convicted felon to be elected to the nation's top office.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, it's an astonishment what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has done, right? It's unlike anything in our political history.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This afternoon, his opponent, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , officially conceding the race.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 While I concede this election, I do not concede the fight that fueled this campaign.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Harris telling her supporters not to telling her supporters not to despair, but rather to fight on for their ideals.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The important thing is don't ever give up. Don't ever give up. Don't ever give up, don't ever stop trying to make the world a better place. You have power. You have power.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Democrats in the party are gonna do a lot of soul searching. There are going to be a lot of questions about whether Harris focused too much on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and not enough on the issues, especially whether she did enough to differentiate herself from President Biden, especially with the issue of the economy top of mind for so many voters.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm excited to see what he does this time around.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Last night, Trump promised to bring people together.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We're gonna help our country heal. We have a country that needs help and it needs help very badly.\par \par \uc2 AVERI HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I am skeptical that a Trump administration would usher in any sort of unification. I think the fact that president-elect Trump won the popular vote actually emboldens him to pursue the most extreme parts of his agenda.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 He won't accomplish nothing. He's just for the rich.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I hope there's not retaliation for the Democrats who voted against him.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We'll give them the benefit of the doubt. We'll even make ourselves available to see how we can work together.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Douglas King is a Michigan voter who we first talked to over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS KING (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 I look forward to Trump bringing back the United States economy. I look forward to a secure border and I look forward to peace in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A member of the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2 , he told us that Trump had more support than people realized.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS KING (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 Under Trump was the most secure I felt in the auto industry. I was always afraid of losing my job through Clinton, through Obama and through Biden.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MOST IMPORTANT POLICY ISSUES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ECONOMY 31%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION 14%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IMMIGRATION 11%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FOREIGN POLICY 4%\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The economy ranked as voters' number one policy issue. Nearly half of those polled saying they are worse off compared to four years ago.\par \par \uc2 AVERI HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We know that Trump really leaned into the issue of the economy. As he went on the campaign trail, he would often begin his rallies by saying, "Do you believe that you're better off than you were four years ago?" And that became a really powerful message.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The real driver was people's deep unhappiness with the economy. No matter what the statistics said, I think it wasn't just this present day economy, but the sense that the American future is, is, is being taken from them, that that all American dream economic that they had. I think people feel it's slipping away from them and they're angry about it.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) "Nightline's" Ashan Singh spent some time with young first-time voters in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 ASHAN SINGH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What are some of the issues that have stood out to you?\par \par \uc2 YOUNG VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I pay attention a lot to the economy.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Isaac Gurley (PH) and Caleb Root (PH), both 18, are high school seniors and say Trump's message resonated with them.\par \par \uc2 YOUNG VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Seems like every time I get on social media, you get tons of political ads.\par \par \uc2 YOUNG VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I think it's important to, like, fact check it, though, because there's a lot of lies on there, too.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In deep blue New York City overnight, Austin also talked to Trump supporters.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We need a Republican in the White House to make America great again.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 You run things like a business. That's what America needs.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I like that he's gonna secure the border. I've been to the border multiple times. I don't think Harris was gonna do anything to secure our border.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While most young Americans voted for Harris, white men particularly flocked to Trump.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's so interesting. Young men gonna Trump. I think for a couple of reasons. First, economic opportunity. And I think also, the way Trump performs masculinity and talks about it, I think a lot of young men see that as a more traditional way of being a man that they feel is disfavored by the Democrats and they're suspicious of that.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As Democrats study the gaps in voter demographics, they are also weighing how their message failed to resonate, some pointing to this crucial moment where the Vice President failed to differentiate herself from President Biden.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: "THE VIEW"\par \par \uc2 SUNNY HOSTIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 If anything, would you have done something differently than President Biden during the past four years?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 There is not a thing that comes to mind in terms of, and I've been a part of, of, of most of the decisions that have had impact.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Harris loss is especially frustrating for some, considering that issues like paid family leave and abortion and other typically progressive causes did well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION ON THE BALLOT: MAP OF UNITED STATES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 57% VOTED YES: NEEDED 60%\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Abortion measures passed in eight of the ten states where it was on the ballot, like Missouri, where voters overturned a nearly total ban. Florida did not pass its ballot measure because even though the majority of voters, 57%, supported restoring abortion services up to viability, the state requires a supermajority of 60%.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Harris campaign really put a bet, a big bet, that abortion was gonna drive the election, as it had in other places, but this was a national election for the presidency of the United States and I think that, they overloaded perhaps emphasis on, on abortion.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Republicans are also celebrating the shifting balance of power in Congress, the GOP taking control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNEL (REP) (KENTUCKY)\par \par \uc2 Certainly a happy day for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While the ultimate majority in the House is still in the air, with several races too close to call, Republicans appear to be leading Democrats, which could give Trump a clear road to enact his new agenda.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is going to be supercharged Trumpism.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANTUCCI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It doesn't feel like any guardrails for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It definitely feels like a mandate based on the vote and one of the things that's fascinating to remember here, it's not so much the electoral college victory, which obviously got \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the White House for a second term, it's the popular vote.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump has reportedly promised to put billionaire mega donor and \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  in charge of government efficiency. Musk has said he could cut at least two trillion dollars from the federal budget. Last Friday, during a virtual town hall on X, formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he admitted it would be painful.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK (BILLIONAIRE)\par \par \uc2 We have to reduce spending to live within our means and, yeah, that, that necessarily involves some, some temporary hardship, but it, it, it will ensure long term prosperity.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today we also heard from \uc2 Robert F Kennedy Jr\uc2 , an outspoken anti-vaccine advocate, who talked to NPR about the new Trump administration's health goals.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Of course, we're not gonna take vaccines away from anybody.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kennedy called himself a senior health advisor and Trump has said he would give Kennedy a major role in public health in his new administration. Today, RFK said the new administration plans to recommend against using fluoride in public water, despite its known dental health benefits, a major move medical organizations oppose.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANTUCCI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 He has a very controversial past and a lot of controversial positions. It might make it easier for team Trump if they really want him in the administration to avoid scrutiny. Avoid a Senate confirmation, give him a post that does not need that.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Before Trump gets to inauguration in January, he still has legal battles that must be addressed. His sentencing is supposed to be later this month on those 34 felony convictions for falsifying business records. But when it comes to the Department of Justice investigations into his alleged mishandling of classified documents and his alleged role in the January 6th insurrection, sources tell ABC News the DOJ is now in talks to end those investigations.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He will not be held to account under the criminal law for anything he did leading up to January 6th, trying to overturn the election or inciting that riot. Winners get to determine the way history is written and if he has a successful presidency, history won't pay any attention to it, either.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEXT ON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT HAPPENED?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And when we come back, who showed up for Trump at the polls where Harris fell short? Our powerhouse team breaks it all down.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020241111ekb60002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ELECTION NIGHT 2024 ROAD TO THE WHITE HOUSE\b0\par\par\uc2 21,026 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ELECTION, HARRIS, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2024\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 This is an ABC News special. The time has finally come. Right now all across America, votes are being counted. Who will be your next president on this historic night, with the stakes so high. Live from ABC News election headquarters in New York City. Now reporting, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to ABC News live election night coverage. A majority of polls across the US are now closed, including six of the seven battleground states. Nine states still open, including the last remaining swing state of Nevada. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  racing to the magic number, they have to get there, 270, as we showed you. At this hour, millions of voters remain in line waiting to cast their ballots in what - many consider to be the highest stakes election, the most consequential election in recent history.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLORIDA AMENDMENT, RIGHT TO ABORTION BEFORE FETAL VIABILITY\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's get right to it at this hour. First of all, we wanted to take note of the Florida abortion amendment. Amendment 4 in Florida, which would have added language to the Florida constitution, the declaration of rights to protect the right to abortion before fetal viability or when necessary to protect a patient's health. Florida voting that down, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (inaudible) David, abortion is on the ballot in ten states across the country, Florida being one of them. I want you to take a look. They needed to get 60% to pass here. 57% said yes. 43% saying no. Florida has a six week abortion ban right now. That is what Florida governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed into law there. Former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself has even called that too harsh. We're going to be watching some other states tonight. These are the ten states where abortion is going to be on the ballot. This from Florida, though, David is notable. It's the first time that we are seeing abortion on the ballot actually fail, because you remember that abortion on the ballot has won in every single state, including conservative states like Kentucky, Ohio and Kansas.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION ON THE BALLOT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE OF ABORTION ACCESS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's also going to be on the ballot tonight in two battlegrounds, Nevada and Arizona. This is going to be a major blow to Democrats and also just abortion rights advocates. Take a look at this map. This is the abortion restrictions after Roe vs. Wade was overturned. And I really want you to pay attention here to the South. Florida has a six week abortion ban. So does Georgia, as well as South Carolina. There's almost nowhere to go in the South for women who are seeking abortion care. They have to travel very far. That's why Democrats really wanted to see this pass.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's go over to Kate Shaw, our legal analyst. Kate, what do you make of this?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, so, of course it's right that under Florida's rules this amendment failed. But you look at the numbers, 57% of Floridians actually did vote to protect abortion under the state's constitution. So it just has a higher threshold than other states. So of course it's right this amendment failed. But I also think the data tells us that even a lot of voters presumably who voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  also voted to protect abortion. I think the question is, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has tried to make the case that even though abortion is only on the ballot in ten states this cycle, it's really on the ballot everywhere, because it matters so much who the president is, what the law surrounding abortion will be. The question is how that message resonates if at all in other states.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And in particular in Florida, Kate, to vote it down and to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when you look out west to Nevada and Arizona where it's also on the ballot, it's going to get into this whole notion of sort of a split ticket, whether or not you vote for the amendment, but then you don't vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , you don't tie the two together.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So - I think that's, we're going to see throughout the night how much voters are separating these issues out. But of course in these states there are lower thresholds for actually voting to protect abortion and maybe you will see if Florida voters thought, well, abortion will be protected in our state, we can still vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because we'll have protections here for voting for this amendment, that may or may not be true depending on what, if Trump is elected president, what happens at the federal level with respect to abortion. But there may be complex calculuses going on about what state law will mean with respect to abortion, and what the federal government could do with respect to abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. We're going to continue to track it, particularly in those western swing states. Let's go back over to the map. Rick, you're studying North Carolina right now. 41% of the vote in. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leads by about 100,000 votes at this point.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. It's really kind of a microcosm of the divided country. It's not that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  isn't hitting her numbers. She is largely hitting the numbers, doing about what she needs to against the FiveThirtyEight benchmarks, about what she needs to do when you compare it to what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did. We're looking at Asheville, which is kind of the blue dot in that western mountain range that was hit so hard by the hurricane. She's over performing the expectations there. A lot of this so far is the early vote. We'll see what else rolls in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing exactly what he needs to be doing outside of Charlotte, in the suburbs, in the more rural areas outside of there. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  running ahead of where he needs to be to win the state, according to our FiveThirtyEight numbers. That's a county, union county now, that has 94% of the vote in already. So it's a significant amount of vote. Davidson County, another example of this where he's slightly ahead of his benchmark. We're seeing them run, you know, kind of an even race in the places we have a lot of data right now. We'll see where it lands at the end of the night. But Trump is turning out people in very red parts of the state and across the country. It's a trend we're seeing even in states that aren't that competitive. The Trump base is out in force.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's look at Nash County. Can you find Nash County for me? It's one of these boomerang counties that went for Trump but then went for Biden. Also New Hanover in North Carolina here. We probably should take a slightly deeper dive on whether or not...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Nash County is, Trump is winning.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah - so it's right here, right outside of Raleigh. This is one of those that we're watching as you mentioned, David, because there's a possibility at least for Democrats to flip counties like this. You see how close Hillary Clinton came, how close \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came. Right now \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  not quite over the finish line. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  winning by about 1,800 votes in - Nash County. And the benchmark would suggest - he doesn't even need to win that county. It could slip away from him and he'd still be in line. This is a small county. But you're right, it's emblematic of what's going on across the state and really across the region of the country.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and it could be a bellwether in fact because this is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to do, is flip some of this territory back his way from four years ago. We'll just keep our eye on it. I want to take this out to \uc2 Byron Pitts\uc2  because he's in Asheville tonight. Byron, there was a lot of concern obviously over the victims of Hurricane Helene and whether or not they would be able to vote. Both campaigns saying that they did like what they saw on the ground as far as the effort to help people have their voices heard. What have you seen there on the ground?\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 David, this has to be one of the feel-good stories of this election night regardless of which side wins. David, you spent a lot of time here after the hurricane and you saw the devastation firsthand. It is remarkable the turnout here. Western North Carolina outperformed the rest of the state in early voting. And that's a good sign for the Trump campaign. As you guys have been discussing, western North Carolina has been reliably Trump country and he has to do well here and he is doing well here. The voting is high. There are parts of the western part of the state where they've had to vote in tents. We talked to one voter, a pastor, who called the response here in western North Carolina, he calls it the miracle in the mountains.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is a man, consider this, he had to drive an hour and 45 minutes to vote. He had to leave his house, drive into Tennessee, circle a mountain, come back to this community, cross a river, before he could vote. And it speaks to not just the resilience of the people in this part of the country but also their commitment to democracy. David, I talked to a member of the Trump campaign here in North Carolina. He said the race in North Carolina tonight is as tight as a facelift. And so while they're doing well in North Carolina there are other areas of the state that look favorable for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . 55.4% of the early vote in North Carolina, that was, that went to women. And that's a record in the state. 24% of the - uncommitted voters were first-time voters, which is an indication that young people are voting in high numbers here.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There are people standing in line still in Charlotte in some of the university areas, likely young voters. Also potentially a good sign for the Harris campaign. Earlier you and Linsey were talking about black voters in North Carolina. I want to mention to you the central part of the state and \uc2 North Carolina A&T\uc2 . \uc2 North Carolina A&T\uc2  is the largest HBCU in the country. Aggie pride runs deep in this state. So expect Harris to do well in that - part of the state. Also eastern North Carolina, where A&T has its largest, one of its largest alumni chapters. So there's a lot going on in North Carolina. Still tight. And both camps see reason to be optimistic at this point.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Byron Pitts\uc2  on the ground in Asheville. You can see they still have so much work ahead of them in the cleanup of the aftermath of Hurricane Helene. Rick, let's pull up North Carolina since we've heard so much from Byron on this and let's visit a couple more of these areas. Can you do the Wake County, obviously the research triangle, Raleigh, Durham County, right around there, let's just see how both candidates are performing according to the benchmark of where they would need to be at this hour.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, the research triangle where you have Durham with Duke University and University of North Carolina, just one county over, this is an area where Democrats have really leaned in and tried to get as many of the votes as they can. You see \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's numbers tick up a little bit to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  so far with about 3/4 of the vote in, doing better than either of the previous two Democratic candidates. And that is about what she needs to be getting in a place like Wake County. You see these big numbers, and you see her getting 140,000 votes more than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  there. That is what she needs to be doing in a state where she currently is trailing.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We don't have a lot of vote yet in Durham, but this is going to be heavily for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . The numbers in Orange County, again, that's where \uc2 UNC Chapel Hill\uc2  is. You're seeing, again, Harris about hitting the numbers that she needs to at the FiveThirtyEight - in the FiveThirtyEight benchmarks. So this area is coming through for her. There's no question about that. Again, though, those gains are being offset by Trump gains elsewhere in the state. Just to look down at Brunswick County, it's an area where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's running a little ahead of his benchmark, and as we were discussing a little earlier, outside Charlotte, again, these are places where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should be winning and he is winning. Winning by a little bit more than he'd be expected to.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's kind of interesting what we're seeing here in some of these counties. He's outperforming slightly and so is she in some of the urban areas, and some of the areas, the research triangle in particular, and with students. Let's go over to Charlotte. Let's just check out Charlotte one more time, Mecklenburg, and see the where we are.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we have almost nothing in from Charlotte so far. It's just about all the early by mail vote. So we're gonna have to wait to see how that comes in.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And at least in the early by vote, she's outperforming where she needs to be. So it's kind of an unfinished story here obviously in North Carolina. 41% of the vote in and appears to be quite a tight race here in North Carolina. What do we know about Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Pennsylvania we've been watching really closely. Because so far the Harris team has to like what it sees. A lot of the vote so far has been early and as we mentioned, early vote is not as big a story as it is elsewhere. But Harrisburg as an example right now, that county, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not getting anywhere near where he would need on the FiveThirtyEight benchmarks and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , again, it's early, we'll see where it goes, running significantly ahead of where the previous Democrats have. You asked me a little bit earlier about Scranton and Wilkes-Barre, Lackawanna County, another story where Harris, with about 2/3 of the vote in, running ahead of the FiveThirtyEight numbers, running ahead of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Luzerne County, that's one that has slipped away from Democrats. If these numbers hold, that would be a true surprise. I wouldn't be surprised if they change as the night goes on. And we've been focused in on those collar counties. We're waiting for a lot of vote to come in from them. But Harris running ahead of the FiveThirtyEight benchmarks and ahead of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's numbers. That is probably the most closely watched of the Philadelphia suburban counties, about 5%, 6% of the vote overall. And we're of course waiting on a lot of vote out of Philadelphia. If you just look at this, you see those big blue dots at the eastern parts of the state. Once again, those are places we're waiting for a vote. Those red dots, though, are significant and they add up as they move across the state.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 20% of the vote in in Pennsylvania. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with an early lead here. And again, so much of the vote still needs to be counted but you've got to wonder if we're looking, and it's early. I'm just saying if we're looking at this blue wall strategy coming to life, as we're watching these numbers come in from Georgia, where she's not hitting some of the benchmarks she needs. In North Carolina, still kind of an untold story. Pretty close, actually, when you dig into those counties and see where the vote still remains. But in Pennsylvania at least with the early vote so far, it appears that she's hitting some of the benchmarks that she hoped to and outperforming them, particularly in the collar counties there, with some - more conservative leaning suburbs of Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, quite a story unfolding. So this is where the numbers come in, this is where it actually gets really interesting. So don't go away. We'll dig back into Pennsylvania. Michigan also, the first numbers coming in from Michigan. We'll look at it all right after the break. Don't go away. ABC News live coverage of the presidential election continues right after this.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 270 NEEDED TO WIN\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to our live coverage of the 2024 presidential election. Here's a quick look at where things stand here. Take a look, it's the path to 270 as you can see on your screen. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  30 electoral votes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's 154 tonight. They need 270 to win. Still very early. Georgia hasn't been called. North Carolina hasn't been called. It's getting interesting in Pennsylvania and we'll look at those numbers. The first numbers coming in from Michigan, right after the break. Our live coverage of election night continues right after this.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to election night 2024 on ABC. Here again, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DELAWARE, PROJECTED WINNER HARRIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE DELAWARE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to our live election night coverage right here on ABC. We do have a winner, a projected winner in the state of Delaware. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  picking up the three electoral votes. She wins the state of Delaware. And in the Senate race there, the Democrat, Congresswoman \uc2 Lisa Blunt Rochester\uc2 , four term congresswoman looking to become the new senator in that open seat. She wins that race. And Rachel Scott, this is quite a win.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it is, David. History made here in Delaware. \uc2 Lisa Blunt Rochester\uc2  becoming the first black woman to win a Senate seat in the state. David, I had a chance to sit down with her just a few weeks ago. We talked about the history that she was going to be potentially making. She told me she's going to be thinking about all the little girls, not only just having Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  at the top of the ticket, but also the history she could be making in this state as well. David, we're also going to be keeping our eye also on one more state and this is going to be Maryland, where Angela Alsobrooks is running, also has a chance to make history tonight.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She's running against former governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  in this state as well. Angela Alsobrooks close ally of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . A very close friend. But if she were to win this race, this would be the first time that we would ever see two black women serving in the Senate at the same time.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This has been a fascinating race to actually watch because the two are somewhat friendly in their debate. I don't know, it was the most cordial debate I saw in this entire election cycle.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It really was.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They started out by saying, I have respect for you, I have respect for you, we worked together when he was the governor and she was the county executive. But we'll watch that race very carefully as well tonight. Of course, popular Republican governor who went against Trump at certain points during the Trump administration, looking to compete in a heavily Democratic state. But history already made, though, in the state of Delaware. Rachel, thanks. Rick Klein, what are you seeing in Michigan?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah we're just, our first glimpse into the numbers in Michigan. It's an interesting state because it's one of them that actually made it easier to start processing and counting those ballots. They also did in person voting in Michigan for the first time on a widespread way. We should note that everything we're seeing here, it's kind of mixed. Some election day, some early vote. We also have seen nothing at all from Wayne County, which is Detroit and Dearborn. Something we're going to talk about a lot so far.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But Washtenaw County right outside of Detroit, that's an area where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  needs to be doing extremely well. We're tracking her below so far her benchmark with about half the vote in, she's not quite hitting that number. It's an area where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  really cleaned up, did boater than Hillary Clinton's number. Right now at least, again it's hard to tell because we don't have a full portrait of the pictures, those are not great numbers...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , that's Ann Arbor.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ann Arbor, \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , a liberal area of the state. It's also got some suburban areas, - a good number of Republicans live there as well. Oakland County, another big county that we're going to be watching all night tonight. You can see from the FiveThirtyEight numbers, Harris a little bit ahead of where she would want to be, but we're going to see this storyline continually. Republican parts of the state, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hitting his numbers just about everywhere. I haven't found many places yet where he isn't hitting where he wants to be in terms of the FiveThirtyEight benchmarks or in terms of the history. The turnout machine, even if it wasn't on the ground the same way it was for the Democrats, seems to have been working at least in these initial numbers in Michigan and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's get right to Juju Chang. She's in Detroit for us. President Biden, this was his biggest win of the swing states, about 154,000 votes, and obviously considered part of this blue wall that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is trying to keep.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And they're trying to shore up the cracks in the blue wall. Absolutely, David. The Secretary of State was here just moments ago telling us she believes the votes here will be tabulated between midnight and 6:00 am throughout Michigan. We spoke earlier to the Macomb County clerk and he believes it's a big night for team Trump. That said, we spoke earlier to the COO of the Detroit Board of Elections and he said, look, we're on pace to have the count tonight, and we believe there's going to be turnout of more than 50% in Detroit, which is a number that might be of interest to the Harris campaign.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, the Detroit election official says there's been no chaos, no pandemonium, no banging on the windows like there was four years ago. He says he, jokingly said he thought he heard them singing "Kumbaya" in the hall.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Juju Chang in Michigan. We'll be checking back in with you, Juju. I'm looking at North Carolina. Less than 100,000 votes, about a 70,000 vote margin between the two candidates. Stay tuned here. The numbers are just coming in, they're just getting interesting. Your local news standing by. We'll see you in just a couple of minutes here. Don't go away.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLEGROUND STATE POLL CLOSINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 270 NEEDED TO WIN\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And good evening, everyone. I'm Kyra Phillips. You are watching ABC News and the race to the White House. At 9:00 pm Eastern time, more polls closed across the country, including six of the seven battleground states. And we are seeing results come in from several states now. ABC News projecting Trump will win North Dakota, South Dakota, Louisiana and Texas. ABC News also projects Harris will win Delaware, Maryland, Rhode Island and Massachusetts. As for electoral votes, right now, 30 for Harris, 162 to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . As you know, to win, a candidate needs 270.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLORIDA AMENDMENT 4\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In Florida with 86% of the expected vote reporting, the Florida abortion ballot initiative is projected to fail. It got 57% of the vote. It needed 60% of the vote to pass. The referendum would have put abortion rights into the state constitution. It's the first time since Roe was overturned that abortion rights have been on the ballot put directly to voters and failed. Let's get to our deputy political director Avery Harper. Avery, exit poll numbers starting to come in as well as voters begin to fill in their ballots. What are some of the key concerns?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRELIMINARY EXIT POLL NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 AVERY HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, when we look at some of this preliminary exit poll data, what we see is that the state of democracy has actually edged out the economy as the number one issue that voters care about as they consider who to vote for, followed by the economy. Abortion, you see there in the middle with 14% of voters saying that. Immigration, which has been an issue that the Trump campaign has really leaned into, falling there at the fourth slot at 11%. And foreign policy at the end.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. All right, Avery Harper, thank you so much. We'll talk through the night. Let's head to Arizona now where we find ABC's Elizabeth Schulze. You're in Phoenix, which is in Maricopa County, the most populous county in the state. The polls there have now closed. What can you tell us about early voting there so far?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So Kyra, it was record voting across the state of Arizona. Here in Maricopa County, now that the polls have closed, they will start to tabulate those ballots. About 60% of all of Arizona's ballots will be counted here. We asked the Secretary of State today how long he expects the process will take. He said this could take 10 to 13 days. So these results could still take some time, Kyra. A reminder that President Biden just narrowly won this state by 10,000 votes back in 2020. That was the first time that a Democrat won Arizona since 1996. So this is expected to be a tight race. But we are hearing from an official from the Arizona GOP. They're optimistic about turnout today from Republicans. We're really going to be closely watching those independent voters here, Kyra.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Elizabeth Schulze there in Phoenix for us. Thank you so much. All right. So Avery, let's talk about the economy. It was interesting looking at how the various concerns for voters in the exit polls fanned out there. The economy clearly at the top there. I was actually surprised at how low immigration stood, considering all the coverage we've been doing on both of those issues for the past couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 AVERY HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We know that perceptions of the economy have been something that have been driving folks to head to the polls. And when you look at this exit poll data, preliminary exit poll data that we're seeing, you see that 32% say the condition of the economy is excellent or good, and 67% say not so good or poor. And that is something that the Trump campaign has been leaning into.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Avery, thank you. I'm Kyra Phillips. Stay with ABC News. Our team is bringing you election results all throughout the night. And - this just in. Go ahead. New York for Harris. Okay. There, projected by ABC News. There you go. News happening by the second. Stay with us. We'll continue to bring you all the latest results throughout the night.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2024\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 This is an ABC News special. Election night in America. Who will be the next president of the United States? From ABC News headquarters in New York, here again, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to ABC News live election night coverage. Now 9:30 here in the East. The polls have now closed in 41 states across this great country, including six of the seven battleground states. The battle will close in just about 30 minutes. At this hour, here are the results we can report so far.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In Pennsylvania, again, this is just still under way in these swing states. Pennsylvania, 55% to 43%. Again, we have to look at the counties and what we're talking about when we're looking at that vote. Still very early. In Michigan, you can see \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  taking an early lead in Michigan. And in Wisconsin tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with the early lead. We'll dive into where these numbers are actually coming from in just a moment. We're also tracking Georgia. We're tracking North Carolina and Arizona at this hour. No numbers from Arizona, though the polls have now closed. In Georgia, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  holding on to that lead. It looks like it's now just about more than 100,000 votes now. Widening his lead and holding firm.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In North Carolina, still a close race. Again there's some outstanding vote in some of the Democratic areas. We'll dig deeper into that in just a moment. New York we can now project, ABC News projecting that the state of New York, 28 electoral votes, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has won the state of New York.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW YORK, PROJECTED WINNER HARRIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RHODE ISLAND, PROJECTED WINNER HARRIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOUISIANA, PROJECTED WINNER TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 270 NEEDED TO WIN\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In Rhode island, four electoral votes. Vice President Harris wins the four votes there. Former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  winning the state of Louisiana, eight electoral votes he picks up from Louisiana. Let's go right to the map. The race to 270. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  winning six states and Washington, DC, 62 electoral votes. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  winning 15 states, 162 electoral votes, in the march to 270. We do have a Senate projection. ABC News can report that in the state of New York, Senator Kirsten Gillibrand will seek, will serve a third term. She was seeking a third term. She wins it handily tonight. She'll return to the Senate. Let's get right to the team here. Rick, what are you seeing there in North Carolina?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE NEW YORK\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, North Carolina's one we have our eyes on. It seems like every - serve is being met with a volley as we get into the numbers here. Look at Raleigh, Wake County, that's a place where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  needs to be doing very well. She's doing almost exactly what she needs to be doing and of course very well in the bluer parts of the research triangle area. But when you get out into Trump country, it looks like Trump numbers. It's really remarkable to see these numbers tracking how close they are, within a tenth of a percentage point of our FiveThirtyEight numbers, and just about within a percentage point or so of what we've seen in the past, with past Democrats.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We've been talking a little about Nash County. Up to almost 90% reporting there in something of a bellwether county. A potential swing county. Biden was able to eke out a victory here. Trump is now on the other side of a potential win in that part of the state. It's really interesting because you can see the candidates' strategies in action with Harris seemingly effectively driving up the vote in some more liberal areas. We're waiting on a lot of vote right here in Mecklenburg County. That is Charlotte.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Charlotte, yeah.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Only about 10% in right now. Too early to draw too much into what is a big part of the state, but an area where Democrats have had turnout problems in the past.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, only 10% of the vote in. What's the benchmark she needs to be hitting there?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She needs to be getting about - let's look at the number. She needs to be at 67%. She's at 63. Again, it depends on the vote type. But, and we don't really have a lot to say about it yet. But that's going to be key if she's going to overcome that deficit of now 115,000 votes with a little more than half of the vote in.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's look at Georgia, what do we see?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, so Georgia's trending a little bit worse, at least in our numbers right now, for Harris. 210,000. Trump seems to be in a pretty good position right now. But we're still waiting on so much vote. But just to take you around a little bit, the Atlanta metro area, we've seen a significant amount of vote in Fulton County. It's a place where Harris has to be winning big and she is winning big. Almost exactly hitting her benchmark. DeKalb County again, almost exactly at that number. Places where she's racking up big numbers because she's then bleeding support elsewhere in the state, including in some of the outer lying suburbs of Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doing very well in a lot of these places and just bringing up these 19,000, 20,000, 40,000 votes in all these different counties across the state. That's why you see now this - edge beginning to emerge of 211,000 votes. Gwinnett County's going to be very important here. About 8%, 9% of the entire state. Right now, Harris up about 60-40 there with only about 16% of the vote in. We'll look at this all night. These are where we think the votes are. Those big blue dots seem to suggest there's a lot there. But all those red dots add up as well. So there's still a good amount of Republican vote, and again, Trump has an edge right now...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just explain if you will, again for the audience looking, the size of those dots actually is proportional to the amount of vote that we believe is still outstanding.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, for every country, some of them are blue counties, some of them red counties, some more purple. And so if it's a 50-50 county, you're not going to get a lot of vote edge one way or the other. But this is really Atlanta and the suburbs, some of these overlapping dots, they're so big because the vote share is so large in that area. Some of the smaller blue dots. All of it adds up to a lot of vote that is still out. We're edging up to about 3/4 of the vote in. And we've seen nights like this develop where it gets harder and harder to make up a gap.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it's really interesting. A lot of what you just showed us, shows \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  hitting her benchmark or surpassing it marginally, but then \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually doing even better than what he had to do in some of these rural counties in Georgia. Reince, what are you hearing from the Trump campaign?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm hearing that they're still very excited about what they're seeing. They're cautiously optimistic about North Carolina. They feel like there's not enough there for the Harris campaign to make up the ground that they need to make up. They're happy about what's happening in Ohio, that they seem to be up by 10 at this point. Historically Ohio's been in our party sort of a mental issue, where if we're not winning by 10 in Ohio, we're worried about everything else. And that number looks good. There is some, you know, they're nervous about Pennsylvania. There's a lot of vote that's out.\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They - know that there is a ton of early vote that hasn't even been touched. So there's no arrogance about that but now all the attention's going to start moving to that blue wall. Virginia being tied is another issue that is getting them excited. They weren't expecting Virginia to be tied. There's 60% of the vote in, I think, about now in Virginia. The fact that that's tied and in the suburbs Trump did not do that badly around Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Could be a signal for other states. The Harris campaign not expecting to lose Virginia. But Rick, can you show us the map that gives us the proportional look at the outstanding vote in Virginia?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIRGINIA\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and - look, there's plenty of Democratic vote. This is the Washington, DC suburbs. This is Richmond in the area. This is Norfolk, Virginia Beach area. Lots of Democrats. Some of these are lighter blue, which means there could be more of a mix. And we still have a lot of vote left from Roanoke and other redder parts of the state. Yeah, you add it all up and Reince is right to point this out, because right now there's something going on where you're just not seeing the kind of numbers that you would expect in some of these suburban areas. Biden in Prince William County 62% of the vote. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  58%. Harris is down now to 57. She's still winning, just not winning by the same kind of numbers.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And when you balance that out with the redder parts of the state where you see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  racking up the big numbers over and over again, that's how you get as close as it is right now in Virginia. And just remember how close Virginia is to North Carolina and Georgia. We're talking about similar types of voters. The fact that they're battling it out in Virginia shouldn't really be a surprise. A lot of the folks that live there are seeing some of the same media, they're seeing some of the same advertising, and they may be responding to some of the same cross pressures. Similarly as we talk about the blue wall, that Reince was mentioning in the upper Midwest, a lot of those voters have overlap as well.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which is why Reince says they're not getting ahead of themselves either, because Pennsylvania so star, at least at this point, looks like it has some promising signs for the Harris campaign and their effort to try to reach suburban voters in the collar counties around Philadelphia, which you had showed us. She seems to be doing well, - in Allegheny County as well. Where do those numbers stand?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, so I can show you Allegheny, which is Pittsburgh. That is just a Democratic stronghold right now. Used to be even stronger Democratic but you've seen the numbers get a little bit higher with the last couple of elections and with about half the vote in right now in the Pittsburgh area. The numbers are getting hit. The FiveThirtyEight benchmark would suggest she's running ahead of where she needs to go. You mentioned the Philadelphia suburbs. Still not a lot of vote in places like Chester County, but Harris is getting at least close to her numbers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Montgomery County hitting a little bit above it right now. And Bucks I'm still watching because that kind of margin, if that carries through the night, would be really surprising and would bode well for her chances. We're still waiting on about 3/4 of the vote in Philadelphia, which is just a monster in that state. There's so much vote out there. And as you'd expect, Harris is carrying it handily. 112,000 votes that she's getting out of the city of Philadelphia. That's almost about 2/3 or 3/4 of the margin she has in the entire state right now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's swing over to Rachel Scott at the other board. And Rachel, you're looking at the Senate race, I know Bob Casey looking to hold on to his seat. Famous name in Pennsylvania, obviously, his father in politics as well many years ago. \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  challenging him. And where does that race stand? I saw a moment ago it looked like he was underperforming Harris in some respects, at least in Allegheny County.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, let's take a look at where it stands statewide right now. This is how we can compare how the presidential candidates are doing at the top of the ticket, to the Senate candidates in this race. So you have Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  statewide 52%. Again, very early, 30% of the vote in right now. Bob Casey also at 52. You mentioned the area around Pittsburgh, Allegheny County. Let's take a look at where that stands and how these two compare here. You have Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  65%. Bob Casey 65 as well. Also going to be looking at Scranton. He's from Scranton. Home to President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can see he's performing a little bit better than Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  here. In the final stretch, Bob Casey has been running ads in this state,