y are completely done.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So they've got rotating crews going round the clock to count the votes. Most of the counties tell me they hope to have it done at some point tomorrow afternoon. One thing to look at is Erie County. At last glance they have only about 1% in. Officials there telling me there is massive voter turnout and because there was a snafu with their mail in ballots, the vendors sent them to the wrong addresses, some people didn't get them, I was told that counting the mail in ballots could take additional days, possibly even weeks. So that could take a long time. And of course, Erie County is a pivotal swing county that has swung from side to side over the past several elections.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, one of those boomerang counties we'll be watching very closely here up in the Northwestern part of Pennsylvania. Matt, thank you. Let's go to the wall here, you can see the three blue wall states up there right now. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with the lead early in the night in all three of those states, Pennsylvania, Michigan and Wisconsin. All about the same margin, by the way, 52% to 47% in Pennsylvania. Similar lead in Michigan. And with 18% of the vote in, in Wisconsin, 51 to 47. Linsey, I know we're getting some exit poll data from voters, how independents are breaking in these so-called three blue wall states or rust belt states, as they're called.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And obviously the independent voter highly coveted by both campaigns. It's not boding so well for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for those independent voters in Pennsylvania. You see 24% of those voters, pretty significant amount, about a quarter of those voters in Pennsylvania identify as independent and they are breaking by six points for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He actually is doing six points better this election than he did in 2020. But Michigan and Wisconsin a little bit more hope for the Harris campaign. She's actually leading when it comes to the independent voters by three points compared to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WISCONSIN\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Wisconsin more of the same there where she's leading by four points with the independent votes. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doing five points better with the independent voters in Wisconsin in 2020. He's doing one point better in Michigan when it comes to the independent voter.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary Bruce, this makes they think of you and your reporting these past couple days, the Harris campaign telling you that this was going to be very close, and they said they knew that.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that is what we are seeing here tonight. Very close, still very tight and a long way to go. That is the message coming out of the Harris campaign tonight. I think they are encouraged and buoyed by what they are seeing in Pennsylvania. I think when it comes to Georgia, I'm hearing that they are pointing particularly to the suburban support that they're getting there. They say they are tracking slightly above expectations among suburban early voters in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They also are pointing to the rural turnout in Georgia. They say they're not seeing signs of some surging rural turnout. They of course are waiting for results from those larger rural counties. But look, they have said all along their most straightforward path is to win the blue wall states, Michigan, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin. They have put a lot of energy and effort into those three states. But again, they always said this was going to be a tight race that was going to be won in the margins.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) John?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, there are clearly troubling signs for Democrats throughout the sun belt. Whether it's in the non-battleground states like Virginia or like Florida, or whether it's in the battleground states, Georgia and North Carolina. Look, anybody can still win there, but you don't see any sign that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is running away with anything. What I just heard from a top Democrat who is following this as close as anybody, it was always going to come down to the blue wall. Everything else is gravy.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Think about what that means, David. If those are the only states that she wins in the battleground, you get to 270 electoral votes for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She wins because she won a single electoral vote in one of two states that divides their votes. The city of Omaha in Nebraska. And that's what they're looking at right now. I mean, look, we have a long, long way to go. But that ultimately is looking more and more like the path that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going to need to get elected president.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And part of that coalition if she hopes to get to 270 tonight includes the independent voters. I would think that seeing the way they're breaking in Pennsylvania at least early in the preliminary exits is not terribly encouraging, though independents, Linsey was pointing out, in Michigan and Wisconsin are breaking her way. Again, according to the preliminary exit polls. In fact we've got some great new numbers coming in from Wisconsin that we can dig into and really see how both campaigns are doing here right after the break. Don't go away. ABC News live coverage of the presidential race continues. Wisconsin up on deck here. We're going to look at the numbers from that state in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we're live in the battleground zones as election night 2024 continues on ABC.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2024\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Harris/Walz, Trump/Vance. Election night 2024 continues. Here again, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Here's something to keep in mind. Four out of the last six presidential elections, the state of Wisconsin was decided by less than one percentage point, which is why you feel like that pit in your stomach, no matter what side you're on tonight, as you look at the map coming together. A very competitive race. And it would look like, Rick, if the Harris campaign has a path to 270 it's looking more and more like it's got to be through that blue wall, the rust belt, and Wisconsin. Let's take a look at where those numbers stand tonight. It's funny when you think back, you know, Biden won it by, what, 21,000 votes, four years earlier, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had won it by 23,000 votes. This is a state that does go back and forth. It's sort of the bellwether state of the country.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and these are the first numbers we're seeing tonight. About a quarter of the vote in. Big caveat, we don't know exactly what vote it is. We think a lot of it is actually election day vote. We don't think we've seen any of the early vote from Milwaukee, which of course is the biggest city. Starting with the way the Democrats look, they have to get big numbers out of Milwaukee for starters, and that seems to be happening so far. Harris pretty close to what we think she would need to do in the city of Milwaukee to be competitive across the state with about 40% of the vote in.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Madison is just a huge vote engine for Democrats. She's getting close to that FiveThirtyEight benchmark but not quite there, running a little bit below Biden's numbers from four years ago. But here's some big news, and hat tip to Reince Priebus, Mr. Wisconsin over there, for highlighting some of this. In Kenosha, which is a swingier part of the state, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  carried it just by 50%, 50.7% last time around. He's up seven points. Harris down five points from where she was. That's significantly below the benchmark. This is one we've been looking for as well in the western part of the state, Sauk County.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Again, Harris a little below the benchmark. Up here, Door County, this is one that people love to talk about as the classic bellwether type county. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  right now a little up in this county. The numbers are small. We're not talking about huge counties. But as the numbers come in we're seeing Trump continue to hit numbers. Waukesha County, which is in the suburbs there. Trump just slightly below the benchmark. A lot of vote still to go. Less than a third of it in, and a lot of it is going to be the early vote. It looks like it's going to be a tight race there. And you're right, David, especially if Georgia fades a little bit toward \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and North Carolina trends there as well, that really leaves Harris with that path that you guys were just talking about that includes Pennsylvania, Michigan and Wisconsin, all of which she's leading now at least numerically.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But it's not an easy path if that's your only path. And Wisconsin, I took note of all the counties you just showed. She's almost hitting her benchmark but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is surpassing his benchmark. They're small counties, we're not talking about huge amounts of vote, but in every one of those counties you just showed, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was slightly above his benchmark in each of those Wisconsin counties. Let's go to Deborah Roberts on the ground in Wisconsin. What are authorities telling you there?\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, David. Once again they're expecting that Wisconsin will keep us on the edge of our seats until late into the night, because as you mentioned, those late - absentee ballots tend to come in a little later and that happens in Milwaukee. They get a lot of those absentee ballots. But where I am here tonight in Madison, which is a university town, might be the one place that Harris may be able to pick up a little bit of good news because the \uc2 University of Wisconsin\uc2  is here and those young voters, those first-time voters tell me today that they're really energized. I spent the morning at \uc2 University of Wisconsin\uc2  in the classroom of Professor Allison Prasch. She teaches a presidential rhetoric class.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the students there told me they became more energized over the last couple of weeks because they just didn't like what they were hearing from the Trump campaign. Also I should tell you that this particular city has just seen tremendous growth over the last few years and a lot of those folks who have moved in here have been highly educated, blue leaning voters. So this is becoming a much deeper blue part of the state too. So that might cut into some of those margins that Trump might have in those red counties. So this might be the place where Harris might find a little bit of good news with those youth voters like around the country, they've been standing in lines today, waiting to vote.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So we'll see what it actually produces as the night goes on. But waiting for those absentee ballots to be processed. That is the thing that takes so long. So that will keep us sort of waiting throughout the night. But they tell us that it's moving along smoothly. They had a small snafu earlier but it was just a small technical glitch. They think that's going to move along. So Milwaukee and of course Madison will probably deliver well for her. And the big question is going to be how big, because as you said, 20,000 votes or so for Biden last time. Can she do better than that? That's what she's going to have to probably do to feel solid here in Wisconsin. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Deborah Roberts in Madison. Deborah, appreciate it. 28% of the vote in. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  up by about 41,000 votes. Reince, got about a minute before we have to hit a break here. What do you make of what you saw? I saw \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  surpassing his benchmarks that he needed to ever so slightly but in each one of those counties Rick showed.\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, absolutely. Rick showed Kenosha, Wisconsin where I grew up. That always was a Democrat town. That's where Chrysler, American Motors was. 57% is huge. He was at 50% in 2020. About 47% in 2016. The key for Wisconsin right now, though, is Madison, Dane County, that blue in the middle. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can stay above 24%, I think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going to have a tough time. The difference in 2016 and 2020 was about 80,000 votes, which was a difference, it was one percentage point. And he's running two percentage...\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 270 TO WIN\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And good evening, everyone. I'm Kyra Phillips. You are watching ABC News and the race to the White House. ABC News now projecting Trump will win North Dakota, South Dakota and Texas. ABC News also projecting Harris will win Colorado, Illinois and New York. New York coming in, 28 electoral votes. Here's each candidates and where they stand right now. 91 going to Harris. 162 to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . As you know, to win, a candidate needs 270. And one more note from New York.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW YORK PROPOSAL 1\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The equal rights proposition, which adds new antidiscrimination protections to the state's constitution, is projected to pass. New York is one of the ten states with abortion related initiatives on the ballot. Let's check in now on battleground Pennsylvania. We see \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  at 50%. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  49.1. It's close. 41% of the vote that's in. Let's head to Matt Gutman. He's in Philly tonight. Matt, we're seeing roughly 22% of the vote now. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  seems to be holding on to her lead over the former president. As we know, Pennsylvania has the most electoral votes of any swing state, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 19 of them, Kyra. And the math doesn't work for either candidate unless they win Pennsylvania. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  jumped out to a very big and early lead. That has dwindled to what we are seeing right now. And this is why there's so much focus tonight on the count, when it will be done. Governor Josh Shapiro telling me two days ago and coming out again saying we aim first to be accurate. That is the most important. But everybody knows here that there is this dissonance at play. They've got to be fast and accurate. In 2020 it took them five days to tabulate the entire vote. They now hope to do it in a much shorter amount of time. All the counties here have committed to not stopping the count until they are fully done, every vote is tabulated. Kyra.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our Matt Gutman. In historic Philadelphia. Thanks, Matt. I'm Kyra Phillips. Glad you're with us tonight. I hope you'll stay with ABC News. Our team is bringing you election results all throughout the night, led by David Muir. Don't go far. Our race for the White House coverage continues.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEVADA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, it is now 10:00 pm in the East, 7:00 pm in the West. Polls are now closed in 44 states, including the swing state of Nevada with its six electoral votes. As those numbers start coming in, we'll go right back to the Nevada board. At this hour, all seven battleground states are now closed. And ABC News can take a look at the numbers coming in from each one. Georgia, 79% of the vote is in. They said they would have the vote in tonight. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in that state. We'll dive in on whether or not she can make that up anywhere in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also, North Carolina tonight, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He leads 52 to 46. 16 electoral votes on the line. 66% of the vote in. We'll dive into where that vote remains. In Pennsylvania at this hour, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  holding on to a very slim lead, in the very important state, Keystone State. 19 electoral votes. Very difficult to win the White House without the state of Pennsylvania. As you can see, about 43% of the vote in at this hour. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with the slightest of leads there. In Michigan at this hour, another battleground state, part of that blue wall that the Democrats are hoping to keep. 15 electoral votes. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with a lead over former president Trump, 466,000 votes to his 419,000. Just 16% of the vote in at this hour.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WISCONSIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wisconsin, we were looking at a moment ago, Wisconsin, the numbers starting to come in tonight. Very close race there. About a point between them, and as I mentioned, four out of the last six presidential elections decided in that state by less than a point. Arizona tonight, another key battleground state. The numbers not in yet. The polls have closed. 11 electoral votes on the line there. We do have states ABC News is ready to project at this hour. Let's take a look.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UTAH, PROJECTED WINNER TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MONTANA PROJECTED WINNER TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COLORADO PROJECTED WINNER HARRIS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Utah tonight, 6 electoral votes going to former president Trump. Montana, the four electoral votes going to Trump there. We're gonna watch that Senate race very closely. The Democrat, John Tester, trying to hold on in a state Trump has now won repeatedly. Colorado, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wins the state of Colorado. Nine electoral votes goes to the Vice President. So take a look at the map. The road to 270. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  winning seven states and Washington, DC. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  winning 17 states, 172 electoral votes for him, up to 91 for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  at this hour. Our coverage of the 2024 presidential election continues right now.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 This is an ABC News special. The time has finally come. Right now, all across America. Votes are being counted. Who will be your next president? On this historic night, with the stakes so high, live from ABC News election headquarters in New York City, now reporting, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2024\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back. ABC News live election night headquarters here in New York City, and we are getting not only numbers in but more exit polling data from the key battlegrounds. Linsey, what do you have?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEVADA\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, well so, interestingly enough, the Harris campaign has really doggedly pursued the Latino vote. We've seen that really ratchet up in particular in recent weeks. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  equally. He was in Pennsylvania recently. Just last weekend, he said I love the Latinos. He was in New Mexico a few days ago, he said, I love the Hispanics. And it - appears that there is some appeal there for the Latino voters when it comes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . If you look at the race in Nevada in particular, the Latino vote making up 17% of the electorate there. Here we go. 48% going for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  there in Nevada. 46% for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is actually doing four points better oh, actually, I'm sorry, that's in Arizona. Harris is doing four points worse than Biden in Arizona, and Trump doing four points better than he did in 2020, if we want to look at that board there in Arizona. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  getting 55% of the Latino vote. They make up more than a quarter percent of the population in Arizona. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  getting 41%. But, in both cases, he's doing much better. Trump, 11 points better than he did in 2020 in Nevada. And, again, four points better than he did in Arizona in 2020.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this is exactly what the Trump campaign wanted to do, was to peel away some of the Latino support that President Biden got four years ago. And Martha, you traveled through Nevada, and through the Sun Belt, and what did you hear?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think what I heard was surprising in some ways. You think, oh, the Latino vote is going to go to the Democrats. Absolutely not. That's not the impression you got on the ground. You had unions out there canvassing for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . But it all came back, to the voters I spoke to, to the economy. And even those Democrats whose were out canvassing for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  knew that was an issue, like, how do you explain to people the economy that people trust \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  more on that. And they said, well, we tell them it just takes time.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So that message was not really resonating. Nevada has the highest unemployment rate of any state in the country. They were devastated after COVID, and that is the issue, you know, you can talk about exaggeration. You can talk about them calling Puerto Ricans garbage. You can talk about all those things. But it came back to the economy. They can hear lies. They can hear mistruths. They can hear exaggerations, but their truth is their grocery bills, their gas bills, and that's what it came back to on many, many people I interviewed.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the high cost of housing.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. Very, very high cost of rents. And immigration, of course. And you could talk to Latino voters. There was one man, he said I absolutely, I don't care about any of that other stuff. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to be best for the border. This is a man who came into the country 50 years ago illegally, he is now a citizen. He doesn't want any more migrants, and that's why he's voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, really interesting. And part of what Linsey was showing us, she still is leading among Latino voters, but not by the margin that President Biden did four years ago, and that's got to be very concerning for the Democrats and the Harris campaign, Mary.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, look, I would just remind over and over again that they had said, from the very beginning, and they are saying again tonight, their most straight forward path is through those blue wall states. When you look at what we're seeing coming in, that certainly seems to still be the case. And I suspect is still their hope. The Harris campaign is well aware of all those issues, especially in Nevada. That's why she unveiled her housing plan in that state. She knew that she had to hammer home the issue of the economy. And make clear that that was going to be her top priority. The lowering the cost of groceries was going to be the very first thing or very first priority when she gets into office. She always said she would pass, if elected, this big economic package of bills to do just that. The question is whether that message got across and was enough.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rick Klein, what's the blue wall looking like at this point?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we've seen some numbers come in that shifted the lead. Again, numerically, because it just has to do with the pace of vote. But right now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with a 33,000 vote edge. We've gotten a lot of rural vote, we also...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is Pennsylvania. Yes. You asked earlier about Wilkes-Barre, Luzerne County. That has flipped red, at least as of now, with 57% of the vote in. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  racking up some numbers there in an area where Democrats had some hopes to build things up. You can - also see what's starting to happen in the western part of the state. We've got some significant numbers north of Pittsburgh in Butler County. This is a place where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is coming close to his benchmark number. Really, this is a function of a lot of vote coming in in the redder parts of the state. We still have a lot to go in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's kind of the, by far the biggest city. We're seeing Delco go very strongly for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She's outperforming where she would need to be. Also outperforming where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was. And the same story up through Montgomery. And this is still an interesting one. Bucks County, a lot of folks are keyed into that. A lot of vote still to go. Unclear exactly what kind of vote that is. But at least now, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  outperforming that benchmark