d then again, right here, David, he's already performing really well here. While he was campaigning here in Reading, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was in Allentown. This is Lehigh County. You can see just how well she's doing here. She's ahead by 6,000 votes or so, but still a lot of vote left to be counted. 43% in right now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks, Rachel. Rick, one of the things that's very telling is the map with the circular diagram that shows you where the vote still remains. Can you show us Pennsylvania at this hour where the vote still has to be tallied? I want to take this over to Donna and Reince. The one that shows the outstanding vote.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. You can see that's Philadelphia and the suburbs, - a good amount of vote in Pittsburgh as well. When you see a purple dot, that's more of a battleground county. So it's probably not going to advantage either candidate, but there's a significant amount of Republican votes still outstanding as well. It looks like it's going to be a close state.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The battle for Pennsylvania, Donna, what do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) First of all, I've taken my meditation early, because once again, Democrats are going to come in late. They're going to come in late in Pennsylvania. And clearly in Georgia, what's happening is Democrats are not over performing in DeKalb and Cobb and Fulton. You have to over perform, because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing four points better in some of the rural areas. So, again, Democratic cities, they tend to come in late.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm seeing that pattern once again. I have good information that Philadelphia's going to come in much stronger than in 2020. And by the way, in some of these areas you can compare apples to apples, but \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has had to build a different coalition. So I'm also looking in some of these suburbs. If the issue of abortion, the threat of democracy, if it worked in those areas in terms of pulling out her vote.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And those are the collar counties around Philadelphia. Can we take another look at that? Based on Donna's point here, whether or not Harris is having success in those counties, getting the numbers up to where they need to be.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, - for sure. Chester County is a key one there, it's about 5% of the whole statewide total, not a lot of information in yet. We're seeing Harris run a little below where she'd need to do to hold there. Montgomery County, and outperforming so far, again, with less than a third of the vote in. And I've kept my eye on Bucks to try to track this, because this is good news if you're the Harris campaign. Delco, similar story. And, as Donna said with Philadelphia, one of the interesting little quirks is how Clinton's vote share actually went down a little bit with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Biden wins the state. But Clinton actually did a little bit better in the - city of Philadelphia, even though it was elsewhere in the state that - he was able to make up votes.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So what kind of coalition is built becomes really important through all of this. You can also look at some of the more battleground, closely divided places. Again, it's hard to know how much of the vote is election day versus vote by mail. There's still a lot of it that is the in person vote that might shift some of these numbers. These are not horrible numbers. You asked me earlier about Erie County, that boomerang county. Very little of the vote in right now, but this just looks like it's a battle.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A real battle. Reince, what do you make of what you're seeing in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think it's going to take a long time. We're just going to have to be patient with Pennsylvania. They're pretty slow at getting these results in. Even though not as much as 2020, just a boat load of early votes that still need to be counted. A lot in Philadelphia County. So I'm a little bit slow to opine on Pennsylvania, but I, I do think things are looking very interesting south of Pennsylvania in Virginia, still. And I think that's something that we're, it's not getting a lot of attention yet, but I think people are going to start focusing on Virginia.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The closeness of Virginia. Let's take a look, Rick. Where does Virginia stand right now?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is just one of those really strange ones to see a 4,000 vote edge. A lot of votes left in the Democratic areas, those are our bluer dots. But it's just not, one of the things that strikes me, David, looking at the map as it's filled in so far, the fact that the Democrats are sweating some of the places that should have been safe. We haven't made a projection in New Hampshire. We haven't made a projection in Connecticut. Rhode Island, we talked about earlier, closer than we thought. Of course, Virginia, these are places that Democrats flat out have to win if they're gonna have a chance...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Take us through some of those states, let's see where New Hampshire stands. We saw some tightening in the polls leading up to election day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we did, and this is a state that we kind of thought would be called earlier in the night, 'cause they have a pretty true tradition. Most of the Democratic votes are in the southern part of the state. And, again, Harris is leading. The question is going to be really how much she wins by...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Outperforming herself.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Outperforming there, but to me, the fact that we're even talking about states like this tells you how things are at least tipping early in the night. And the southern states, clearly there's an edge right now in Georgia and North Carolina. North Carolina, I've been tracking a lot. 230,000 vote margin. That's hard to make up when you're at 70% of the vote. The thing that is keeping it out of anyone's win column is Mecklenburg County, that's Charlotte. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is winning there, she's not hitting the FiveThirtyEight benchmark number. But you want to see a really big dot emerge. There it is. That's almost half a million votes that are still out in the Charlotte area. But if she's winning at 60-40 instead of 68-20, or 68-30, that means you're just not getting as many votes out of it. That makes it hard to catch up against that number.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No question about that. We're going to check in with Trump headquarters in Florida and Harris headquarters, see what the mood is on the ground at this point in the night. And we're gonna keep digging into these Rust Belt states. Pennsylvania, Michigan, Wisconsin. Because that appears to be the path, as we continue into the night, at least for the Democrats to keep this alive, as Rick Klein points out, and smartly so, the reason North Carolina can't be called is because of the outstanding vote in Charlotte, which is a Democratic area. But given the margin between the two, it might be difficult to make that up. We'll keep our eyes on the map and we'll get right back to it after the break.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 You're watching ABC News live coverage of election night 2024. Here again, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MISSISSIPPI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE- OHIO\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to ABC News election night headquarters in New York. We have some races to call at this hour. We can project that in Mississippi, the winner of that race, former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  picks up six electoral votes in Mississippi. He also wins Ohio, 17 electoral votes. Used to be a battleground state. Trump has won Ohio before. He wins it again. Of course, we're watching the Ohio Senate race right now. The Democrat, Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , seeking a fourth term, trailing businessman Bernie Moreno, who's never run before.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE- MARYLAND\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Colombian roots. He leads by almost five points here. It's going to be a difficult race to make up that kind of ground with 76% of the vote in. We're gonna keep our eye on that. We do have another race to call, and a race that makes history tonight, in Maryland, voters have elected Democrat Angela Alsobrooks to serve in the Senate. Republican \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , the popular governor there, had been challenging her for that open seat. Angela Alsobrooks becomes the next senator from the state of Maryland. And Linsey Davis, she makes history, and a lot of history made tonight. She'll be the second black woman elected to the Senate on this election night.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're talking about Carol Moseley Braun, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , obviously. Prior to this day, in our country's nearly 250 year history, you'd only had two black women ever elected to the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 . And tonight you have two more. What's really significant I think, to put this into broader perspective, is we've still never had a black female govern a state in this country. So tonight, the Democratic party is asking American citizens to do something that they haven't even done on a state level. They're asking to vote for this woman to run and rule and reign over this entire country.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which it just really puts into perspective that we're just now getting to this point of having four black women elected into the Senate. It was interesting when I had a chance to speak with Angela Alsobrooks just last week. She talked about how her family is from South Carolina. And it was because her great-grandfather had been shot by a white sheriff's deputy that they ran, they fled to Maryland, and that's what made her decide she wanted to be a prosecutor and ultimately get into politics.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Donna Brazile, Angela Alsobrooks has talked about how as a young prosecutor, she got advice from another prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was elected prosecutor in 2004. She mentored Angela Alsobrooks. But I also have to take a moment of personal pride. She was my first intern on Capitol Hill. So I'm very excited about that. But she's been a terrific executive. In both her primary campaign, she was outraised almost 10-1, as well as in the general election. Look, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  is a great guy. People love him in Maryland. But they understood a vote for Mr. Hogan was a vote for \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and a vote for the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Angela had her message focused on the economy, the issue of abortion, because abortion was on the ballot in the great state of Maryland, but she also had a special appeal, I think, to young women across the state. That's going to be the hidden story tonight after my friend next to me finishes boogie woogying around all of the counties.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Looks like a long night ahead. And Rachel Scott, you're looking at the Senate. Not only is this a win for Angela Alsobrooks, she makes history. But I flashed up that Ohio Senate race, \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  does not look like it's going to be a good night for him.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, this is going to be really hard to make up, as you already said. 78% of the vote that is already in. And you can see that Moreno is ahead right now by 239,000 votes, roughly, compared to \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 . I've been studying sort of how he won the last time around. That was 2018. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not running, so this is the first time that \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , the last remaining statewide Democrat in the state, is really trying to outrun the top of the ticket. I want you to pay attention to some of these counties. Because these are areas, they're red now, but these were blue in 2018.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) These are two counties in which \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  did really well in, in 2018. Now they're red. You can see how well Moreno is doing here. He's ahead by 4,000 votes. \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  underperforming where he was in 2018. And you can see Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  at 41%. This is obviously a red state here. \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , the last remaining Democrat, if he is able to, to carve this one out, it would be pretty remarkable here. We're still waiting for a good number of votes here in Franklin County, which is home to Columbus.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What does that say, John, about the shifting nature of not only Ohio, but you're looking at Florida as well, and \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  very popular in that state. Obviously not enough to win tonight, but the margin became smaller and smaller each time he ran.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and look, this shows really tough night as it's shaping up to be for Democrats in the Senate. I mean, the Democrats talked about 