t's a long process and a late process. We talked to the chairperson of the - Wisconsin Elections Commission. I mean, she tells us that we can expect once again a long night. Wisconsin is one of those states that doesn't begin to process and tabulate those votes until the morning of election day, which is today. And that is really critical when it comes to those absentee ballots. As you said, a lot of them to tabulate, particularly in Milwaukee, the largest city in this state.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And they do this in a very exacting kind of manner where they lay out the votes and they crease them and they process them through the machine, and it just takes a very long time. They had a little bit of a snafu earlier today. They said they rectified that, they've made up some time. But they expect it's going to be a late night here in Wisconsin, and those votes might very well determine how much of a margin Harris will have here, if she continues on this path to being ahead.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Madison was also a handful of places today that received bomb threats at polling sites. What more can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, there have been a number of places, as you know, that have had this kind of thing around the country, and even reports of people getting phone calls today here in Madison, voters, who said that they were very strange calls. People asking personal information. And then these bomb threats at a couple of polling locations. Schools that were, of course, closed, but polling locations. Turned out to be nothing. But once again, still concerning. Election officials say it was just mischief. And they said Ann Jacobs, who is the head of the election commission for the state, said it was just - mischief but bad mischief. But they didn't know what they were up to. So, so far a night that seems to be moving right along, but it's going to be a late one.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It sure is. We're glad to have you, Deborah Roberts. Thank you. I'm Kyra Phillips. Glad you're all with us tonight. I hope you'll stay with ABC News. We'll be bringing you election results all night long, so don't go far. Our coverage of the race to the White House continues.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 This is an ABC News special, election night in America. Who will be the next president of the United States? From ABC News headquarters in New York, here again, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to ABC News live election night coverage, 10:30 here in the East, 7:30 in the West. We're 30 minutes away from polls closing in California, Washington, Oregon, and Idaho. California, of course, with 54 electoral votes at stake. You're looking at the electoral value sort of, of the portion of the map. 270 to win. Let's take you to the floor and show you where things stand at this hour. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with 91 electoral votes. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with 201. You need 270 to win. He's picked up 19 states tonight. She's picked up eight states and Washington, DC. Let's get right to Trump headquarters, Palm Beach Convention Center. John Santucci, who's covered \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for us for quite some time. What's the mood there, John, and who's with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANTUCCI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, - you can hear the mood here at the convention center. They're going through results, and the crowd here supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , very excited. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not here, David, at this convention center. He's over at Mar-a-Lago, where David, I'm told he's been gathered with senior staff and family in a war room that's been set up at his club, going through results. And just in the last couple of minutes, David, he actually went into the grand ballroom at Mar-a-Lago, according to reporting from my colleague, Katherine Faulders and I. Where he actually went around and greeted supporters and donors that are there.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANTUCCI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Our sources tell us, David, that in addition to donors and club members, he's there with his new campaign surrogates, billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and RFK Jr. And in addition to the family, David, who's been advising and campaigning with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , his eldest sons, Don Jr. and Eric, there with Dad tonight, \uc2 Barron Trump\uc2  also. And a photo, David, that Melania Trump, the former first lady, just tweeted in the last couple of minutes, Barron who turned 18 this year, casting a vote for his father earlier today.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) John Santucci with some color inside Trump headquarters, Palm Beach Convention Center. Thank you. Let's go right over to Eva Pilgrim at the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  headquarters, \uc2 Howard University\uc2  in Washington, what's the mood there?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, they're watching the numbers as they come in right now. They are watching a speech from Angela Alsobrooks, who was just declared the winner in that Maryland Senate seat. You can see the crowd here cheering. The flags, those are American flags. Those flags back there, I just want to point out, those are college students in that back area. They're sort of pinned in back there. They've been here since very early. They got here, and they've been cheering and excited. I wish you could hear earlier. There was loud music. Everyone was dancing everywhere they are. We've been speaking with the campaign as they've seen these numbers come in tonight.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And they've sort of stuck with what they told us yesterday when we last spoke with them, which was the idea that this event here tonight